,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336843058,"data":"       | We need to see if we can assign edges such that each low cell's degree satisfied, while each high cell's degree not exceeded. Since total capacity 7200 \u003e 6960, there is slack of 240 edges. So seems plausible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336844507,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336846601,"data":"       | Thus S could be N+1? But earlier we found for small grids, max S = N, not N+1. For 2x2 (N=4), can we achieve S=5? Let's test via brute force: we computed max S=4. So S=N+1 not possible for N=4. Let's analyze why capacity argument fails for small case.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336848112,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336849393,"data":"       | For N=4 (2x2), degrees: all cells degree 2 (corners). A = {1,2} (size 2). Place them on cells (both corners). Total degree = 2+2=4. B = {3,4} capacity = 2*2=4. So capacity matches. Yet arrangement impossible? Let's test: need each low cell (1,2) adjacent to high cells only. In 2x2 grid, each corner adjacent to two other corners (both low/high). Actually each corner adjacent to two other corners (since all cells are corners). So each low cell will be adjacent to both other cells, which includes low cell 2 maybe. So can't avoid low-low adjacency because each corner's neighbors are the other two corners (both adjacent). So the graph structure prevents low cells from being only adjacent to high cells because the grid's adjacency pattern includes edges among low cells inevitably due to geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336851738,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336852763,"data":"       | Thus we need more refined condition: The induced subgraph on low-number cells must have no edges (i.e., low cells must form an independent set). So we need to place low numbers on an independent set of the grid graph. The maximum size of an independent set in a bipartite grid is ceil(N/2). For even N, maximum independent set size = N/2 (since bipartite). Indeed we can color chessboard; each color class has N/2 cells, and they are independent (no edges within same color). So we can place low numbers on one color (say black) and high numbers on white. Then any edge connects low-high. This ensures sums are at least S if we choose S appropriately.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336854868,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336855903,"data":"       | Thus to achieve S \u003e N, we need to assign low numbers to one color class and high numbers to the other. Then any edge sum = low + high. The minimal sum would be min_{low in low set} low + min_{high in high set} high = smallest low + smallest high. Since low numbers are the smallest half, high numbers are the largest half. So smallest low = 1, smallest high = N/2 + 1 = 1801. Sum = 1802. That's far less than N=3600. Wait we need sum \u003e= S. So S cannot exceed 1802 if we use this bipartition. But we can assign numbers differently across colors, not necessarily half-half.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336858001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336859051,"data":"       | Goal: maximize minimal sum. Since edges always connect opposite colors, the minimal sum will be min over pairs (b,w) where b in black, w in white, and they are adjacent. If we assign numbers to colors such that the smallest number in each color is as large as possible, we can increase minimal sum. The best we can do is to assign the smallest numbers to one color and the largest numbers to the other color, but then the minimal sum is small (1 + (N/2+1)). Not good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336860864,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336862159,"data":"       | Instead we want to balance numbers across colors to maximize the minimal sum of adjacent pairs. Since each edge sum is b + w, the minimal sum will be determined by the smallest b plus smallest w among adjacent pairs. Since each black cell is adjacent to up to 4 white cells, the minimal sum could be as low as min_{b} (b + min_{w neighbor of b} w). To maximize minimal sum, we want to avoid having a black cell with small b adjacent to a white cell with small w. So we could assign small numbers to black cells that are far from each other? But adjacency always cross colors, so any black cell's neighbors are white cells. So if we place small numbers on both colors, there could be an edge connecting two small numbers. To avoid that, we need to ensure that the set of small numbers is placed entirely on one color (so they are not adjacent to each other). Then the minimal sum would be smallest small number + smallest large number (since all white cells have large numbers). That sum may be high.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336865826,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.336866863,"data":"       | Thus the optimal arrangement likely puts the smallest 1800 numbers on one color (say black) and the largest 1800 numbers on the other color (white). Then any adjacent pair consists of a small number (≤1800) and a large number (≥1801). The minimal sum would be 1 + 1801 = 1802. But we can perhaps improve by not using exactly half half; we could move some moderate numbers across colors to increase the smallest sum.\n"}
