,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337121348,"data":"       | Let's compute exact max S for 4x4 via exhaustive search? 16! ~ 2e13 impossible. But we can use integer programming maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337122638,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.3371237,"data":"       | But we can try to compute theoretical bound: For any labeling, consider the smallest number a. Its neighbors must be at least S - a. Since a is smallest, S - a is at most S - 1. So neighbors must be at least S-1. The maximum possible neighbor value is N. So we need S-1 ≤ N, i.e., S ≤ N+1. That's trivial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33712738,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337128448,"data":"       | But we need stronger bound: consider the two smallest numbers a=1, b=2. If they are placed on opposite colors, they could be adjacent, giving sum 3 \u003c S. So to avoid that, they must be placed on same color (so not adjacent). Similarly, we need to avoid adjacency of any pair of numbers whose sum \u003c S. So we need to find a partition of numbers into two color classes such that any pair (x,y) with x+y \u003c S are placed on same color (nonadjacent). This is reminiscent of graph coloring problem: we need a 2-coloring of numbers where edges represent \"dangerous\" pairs (sum \u003c S). Since grid adjacency is bipartite, we can assign colors to cells. So the condition is that the graph of dangerous pairs must be bipartite with each part fitting into one color class.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337130944,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337132177,"data":"       | Thus we need to find largest S such that the graph G_S with vertices {1..N} and edges between numbers whose sum \u003c S is bipartite (i.e., 2-colorable). Because we can assign one color class to black cells and the other to white cells, ensuring that any adjacent cells (which are opposite colors) have sum \u003e= S.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337133831,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337134859,"data":"       | Thus problem reduces to: Find maximum S such that the graph where vertices i,j are connected if i+j \u003c S is bipartite. Since the graph is complete bipartite between low numbers? Let's analyze structure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337136266,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337137292,"data":"       | Define threshold T = S-1. Edge exists between i and j if i + j ≤ T (since sum \u003c S). This graph is basically a comparability graph: pairs of small numbers sum ≤ T.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337138728,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337139748,"data":"       | Observation: This graph is a \"threshold graph\" where vertices can be ordered by value. It's known that such graph is a \"split graph\"? Actually it's a \"co-interval\"? But we can analyze bipartiteness.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33714131,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33714233,"data":"       | If we order numbers ascending, edges only between numbers that are both relatively small. For a given T, the set of vertices that can be incident to edges are those ≤ T-1 (since smallest possible sum with another positive integer). Actually if i \u003e T-1, then i + j \u003e T for any j ≥1, so i has no edges. So vertices \u003e T-1 are isolated (no edges). So the graph consists of vertices 1..T-1 with edges between pairs whose sum ≤ T.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337144272,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337145306,"data":"       | Now, is this graph bipartite? Let's examine small T.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337146558,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337147605,"data":"       | For T=4 (S=5), vertices 1..3, edges: (1,2) sum=3 ≤4, (1,3) sum=4 ≤4, (2,3) sum=5 \u003e4 not edge. So graph is a path of length 2 (1-2, 1-3). That's bipartite.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337148994,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33715004,"data":"       | For T=5 (S=6), vertices 1..4, edges: pairs with sum ≤5: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,3). That's a triangle? Let's list: (2,3) sum=5, (2,4) sum=6 \u003e5, (3,4) sum=7 \u003e5. So edges: 1 connected to 2,3,4; plus edge (2,3). This graph contains triangle? vertices 1,2,3: edges (1,2),(1,3),(2,3) =\u003e triangle, which is not bipartite. So S cannot be 6? But we have example for N=4 (2x2) achieving S=4, not 5. Wait S=5 would be N+1? For N=4, we cannot achieve S=5. Indeed max S=4. So threshold where graph becomes non-bipartite corresponds to S exceeding max possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337153116,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337154469,"data":"       | Thus the condition for existence is that the graph of \"dangerous pairs\" (sum \u003c S) must be bipartite. Since the grid graph is bipartite, we can map its two color classes to the two parts of a bipartition of the numbers. So we need to find largest S such that the dangerous graph is bipartite.\n"}
