,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337366065,"data":"       | analysisWe need to understand the problem: we have a 60x60 grid (3600 cells). Fill with numbers 1..3600 (each used exactly once). We want to maximize the minimum sum of numbers in any adjacent pair (sharing a side). So we want to arrange numbers to maximize the worst-case adjacent sum. Equivalent to find arrangement that maximizes the minimal adjacent sum S*; we need to find the maximum possible S such that there exists arrangement with all adjacent sums \u003e= S. Then we output that maximum S.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33736796,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337368981,"data":"       | We need to compute S_max.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337370067,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33737109,"data":"       | We need to think about combinatorial arrangement. This is similar to \"maximin adjacency sum\" problem. For a grid, each cell has up to 4 neighbors (except edges). The worst adjacent sum will likely involve the smallest numbers placed near each other, because they produce low sums. To maximize the minimum sum, we must avoid placing small numbers adjacent to each other, and also avoid placing a small number next to a moderately small number that sum below threshold.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33737298,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337373994,"data":"       | Essentially we need to find the largest S such that we can pair each cell with its neighbors such that each adjacent pair sum \u003e= S.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337375312,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337376331,"data":"       | Observation: The sum of all numbers is N = 1+...+3600 = 3600*3601/2 = 3600*1800.5 = 6,481,800? Let's compute: 3600*3601/2 = 3600*1800.5 = 6,481,800. Yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337377649,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33737867,"data":"       | But adjacency constraints involve edges; each adjacent pair counted twice if we sum over all edges? Actually each edge connects two cells; each edge's sum must be \u003e= S. There are total number of edges in 60x60 grid: horizontal edges: 60 rows * (60-1) = 60*59 = 3540. Vertical edges: same 3540. Total edges = 7080.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337380284,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337381512,"data":"       | If we sum over all edges the sums of the two numbers, we get each cell's number counted as many times as its degree (number of adjacent edges). For interior cells degree 4, edge cells degree 3, corner cells degree 2. Let's compute total degree sum: sum of degrees = 2*E = 2*7080 = 14160. Also sum of degrees = sum over cells of degree_i. So each number appears degree_i times in the total sum over edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337383261,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337384288,"data":"       | Let total sum over edges of numbers (i.e., sum of both numbers per edge) = sum_i degree_i * a_i, where a_i is number in cell i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337385543,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337386561,"data":"       | Thus total sum over edges = Σ degree_i a_i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337387741,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33738976,"data":"       | Since each edge sum must be \u003e= S, total sum over edges \u003e= S * E = S * 7080.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337391025,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337392046,"data":"       | Thus we have Σ degree_i a_i \u003e= 7080 S.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337393266,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337394296,"data":"       | We can compute maximum possible S using this inequality: we need to find arrangement that maximizes minimal sum; the inequality provides an upper bound: S \u003c= (Σ degree_i a_i)/7080. But Σ degree_i a_i depends on arrangement because degree_i is fixed per cell (2,3,4). So we can compute maximum possible Σ degree_i a_i by assigning larger numbers to higher-degree cells (since we want to maximize the bound). Because the inequality gives an upper bound; to get the best possible S, we want to maximize the RHS, i.e., maximize Σ degree_i a_i. Since we can assign numbers arbitrarily, we should assign the largest numbers to cells with highest degree (interior cells degree 4), next largest to edge cells degree 3, smallest to corners degree 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33739672,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.337397731,"data":"       | Thus compute the maximum possible Σ degree_i a_i = sum over cells of degree_i * assigned number, with numbers sorted descending matched to degree_i sorted descending.\n"}
