,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338646872,"data":"       | Maybe we need to place numbers such that each pair of adjacent cells includes numbers that sum to at least mn. This is reminiscent of constructing a \"complementary\" arrangement: For each cell, assign number a, and its neighbor across edge maybe assign mn+1 - a? That would make sum = mn+1, which is \u003e mn. But each cell has multiple neighbors; we cannot assign different complements to each neighbor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338648702,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338649747,"data":"       | But we can try to assign numbers such that each cell's value plus each neighbor's value \u003e= mn. This is similar to a \"graph labeling\" problem known as \"graceful labeling\"? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338651309,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338652328,"data":"       | Observation: For 2x2 grid, arrangement achieving S=4 is:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338653476,"data":"       | 1 3\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338654536,"data":"       | 4 2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338656653,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338657917,"data":"       | Here sum of opposite corners 1+2=3 (\u003c4) but they are not adjacent. Adjacent sums: 1+3=4, 1+4=5, 3+2=5, 4+2=6. So each edge includes at least one of the numbers 1,2,3,4 such that sum \u003e=4. Notably, the smallest number 1 is adjacent to 3 and 4 (both \u003e=3). The second smallest 2 is adjacent to 3 and 4 (both \u003e=2). So each small number is adjacent to relatively large numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338659731,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338660772,"data":"       | Generalizing: maybe we can place numbers such that each small number is adjacent only to numbers that are at least mn - small + something? Actually for S=mn, we need for each a, each neighbor b \u003e= mn - a. So for a small a, neighbor must be large (close to mn). For a near mn, neighbor can be small.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338662356,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.3386634,"data":"       | Thus we can think of constructing a \"gradient\" where numbers increase outward from a central region of large numbers, with small numbers at periphery, each adjacent to larger numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338664837,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338665868,"data":"       | In a large grid, we can place the largest numbers in a central block, and the smallest numbers on the outer ring, ensuring each outer cell neighbor is interior large numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33866722,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338668246,"data":"       | But we also have adjacency among outer ring cells themselves (edges along the perimeter). Those adjacent outer cells both have small numbers, which could violate sum \u003e= mn.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338669645,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338670738,"data":"       | Thus we need to ensure that along the perimeter, adjacent cells also sum \u003e= mn. That seems difficult because both may be small.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338672043,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338673346,"data":"       | Thus to achieve S=mn, we need to ensure that the smallest numbers are not adjacent to each other. In a grid, it's possible to arrange numbers such that no two small numbers are adjacent, by using a checkerboard pattern: assign small numbers to black squares, large numbers to white squares. Then any two adjacent cells are one small, one large. So sum = small + large. To guarantee sum \u003e= mn, we need for each small number a, its neighbor large number b must satisfy a + b \u003e= mn. Since we can assign large numbers arbitrarily to white squares, we can pair each small number with sufficiently large neighbor(s). However each white square may be neighbor to up to 4 black squares, so a large number may need to be large enough to accommodate up to 4 small neighbors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338675768,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338676792,"data":"       | Thus we need to assign large numbers to white squares such that each large number is at least mn - min_adjacent_small? Actually each white cell's value must be \u003e= mn - a for each adjacent black neighbor a. Since a can be as low as 1, we need white cells adjacent to 1 to be at least mn - 1 = mn-1 (i.e., 3599). So we need some white cells with values near 3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338678498,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338679545,"data":"       | But each white cell may have up to 4 black neighbors; if we place 1 at a black cell, its four white neighbors must be \u003e= mn-1 = 3599. That's possible only if we have enough numbers \u003e= 3599 (i.e., 3599 and 3600). That's only two numbers, not enough for four neighbors. Thus we cannot place 1 at a black cell with degree 4. But we could place 1 at a corner black cell (degree 2) so only two white neighbors needed, which could be 3599 and 3600. That's possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338682548,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33868361,"data":"       | Thus we need to carefully place the smallest numbers at low-degree cells (corners, edges) to reduce number of high neighbors required.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338684911,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338685973,"data":"       | Similarly, number 2 would need neighbors \u003e= mn-2 = 3598. Could place at edge black cell degree 3, requiring three neighbors \u003e= 3598; we have numbers 3598, 3599, 3600 (three numbers) available. So we could place 2 at an edge black cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338687463,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338688526,"data":"       | Thus we can assign the smallest numbers to low-degree black cells such that we have enough large numbers to satisfy neighbor constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338690258,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338691313,"data":"       | In 60x60 grid, there are 4 corners (degree 2), 236 edge cells (degree 3), interior (degree 4). Since we have two colors, each color has half of each type? Let's compute distribution of black squares among corners etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33869275,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338693986,"data":"       | In a checkerboard coloring of even dimensions, each color has equal number of cells, and corners are of same color? Let's examine 60x60 board: Since both dimensions even, the four corners are all same color? Actually on a chessboard with even rows and columns, opposite corners are same color. For 60x60, top-left (0,0) black, then top-right (0,59) is opposite parity? Since 59 is odd, (0+59) odd =\u003e white. So corners alternate colors. So each color has 2 corners (degree 2). Similarly, each color has half of edge cells: total edge cells 236, each color gets 118 edge cells (including 2 corners). So each color has 2 corners (degree 2), 118 edge cells (including corners) with degree 3 for non-corners, but corners degree 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338696275,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338697319,"data":"       | Thus each color has 2 corners (deg2), 116 edge non-corner cells (deg3), and interior cells (deg4). The number of interior cells per color: total interior cells 3364, each color gets 1682 interior cells (deg4).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338698755,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33869985,"data":"       | Thus each color's degree distribution: deg2:2, deg3:116, deg4:1682.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338701006,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338702026,"data":"       | Now we can attempt to assign smallest numbers to black squares (or white) such that each small number's required neighbor count (its degree) can be satisfied by large numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338703361,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338704403,"data":"       | We need to allocate large numbers to opposite color cells to serve as neighbors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338705625,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338706648,"data":"       | Thus we need to ensure that for each small number a placed on a black cell with degree d, we have at least d distinct large numbers (on white cells) each \u003e= mn - a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.3387092,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338710256,"data":"       | But a white cell may serve as neighbor for up to 4 black cells, but we need to ensure its value is large enough to satisfy all adjacent black cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338711551,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.338712587,"data":"       | Thus the constraints become more complex.\n"}
