,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33927036,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of graph labeling problem: we have a grid graph with 60x60 vertices, each vertex assigned distinct numbers 1..n (n=3600). Want to maximize the minimum sum across edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339271713,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339272764,"data":"       | We can think of ordering numbers such that large numbers are adjacent to small numbers may reduce sums. To maximize minimum sum, we want to avoid having small numbers adjacent to each other, because sum of two small numbers would be low. So we need to separate small numbers, perhaps place them far apart, and ensure each small number is adjacent only to relatively large numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339274436,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339275651,"data":"       | The worst-case edge sum will likely involve the smallest numbers. The minimal sum will be at most sum of the two smallest numbers that are adjacent somewhere. Since we can place them not adjacent, we can avoid that. But there will be many edges, each vertex has up to 4 neighbors. We need to ensure each vertex's neighbors have numbers such that sum \u003e= S.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339277377,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339278406,"data":"       | We can think of constructing a bipartite arrangement: grid is bipartite (checkerboard). Adjacent cells are opposite colors. So we can assign numbers to black cells and white cells. Then each edge connects a black and a white number. So the sum across any edge is sum of a black number and a white number.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339279948,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339281009,"data":"       | Thus the minimal sum across edges is min_{(b,w) edge} (b + w). Since edges connect each black cell to up to 4 white cells, the minimal sum will be determined by the smallest black number plus the smallest white number that are adjacent somewhere. But we can arrange numbers such that the smallest black numbers are placed far from the smallest white numbers (i.e., not adjacent). However due to adjacency constraints, each black cell is adjacent to up to 4 white cells; each white cell adjacent to up to 4 black cells. So each black number will be adjacent to several white numbers; the smallest black number will be adjacent to some white numbers; we can try to assign relatively large white numbers adjacent to small black numbers to keep sums high.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339283277,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339284326,"data":"       | Thus we need to assign numbers to black and white cells such that for each black cell, its sum with each of its white neighbors is \u003e= S, and similarly for each white cell with its black neighbors. Since each black cell can have up to 4 white neighbors, the condition is that for each black cell b, the minimum of its neighboring white numbers \u003e= S - b. So we need to assign white numbers such that each black cell's adjacent whites are all \u003e= S - b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33928771,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339288766,"data":"       | Similarly for each white cell w, its neighboring blacks must be \u003e= S - w.\n"}
