,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339566935,"data":"       | Let S = 3658. For degree 2 vertex (corner), if its value a, its neighbors must be \u003e= 3658 - a. Since each neighbor can be as large as 3600, we need a \u003e= 3658 - 3600 = 58. So a must be at least 58 to have a feasible neighbor (since neighbor max 3600). Actually if a \u003c 58, then required neighbor value \u003e 3600, impossible. So corners must have values \u003e= 58.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339570183,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339571228,"data":"       | Similarly for degree 3 vertices (edge non-corner), need a \u003e= 3658 - 3600 = 58 as well? Wait same because neighbor max 3600. But they have three neighbors; each neighbor must be \u003e= 3658 - a. So a must be at least 58 as well. For interior degree 4, same.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339572763,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33957408,"data":"       | Thus any vertex must have value at least 58. But we have numbers 1..57 which cannot be placed anywhere if S=3658. So S cannot exceed 3600+57? Actually we need to place numbers 1..57 somewhere; they would require neighbor values \u003e 3600, impossible. Thus S cannot be larger than 3600+57 = 3657? Wait compute: For a vertex with value a, the maximum possible sum with neighbor is a + 3600. To achieve S, need a + 3600 \u003e= S =\u003e a \u003e= S - 3600. So the smallest possible a is S - 3600. Since we must place numbers as low as 1, we need S - 3600 \u003c= 1 =\u003e S \u003c= 3601. That's earlier bound. But we also need to consider degree constraints? Actually the neighbor's max is 3600, but each vertex has at most 4 neighbors, but each neighbor individually can be 3600. So a can be as low as S - 3600. So for S=3658, S-3600=58, meaning the smallest allowed number is 58. But we have numbers 1..57, which cannot be placed anywhere. So S cannot exceed 3600+1 = 3601? Wait earlier we said S \u003c= 3601 because smallest number 1 + largest 3600 = 3601. Indeed S cannot exceed 3601. So S=3658 is impossible. So our earlier bound using weighted sum gave 3658 but that's too high; the trivial bound 3601 is tighter.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339577306,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33957834,"data":"       | Thus maximum S \u003c= 3601.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339579448,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339580487,"data":"       | Now can we achieve S = 3601? That would require that every edge sum \u003e= 3601. Since max sum of any two numbers is 3600+3599=7199, but we need each edge sum at least 3601. This is plausible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339581893,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339582908,"data":"       | But we need to place numbers 1..3600 such that each adjacent pair sum \u003e= 3601. For any pair, sum \u003e= 3601 implies that the two numbers are complementary in sense that one must be at least 3601 - other. So small numbers must be paired with large numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339584457,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33958551,"data":"       | Specifically, for number a, any neighbor must be at least 3601 - a. So for a = 1, neighbor must be at least 3600. So 1 must be adjacent only to 3600. Since 1 has degree at most 4 (corner degree 2), we need to place 3600 adjacent to 1 in all its neighbor slots (i.e., all neighbors of 1 must be 3600). But 3600 can only occupy one cell, cannot be neighbor to multiple cells simultaneously. Actually a cell can be adjacent to multiple cells, but each neighbor is a distinct cell. So 1 can have up to 4 neighbors, each must have value \u003e= 3600. Since only one number equals 3600, impossible unless degree of 1 is 1 (i.e., placed in a corner with only one neighbor? But corner has degree 2). So S=3601 impossible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339587766,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339588791,"data":"       | Thus S must be less.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339589875,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339591193,"data":"       | Let's compute the maximum S such that we can place the smallest numbers with enough large neighbors, given each large number can serve as neighbor to up to its degree (max 4). This becomes a matching problem.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339593684,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339594732,"data":"       | We can think of constructing pairs (small, large) across edges such that each small number's all incident edges are to large numbers, and each large number can serve multiple small numbers up to its degree.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339596106,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33959714,"data":"       | Thus we need to allocate the numbers 1..3600 across vertices, with constraints on adjacency.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339598374,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339599417,"data":"       | We can attempt to find maximal S via integer programming using python's pulp or ortools? Might be heavy but we can try for 60x60 grid maybe feasible with ILP? 3600 binary variables for assignment? That's huge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339600828,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339601886,"data":"       | But we can use a heuristic search for S value using greedy algorithm: assign numbers in decreasing order to vertices, ensuring constraints satisfied for already placed numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339603266,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33960429,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can reason about the necessary condition based on degree counts.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339605481,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33960651,"data":"       | Let’s define for a given S, the set of \"dangerous\" numbers: those a such that S - a \u003e 3600, i.e., a \u003c S - 3600. Those numbers cannot be placed anywhere because they'd need neighbor \u003e 3600. So we need S \u003c= 3600 + 1 = 3601. That's fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339608235,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339609273,"data":"       | Next, consider numbers a where S - a is close to 3600, requiring neighbor values at least some high threshold. For each a, we need enough high numbers to serve as neighbors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339610624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33961165,"data":"       | Define for each a, required neighbor minimum = L_a = S - a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339612816,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339613831,"data":"       | Now consider the multiset of required neighbor slots: each vertex a contributes deg(v) slots each requiring a neighbor value \u003e= L_a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339615148,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339616196,"data":"       | Thus total required slots for neighbors with value \u003e= t is sum_{v: L_a \u003e= t} deg(v). This must be \u003c= total capacity of vertices with value \u003e= t, which is sum_{u: value \u003e= t} deg(u). This is a necessary condition (similar to majorization). This condition is also sufficient for bipartite graphs? Possibly due to Gale's theorem for bipartite degree sequences? Might be.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339617984,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339619169,"data":"       | Thus we can test for each S whether there exists an ordering of numbers satisfying this majorization condition. Since we can assign numbers arbitrarily to vertices (we can choose which vertices get which numbers), we can treat the degree multiset as given: we have 4-degree vertices (3364), 3-degree (232), 2-degree (4). We need to assign numbers to these vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339622746,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339623828,"data":"       | Thus we need to find a labeling of numbers to degree classes (i.e., assign each number to a vertex of some degree) such that the majorization condition holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339625153,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339626179,"data":"       | Since vertices of same degree are indistinguishable for this condition, we can think of distributing numbers among degree categories.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339627449,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339628459,"data":"       | Thus we need to decide how many of the smallest numbers we assign to each degree class.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339629659,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339630688,"data":"       | Goal: maximize S such that there exists a distribution of numbers to degree classes meeting the condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339631908,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339632932,"data":"       | We can treat the problem as: we have multiset of degrees d_i (size 3600). We need to assign numbers a_i (permutation of 1..3600) to each d_i such that for all thresholds t, sum_{i: a_i \u003e= t} d_i \u003e= sum_{i: S - a_i \u003e= t} d_i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339634478,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33963552,"data":"       | But note that the RHS uses a_i as well. This is complex.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339636682,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339637702,"data":"       | But perhaps we can use a simpler bound: Since each vertex of degree d contributes d to total weighted sum, and we already computed max weighted sum = 25,899,224. For S, we have inequality: ∑ d_i a_i \u003e= 7080 S. Since we can achieve max weighted sum, the maximum S possible is floor(max_weighted / 7080) = 3658, but we already know S \u003c= 3601, so the tighter bound is 3601. So we need a better bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339639539,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339640555,"data":"       | Consider also that each edge sum \u003e= S, and each edge sum \u003c= 3600 + (3600 - 1) = 7199 (max). Not helpful.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339641851,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339642921,"data":"       | Maybe we can use a counting argument based on low numbers and degree.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339644129,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339645452,"data":"       | Let’s denote k = number of vertices assigned numbers ≤ x. For each such vertex, its degree d contributes requirement that its neighbors must have numbers ≥ S - a ≥ S - x (since a ≤ x). So each of its incident edges must go to vertices with numbers at least S - x. So the total number of edges incident to low-number vertices must be at most the total number of edges incident to high-number vertices (≥ S - x). This yields inequality.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339647321,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339648426,"data":"       | Let’s formalize: Let L = set of vertices with a ≤ x. Let H = set of vertices with a ≥ S - x. (Note that S - x may be \u003e x). For each vertex v in L, all its incident edges must go to vertices in H (since neighbor must have value ≥ S - a ≥ S - x). Thus the total number of incident edges from L to outside must be ≤ total incident edges of H (since edges counted from H side). However edges between L and H are counted twice (once from each side). But we need to ensure that each edge incident to L is incident to a vertex in H. So the number of edges incident to L (i.e., sum_{v∈L} d_v) must be ≤ sum_{u∈H} d_u (since each such edge contributes 1 to L side and 1 to H side, but H may have extra edges to other vertices as well). Actually each edge between L and H contributes 1 to the sum of degrees of L and 1 to sum of degrees of H. So total degree sum of L cannot exceed total degree sum of H, because each edge incident to L must also be incident to H, and edges incident to H may also be incident to other vertices not in L, but that only increases RHS. So inequality: D(L) ≤ D(H), where D(S) = sum_{v∈S} d_v.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339654127,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339655208,"data":"       | Thus for any x, define L(x) = vertices with values ≤ x, H(x) = vertices with values ≥ S - x. Then we need D(L(x)) ≤ D(H(x)). This is a necessary condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339656582,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339657592,"data":"       | Now we can try to find maximal S such that there exists a labeling meeting this condition for all x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339658813,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339659847,"data":"       | Since we can choose which vertices get which numbers, we can think of ordering numbers and assign them to vertices with certain degrees to satisfy inequality.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339661173,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339662195,"data":"       | We can attempt to test S via constructing a feasible labeling using greedy algorithm based on degree sorting.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339663421,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339664651,"data":"       | One approach: Use the \"rearrangement inequality\" maybe the optimal assignment to maximize minimal sum is to pair smallest numbers with highest degree vertices? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339666143,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339667166,"data":"       | But we can attempt to search for S using integer linear programming with binary variables for assignment of numbers to vertices? That would be huge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339668469,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339669483,"data":"       | But we can perhaps use a heuristic to find high S and see if feasible via greedy algorithm.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339670798,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339671838,"data":"       | Given the size, maybe the maximum S is around 3600? Let's test small S values: maybe S = 3599? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339673081,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339674124,"data":"       | If S = 3599, then smallest number 1 requires neighbor \u003e= 3598. So 1 must be adjacent only to numbers \u003e= 3598 (i.e., 3598, 3599, 3600). That's three numbers, but 1 has degree at most 4, but we need each neighbor to be at least 3598. Could we assign 1 at a corner (degree 2) and have its two neighbors be 3600 and 3599 (both \u003e= 3598). That's okay. Similarly number 2 requires neighbor \u003e= 3597, we can place 2 at another corner with neighbors 3598 and 3597? Wait 3597 is allowed (\u003e= 3597). So we could assign 2's neighbors accordingly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339676205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339677249,"data":"       | But we need to ensure that each high number can serve as neighbor to multiple small numbers. Since there are many small numbers (1..some), we need enough high numbers to serve as neighbors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339678623,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33968082,"data":"       | Let's attempt to compute capacity: Each high-number vertex (value \u003e= T) can be neighbor to up to d edges. So total capacity for edges incident to low numbers is sum_{v: a ≤ x} d_v, which must be ≤ sum_{u: a ≥ S - x} d_u.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339682357,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339683373,"data":"       | Thus we can try to find S such that this inequality holds for all x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339684546,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339685595,"data":"       | Given we can assign numbers to vertices arbitrarily, we can choose which vertices are low-degree (i.e., assign low numbers to low-degree vertices) to reduce D(L). So for each x, we want D(L) as small as possible while still having x numbers assigned. Since we have limited low-degree vertices (degree 2 and 3), we can assign the smallest numbers to those vertices to minimize D(L). That's the best we can do.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33968733,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339688678,"data":"       | Thus the best-case scenario for a given x is: assign the x smallest numbers to the vertices with smallest degrees possible (i.e., corners first (degree2), then edge (degree3), then interior (degree4)). This yields minimal D(L) for that x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339690108,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339691119,"data":"       | Similarly, to maximize D(H) for given threshold, we assign the largest numbers to vertices with largest degrees (interior). That yields maximal capacity for high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339692445,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339693466,"data":"       | Thus the necessary condition becomes: For each x, D_min(x) ≤ D_max(S - x), where D_min(x) is minimal possible sum of degrees for any set of x vertices (choose vertices with smallest degrees), and D_max(y) is maximal possible sum of degrees for any set of y vertices (choose vertices with largest degrees). Since we can assign numbers arbitrarily, we can achieve these extremes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339695175,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339696178,"data":"       | Thus we can compute D_min(x) for x from 0 to 3600: choose first x vertices from sorted list of degrees ascending: we have 4 vertices degree2, 232 degree3, 3364 degree4. So D_min(x) is:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33969752,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339698526,"data":"       | - If x ≤ 4: D_min = 2*x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339699679,"data":"       | - If 4 \u003c x ≤ 4+232=236: D_min = 2*4 + 3*(x-4) = 8 + 3*(x-4) = 3x -4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339700907,"data":"       | - If x \u003e 236: D_min = 2*4 + 3*232 + 4*(x-236) = 8 + 696 + 4*(x-236) = 704 + 4x - 944 = 4x - 240? Wait compute: 8+696=704. Then D_min = 704 + 4*(x-236) = 704 + 4x - 944 = 4x - 240. Let's verify: For x=236, D_min = 4*236 - 240 = 944 - 240 = 704, matches earlier. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33970244,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33970344,"data":"       | Similarly D_max(y) for y vertices with largest degrees (choose interior first):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339704609,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339705597,"data":"       | - If y ≤ 3364: D_max = 4*y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339706757,"data":"       | - If 3364 \u003c y ≤ 3364+232=3596: D_max = 4*3364 + 3*(y-3364) = 13,456 + 3*(y-3364) = 3y + (13,456 - 3*3364) = 3y + (13,456 - 10,092) = 3y + 3,364. Let's compute: 4*3364 = 13,456. Subtract 3*3364 = 10,092, difference = 3,364. So D_max = 3y + 3,364.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.33970972,"data":"       | - If y \u003e 3596 (i.e., up to 3600): D_max = 4*3364 + 3*232 + 2*(y-3596) = 13,456 + 696 + 2*(y-3596) = 14,152 + 2y - 7,192 = 2y + 6,960. Let's compute: 14,152 - 2*3596 = 14,152 - 7,192 = 6,960. So D_max = 2y + 6,960.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339711185,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339712201,"data":"       | Check for y=3600: D_max = 2*3600 + 6,960 = 7,200 + 6,960 = 14,160, which equals total sum of degrees (since each edge counted twice). Let's compute total degree sum: 2*4 + 3*232 + 4*3364 = 8 + 696 + 13,456 = 14,160. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339713612,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339714641,"data":"       | Now the condition: For each x (0..3600), we need D_min(x) ≤ D_max(S - x). Here S - x is the count of numbers \u003e= S - x? Wait careful: H(x) = vertices with values ≥ S - x. The number of such vertices is at least (3600 - (S - x - 1))? Actually numbers are 1..3600. The count of numbers ≥ t is 3600 - (t - 1) = 3601 - t. So if threshold is T = S - x, then number of vertices with value ≥ T is y = 3601 - (S - x) = 3601 - S + x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339716561,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.339717598,"data":"       | Thus y = x + (3601 - S). So D_max(y) = D_max(x + (3601 - S)). Condition: D_min(x) ≤ D_max(x + (3601 - S)) for all x.\n"}
