,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340906644,"data":"       | analysisThe naive backtracking is too slow for 4x4 (16!). Need pruning and heuristics.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340907991,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34090902,"data":"       | We can use constraint programming library? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340910164,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340911183,"data":"       | But maybe we can reason analytically for large grid.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340912411,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340913471,"data":"       | Observation: For S=N (3600), the requirement for each vertex v: all its neighbors must have numbers at least N - a(v). Since a(v) ranges up to N, the required neighbor threshold ranges from N-1 down to 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340914873,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340915881,"data":"       | Thus the hardest constraints are for small a(v). For a(v)=k, neighbors must be \u003e= N - k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34091725,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340918511,"data":"       | If we place the smallest numbers at corners (degree 2), we need for each small k at corner, two neighbors each \u003e= N - k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340919826,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340920847,"data":"       | Let's compute needed high numbers for each corner small number.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340922081,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340923178,"data":"       | Suppose we assign numbers 1,2,3,4 to the four corners (degrees 2). Then for k=1, need two neighbors \u003e= N-1 = 3599. For k=2, need two neighbors \u003e= 3598. For k=3 need \u003e= 3597, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340925986,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340927084,"data":"       | We have many high numbers available (the top ~2*corner_degree = 8 high numbers needed). Actually each corner's neighbors are edge cells (degree 3). Some edge cells may be neighbor to two corners (if grid small) but not for 60x60 (corners far apart). So each corner's neighbor cells are distinct. So we need at least 8 distinct high numbers: two per corner. That's fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340928774,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340929814,"data":"       | Now edge cells (degree 3) could hold small numbers as well, but they'd need three high neighbors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340931048,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340932078,"data":"       | We can allocate small numbers to edge cells but need enough high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340933248,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340934279,"data":"       | The total number of neighbor slots for small numbers must be satisfied by high numbers, but high numbers can serve multiple small numbers if they are adjacent to multiple small cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34093562,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340936659,"data":"       | In a large grid, we can design a pattern where high numbers are placed in a \"checkerboard\" pattern such that each low number cell is surrounded by high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340938071,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340939109,"data":"       | One natural pattern: Use a chessboard coloring, assign all black cells with high numbers (top half) and white cells with low numbers (bottom half). Then each edge connects high+low, sum maybe around N+something? Let's compute min sum: smallest low number is 1 (white), smallest high number is N/2+1 = 1801 (if we split). Sum = 1802. That's far less than 3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340940757,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34094177,"data":"       | But we can choose threshold higher: assign high numbers to all cells (maybe 75% high) and low numbers to few cells (like corners). Then min sum may be high.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340943063,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.3409441,"data":"       | Goal: maximize minimal sum, so we want to minimize number of low numbers and ensure each low number is adjacent only to high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340945771,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340946806,"data":"       | Thus we can consider placing the smallest numbers only on cells that have degree 2 (corners) maybe also some edge cells but ensure they are surrounded by high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340948119,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340949139,"data":"       | Potentially we could place the smallest 1800 numbers on one color (say black) and the largest 1800 numbers on the other color (white). Then each edge sum will be at least (smallest black) + (smallest white) = 1 + 1801 = 1802. That's not high.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340950593,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340951614,"data":"       | But we can reorder: maybe we can place numbers such that each edge connects two relatively large numbers, and only a few edges involve small numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340952938,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340953954,"data":"       | But the grid is regular; each cell has multiple edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340955087,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.340957421,"data":"       | Consider we assign numbers in decreasing order along rows, like snake pattern. Then adjacent cells horizontally have large difference, but vertical adjacency may pair small with large.\n"}
