,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.342321418,"data":"       | Our greedy algorithm assigned the smallest number meeting each requirement. For requirement 3598, smallest \u003e=3598 is 3598. So first cell gets 3598, second cell gets 3599 (since 3599 is next smallest \u003e=3598). That uses up 3598 and 3599. Then for requirement 3597, smallest \u003e=3597 among remaining numbers is 3600 (since 3598,3599 used). So both cells with 3597 get 3600? But only one 3600 exists, so second fails later. That's why greedy fails.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.342323253,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.342324307,"data":"       | Thus we need to allocate numbers more cleverly: we should assign the highest numbers to highest requirements, but we need enough high numbers to cover each requirement. Since each requirement level may need multiple numbers, we need to allocate numbers accordingly.\n"}
