,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.342661139,"data":"       | One can think of bipartite nature: The grid is bipartite (checkerboard). Adjacent cells have opposite colors. So each edge connects a black cell to a white cell. So we can assign low numbers to one color and high numbers to the other color to increase sums across edges. Since each edge sum = low+high. If we put all low numbers on black, high numbers on white, then each edge sum will be at least (minimum low) + (minimum high). But we have to allocate numbers distinct across colors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.342662998,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.342664338,"data":"       | Let’s denote we have 3600 cells, 1800 black and 1800 white (since even grid). Suppose we assign the 1800 smallest numbers (1..1800) to black cells, and the 1800 largest numbers (1801..3600) to white cells. Then any edge sum = a (from black) + b (from white) where a ≤ 1800, b ≥ 1801. Minimum sum occurs when a is smallest (1) and b is smallest among whites (1801). So min sum = 1+1801=1802. That's a lower bound for S if we can arrange adjacency such that each black cell adjacent to whites with numbers at least 1801. Since all whites have numbers \u003e=1801, any edge sum \u003e= a + 1801. The smallest a is 1, so min edge sum = 1802. So S could be at most 1802? Wait we need to find maximum S such that there exists arrangement; we have found an arrangement achieving S=1802 (maybe). But can we achieve larger S? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.342666861,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.3426679,"data":"       | If we shift some low numbers to white side and high numbers to black side, the minimal sum could increase? The worst-case edge sum will involve the smallest number among all cells and the smallest number among opposite color cells adjacent to it. Since each cell has at most 4 neighbors (except edges). The minimal sum across all edges is at least min_{edge} (value at u + value at v). The global minimum sum will involve the two smallest numbers that are adjacent. So to maximize the minimum sum, we need to avoid adjacency of small numbers.\n"}
