,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.342675266,"data":"       | Given the numbers are 1..3600, the smallest possible numbers are 1,2,... . The minima of A and B will be some numbers a_min and b_min. Since the sets partition the numbers, the two minima are the two smallest numbers overall, i.e., 1 and 2, unless we put both 1 and 2 in same set, leaving the other set's minimum larger. But then the edge connecting a cell with 1 to a neighbor with 2 would be within same color? Wait edges only connect opposite colors. So if we put both 1 and 2 in same color (say black), then they are not adjacent (since same color cells are not adjacent). So the minimal edge sum will involve the smallest number in black (1) and the smallest number in white (which would be at least 1801 if we allocate low numbers to black). However we could allocate low numbers to both colors but ensure that the smallest numbers of each color are not adjacent? But adjacency across colors is forced; each black cell has at least one white neighbor (except maybe corners? Actually each black cell adjacent to whites). So the minimal edge sum will be min_{black cell} (value black + min neighbor white). Since we can arrange values arbitrarily, we could place the smallest white number far from the smallest black number, but they will still be adjacent somewhere else? Let's think.\n"}
