,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343126882,"data":"       | Now pattern: numbers increase along a Hamiltonian path that alternates low and high? Let's examine ordering: 1,8,2,9,3,7,4,6,5. This appears to be low-high-low-high... along a snake path maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343128281,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343129335,"data":"       | Sequence: low 1, high 8 (max of remaining?), low 2, high 9 (max), low 3, high 7, low 4, high 6, low 5. So pattern: start with smallest, then largest remaining, then next smallest, then next largest, etc. This is \"alternating low-high\" arrangement along a Hamiltonian path covering all cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343130926,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343132161,"data":"       | If we place numbers on a Hamiltonian path (i.e., a snake covering all cells) such that they alternate low-high, then each adjacent pair along the path will have sum roughly N+1 (since low + high = N+1). For N=9, low+high = 10? Wait low+high = 1+9=10, 2+8=10, etc. But the minimal sum we got is 9, not 10, because some adjacency is not along the path (grid edges not part of path) may involve two low numbers or two high numbers. However the arrangement seems to still guarantee min sum = N.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343134005,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343135032,"data":"       | Maybe we can generalize: Use a \"checkerboard\" pattern where black cells get numbers 1..N/2 (low) and white cells get numbers N/2+1..N (high). Then each edge sum \u003e= (N/2+1)+1 = N/2+2. That's less than N for large N. So not enough.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343138257,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343139347,"data":"       | But we can perhaps assign numbers to black cells as low numbers but arranged such that each black cell's low number is paired with a high number neighbor that is at least N - low + something.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343140731,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343141771,"data":"       | If we assign numbers to black cells as low numbers, and to white cells as high numbers, the minimal sum across edges is min_{black} (value_black + min_{white neighbor}) . Since each black cell may have up to 4 white neighbors, we could assign the white neighbors of low black cells to be high enough. So we can raise minimal sum beyond N/2+2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34314341,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343144478,"data":"       | Potentially we can achieve S=N by ensuring that for each black cell with value a, all its white neighbors have values \u003e= N - a. Since a ranges from 1 to N/2, we need white neighbor values at least N - a, which is at least N - N/2 = N/2. So we need white cells to have values at least N/2 (i.e., high half). That's satisfied if we assign high numbers to whites. But also we need for white cells with value b, all black neighbors must have values \u003e= N - b. Since b may be as high as N, N - b could be small (maybe zero). So that's fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343146466,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343147487,"data":"       | Thus we can achieve S=N if we assign low numbers to black cells (1..N/2) and high numbers to white cells (N/2+1..N). Then for any edge (a,b) with a low (≤ N/2) and b high (≥ N/2+1), the sum is at least a + (N/2+1). The worst case is a=1, sum = N/2+2, which is less than N for N\u003e4. So not enough.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343149157,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343150489,"data":"       | Thus we need more refined assignment: Not all low numbers need to be as low as 1; maybe we can assign the smallest numbers to cells with low degree (corners) to reduce impact. The smallest numbers (1,2) placed at corners (degree 2). Their neighbors are white cells (if corner is black). Those white cells could be assigned relatively high numbers to ensure sums \u003e= N.\n"}
