,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343779591,"data":"       | But we need maximum minimal sum, which is at most floor( (total edge sum) / E ), but we already have that bound (19 for 4x4). So S could be up to 19, but we suspect actual max is 15 maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343780949,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343781962,"data":"       | We need better bound using degree distribution and ordering of numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343783132,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343784171,"data":"       | Consider that each number appears in at most 4 edges (degree). The smallest number 1 appears in at most 4 edges, so it can affect at most 4 edge sums. To have all edge sums \u003e= S, each neighbor of 1 must have value \u003e= S-1. So we need at least deg(1) neighbors with values \u003e= S-1. Since deg(1) ≤ 4, we need at least 4 numbers \u003e= S-1. Similarly for number 2, its neighbors must be \u003e= S-2, etc. This leads to a counting argument: For each k, there must be at least deg(k) numbers \u003e= S - k. But deg(k) depends on placement, not predetermined.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343786291,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343787317,"data":"       | But we can bound total number of cells with value \u003e= t needed to support low values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343788788,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343789807,"data":"       | Simplify: Suppose we place the smallest numbers in cells with smallest degrees (corners degree 2, edge degree 3). Then each low number has limited number of neighbors, reducing requirement for high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343792717,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343793759,"data":"       | Thus to maximize S, we should place the smallest numbers at corners (degree 2) and maybe some edge cells.\n"}
