,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343881936,"data":"       | Now consider 2: place at top-right corner (neighbors (0,2) and (1,3)). Need neighbors \u003e=14. Assign 14 and maybe 13? Wait need \u003e=14 for sum \u003e=16, so neighbor values must be at least 14. So assign 14 and maybe 13? Actually 13+2=15 \u003c16, not allowed. So need \u003e=14. So assign 14 and maybe 13? Not allowed. So both neighbors must be at least 14. We have numbers left: 13,12,... etc. But we already used 15,16. So we can assign 14 and maybe 13? Not allowed. So we need 14 and something else \u003e=14, but only 14 left. So can't place 2 at a corner because we need two distinct numbers \u003e=14, but only 14 is left. So maybe we need to place 2 not at corner but at edge cell degree 3 where we can have three neighbors, but still need each neighbor \u003e=14? Actually each edge sum must be \u003e=16, so for each neighbor we need neighbor \u003e=14. So we need at least 3 numbers \u003e=14, but we only have 14,15,16 left (three numbers). That's possible if we place 2 adjacent to 14,15,16. But 2's degree is at most 4, but we can place 2 at a cell with degree 3 (edge) and make its three neighbors be 14,15,16. However 15 and 16 already used as neighbors of 1. They could also be neighbors of 2 if they are adjacent to both? But 1 is at top-left corner, its neighbors are (0,1) and (1,0). Those cells could also be adjacent to other cells. For 2 to be adjacent to 15 and 16, we need 2 to be placed at (0,2) maybe? Let's think.\n"}
