,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343891252,"data":"       | Maybe we can attempt to use constraint programming via python's itertools with pruning using degrees.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343892484,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343893531,"data":"       | Given 16 cells, we might still be able to brute force with pruning using constraints. Let's try improved backtracking with ordering of cells by degree descending and also using value ordering descending, but also we can prune using remaining high numbers needed for low cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.343895065,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.3438963,"data":"       | But earlier attempts timed out after 30 seconds. Maybe we can improve with forward checking: For each unassigned cell, compute minimal possible sum with its assigned neighbors; if any assigned neighbor already violates S, we prune (already). Additionally, we can compute lower bound on possible sum for each edge where both endpoints unassigned: the minimal possible sum would be sum of two smallest remaining numbers. If that is less than S, we cannot guarantee the edge will meet S, but we could assign larger numbers later. However we cannot guarantee, but we can still hope.\n"}
