,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344082597,"data":"       | Now is S=16 impossible? Let's attempt to prove impossibility. Could use counting argument: Suppose we have arrangement with min edge sum \u003e=16. Consider the four corners (degree 2). Let their values be a,b,c,d (distinct). Their neighbors must each be \u003e= 16 - a, etc. Since each corner has two neighbors, and corners are not adjacent to each other (they are at distance). The neighbors of different corners may overlap (e.g., edge cells adjacent to two corners? Actually corners share an edge cell only if grid size 2? For 4x4, corners are far; each corner's adjacent cells are distinct (except maybe edges share a cell if grid size small). For 4x4, top-left corner neighbors: (0,1) and (1,0). Top-right corner neighbors: (0,2) and (1,3). Bottom-left: (2,0) and (3,1). Bottom-right: (2,3) and (3,2). All distinct, 8 distinct neighbor cells (which are edge cells). So we need 8 distinct numbers each \u003e= 16 - corner value.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344085252,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344086315,"data":"       | Since corner values are among smallest numbers perhaps, to make condition easier we want corner values as large as possible (since then required neighbor thresholds are lower). However corners also have low degree, we might assign relatively small numbers there. But to achieve S=16, we need each corner value a such that 16 - a \u003c= max possible neighbor value (which is 16). Since neighbor values max 16, we need a \u003e= 0. That's trivial. But we need distinct neighbor numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344088214,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344089231,"data":"       | Let's attempt to see if any arrangement possible via exhaustive search for 4x4 with S=16 using smarter pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344090479,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344091504,"data":"       | We can implement backtracking with constraint propagation and maybe it will finish quickly because constraints are strong.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344092741,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344093805,"data":"       | Let's attempt again but with better ordering: assign cells in order of decreasing degree, and assign values also in order of decreasing value (since high values help meet constraints). Additionally, we can compute for each unassigned neighbor the minimal possible value left (smallest remaining) and check if for any assigned cell, the sum with that minimal possible value would be \u003c S, then prune.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344095546,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344096589,"data":"       | Implement.\n"}
