,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34452399,"data":"       | analysisWe need to find maximum S such that we can fill numbers 1..3600 in a 60x60 grid, each number used exactly once, such that sum of numbers in any two adjacent cells (sharing side) is at least S. We need to maximize minimal adjacent sum. Equivalent to find arrangement that maximizes minimum sum over all edges of grid.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344525606,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344526675,"data":"       | We need to find maximum possible S. This is like a combinatorial optimization: we have a graph (grid) with vertices 3600 cells, edges between orthogonal neighbors (total edges = (60-1)*60*2 = 59*60*2 = 7080). We assign distinct numbers 1..3600 to vertices. Want to maximize minimum sum over edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344528219,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34452926,"data":"       | We need to find optimal arrangement and compute S. Might be related to pairing high numbers with low numbers to increase sums? Actually to maximize minimum sum, we want to avoid having two low numbers adjacent (since that would give low sum). So we want to arrange numbers such that each low number is adjacent only to high numbers, and high numbers can be adjacent to anything.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34453095,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344532285,"data":"       | This resembles constructing a \"checkerboard\" pattern: place smallest numbers on one color (say black squares) and largest numbers on white squares. Then each edge connects black-white, sum of a black (low) and white (high). The minimal sum would be the smallest low + smallest high adjacent pair. If we assign numbers such that black squares get the 1800 smallest numbers, white squares get 1800 largest numbers, then the minimal sum would be (smallest black) + (smallest white). Smallest black = 1, smallest white = 1801 (since white gets numbers 1801..3600). Then min sum = 1802. But perhaps we can do better by interleaving numbers more cleverly: maybe we can assign numbers such that each low number is paired with a high number that is not too low. The minimal sum would be determined by the smallest low number plus the smallest high number that is adjacent to it. If we can ensure that each low number is adjacent only to numbers \u003e= some threshold T, then min sum = min low + T. Since low numbers range from 1 upward, we might try to place 1 adjacent only to high numbers near 3600. But each cell has up to 4 neighbors; we could assign 1 at a corner (2 neighbors) to two high numbers. Similarly, we could place low numbers on one color, high numbers on the other, but we could also order them such that low numbers increase gradually across the board, while high numbers decrease, perhaps raising the minimal sum beyond 1802.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344535775,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34453683,"data":"       | We need to find the maximum possible S. This is reminiscent of a problem about \"grid graph labeling\" and \"minimum edge sum\". Could be solved by considering a lower bound: For any labeling, the sum over all edges of the sums of adjacent numbers equals sum over each vertex of its number times its degree (since each edge contributes its two endpoints). Let total sum over edges of (a+b) = sum_{v} deg(v) * value(v). For a grid, interior vertices degree 4, edge (non-corner) degree 3, corner degree 2. Compute total degree sum: each interior cell: (60-2)*(60-2) = 58*58 = 3364 interior cells degree 4. Edge non-corner cells: there are 2*(60-2) + 2*(60-2) = 4*58 = 232 cells degree 3? Wait edges: top row (excluding corners) 58 cells degree 3, bottom row 58 cells degree 3, left column 58 cells degree 3, right column 58 cells degree 3. So total 4*58 = 232 cells degree 3. Corners: 4 cells degree 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344541027,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344542112,"data":"       | Check total number of cells: 3364+232+4 = 3600 correct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344543265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34454433,"data":"       | Total sum of degrees = 4*3364 + 3*232 + 2*4 = 13456 + 696 + 8 = 14160. Indeed each edge counted twice (once per endpoint), number of edges = 14160/2 = 7080 matches earlier.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344545672,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344546709,"data":"       | Now sum over all edges of (a+b) = sum_v deg(v) * value(v). Let total = T = sum_v deg(v) * value(v). Since each edge sum \u003e= S, we have T \u003e= S * number_of_edges = S * 7080. Therefore S \u003c= T / 7080. So we can bound S by average edge sum weighted by degrees.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344548279,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344549575,"data":"       | Compute T for any labeling: sum_v deg(v) * value(v). Since deg(v) varies, we can compute maximum possible lower bound for S by maximizing T? Wait inequality: For any labeling, min edge sum \u003c= average edge sum = T / 7080. So S (the minimal edge sum) cannot exceed the average edge sum. So the maximum possible S is at most the maximum possible minimum edge sum, which is at most the maximum possible average edge sum? Actually average edge sum depends on labeling; we can try to maximize the minimal edge sum, which cannot exceed the average edge sum for that labeling. So to bound S from above, we can consider the maximum possible average edge sum across all labelings. Since we can choose labeling to maximize average edge sum, then S \u003c= max_average. But we can compute max_average by assigning larger numbers to higher degree vertices (since they contribute more to T). So to maximize T, assign the largest numbers to vertices with highest degree (interior cells degree 4), then next largest to degree 3, smallest to degree 2 corners. This will maximize average edge sum. Then S cannot exceed that average.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344552509,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344553555,"data":"       | Thus compute T_max: assign numbers 3600 down to ... to interior cells (3364 cells). Then assign next numbers to edge cells (232 cells). Then assign smallest numbers to corners (4 cells). Let's compute T_max.\n"}
