,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344929898,"data":"       | analysisInteresting pattern: For 2x2 (N=4) max min sum = 4. For 2x3 (N=6) = 6. For 3x3 (N=9) = 9. Looks like maybe S = N? Actually N = mn = total cells. For 2x2, N=4 =\u003e S=4. For 2x3, N=6 =\u003e S=6. For 3x3, N=9 =\u003e S=9. So maybe for m x n grid, maximum minimal adjacent sum = mn? Let's test 3x4 (N=12). Brute force 12! huge not possible. But we can try heuristic search (maybe using simulated annealing) to see if we can achieve S=12 for 3x4. But maybe pattern continues: S = mn? For 60x60, mn = 3600. That matches our earlier upper bound of 3600 (since max sum cannot exceed 3600+? Actually we found S \u003c= 3600 due to 1's neighbors). So maybe the answer is 3600.\n"}
