,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344955555,"data":"       | We have already shown upper bound S \u003c= 3600 because 1 must be placed somewhere, with at most 4 neighbors (actually degree up to 4). But more precisely, if 1 is placed at interior (degree 4), then each neighbor must be at least S-1. Since max number is mn, we need S-1 \u003c= mn =\u003e S \u003c= mn+1. But also need distinct neighbors, but we can have up to 4 distinct numbers \u003e= S-1. Since there are only mn-1 numbers greater than 1, we can have distinct neighbors as long as S-1 \u003c= mn. So S \u003c= mn+1? Wait we need S-1 \u003c= mn, which gives S \u003c= mn+1. But we also need for each neighbor y \u003e= S-1 and y \u003c= mn. So S-1 must be \u003c= mn, which is always true for S \u003c= mn+1. But we also need distinctness: we need at least deg(1) numbers \u003e= S-1 distinct. Since there are mn - (S-2) numbers \u003e= S-1? Actually numbers \u003e= S-1 are from S-1 to mn inclusive. Count = mn - (S-2). For interior degree 4, we need at least 4 distinct numbers \u003e= S-1. So require mn - (S-2) \u003e= 4 =\u003e S \u003c= mn -2. Wait compute: count = mn - (S-2) = mn - S + 2. Need \u003e= degree d. So mn - S + 2 \u003e= d =\u003e S \u003c= mn - d + 2. For d=4 interior, S \u003c= mn - 2. For d=2 corner, S \u003c= mn. So the most restrictive is interior placement of 1 giving S \u003c= mn -2? Let's check: If we place 1 at interior, we need 4 distinct numbers \u003e= S-1. The numbers \u003e= S-1 are from S-1 to mn. Count = mn - (S-2) = mn - S + 2. Need \u003e=4 =\u003e mn - S + 2 \u003e=4 =\u003e S \u003c= mn -2. So S cannot exceed mn-2 if 1 is interior. But we can place 1 at corner (degree 2) to get less restriction: need 2 distinct numbers \u003e= S-1, count \u003e=2 =\u003e mn - S + 2 \u003e=2 =\u003e S \u003c= mn. So S \u003c= mn. So upper bound S \u003c= mn.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344960322,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344961351,"data":"       | Thus S_max \u003c= mn = 3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344963179,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344964215,"data":"       | Now we need to construct arrangement achieving S = mn = 3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344965515,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344966556,"data":"       | We need labeling of 60x60 grid with numbers 1..3600 such that each adjacent sum \u003e= 3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344967788,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344968822,"data":"       | Observation: To achieve sum \u003e= 3600, each pair must consist of one low number and one high number such that low + high \u003e= 3600. This suggests a pairing of numbers that sum to at least 3600. Since max sum of two numbers is 3600+3599=7199, we can pair low numbers with high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344970561,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344971618,"data":"       | A natural construction: Use a checkerboard pattern where each black cell gets numbers from 1..1800 (low) and each white cell gets numbers from 1801..3600 (high). Then any adjacent pair (black-white) sum at least 1+1801=1802, not enough. Need 3600. So we need to assign numbers such that for each adjacent pair, the sum is at least 3600. That suggests we need to pair each low number with a high number at least 3600 - low.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344975105,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344976349,"data":"       | If we assign low numbers to black cells and high numbers to white cells, we can choose mapping such that each black cell's low number x is paired with a specific white neighbor y \u003e= 3600 - x. Since each black cell has up to 4 white neighbors, we can choose high numbers accordingly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344978031,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344979094,"data":"       | Idea: Use a \"Latin square\" style: assign numbers such that each row is decreasing from left to right, and each column also decreasing? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344980468,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344981506,"data":"       | Perhaps we can achieve S = mn by arranging numbers in decreasing order along a Hamiltonian path that alternates high and low numbers. For each edge in grid, we need sum \u003e= mn. If we order numbers along a snake path, adjacent cells along the path will have numbers that differ widely, but not guarantee sum condition for all edges (including those not along path). So not sufficient.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344983355,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344984375,"data":"       | Better: Use a construction based on pairing numbers that sum to mn+1? Wait 1+3599 = 3600? Actually mn = 3600. So we need sums \u003e= 3600. The smallest possible sum that meets this is exactly 3600. So we need to ensure that for each edge, the two numbers sum to at least 3600. That suggests we can try to assign numbers such that each pair of adjacent cells sum to exactly 3600 or more, maybe using complementary pairs that sum to 3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344986192,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344987239,"data":"       | If we could arrange numbers such that each adjacent pair is a complementary pair (i.e., numbers a and 3600 - a). But note 3600 - a may be less than a for a \u003e1800. Actually complement to 3600: a + b = 3600. Then b = 3600 - a. Since numbers range 1..3600, this pairs numbers (1,3599), (2,3598), ..., (1799,1801), and 1800 pairs with 1800? Wait 1800+1800=3600, but we have only one 1800. So we cannot have perfect pairing for all cells because we have odd number of each? Actually total cells 3600 even. Complement pairs: For a from 1 to 1799, pair with 3600 - a (which ranges 3599 down to 1801). That's 1799 pairs (2*1799=3598 cells). Remaining numbers: 1800 and 3600? Wait 3600 - 1800 = 1800, so 1800 pairs with itself, but we have only one 1800. Also 3600 is unpaired because 3600 - 3600 =0 not in set. So complement to 3600 not perfect.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344989938,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344991246,"data":"       | Maybe we need sum \u003e= 3600, not exactly 3600. So we can pair low numbers with high numbers such that sum \u003e= 3600, leaving some high numbers to pair with each other (which sum \u003e 3600). That's okay.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344992712,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344993738,"data":"       | Potential construction: Place numbers 1..1800 on one color (say black squares). Place numbers 1801..3600 on white squares. Then each adjacent sum \u003e= 1+1801=1802, not enough. But we can permute numbers within each color to increase sums? The minimal sum will be min_{black cell} (value_black + min neighbor white). Since white values are all at least 1801, the minimal sum will be 1+1801=1802 regardless of arrangement. So not enough.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344997179,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344998252,"data":"       | Thus we need a different distribution of low numbers: maybe we need to intermix low numbers with high numbers more densely, not segregated by color.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.344999555,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345000611,"data":"       | Observation: The condition sum \u003e= 3600 is strong; for a low number like 1, its neighbor must be at least 3599. So 1 must be adjacent only to numbers 3599 or 3600. Since 1 has degree at most 4, we need at most 4 such high numbers. So we can place 1 at a corner (degree 2) with neighbors 3599 and 3600. That's possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345002239,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345003314,"data":"       | Similarly, number 2 must be adjacent to numbers at least 3598. So we can place 2 at another corner with neighbors 3598 and 3599 (or 3600). But note numbers must be distinct. So we need to allocate high numbers to satisfy constraints for all low numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345004766,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345005792,"data":"       | Thus we can think of constructing arrangement where low numbers are placed at corners and edges, each adjacent to high numbers that are just enough to meet sum \u003e= 3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345007185,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345008225,"data":"       | Potential systematic construction: Fill the grid with numbers such that each row is increasing left to right, each column increasing top to bottom? Then adjacent sums would be relatively large for large numbers but small for small numbers near top-left. For example, if we fill numbers in increasing order row-major (1..3600), then adjacent sum of (1,2) =3, far less. Not good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345009882,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345011108,"data":"       | We need to allocate high numbers near low numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345012248,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345013302,"data":"       | Idea: Use \"anti-magic square\" where each row and column sums are constant? Not relevant.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345014571,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345015618,"data":"       | Maybe we can construct using a \"snake\" arrangement where we pair low numbers with high numbers along each edge direction: For each column, we could fill from top to bottom: high numbers at top, low numbers at bottom, so vertical adjacent pairs have high+low \u003e= 3600. Similarly for horizontal adjacency within rows, we need high+low \u003e= 3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345017298,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345018322,"data":"       | If we fill each row with alternating high and low numbers such that each adjacent pair across columns also satisfies condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345019568,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345020608,"data":"       | Simplify: Let's try to construct using a pattern: For each cell (i,j), assign value = i*60 + j + 1 (row-major). Not good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345021848,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345022895,"data":"       | Maybe assign value = (i)*60 + (61-j) (reverse columns) for each row? That would make horizontal adjacent pairs sum to (i*60 + j) + (i*60 + (61-(j+1))) = 2*i*60 + j + 60 - j = 2*i*60 + 60 = 60*(2i+1). For i from 0 to 59, this sum ranges from 60 to 60*119 = 7140. Minimum is when i=0: sum = 60. Not enough.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345025892,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345026945,"data":"       | Thus need high numbers near low numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345028059,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345029089,"data":"       | Potential approach: Pair each low number k with high number 3600 - k + 1? That sum = 3601, which exceeds 3600. But we need to place them adjacent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345030379,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345031442,"data":"       | If we could pair each low number with its complement (3601 - k) as neighbor. Since we have 3600 cells, we could create a perfect matching of the grid edges such that each low number is matched with its complement. However each cell has up to 4 edges, but we can choose a matching covering all vertices (perfect matching) because grid is bipartite and has even number of vertices. Indeed a 60x60 grid (bipartite) has a perfect matching. So we can pair each vertex with exactly one neighbor (matching). If we assign numbers such that each matched pair sums to at least 3600, we still need to ensure that other edges (not in matching) also satisfy sum \u003e= 3600. That's more restrictive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345033649,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345034958,"data":"       | But maybe we can design labeling where every edge sum \u003e= 3600, not just matching.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345036175,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345037202,"data":"       | Consider constructing a labeling where each row is decreasing from left to right, and each column is decreasing from top to bottom. Then any adjacent pair will have one larger, one smaller, but difference may be small near the middle. For example, if we assign numbers decreasing from 3600 at top-left to 1 at bottom-right, then adjacent sums near bottom-right will be small. So not good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345038875,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345039915,"data":"       | Better: Use a \"checkerboard\" but with numbers assigned such that each black cell gets a low number k and each white neighbor gets a high number \u003e= 3600 - k. Since each black cell has up to 4 white neighbors, we can assign high numbers to satisfy all its edges if we ensure that the minimal high neighbor across its four edges is \u003e= 3600 - k. So we need for each black cell, all its white neighbors are at least 3600 - k. This can be achieved if we assign high numbers in a monotone way: high numbers are all above a certain threshold, depending on adjacent low numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345041992,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345043292,"data":"       | Potential systematic construction: Partition numbers into two sets: low L = {1,...,a} and high H = {b,...,3600}. Choose a such that for each low number k in L, the threshold 3600 - k is at most the minimum of H. That is, we need min(H) \u003e= 3600 - max(L). Let max(L) = a. Then we need min(H) \u003e= 3600 - a. Since H = {a+1,...,3600} if we partition contiguous, min(H) = a+1. So condition becomes a+1 \u003e= 3600 - a =\u003e 2a \u003e= 3599 =\u003e a \u003e= 1800 (since integer). So need a \u003e= 1800. So low numbers must be at least 1800 numbers. That means we need to assign at least half the cells low numbers, and half high numbers. That's exactly the checkerboard partition. But earlier we saw that with contiguous partition low numbers 1..1800 and high numbers 1801..3600, the minimal sum is 1+1801 = 1802 \u003c 3600. So condition fails because min(H) = 1801, but we need min(H) \u003e= 3600 - max(L) = 3600 - 1800 = 1800. Actually 1801 \u003e= 1800, condition satisfied! Wait compute: For k up to 1800, threshold 3600 - k is at least 1800. Minimum threshold is when k = 1800 =\u003e 3600-1800=1800. So we need each neighbor \u003e= 1800. Since high numbers are \u003e=1801, condition holds. So any low number k (\u003c=1800) adjacent to any high number (\u003e=1801) will have sum \u003e= k+1801 \u003e= 1802, not necessarily \u003e=3600. Wait we need sum \u003e=3600, not just threshold 3600 - k? Actually the condition is sum \u003e= 3600. For a low number k, we need neighbor y such that k + y \u003e= 3600 =\u003e y \u003e= 3600 - k. For k up to 1800, 3600 - k \u003e= 1800. So neighbor must be at least 1800. High numbers \u003e=1801 satisfy this only for k \u003c= 1800? Let's test worst case k=1800 =\u003e need y \u003e= 1800. 1801 works, sum = 3601 \u003e 3600. Good. For k=1799 =\u003e need y \u003e= 1801, high numbers start at 1801, sum = 3600 exactly? 1799+1801=3600. So works. For k=1 =\u003e need y \u003e= 3599, but high numbers include up to 3600, but also include numbers like 1801 which is not enough. So the condition fails for low numbers far below 1800. Indeed we need y \u003e= 3599 for k=1, but high numbers only go up to 3600, includes 3599 and 3600 but also many lower numbers not sufficient. So we need to ensure that each low number's neighbors are specifically among the highest numbers, not just any high number.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345050572,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345051689,"data":"       | Thus we need to allocate the highest numbers to be adjacent to the lowest numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345052891,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345053926,"data":"       | Potential construction: Place numbers in decreasing order along each row, but also alternating high and low across columns such that each low number is adjacent to high numbers that are near the maximum.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345055321,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345056401,"data":"       | Idea: Use \"pairing\" across each edge: For each edge, we can assign a pair of numbers that sum to at least 3600. Since each vertex participates in multiple edges, we need to ensure that all its incident edges have high enough partner.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345057876,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345059149,"data":"       | One approach: Use a \"gradient\" where numbers increase from one corner to opposite corner, but the increase is large enough that any adjacent pair sum \u003e= 3600. That would require that the smallest number plus its neighbor (which is larger) \u003e= 3600. So the smallest number must be at least 3600 - (neighbor). Since neighbor is at most 3600, the smallest number could be as low as 0? Not helpful.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345060958,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345061995,"data":"       | Better: Place numbers such that each cell's value is at least 1800 (i.e., all numbers \u003e=1800). But we must use numbers 1..1799 somewhere. So not possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345063309,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345064328,"data":"       | Thus we need to intermix low numbers with high numbers but ensure each low number is surrounded by sufficiently high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345065586,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345066626,"data":"       | Observation: Since each low number has at most 4 neighbors, we can assign the top 4*1800 = 7200 high numbers to be neighbors of the low numbers. But we only have 1800 high numbers (since total 3600). However each high number can serve as neighbor for up to 4 low numbers. So we could potentially satisfy constraints.\n"}
