,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345374519,"data":"     [Reasoning] (8918 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345375631,"data":"       | analysisThe backtracking naive may be too slow. Let's add pruning: we can compute lower bound for each unassigned cell based on S and assigned neighbors: for each neighbor assigned value v, the cell must be at least S - v. So we can restrict possible values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34537714,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345378152,"data":"       | Also we can order cells by constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345379298,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345380315,"data":"       | But maybe we can find a constructive pattern for general m,n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345381469,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34538251,"data":"       | Observation: For S = mn, we need each adjacent pair sum \u003e= mn. This is reminiscent of constructing a \"magic\" labeling where each edge sum is at least mn. Perhaps we can use a \"Latin rectangle\" where each row is a permutation of numbers such that each adjacent horizontally sum \u003e= mn, and each column also.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345384237,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345385305,"data":"       | Consider constructing grid where each row is decreasing from left to right, and each column is decreasing from top to bottom, but with a large offset between rows.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345386624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345387672,"data":"       | Specifically, let’s define value at (i,j) = a_i + b_j where a_i and b_j are sequences such that a_i + b_j are distinct and cover 1..mn. Since we need distinct values, we can use a_i = i*L, b_j = j*something? But sum may not be distinct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345389142,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345390185,"data":"       | But we can use a Latin square of order 60: assign each cell a pair (i,j) and map to number = i*60 + j + 1 (standard). Not good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345391452,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345392485,"data":"       | Maybe we can use \"snake\" ordering: Fill grid with numbers such that each row alternates direction, and numbers increase by 1 along a Hamiltonian path. Then adjacent cells horizontally or vertically may have difference up to 60? Not enough.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345393982,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345395245,"data":"       | Let's attempt to construct using \"complementary pairs\" across each edge. Since grid is bipartite, we can assign numbers to black cells as low numbers and to white cells as high numbers such that for each black cell with value x, all its white neighbors have values \u003e= mn - x. Since each black cell may have up to 4 white neighbors, we need to assign to each white cell a value that satisfies constraints from its black neighbors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34539869,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345399742,"data":"       | Thus we can think of solving inequality system: For each black cell b, for each adjacent white cell w, we need x_b + x_w \u003e= mn.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345401042,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345402077,"data":"       | Equivalently, for each white cell w, we need x_w \u003e= mn - max_{b neighbor of w} x_b? Actually need to satisfy for each neighbor individually, so x_w must be \u003e= mn - x_b for each adjacent black neighbor b. So x_w must be \u003e= max_{b neighbor} (mn - x_b) = mn - min_{b neighbor} x_b. So the constraint for a white cell is x_w \u003e= mn - min_adjacent_black_value.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345403848,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345404902,"data":"       | Thus if we ensure that each white cell's value is at least mn minus the minimum black neighbor value, we can satisfy all edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345406165,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345407192,"data":"       | Thus we can design values for black cells (low) and then assign white cells accordingly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345408375,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.3454094,"data":"       | Goal: assign distinct numbers to all cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345410522,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345411562,"data":"       | Idea: Choose black cells values as the smallest numbers (1..1800) in some pattern, and assign each white cell the value mn - (minimum black neighbor) (or maybe plus some offset) ensuring distinctness and staying within remaining numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345412984,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345414032,"data":"       | Since mn=3600, for black cell value k, the required minimum for its adjacent white cells is 3600 - k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345415238,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345416265,"data":"       | If we place black cells with small values, the required white values are large.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345417441,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345418479,"data":"       | Thus we can assign white cells values equal to 3600 - (minimum black neighbor) maybe plus some ordering to keep distinctness.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345419739,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345420761,"data":"       | Because each white cell may be adjacent to up to 4 black cells with different values; the minimum black neighbor will be the smallest among them, thus the required lower bound is highest (since 3600 - min black). So we need to ensure white cell value meets that.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345422265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345423542,"data":"       | Thus to minimize required white values (so we can fit within available numbers), we should make black neighbor values as large as possible (i.e., avoid very small black cells adjacent to many whites). So we should place the smallest black numbers at corners (degree 2) and maybe isolated.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345425068,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345426111,"data":"       | Thus we can attempt to construct as follows: assign black cells values in increasing order from corners outward, while white cells values are assigned as complementary high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345429119,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345430198,"data":"       | Potential explicit construction: Use a \"checkerboard\" where black cells are assigned numbers 1..1800 in a snake order such that each black cell's value is relatively high near interior. Then white cells assign numbers 3600, 3599, ..., 1801 in corresponding positions such that each white cell's value is at least 3600 - (minimum adjacent black). Since interior black numbers may be as high as 1800, the required white values may be as low as 1800 (which is okay). The smallest white values would be near interior where black numbers are near 1800. So we could assign white values descending from 3600 at corners to 1801 near interior.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345432348,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345433362,"data":"       | Thus we can design mapping: For each cell (i,j), define parity p = (i+j) mod 2. If p=0 (black), assign value = f(i,j) where f ranges from 1 to 1800 increasing outward? For p=1 (white), assign value = 3601 - f(i,j) maybe? Let's test: Suppose we set value(i,j) = (i*60 + j + 1) for black cells, and value = 3601 - (i*60 + j + 1) for white cells. This ensures each black-white pair across edge sum = 3601? Not exactly because the coordinates differ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345435144,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345436175,"data":"       | But if we take a black cell at (i,j) and a white neighbor at (i+1,j) (vertical), then black's value = g(i,j), white's value = 3601 - g(i+1,j). Sum = g(i,j) + 3601 - g(i+1,j). Not guaranteed \u003e=3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345437587,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34543859,"data":"       | However if we set g(i,j) = something monotone such that g(i+1,j) \u003c= g(i,j) + 1? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345439822,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345440865,"data":"       | Better: Use \"pairing by complement across each edge\" but we cannot guarantee for all edges simultaneously.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345442264,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345443492,"data":"       | Maybe we can achieve S = 3600 by constructing a \"Latin chessboard\" where each black cell's value plus each of its four white neighbors sum to at least 3600. If we assign white cells values as 3600 - (value of the black neighbor with smallest value among its adjacent blacks). Since each white cell may have multiple black neighbors, we need to assign a single value meeting all constraints, which is the maximum of required lower bounds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345445332,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345446364,"data":"       | Thus we can assign white cell value = 3600 - min_adjacent_black_value (or maybe larger). This ensures all edges satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345447615,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345448614,"data":"       | Now we need to ensure distinctness and that values are within 1..3600 and not overlapping with black values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345449845,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345450885,"data":"       | If we assign black values as a permutation of 1..1800, then for each white cell, its value will be 3600 - min_adjacent_black_value, which will be in range 1800..3599 (since min_adjacent_black_value between 1 and 1800). So white values will be at least 1800. Distinctness: Different white cells may get same value if they have same minimum black neighbor value. To avoid duplicates, we need to adjust.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345454192,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345455258,"data":"       | We could assign white values as 3600 - (some function) ensuring uniqueness, maybe using max of required lower bounds plus offset.\n"}
