,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345847654,"data":"       | analysisMaybe 4x4 cannot achieve S=16? Let's compute theoretical bound: For 4x4, mn=16. Upper bound: place 1 at corner, need neighbors \u003e=15. There are two neighbors (corner degree 2). So need numbers 15 and 16 adjacent to 1. That's okay. For number 2, also corner maybe need neighbors \u003e=14. Could place 2 at opposite corner with neighbors 14 and 15? But 15 already used adjacent to 1. Could still be okay if 2 adjacent to 14 and maybe 16 (unused). So seems plausible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34585119,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345852259,"data":"       | But maybe there is a parity or bipartite issue that prevents achieving S=mn for even dimensions \u003e2? Let's test 4x4 with MILP using brute force maybe using Python's 'python-constraint' library? Not available.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345853692,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345854706,"data":"       | We can attempt backtracking with better heuristics for 4x4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345855855,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345857145,"data":"       | Let's implement backtracking with ordering of cells by degree and also try values in descending order but also respecting lower bound. Also incorporate forward checking: for each unassigned cell, maintain domain of possible values based on constraints with assigned neighbors (lower bound). Use simple recursion.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345858731,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.345859778,"data":"       | But still may be large.\n"}
