,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346144052,"data":"       | We can think of bipartite nature: The grid is bipartite (checkerboard). Adjacent cells are opposite colors. So each edge connects a black cell to a white cell. So we can assign numbers to black cells and white cells. The sum across edge is sum of a black number and a white number. To maximize the minimal sum, we should assign large numbers to one color and small numbers to the other? Wait each edge sum uses one black and one white. If we assign all large numbers to black cells and small numbers to white cells, then each edge sum = large+small, which could still be relatively large if the smallest large number + largest small number is high.\n"}
