,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346147407,"data":"       | But we need to allocate numbers to each color set: grid has equal number of black and white cells? For even dimensions 60x60, total cells 3600, black = white = 1800 each.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346148748,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346151108,"data":"       | Thus we partition numbers 1..3600 into two groups of 1800 each: assign one group to black cells, the other to white cells. Then each edge sum = a number from group A + number from group B. The minimal sum across edges will be the minimal possible sum of a black number plus a white number that are adjacent. Since adjacency can be arranged arbitrarily (we can place numbers within each color arbitrarily), we can try to maximize the minimal sum by pairing each black cell with a white neighbor that yields high sum. However each black cell has up to 4 neighbors (except edges). So each black number will be adjacent to up to 4 white numbers. So the minimal sum across all edges will be determined by the smallest sum among all adjacent pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346153389,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346154425,"data":"       | If we can arrange such that each black number is adjacent only to relatively large white numbers, and each white number adjacent only to relatively large black numbers, we can increase the minimal sum.\n"}
