,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346159546,"data":"       | Consider we assign the 1800 smallest numbers to black cells, and the 1800 largest numbers to white cells. Then each edge sum = small+large, which could be as low as 1 + 1801 = 1802 (since smallest large number is 1801). So minimal sum would be 1802. Could we do better? If we instead intermix numbers more evenly, maybe we can increase minimal sum beyond 1802? Let's test: Suppose we assign numbers such that each black cell gets numbers from the top half (large) and white cells get numbers from bottom half (small). That yields minimal sum at least 1802. Could we achieve S \u003e 1802? To increase minimal sum, we need to avoid any edge where sum is less than target. That would require that for any black number b and any white neighbor w, b + w \u003e= S. Since each black cell has up to 4 white neighbors, the smallest sum for that black cell is b + (minimum of its adjacent white numbers). So to ensure all sums \u003e= S, we need that for each black cell, the minimum adjacent white number \u003e= S - b. So we need to assign white numbers such that each black cell's neighboring whites are all at least S - b.\n"}
