,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346391222,"data":"       | Thus to maximize S, we need to be able to place numbers 1..k (where k = floor((S-1)/2)) in the grid such that none are adjacent (i.e., they occupy an independent set). The maximum size of an independent set in a grid graph (bipartite) is half of the vertices (i.e., 1800) for even dimensions. So we can place at most 1800 numbers with value \u003c= k without adjacency. So we need k \u003c= 1800? Actually we can place up to 1800 cells (one color) with no adjacency (since bipartite). So we can place numbers 1..1800 on one color, and numbers 1801..3600 on other color. This yields S = smallest sum across edges = 1 + 1801 = 1802. But we can perhaps place numbers up to some larger k while still independent set because we can also use both colors but ensure low numbers are not adjacent to each other; they could be placed on both colors as long as they are not adjacent. However any two cells on same color are not adjacent (since grid bipartite). Cells on opposite colors are adjacent if they share side. So to avoid adjacency among low numbers, we must ensure low numbers are placed all on same color (or at least not adjacent across colors). The maximum independent set size is 1800 (one color). So we can place at most 1800 low numbers (any values) without adjacency.\n"}
