,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34639798,"data":"       | Thus we can have low numbers up to 1800 placed on black cells, and the rest numbers (1801..3600) on white cells. This arrangement ensures any edge sum is at least 1802. So S \u003e= 1802 is achievable (by this simple bipartition). But maybe we can do better by allowing some low numbers to be adjacent to high numbers but not to each other, still maintain higher minimal sum \u003e 1802? Wait the minimal sum is determined by the smallest low number + its neighbor's value. If we place low numbers on black cells and high numbers on white cells, the smallest sum is 1 + (minimum white number). The minimum white number is 1801 (since we assign the smallest 1800 numbers to black). So minimal sum = 1802. To increase S, we need to increase the minimum white number (i.e., assign larger numbers to white cells). But then some black cells will have to take numbers \u003e 1800, reducing the smallest sum maybe? Actually the minimal sum will be min_{black b} (b + min_{white neighbor of b}). Since each black cell has at least one white neighbor (except maybe corners? but corners have white neighbor). So the minimal sum will be (minimum black number) + (minimum white number among its neighbors). If we increase minimum white number, we need to also perhaps increase minimum black number to keep numbers distinct. But we could assign numbers such that both colors have numbers roughly balanced, but the smallest sum could be larger than 1802.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346401443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34640249,"data":"       | Consider we assign numbers 1..1800 to black cells, but not all of them; we could assign 1..1799 to black, and 1800 to white maybe, increasing min white to 1800. Then minimal sum could be 1+1800=1801 (still less). Actually we need to increase min white beyond 1801 to increase S. But we have only 1800 white cells; the smallest white number cannot exceed 1801 because there are 1800 numbers smaller than 1801 that must go somewhere (they could go to black cells). The smallest white number could be 1801 if we assign numbers 1..1800 to black, 1801..3600 to white. That's the minimal possible white number. So we cannot increase it unless we move some small numbers to white cells, but then they'd be adjacent to other small numbers maybe causing small sum.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346404758,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346405976,"data":"       | Thus the bipartite split yields S=1802. But maybe we can achieve higher S by not using a strict bipartition but mixing numbers such that each low number's neighbor is not the absolute smallest of the opposite color. For example, we could assign 1 to a black cell, and its white neighbors could be among the largest numbers (3600, 3599, etc.). The minimal sum would then be 1 + (some large number). However other black cells with slightly larger numbers may have neighbors that are not as large, potentially giving smaller sum. So the minimal sum will be determined by some pair of relatively low numbers that are forced to be adjacent due to degree constraints.\n"}
