,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346699695,"data":"       | Let's test for 2x2 (Δ=2 for corners). N=4, S_max=4 = N. Δ=2, N+Δ=6 \u003e not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346700987,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346701999,"data":"       | Maybe S_max = N + floor(N/(Δ+1))? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346704565,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346705721,"data":"       | Let's compute for 2x2: N=4, S_max=4. For 2x3: N=6, S_max=6. For 3x3: N=9, S_max=9. So far S_max = N.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346706968,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346707993,"data":"       | Let's test for 1xN line: N cells, Δ=2 (except ends). S_max = N+1. So differs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346709222,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346710275,"data":"       | Thus perhaps for 2D grid with both dimensions \u003e=2, S_max = N (i.e., mn). Let's test for 2x2 (N=4) works. For 2x3 (N=6) works. For 2x4 (N=8) works. For 3x3 (N=9) works. Could test for 3x4 using ILP to confirm.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346711753,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346713076,"data":"       | But maybe the pattern holds: S_max = mn for m,n \u003e=2. Then for 60x60, N=3600, S_max = 3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346714302,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346715324,"data":"       | But earlier we argued S cannot exceed 3601 due to neighbor constraints, and 3600 seems plausible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346716533,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346717575,"data":"       | Now we need to construct arrangement achieving S=3600. That would require each adjacent pair sum \u003e= 3600. Since max number is 3600, the smallest sum possible is 1+3599=3600, 2+3598=3600, etc. So each edge must be a complementary pair summing to at least 3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346719092,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346720145,"data":"       | Thus we need to pair each low number with a high number such that sum \u003e=3600. This is reminiscent of arranging numbers in a \"checkerboard\" pattern where each black cell gets a low number, each white cell gets its complement 3601 - low number (so sum = 3601). But we need distinct numbers, and we have 1800 pairs. Indeed we can pair numbers (i, 3601-i) for i=1..1800. This gives sums = 3601, which is \u003e3600. So we can achieve S=3601? Wait sum = 3601 \u003e 3600, but we need each edge sum \u003e= S. If we achieve all edges sum \u003e= 3601, then S could be 3601. But earlier we argued S cannot be 3601 because number 1 would need four neighbors each \u003e=3600, but we could assign 1's neighbors all 3600? Only one 3600 exists. However if we pair 1 with 3600 (sum 3601), but 1 has up to 4 neighbors, each neighbor must be a distinct high number (since numbers are distinct). So we would need four distinct numbers each \u003e=3600? Actually to have sum \u003e=3601, neighbor must be at least 3600 (since 1+3600=3601). So each neighbor of 1 must be 3600, but only one cell has 3600. So impossible. Therefore we cannot have all edges sum \u003e=3601. So S cannot be 3601.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346723353,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346724582,"data":"       | Thus S_max likely 3600. Let's test feasibility: Need each edge sum \u003e=3600. For a cell with value v, its neighbor must be at least 3600 - v. For v=1, neighbor must be \u003e=3599. There are numbers 3599 and 3600 (two numbers). Since 1 may have up to 4 neighbors, we need at most 4 distinct neighbors \u003e=3599, but we have only two numbers (3599,3600). However we could place 1 at a corner (degree 2) to need only 2 neighbors, both can be 3599 and 3600. That works. For cells with value 2, neighbor must be \u003e=3598. There are numbers 3598, 3599, 3600 (three numbers). If we place 2 at a corner also degree 2, we need two neighbors \u003e=3598, possible. So we can place low numbers at corners and edges to limit neighbor requirements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346726913,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346729299,"data":"       | Thus we need to assign low numbers to low-degree cells to satisfy constraints. Since there are only 4 corners (degree 2) and 232 edge cells (degree 3). So we can place the smallest numbers there. Let's see if we can assign numbers 1..k to low-degree cells such that each low number's neighbor requirement can be satisfied with distinct high numbers.\n"}
