,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346736687,"data":"       | Let's attempt to construct algorithmically for 60x60 using greedy assignment: assign numbers to cells in decreasing order of degree (interior first) with high numbers, and assign low numbers to low-degree cells. Ensure that each low number's neighbor requirement satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346738199,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346739225,"data":"       | We can try to construct using integer programming with constraints: For each edge (u,v), a_u + a_v \u003e= S. For S=3600, we need to find labeling.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346740531,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346741584,"data":"       | We can attempt to use Python with MILP using pulp (maybe not available). Could implement using OR-Tools CP-SAT (maybe not available). Could use simple heuristic: start with empty grid, assign numbers gradually.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346742976,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.346744244,"data":"       | But maybe there is known constructive pattern: Place numbers in pairs that sum to 3600 (or 3601) along edges. For example, we can fill the grid with a \"Latin square\" pattern where each cell's value plus its neighbor's value = 3600 (or 3601) for all edges? Not possible because each cell has multiple neighbors; can't satisfy all sums simultaneously unless all neighbors have same complementary value, which is impossible.\n"}
