,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347229717,"data":"       | analysisThus we can select L as 1680 interior cells, 120 edge cells, 0 corners (or other combos with some corners). So it's possible to have independent set of size 1800 with total degree 7080 (as required). So we can have L being independent set covering half cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347231208,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34723253,"data":"       | Thus we can assign low numbers to L (size 1800) and high numbers to H (size 1800). Since edges are only between L and H, each edge sum = low + high. The minimal sum will be min_{low} (low + min_{high neighbor}) which is at least min_low + min_high (since each low is adjacent to some high). The minimum high number will be the smallest number assigned to H. To maximize minimal sum, we should assign the smallest high number as large as possible, i.e., assign numbers 1801..3600 to H, and numbers 1..1800 to L. Then min sum = 1 + 1801 = 1802. So S=1802 achievable.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34723448,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347235515,"data":"       | But can we do better than 1802 while still having independent set? If we allow some edges between low-low (i.e., allow low numbers to be adjacent), then we need to ensure those sums also \u003e= S, which may be higher than 1802. However allowing low-low edges would potentially lower minimal sum unless we assign low numbers carefully.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347237177,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34723826,"data":"       | But maybe we can increase S beyond 1802 by not using independent set of size 1800; we could have less low numbers, i.e., choose L smaller, assign more numbers to high set, making minimum high number larger, increasing min sum. However we also need to place the remaining numbers (the ones not in L) somewhere; they could be medium numbers placed maybe in H as well. The minimal sum would then be determined by the smallest number overall (maybe still low) plus its neighbor's number (which could be relatively high). If we reduce number of low numbers, the smallest number is still 1, but its neighbor can be high. So min sum could be 1 + something high. To increase S, we need to ensure that 1's neighbors are high enough. Since 1 can have at most 4 neighbors, we can allocate high numbers (like 3600, 3599, 3598, 3597) to be its neighbors. So we could have S up to 1+3597=3598? Actually need each neighbor \u003e= S-1, so S \u003c= 1 + max neighbor value. Since max neighbor value is 3600, we can have S up to 3601 but earlier impossible due to multiple neighbors needing same high value. But we could allocate distinct high numbers to each neighbor. So S could be up to 1+3597=3598 if we assign four distinct numbers \u003e= S-1. Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347245863,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347247362,"data":"       | If we want S = 3598, then for v=1, neighbors need \u003e= 3597. There are numbers 3597, 3598, 3599, 3600 (four numbers). So possible if we place 1 at a corner (degree 2) we need only two of them. So S could be as high as 3599 maybe? Let's test S=3599: need neighbors \u003e= 3598 (numbers 3598, 3599, 3600). 1 at corner degree 2 -\u003e need two numbers \u003e= 3598, possible. For v=2, need neighbors \u003e= 3597 (numbers 3597-3600). If 2 also at corner (different corner) fine. So maybe we can achieve S close to 3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347249395,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347250451,"data":"       | Thus the limiting factor is not just low numbers but the capacity of high numbers to serve as neighbors for many low numbers. Since each high number can serve up to 4 low neighbors, we can allocate many low numbers around each high number.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347251917,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347252998,"data":"       | Thus we need to find maximal S such that there exists labeling meeting constraints. This looks like a \"graph labeling with sum constraints\" problem. Could be solved via constructing a \"pairing\" of numbers into complementary pairs that sum to at least S, arranged in a checkerboard pattern where each high number is surrounded by low numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347254745,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347255794,"data":"       | Potential approach: Use a \"alternating\" pattern where each high number (say \u003e S/2) is placed on interior cells, each low number (\u003c S/2) placed on adjacent cells (like a chessboard). But we need to ensure each low number's neighbor set includes only high numbers (since low+low would be \u003c S). So we need a bipartition where low numbers are on one color and high numbers on the other. That's the independent set scenario again. However if we set low numbers on black cells, high numbers on white cells, then each edge sum = low+high. The minimal sum will be min_low + min_high. To increase S beyond 1802, we need min_high larger than 1801. That would require moving some low numbers to white cells (i.e., mixing) while ensuring low-low adjacency avoided or sum still \u003e= S.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347258267,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347259375,"data":"       | But if we move a low number to white side, it will be adjacent to other low numbers (black side) possibly causing low+low edges. However we could ensure that any low number placed on white side is still large enough such that sum with adjacent black low numbers is \u003e= S. That would require those black numbers to be not too low. So we could shift the threshold upward.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347262525,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347263823,"data":"       | Thus the optimal arrangement may involve splitting numbers into two sets not based purely on size, but such that each edge sum \u003e= S.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347265138,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347266159,"data":"       | Maybe the maximum S is 2400? Let's test via heuristic for moderate grid (e.g., 6x6) to see max S we can achieve via simulated annealing. We'll try to maximize minimal sum.\n"}
