,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347776592,"data":"       | We can formulate as: we want to maximize minimal sum S. Equivalent to find labeling such that all edges have sum \u003e= S. This is similar to graph labeling problem known as \"graceful labeling\"? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347778083,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34777942,"data":"       | Maybe we can use concept of \"bandwidth\" or \"anti-bandwidth\"? Actually anti-bandwidth problem: assign numbers to vertices to maximize minimum difference across edges. Here we care about sum, not difference. But sum condition can be transformed by mapping numbers to complement: define new label x_i = i (original). The sum condition a_u + a_v \u003e= S can be rewritten as (M - a_u) + (M - a_v) \u003c= 2M - S where M = max number = N (i.e., 3600). Define b_i = N+1 - a_i (complement). Then a_u + a_v \u003e= S \u003c=\u003e (N+1 - b_u) + (N+1 - b_v) \u003e= S =\u003e 2(N+1) - (b_u + b_v) \u003e= S =\u003e b_u + b_v \u003c= 2(N+1) - S.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347781879,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347782928,"data":"       | Thus maximizing minimal sum S is equivalent to minimizing maximal sum of complements across edges. Since b_i also range 1..N (bijection). So we want to label vertices with numbers 1..N such that the maximum sum across edges of b_u + b_v is minimized. That's analogous to anti-bandwidth but with sum.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347784458,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347786848,"data":"       | If we aim for S maximal, then the complement bound becomes minimizing max edge sum of b's.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347788121,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347789206,"data":"       | Now note that b_i are also a permutation of 1..N. So we need to minimize the maximum sum across edges. This is similar to the \"bandwidth\" problem where we minimize maximum difference across edges. However sum is different.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347790737,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347792089,"data":"       | But perhaps the optimal arrangement is to assign numbers in a \"checkerboard\" pattern of high and low numbers alternating, which makes sums relatively balanced. For complement, that would correspond to minimizing max sum, which might be achieved by assigning numbers such that each edge connects a low and a high number (i.e., alternating). Then the sum of complements on an edge would be (low complement + high complement). Since complement of low is high, complement of high is low. So sum of complements across edge would be also high+low = N+1 (since a + b = N+1 for complementary pairs). Indeed if we pair numbers i and N+1-i, their sum is N+1. So if we assign complementary pairs to adjacent cells, each edge sum of complements would be N+1 (constant). That would minimize the maximum sum to N+1 (since any edge sum cannot be less than N+1? Actually we want to minimize max sum, so N+1 is maybe minimal possible maximum sum. Then original S = 2(N+1) - (max sum) = 2(N+1) - (N+1) = N+1. Wait compute: S = 2(N+1) - max_sum_complement. If max_sum_complement = N+1, then S = N+1. For N=3600, S = 3601. But we earlier argued S=3601 impossible due to degree constraints. So maybe we cannot achieve constant N+1 across all edges because each vertex has multiple edges, requiring each vertex's value to be complementary to all its neighbors, which is impossible unless degree 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347795425,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347796447,"data":"       | Thus the minimal possible max sum of complements is larger than N+1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347797622,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347798657,"data":"       | We can think of each vertex's complement value b_i. For each vertex, its incident edges sums are b_i + b_j for neighbors j. The maximum over all edges is at least max_i (b_i + max_{neighbor} b_j). To minimize overall max, we want each vertex's value to be small when its neighbors are also small, but that's contradictory.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347800222,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34780126,"data":"       | Maybe the optimal S is around N - O(sqrt(N))? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347802413,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347803437,"data":"       | Let's attempt to compute using integer programming for moderate size (e.g., 4x4 or 5x5) to see exact maximum S, then see pattern.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34780469,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347805706,"data":"       | We can brute force for 4x4 using MILP? 16 variables with all-different constraints is tough but we can use CP-SAT but not available. Could use backtracking search with pruning using constraints of sum \u003e= S to test feasibility for given S, using recursion and constraint propagation. For 4x4 maybe feasible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347807299,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347808326,"data":"       | Let's implement a backtracking search for 4x4 to find maximal S exactly, using pruning: assign numbers to cells one by one, keep track of adjacency constraints, and prune if any edge sum \u003c S. We can also use symmetry reduction (coloring). Let's attempt for 4x4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347811786,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347812848,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codefrom functools import lru_cache\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347814047,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34781507,"data":"       | def max_S_bruteforce(m,n):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347816165,"data":"       |     N=m*n\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347817227,"data":"       |     cells=[(i,j) for i in range(m) for j in range(n)]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347818389,"data":"       |     # adjacency list\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347819458,"data":"       |     adj = {cell: [] for cell in cells}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347820775,"data":"       |     for i in range(m):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347821861,"data":"       |         for j in range(n):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347822995,"data":"       |             if i+1\u003cm:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347824103,"data":"       |                 adj[(i,j)].append((i+1,j))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347825216,"data":"       |             if j+1\u003cn:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347826318,"data":"       |                 adj[(i,j)].append((i,j+1))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34782744,"data":"       |             if i-1\u003e=0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347828562,"data":"       |                 adj[(i,j)].append((i-1,j))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347829692,"data":"       |             if j-1\u003e=0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.3478308,"data":"       |                 adj[(i,j)].append((i,j-1))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347831917,"data":"       |     # order cells maybe by degree descending\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347833036,"data":"       |     order = sorted(cells, key=lambda c: -len(adj[c]))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347834173,"data":"       |     # Use backtracking with pruning for given S\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347835315,"data":"       |     def feasible(S):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347836411,"data":"       |         used = [False]*(N+1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347837523,"data":"       |         assignment = {}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.347838596,"data":"       |         # recursive\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34783967,"data":"       |         def backtrack(idx):\n"}
