,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348257441,"data":"       | We can treat the problem as: find the largest S such that there exists labeling where for all edges (i,j), a_i + a_j \u003e= S. This is reminiscent of \"graph labeling with sum constraint\" also known as \"anti-bandwidth\"? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348259062,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348260227,"data":"       | We can attempt to formulate as integer programming: binary variables for assignment of numbers to cells. But that's huge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348261485,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348262773,"data":"       | We can search for theoretical bound: Since each number appears once, consider total sum of numbers = 3600*3601/2 = 3600*1800.5 = 6481800? Actually compute: 3600*3601/2 = 3600*1800.5 = 6481800. Yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34826415,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348265191,"data":"       | Consider sum over all edges of (a_i + a_j). Each edge counted both endpoints? Actually sum over edges of a_i + a_j = sum_{edge} a_i + sum_{edge} a_j = each vertex appears in degree(v) edges, so total = sum_{v} degree(v) * a_v. For grid interior cells degree 4, edge cells degree 3, corners degree 2. Count total degrees: sum degrees = 2*E where E is number of edges. For 60x60 grid, number of horizontal edges = 60*(60-1)=60*59=3540. Same vertical edges = 3540. So total edges E = 7080. Sum degrees = 2E = 14160.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348267101,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348268157,"data":"       | Thus sum over edges of (a_i + a_j) = sum_v degree(v) a_v.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348269302,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348270323,"data":"       | If each edge sum \u003e= S, then total over edges \u003e= S * E = S*7080.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348271514,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348272567,"data":"       | Thus S \u003c= (sum_v degree(v) a_v) / E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348273723,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348277877,"data":"       | But we can bound numerator using degrees. Since degrees vary, we can compute maximum possible value of sum_v degree(v) a_v given we can assign numbers to cells with given degree distribution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348279268,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348280316,"data":"       | We want to maximize lower bound S, i.e., we want to maximize minimal edge sum, which is at most the average edge sum (since min \u003c= average). So maximum possible S cannot exceed the maximal possible minimal edge sum, which is at most the maximal possible average edge sum (since min \u003c= avg). However we can try to maximize average edge sum by assigning larger numbers to higher degree cells (interior cells degree 4) and smaller numbers to corners degree 2, edges degree 3. This will maximize sum_v degree(v) a_v.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348282265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348283283,"data":"       | Thus compute degree distribution:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348284377,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348285403,"data":"       | - Corners: 4 corners each degree 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.348286528,"data":"       | - Edge cells (non-corner) along perimeter: there are (60-2)*4 = 58*4 = 232 cells, each degree 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34828775,"data":"       | - Interior cells: total cells 3600 - corners 4 - edge non-corner 232 = 3364 cells, each degree 4.\n"}
