,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349375399,"data":"     [Reasoning] (11890 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349376466,"data":"       | analysisThese average bounds are not tight.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349377586,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349378648,"data":"       | Maybe there's known result: For a grid graph, the maximum minimal sum of distinct labels 1..N is N - floor(N/ (max degree + 1))? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349379933,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349380971,"data":"       | Let's attempt to solve exact for n=4 using integer programming (ILP) with binary variables for ordering? Could model as linear constraints using big-M: For each edge (i,j) we need a_i + a_j \u003e= S. Variables a_i integer 1..N, all distinct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349382463,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349383503,"data":"       | But we can treat S as variable to maximize, and we can linearize distinctness using all-different constraints, not linear.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349384746,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349385781,"data":"       | Thus not easy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349386855,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349387886,"data":"       | Maybe there is known result for \"grid antimagic labeling\"? Actually there is concept of \"graceful labeling\" where edge labels are absolute differences, but not sum.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349389341,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349390378,"data":"       | But maybe the answer is simply 3600? Let's test constructing arrangement for n=60 achieving S=3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349391596,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349392618,"data":"       | We need to ensure each adjacent pair sum \u003e=3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349393769,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349394796,"data":"       | Consider placing numbers 1..1800 in one color (white) and 1801..3600 in black. The smallest possible sum of a white and black pair could be 1+1801=1802 (\u003c3600). Not good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349396149,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349397206,"data":"       | Thus we need to avoid small numbers being adjacent to relatively small large numbers; we need to pair each small number with a large number such that sum \u003e=3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349398578,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349399601,"data":"       | If we can ensure that each small number a is adjacent only to numbers \u003e= 3600 - a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349400845,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349401888,"data":"       | Since we have many large numbers, perhaps we can assign each small number's neighbors to be among the largest numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349403116,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349404183,"data":"       | The hardest small numbers are 1,2,... up to maybe 1800? Actually for a up to 1799, need neighbor \u003e= 3600 - a, which is at least 1801. So we need for each a \u003c= 1799, neighbor \u003e= 3600 - a \u003e= 1801. So we need enough high numbers to serve all low numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349405961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34940832,"data":"       | How many neighbor slots needed? For each low number a (say a \u003c= 1799), its degree d (2-4). Sum of degrees for all low numbers up to 1799 must be \u003c= total degree capacity of numbers \u003e= 1801 (the high numbers). Since high numbers are many (1801..3600 = 1800 numbers) each with degree up to 4, total capacity = sum of degrees of high cells (which we can allocate). The degree distribution for high cells we can choose; we can assign high numbers to interior cells to maximize capacity (degree 4). So capacity = 4*interior_high + 3*edge_high + 2*corner_high. At most 4*1800 = 7200 if we could place all high numbers in interior, but there are only 3364 interior cells. So capacity limited.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349410683,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349411701,"data":"       | But we have 1800 high numbers; we can place them all interior? No, interior cells are 3364 \u003e 1800, so yes we can place all high numbers in interior cells, each degree 4, capacity = 4*1800 = 7200.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349413099,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349414119,"data":"       | Now sum of degrees of low numbers (the rest 1800 numbers) must be \u003c= capacity 7200. Since each low cell degree at most 4, total degree of low cells \u003c= 4*1800 = 7200. So capacity matches. So feasible in terms of degree counts.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349415598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349416645,"data":"       | Thus S=3600 may be feasible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349417733,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349418755,"data":"       | But we also need to ensure that each low cell's neighbors are distinct high cells? Not necessarily distinct; a high cell can be neighbor to up to 4 low cells, which is okay.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349420093,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349421123,"data":"       | Thus seems plausible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349422221,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34942326,"data":"       | Now we need to construct explicit labeling for n=60 achieving S=3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349424486,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349425878,"data":"       | Potential construction: Place the 1800 smallest numbers on a checkerboard of one color (say white), but not all; we need to ensure each white cell's neighbors (black cells) are among high numbers (\u003e= 1801). So we can assign all black cells high numbers (1801..3600). That's exactly the checkerboard scheme earlier, which gave min sum 1802, not 3600. Why? Because we placed low numbers 1..1800 on white cells, but their neighbors are black cells with numbers ranging from 1801 to 3600. The smallest sum is 1+1801=1802. So S=3600 not satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349427819,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349428858,"data":"       | Thus we need to assign high numbers to black cells such that each black neighbor of a low white cell is at least 3600 - a, which for a=1 is 3599. So black cells adjacent to white cell 1 must be among {3599,3600}. But there are only two black cells adjacent to that corner. So we can place 3599 and 3600 there. For a=2 (maybe another corner), need neighbors \u003e= 3598, which we can allocate 3598 and 3599 maybe but 3599 already used. Could be okay if 2 is placed elsewhere with degree 3 etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349430756,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34943178,"data":"       | Thus we need to allocate high numbers to satisfy these constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34943294,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349435742,"data":"       | Thus we need to place the very highest numbers near the smallest numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349436942,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349437971,"data":"       | Idea: Place numbers decreasing outward from corners: corners have smallest numbers; adjacent cells (edges) have largest numbers; next cells have next smallest etc. This resembles a \"gradient\" pattern.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349439443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34944047,"data":"       | Specifically, we could assign numbers based on Manhattan distance from a corner: cells at distance d get numbers in some range.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349441725,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34944275,"data":"       | If we place 1 at a corner, its two neighbors need to be 3599 and 3600. Those neighbor cells are edge cells (degree 3). Then those edge cells also have other neighbors which need to satisfy constraints with their own numbers (which are large). Since those edge cells are large, constraints with their other neighbors (which might be interior) are easy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349444363,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349445399,"data":"       | Thus we can perhaps design pattern where each corner has smallest numbers (1,2,3,4) and the adjacent edge cells have the largest numbers (3600,3599, etc.) to satisfy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349446733,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.34944776,"data":"       | Similarly, cells at distance 2 from corner could have next smallest numbers (5,6,... ) and their neighbors (distance 1 or 3) have large numbers accordingly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349449062,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349450076,"data":"       | Thus we can maybe achieve S close to 3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349451221,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349452423,"data":"       | Let's attempt to construct algorithmically for n=60: Fill grid with numbers such that for each cell (i,j), its value = something like f(i,j) where f is monotone increasing with distance from a corner? Let's try to define value = i+j+1 maybe? Not large enough.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349453888,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349454919,"data":"       | Better: Use ordering based on sum of coordinates: cells with low (i+j) get small numbers, cells with high (i+j) get large numbers, but we need adjacency across different sums.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349456233,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349457253,"data":"       | If we order by Manhattan distance from a corner, adjacent cells differ by at most 1 in distance, so sums of numbers may be moderate.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349458551,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349459628,"data":"       | But we need sum \u003e= 3600, which is huge relative to numbers (max 3600). This suggests that the smallest number must be paired with numbers near 3600, but not necessarily all pairs need to be that high; it's sufficient that each adjacent pair sum \u003e= S. So for interior cells with moderate numbers, they need neighbors such that sum \u003e= 3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349461311,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349462336,"data":"       | Thus any cell with value a must have all neighbors \u003e= 3600 - a. So if a = 1800, neighbors must be \u003e= 1800. That's easy because many numbers \u003e=1800.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349471961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349473603,"data":"       | Thus the hardest constraints are for very small a (like \u003c= 1800). For a=1800, need neighbor \u003e= 1800. That's fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349474966,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349476003,"data":"       | Thus we need to ensure that for each a \u003c= 1799, neighbors are \u003e= 3600 - a \u003e 1801. So we need high numbers adjacent to low numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.349477362,"data":"       | \n"}
