,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350265676,"data":"       | Let S be the minimal sum across edges. Then total sum of all edge sums \u003e= S * number_of_edges. However each vertex (cell) appears in degree d_i edges (2-4). So total sum of edge sums = sum_{i} a_i * d_i, where a_i is number in cell i. Because each edge counted twice? Wait each edge contributes a_i + a_j. Summing over edges, each vertex contributes its number multiplied by its degree. So total = sum_i a_i * deg_i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.35027001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350271094,"data":"       | We can compute sum_i a_i * deg_i. Since deg_i varies, we can bound this total by using max degree (4). The minimal degree is 2 for corner cells. So we can get inequality: total edge sum \u003c= 4 * sum_i a_i = 4 * total sum of numbers = 4 * N(N+1)/2 = 2N(N+1). Similarly total edge sum \u003e= 2 * sum_i a_i = 2 * total sum = N(N+1). But we need more precise.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350272803,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350273845,"data":"       | If we let S be minimal edge sum, then total edge sum \u003e= S * E where E = number of edges. So S \u003c= total edge sum / E.\n"}
