,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350452332,"data":"       | Idea: Place numbers in such a way that each small number is adjacent only to large numbers at least 3600 - small. Since max number is 3600, the smallest neighbor must be at least 3600 - small. For small numbers like 1, need neighbor \u003e= 3599. There are two numbers \u003e= 3599: 3599 and 3600. So 1 can be adjacent to at most 2 cells (its degree). In grid corners have degree 2, edges 3, interior 4. So we could place 1 in a corner, adjacent to two cells maybe 3599 and 3600, achieving sums 3600 and 3601. Similarly 2 needs neighbor \u003e= 3598, we have numbers 3598,3599,3600. Could place 2 at another corner adjacent to 3598 and 3599 maybe. This suggests we can place low numbers in corners and edges, each adjacent to high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350454666,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350455709,"data":"       | We need to ensure that each high number also has neighbors that satisfy the condition (but high numbers automatically have large sums with any neighbor because neighbor \u003e=1). However we must also ensure that high numbers are not adjacent to each other such that sum may exceed bound but that's fine; condition is lower bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350459002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350460066,"data":"       | Thus the limiting factor is low numbers needing sufficiently large neighbors. So we need to assign low numbers to cells with low degree (corners degree 2) to reduce number of required large neighbors. Then assign medium numbers to edge cells degree 3, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350461534,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350462739,"data":"       | Hence we can try to assign numbers such that each cell of degree d gets a number a where we have at least d numbers \u003e= S - a to be its neighbors (distinct across edges). But we can reuse large numbers for multiple neighbors (a large number can be neighbor of multiple small cells). So we need not have distinct high numbers for each adjacency.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350471299,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350472901,"data":"       | Thus perhaps we can achieve S=3600 by placing numbers 1..4 in corners, each adjacent to large numbers \u003e= 3599 etc. Since corners have two neighbors, we need for each corner number x, its two neighboring cells must have numbers \u003e= 3600 - x. Since there are many high numbers, we can allocate them.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350474516,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350475529,"data":"       | Similarly edge cells degree 3 need three neighbors each \u003e= 3600 - x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350476718,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350477759,"data":"       | Thus we need to find assignment where for each cell with number x, the set of its neighbors all have numbers \u003e= 3600 - x. This is reminiscent of monotone arrangement: numbers increase as we move away from low-number region.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350479203,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350480225,"data":"       | Potential construction: Fill grid with numbers increasing from a corner outward, such that each cell's number is roughly (row+col) based ordering. For instance, assign number = (i+j)*something? Not.\n"}
