,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350497919,"data":"       | Observation: If we assign numbers to black squares as the first 1800 numbers, the smallest low is 1. That's the bottleneck. To raise min sum, we need smallest low to be larger. So we could shift some of the smallest numbers to white squares? But then they may be adjacent to other small numbers (white-white adjacency not allowed). Since adjacency only across colors, if we place a small number on a white square, its neighbors are black squares, which could be large numbers if we allocate large numbers to black squares. So we could allocate small numbers to both colors, but ensure that each edge involves at least one large number.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350500021,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350501071,"data":"       | Thus we could try to assign numbers such that each edge includes at least one number from the top K largest numbers. That would guarantee sum \u003e= (smallest number) + (some large). But small numbers could be adjacent only to large numbers, not to each other.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350502561,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350503587,"data":"       | So we need a bipartition of edges such that each edge is \"covered\" by at least one large number.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350504859,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350506095,"data":"       | This resembles a vertex cover problem: choose a set of vertices (cells) to hold large numbers such that every edge is incident to at least one of them. Minimum vertex cover size for bipartite grid is known: for m x n grid, minimum vertex cover size equals size of maximum matching = mn/2 (since bipartite). So we need at least half of cells to be large numbers to cover all edges. Indeed, we can assign large numbers to one color (say white) and small numbers to black; then every edge is incident to a large number (white). That's the checkerboard arrangement we considered earlier. So we can have all large numbers on white squares (size N/2), all small numbers on black squares (size N/2). This yields min sum = smallest small + smallest large.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350508349,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350509385,"data":"       | Thus to increase min sum, we could increase the smallest large number (i.e., we could assign not just the top half but perhaps top K numbers to white squares, but also some smaller numbers may be on white squares as long as each black square (small) has only white neighbors (which are large). But each black square's neighbors are white cells (4 of them maybe). So as long as each black square's neighboring white cells have numbers \u003e= S - black_number. So we may allocate some large numbers to black squares as well, but we need to ensure that any edge connecting two black squares does not exist (since adjacency is across colors). So we can place any numbers on black squares, but edges always cross colors. So the condition for each edge is that sum of numbers from opposite colors \u003e= S. This is essentially a bipartite inequality.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350513505,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.350514543,"data":"       | Thus we can treat this as: we have two sets B and W each of size 1800. We need to assign numbers to B and W such that for every edge (i.e., for each pair (b,w) that are adjacent), we have b + w \u003e= S. Since the adjacency graph is a bipartite grid where each b is adjacent to up to 4 w's.\n"}
