,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351161114,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351162131,"data":"       | But to get S \u003e N - floor(n/2) + 1, the required high numbers count may be insufficient.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351163372,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351164396,"data":"       | Let's formalize: Suppose we aim for S = N - t + 1 (i.e., increase by 1). Then the smallest number a_min = 1 would need neighbor \u003e= N - t + 1 - 1 = N - t. That's number \u003e= N - t, which is in the set of numbers {N - t, ..., N}. Count of numbers \u003e= N - t is t+1. For n=60, t = floor(n/2)-1 = 29, so count = 30 numbers (from 3571 to 3600). Actually N - t = 3600 - 29 = 3571. So numbers \u003e= 3571 count = 30.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351167892,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.35116894,"data":"       | Thus we need at least enough high cells to serve low cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351170098,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351171116,"data":"       | Now consider the numbers less than some threshold L. For each such number a, its required neighbor must be \u003e= S - a = (N - t + 1) - a. As a increases, required neighbor threshold decreases.\n"}
