,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351184172,"data":"       | But we can use a combinatorial bound based on ordering numbers along a Hamiltonian path across grid (like snake). For a path covering all cells, adjacent pairs along path are edges. The minimal sum along the path cannot exceed something due to ordering of numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351185664,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351186694,"data":"       | Consider ordering numbers 1..N along a Hamiltonian path. The sum of each consecutive pair must be at least S. The sum of all numbers along path (pairwise sums) is sum_{i=1}^{N-1} (a_i + a_{i+1}) = a_1 + 2*sum_{i=2}^{N-1} a_i + a_N = 2*total_sum - (a_1 + a_N). Since total_sum = N(N+1)/2. Therefore average adjacent sum along the path is (2*total_sum - (a_1 + a_N))/(N-1). Since a_1 + a_N \u003e= 1+N = N+1, the average \u003c= (2*total_sum - (N+1))/(N-1). Compute this bound.\n"}
