,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351366284,"data":"       | But we can consider ordering of numbers: Suppose we assign the smallest m numbers to black cells. Then each of those cells must have a neighbor with number at least S - (its number). The worst-case among them is the smallest number (1) requiring neighbor \u003e= S - 1. So we need at least one white cell with number \u003e= S - 1. That's easy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351368086,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.35136936,"data":"       | But we need enough white cells with high enough numbers to cover all black cells. Since each white cell can serve up to 4 black cells (its degree). So we need at least ceil(m/4) high white cells with numbers \u003e= S - (max low number). But max low number among the m smallest is m. So required neighbor threshold = S - m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351370964,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351372018,"data":"       | Thus we need count of numbers \u003e= S - m to be at least ceil(m/4). So condition: N - (S - m) + 1 \u003e= ceil(m/4). That is N - S + m + 1 \u003e= ceil(m/4). Rearranged: N - S + m + 1 \u003e= m/4 (approx). =\u003e N - S + 1 \u003e= -3m/4. Since RHS negative for positive m, not restrictive.\n"}
