,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351748344,"data":"       | Potentially, we could consider a bipartite nature. The grid graph is bipartite (checkerboard). Adjacent cells have opposite colors. So each edge connects black and white cells. So we can assign numbers to colors: one color gets high numbers, the other low numbers. Then each edge sum = low+high, which may be relatively high if high numbers are large enough. The minimal sum would be the smallest low number plus the smallest high number adjacent to it? Wait if we assign all low numbers to black cells and all high numbers to white cells, then each edge sum is low+high. To maximize minimal sum, we would want the smallest low number to be paired with the smallest high number (since adjacency is across colors). But each low vertex may have multiple high neighbors; the minimal sum across all edges would be min over all low vertex (low value + min neighbor high value). Since we can arrange high numbers arbitrarily among white cells, we could ensure that each low vertex's neighbors are among the highest high numbers possible, but limited by degree.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351751088,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351752129,"data":"       | Thus maybe we can assign numbers to maximize minimal sum by placing the smallest numbers on vertices of degree 2 (corners) and then assign their neighbors the largest numbers, etc.\n"}
