,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351976441,"data":"       | If we color the grid black/white, each vertex has degree up to 4 with opposite color. So each edge connects opposite colors. So if we assign low numbers to white cells and high numbers to black cells, each edge will be low-high. This matches bipartite nature. Then each low vertex's neighbors are all high vertices (since all adjacent cells are opposite color). So each low vertex's degree equals number of high neighbors (2-4). So each low vertex's constraints are satisfied using high numbers only, which is okay.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351978324,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351979468,"data":"       | Thus we can restrict ourselves to bipartite labeling: assign low numbers to one color, high numbers to the other color. Then each edge sum = low+high, and minimal sum = min_{low} low + min_{high neighbor of that low} high. Since each low vertex's neighbors are all high vertices, the minimal sum across edges equals min_{low vertex} (low value + min high among its neighbors). But we can arrange high numbers such that each low vertex's neighboring high numbers are as large as possible, but limited by availability.\n"}
