,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.35199051,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351991537,"data":"       | Thus to maximize the global minimum, we might want to ensure that each low vertex's neighboring high numbers are all relatively large, especially for low a. This suggests we might assign the largest high numbers to be adjacent to the smallest low numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351992994,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.35199403,"data":"       | In bipartite grid, each low cell's neighbor set is fixed: for each position, its neighbor cells are predetermined (adjacent squares). So we can treat this as a \"matching\" problem: assign high numbers to black cells such that for each white cell, the minimum among its adjacent black cells is as large as possible, while also respecting each black cell's label.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351995768,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351996795,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of the \"minimax\" labeling problem: maximize the minimum over white cells of (white value + min of adjacent black values). Equivalent to maximize t such that we can assign numbers such that for each white cell with value a, all its adjacent black cells have value \u003e= t - a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351998505,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.351999718,"data":"       | Thus we can treat constraints: For each white cell (position), any adjacent black cell must have label \u003e= t - a (where a is white cell's label). That is similar to earlier global constraints but now restricted to bipartite.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352001171,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.3520022,"data":"       | Goal: find maximal integer t for which such labeling exists.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352003344,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352004369,"data":"       | This is a kind of feasibility of constraints.\n"}
