,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352006481,"data":"       | We could attempt to solve via integer linear programming or greedy algorithm.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352007661,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352008706,"data":"       | Given the size 60x60 (3600 cells), we might attempt to construct a labeling algorithmically and test for various t via computer search (maybe using heuristic). Since grid is large, but we can use algorithmic approach (e.g., assign numbers descending to black cells, ascending to white cells) and see minimal sum.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352010275,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352011308,"data":"       | But we need to find maximum t; perhaps there is known result: For an m x n grid, the maximum minimal sum is something like floor( (2N+1)/ (something) )? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.35201422,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.35201526,"data":"       | Let's attempt computational search using integer programming maybe with pulp? Not allowed. But we can attempt greedy algorithm: assign numbers to cells to maximize minimal sum using heuristic: place 1 at corner, assign 3600 to its neighbor, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352016737,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352017897,"data":"       | But we need guarantee of optimality. Could try to compute upper bound via counting: For each low number a, we need at least degree(a) distinct high numbers \u003e= t - a. Summing over all low vertices, we get total required high slots.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352019359,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.35202054,"data":"       | Let L be set of low vertices (white). For each low vertex v, with label a_v, need at least deg(v) high numbers \u003e= t - a_v. However a high number can be counted multiple times if it serves as neighbor to multiple low vertices; but each high vertex can serve up to its degree number of low vertices (since each edge is unique). So we can count total low-high edges needed: total edges = E = 7080. Since each edge connects a low and high vertex (if bipartite labeling). So each edge is a low-high pair. So the constraint reduces to: for each edge, sum \u003e= t.\n"}
