,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352046549,"data":"       | But we can choose different partition: maybe choose L as a set of numbers not necessarily the smallest; we can choose L to be some numbers around middle, and H to be numbers above some threshold. The minimal sum across edges will be min_{edge} (a in L, b in H) = (minimum a in L) + (minimum b in H adjacent to that a). Since all high numbers are \u003e= threshold, the minimal sum is at least min(L) + min(H). So to maximize, we want to maximize min(L)+min(H) while still having enough high numbers to surround low numbers.\n"}
