,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352098898,"data":"       | Let low side numbers be the set L = {1,...,k} maybe? Not exactly. But we can think of ordering low numbers ascending. For each low number a, we need at least d distinct high numbers \u003e= t - a. Since high numbers are limited, we need to allocate them.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352100473,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352101505,"data":"       | Simplify: Suppose we aim for threshold t. For each low number a, define required high numbers count = deg(a). However high numbers may be shared across multiple low vertices (since a high vertex can serve multiple low vertices). In bipartite grid, each high vertex is adjacent to up to 4 low vertices (if interior). So each high number can serve up to its degree (2-4) low vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352103183,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352104201,"data":"       | Thus we can model as a flow problem: each low vertex needs deg(v) incident edges each connecting to a high vertex with label \u003e= t - a. Each high vertex can provide up to deg(v) edges, but also its label must be \u003e= threshold required by each low neighbor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352105759,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352106781,"data":"       | Thus we need to assign high numbers (labels) to high vertices such that for each incident low neighbor a, label \u003e= t - a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352108066,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352109088,"data":"       | Thus we could try to assign labels to high vertices as the max of required thresholds from adjacent low vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352110316,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352111336,"data":"       | Thus we can think of constructing labeling by first assigning low numbers to white cells, then for each black cell, its label must be at least max_{adjacent low a} (t - a). To minimize label usage, we can assign each black cell the minimal possible label meeting these constraints, while ensuring distinctness and within range.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352112922,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352114046,"data":"       | Thus the feasibility reduces to: can we assign distinct integers from the set of remaining numbers to black cells such that each black cell's assigned value \u003e= required minimum, and all numbers 1..3600 used exactly once.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352115513,"data":"       | \n"}
