,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352128483,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352129546,"data":"       | But we also need to decide placement of low numbers (which white cells get which low numbers). Since we have freedom, we can try to assign low numbers to cells such that required minima for black cells are as low as possible, to fit within available numbers. This is like we need to assign low numbers to minimize the maximal requirement for any black cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352131186,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352132207,"data":"       | Intuitively, we should place low numbers on cells with smallest degree (corners) because they impose lower requirements on neighboring black cells (since they have fewer neighbors). Also we should spread low numbers such that each black cell is adjacent to as few low numbers as possible, to keep its required minimum lower.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352133791,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352134826,"data":"       | Thus optimal arrangement likely places the smallest numbers at corners and edges, and larger low numbers in interior.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352136058,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352137112,"data":"       | Thus we can design algorithm: assign low numbers to white cells sorted by degree (corners first, edges next, interior last). So smallest numbers go to corners (degree 2), then edges (degree 3), then interior (degree 4). This reduces constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352138566,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352139783,"data":"       | Now we need to decide which cells are white vs black. Since grid is bipartite, we can choose which color gets the low numbers. Without loss, assign low numbers to white cells. The distribution of degrees among colors: corners: there are 4 corners; they are of opposite colors? Actually each corner cell's color depends on parity of coordinates. In a checkerboard, corners (0,0) and (59,59) have same color (since both even sum). The other two corners have opposite color. So each color has 2 corners. So low side will have 2 corners if we assign low to that color.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352141781,"data":"       | \n"}
