,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352476422,"data":"       | analysisThe heuristic yields max feasible t = 3487. This is a lower bound (since we might be able to do better with different assignment). Upper bound is 3600. So optimum lies between 3487 and 3600.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352477732,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352478695,"data":"       | We need to find the exact maximum value.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352479734,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352480705,"data":"       | We can try to improve assignment of low numbers to low cells to increase t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352481824,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352485299,"data":"       | Observation: The limiting factor is the requirement for high cells: required \u003e= max(t - a_adj). Since we want these required values to be low (so they fit within available high numbers). That means we want a_adj to be as large as possible (close to t) for each high cell's neighbors. That suggests we should assign larger low numbers to cells that are adjacent to many high cells (i.e., interior low cells). Actually each low cell is adjacent to up to 4 high cells (if interior). So to reduce the required minima for those high cells, we want low numbers near t (i.e., large) for interior low cells, because each high cell sees multiple low cells, but its required minimum is determined by the maximum of (t - a_adj) across its adjacent low cells (since need to be \u003e= each). Actually we need high \u003e= t - a for each adjacent low a, so the needed lower bound is max_{adjacent low a} (t - a) = t - min_{adjacent low a}. Wait compute: For each high cell, need high \u003e= t - a for each neighbor a, so the most restrictive is the largest required value, which corresponds to the smallest a among its neighbors (since t - a larger when a smaller). So required = t - min_adjacent_low. So the high cell's required minimum depends on the smallest low neighbor value. So to reduce required, we need each high cell's adjacent low cells to have relatively large minimal value; i.e., we need to avoid having any high cell adjacent to a very small low number.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352488654,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352489648,"data":"       | Thus we should place the smallest low numbers at positions where they have as few high neighbors as possible, i.e., corners (degree 2) and edges (degree 3). Additionally, we should ensure that each high cell is not adjacent to two small low numbers; its minimal neighbor should be as large as possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352491117,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352492088,"data":"       | Thus we need to arrange the low numbers such that each high cell's adjacent low numbers have relatively large minimum.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352493264,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352494235,"data":"       | Given the bipartite grid, each high cell is adjacent to up to 4 low cells. The minimal low neighbor for a high cell will be the smallest among those up to 4 low cells. So we need to allocate the smallest low numbers to high cells' neighborhoods carefully.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352495624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352496584,"data":"       | One approach: Put smallest low numbers in corners (each corner adjacent to two high cells). Those high cells each have one small low neighbor (the corner) and other low neighbors maybe larger. That may be okay.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352497907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352498924,"data":"       | We also need to place 2,3,... maybe also at edge positions but ensure they don't share high cell with another even smaller low.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352500102,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352501049,"data":"       | Potentially we can design pattern where low numbers increase outward from corners.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352502155,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352503245,"data":"       | Thus the heuristic of sorting low cells by degree may already achieve this; but maybe we need to also consider spatial arrangement to avoid clustering small low numbers near same high cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352504562,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352505547,"data":"       | Our simple sorting by degree but arbitrary ordering may place small numbers near each other (e.g., two adjacent edge cells both low numbers small, causing a high cell adjacent to both small numbers, making its min small). To improve, we should separate low numbers of similar small magnitude across the grid.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352508837,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352509844,"data":"       | Thus we need more sophisticated placement: maybe fill low numbers in a checkerboard pattern within low parity cells such that low numbers increase along rows.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352511074,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352512032,"data":"       | We can try to use algorithmic search: treat as assignment problem maximizing minimal sum; we can use linear programming with integer constraints? Might be heavy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352513257,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352514228,"data":"       | But we can attempt to use heuristic like simulated annealing to try to increase S.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352515388,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352516361,"data":"       | Given time, we could attempt to use integer programming via pulp (not allowed) but we can implement simple local search.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352517538,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352518499,"data":"       | However maybe there is known result: For m x n board, maximum minimal sum equals m*n + something? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352519638,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352520627,"data":"       | Let's try to compute theoretical upper bound using degree constraints more precisely.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352521744,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352522751,"data":"       | Consider ordering of numbers. Let’s denote the sorted list of numbers assigned to low cells (L) in increasing order: a1 \u003c= a2 \u003c= ... \u003c= a1800. For each low cell with degree d_i (2,3,4). The neighbors are high cells; each such edge imposes a constraint on the high neighbor: high \u003e= S - a_i. Thus each low cell contributes d_i constraints each requiring a high \u003e= S - a_i. Since a high cell may satisfy multiple constraints (one per incident edge), we need to ensure enough high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352524681,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352525844,"data":"       | We can count total number of constraints: sum_i d_i = total edges = 7080 (since each edge counted once from low side). So we have 7080 constraints of type \"some high number \u003e= S - a_i\". If we sort constraints by required minimum (S - a_i). The largest required minimum corresponds to smallest a_i. So there are many constraints with high required value.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352527464,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352528428,"data":"       | Now we have 1800 high numbers to satisfy these constraints, each high number can be used for up to its degree constraints (its degree). So total capacity of high numbers = sum_{j in high} deg_j = also 7080 (since each edge counted from high side as well). So capacity matches constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352529851,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352530817,"data":"       | Thus we can think of assigning each edge to a high vertex (its endpoint) which must have label \u003e= required bound for that edge. Since each high vertex can accommodate up to its degree edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352532147,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352534481,"data":"       | Thus feasibility reduces to: we have multiset of required lower bounds for each edge (based on low endpoint a). We need to assign each edge to a high vertex (its endpoint) such that each high vertex gets at most deg(v) edges, and its label must be \u003e= max of required bounds of edges assigned to it (since label must satisfy all incident edges). Equivalent to labeling high vertices with numbers such that for each high vertex, its label \u003e= max_{incident edges} required bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352536322,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352537298,"data":"       | Thus we can think of constructing high vertex labels as the max required bound among its incident edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352538444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352539412,"data":"       | Given we can choose which low numbers are adjacent to which high vertices (by placement), we can affect distribution of required bounds among high vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352540641,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352541631,"data":"       | Goal: minimize the maximum of these max bounds across high vertices (since labels limited). Since we have a set of high numbers (largest numbers) to assign, we need the sorted required maxima to be \u003c= the sorted high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352543014,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352543978,"data":"       | Thus we need to design placement of low numbers to minimize the sorted list of per-high-vertex required minima.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352545137,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352546111,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of minimizing maximum of minima across high vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352547221,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352548351,"data":"       | Given symmetry, perhaps optimal arrangement is to distribute low numbers uniformly: each high vertex adjacent to low numbers with values roughly similar. Ideally each high vertex sees low numbers of similar magnitude, perhaps around the median of low numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352549787,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352550757,"data":"       | Thus the per-high-vertex required bound would be around S - median low value.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352551899,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352552885,"data":"       | If low numbers are roughly evenly spread between 1 and 1800, median ~ 900, then required bound ~ S - 900. For high vertex label to be among largest 1800 numbers (1801..3600), we need S - 900 \u003c= 3600, which is always true. But we need also that required bound not exceed the smallest high number (1801). So we need S - max_low_adjacent_min \u003e= 1801? Wait we need for each high vertex label \u003e= required bound, and we have smallest high label 1801. So we need required bound \u003c= 1801 for all high vertices, else we need to assign a higher label; but we have higher labels available, so okay. However we must ensure the distribution of required bounds fits into the high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352555042,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352556012,"data":"       | The hardest constraints arise for high vertices adjacent to the smallest low numbers. So we need to allocate small low numbers such that each high vertex adjacent to at most one very small low number, and if it does, its required bound becomes S - that small number. So to keep required bound low, we need S not too large relative to small low numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352557554,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352560071,"data":"       | Thus the maximum S may be limited by ability to assign small low numbers to high vertices without exceeding capacity of high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352561308,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352562307,"data":"       | Let's attempt to compute theoretical bound using capacity argument: For each small low number a, its incident edges count = d_i (2-4) constraints requiring high \u003e= S - a. So each such edge must be satisfied by some high vertex (the neighbor). A high vertex can serve at most deg(v) edges, but its label must be at least max of required bounds of those edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352563887,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352565019,"data":"       | If we consider ordering low numbers ascending, the edges from the smallest low numbers produce high required bounds close to S - a which are large. If we have many such edges, they may need high labels that are very high (close to 3600). Since we only have limited high numbers, this may limit S.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352566491,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352567455,"data":"       | We can model as: For each threshold x, consider low numbers a \u003c= S - x. For any high vertex with label x, it can only satisfy edges where required bound \u003c= x, i.e., a \u003e= S - x. So high vertices with label \u003c= L can only be adjacent to low numbers \u003e= S - L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352569005,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352569964,"data":"       | Thus we can count how many edges can be satisfied by high vertices with labels up to some value.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352571104,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352572079,"data":"       | Let’s define distribution of high labels: we have 1800 high numbers; we can sort them ascending: h1 \u003c= h2 \u003c= ... \u003c= h1800, where hi = 1800 + i (since we can assign any permutation but the set is fixed). So hi = 1800 + i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352573528,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352574495,"data":"       | But we can assign them arbitrarily, not necessarily monotonic with degree.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352575694,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352576659,"data":"       | We need to assign edges to high vertices such that each high vertex with label hi can handle at most deg(v) edges, each requiring low a \u003e= S - hi.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352577921,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352578902,"data":"       | Thus the total number of edges incident to high vertices with label \u003c= L is at most sum_{v: label \u003c= L} deg(v). Since we can assign any low numbers to those edges, but they must satisfy low \u003e= S - L (since required bound \u003c= L). So the number of low vertices with value \u003e= S - L must be enough to provide that many edge endpoints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352580554,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352581507,"data":"       | But each low vertex contributes deg(v) edges, and each low vertex's value must be \u003e= S - L for its edges to be satisfied by high vertices of label \u003c= L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352582787,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352583967,"data":"       | Thus we can formulate a necessary condition: For each threshold L, let E_L be total number of edges that can be incident to high vertices with label \u003c= L, which is sum of degrees of high vertices assigned labels \u003c= L (max capacity). Since we can choose which high vertices get which labels, to maximize capacity we would assign higher degree vertices to lower labels (since they can handle more edges). So to test feasibility, we can assume we assign the 1800 high vertices sorted by degree descending to the smallest high numbers (i.e., assign interior high vertices degree 4 to smallest high numbers, edges to them). This gives maximal capacity for low labels.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352587667,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352588704,"data":"       | Thus capacity for labels up to some value L is the sum of degrees of the high vertices with smallest labels (i.e., those with highest degrees). Since high vertices also have degrees 2,3,4 depending on their position (corners, edges, interior). So we can compute cumulative capacity as we increase label threshold.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352590184,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352591161,"data":"       | Similarly, the requirement side: The number of edges that need high label at most L is the number of edges incident to low vertices with value \u003e= S - L (since those edges have required bound \u003c= L). Wait careful: Edges incident to low vertices with value a produce required bound S - a. For a given L, edges with required bound \u003c= L correspond to low vertices with a \u003e= S - L. So the total number of such edges is sum of degrees of low vertices with a \u003e= S - L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352593024,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352593989,"data":"       | Thus necessary condition: For all thresholds L, sum_{high vertices with label \u003c= L} deg \u003e= sum_{low vertices with a \u003e= S - L} deg.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352595291,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352596271,"data":"       | Because we can assign those edges to those high vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352597381,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352598354,"data":"       | Since we can permute labels among high vertices, we can compute left side as capacity function C(L) = sum of the largest degrees among high vertices up to count of high vertices with label \u003c= L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352599672,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352600644,"data":"       | We can compute distribution of degrees among high vertices: same as low vertices: 2 corners, 116 edges (deg3), 1682 interior (deg4). So high vertices degree distribution identical.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352601901,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352602898,"data":"       | Thus for a given L, the number of high vertices with label \u003c= L is (L - 1800) because smallest high number is 1801. Actually high label set is numbers 1801..3600. So count of high vertices with label \u003c= L is max(0, L - 1800). For L \u003c 1801, count =0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352604387,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352605342,"data":"       | Thus capacity C(L) = sum of degrees of the best (largest degree) vertices among that count.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352606485,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352607738,"data":"       | Since we want maximal capacity, we assign interior vertices (deg4) first, then edge (deg3), then corners (deg2). So for k high vertices (k = L-1800), capacity = 4*min(k,1682) + 3*max(0, min(k-1682,116)) + 2*max(0, k-1682-116).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352609084,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352610108,"data":"       | Now requirement side: For given L, define a_threshold = S - L. The low vertices with a \u003e= a_threshold. Since low numbers are 1..1800 assigned to low vertices arbitrarily, but we can arrange them to maximize feasibility: we would assign larger low numbers to vertices with higher degree, because they contribute more edges needing high capacity. Since requirement side counts edges from low vertices with a \u003e= a_threshold, we can minimize that count by assigning large low numbers to vertices with small degree (so they contribute fewer edges). But we want to make condition easier to satisfy, i.e., requirement edges as low as possible for a given set of low numbers above threshold. So we assign the largest low numbers to low-degree vertices (corners, edges) and smaller low numbers to high-degree interior vertices. That reduces sum of degrees for high a. This is opposite to earlier assignment (we assigned small numbers to low-degree vertices). Wait we need to think: For a given a_threshold, we consider low vertices whose labels are at least a_threshold (i.e., relatively large). To minimize sum of degrees among them, we would like those vertices to have as small degree as possible. So we would assign the large low numbers to low-degree cells. That suggests we should assign largest low numbers (close to 1800) to corners and edges, and smallest low numbers to interior cells. That would reduce capacity demand for high labels.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352614882,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352616225,"data":"       | Thus earlier heuristic of assigning small numbers to low-degree cells is perhaps opposite to optimal for maximizing S. Because we need to satisfy constraints for high S, which requires high required bound for low small numbers, and those edges are many (if small numbers placed in interior, each contributes 4 edges). So we should place smallest numbers in low-degree cells to limit number of edges requiring high bound. So earlier heuristic (small numbers at corners) is correct for capacity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352617971,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352618947,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute feasibility condition using optimal arrangement: assign low numbers sorted ascending to low cells sorted by degree descending? Wait we want smallest numbers at low-degree cells (corners, edges) to minimize edges with high required bound. That's what we did earlier: small numbers to corners (degree 2), edges (degree 3), interior (degree 4). That's ascending degree, which we used.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352620564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352621541,"data":"       | Thus the lower bound we computed (feasibility) using that arrangement may be close to optimum.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352622687,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352623645,"data":"       | But we also need to consider spatial arrangement to avoid clustering of small numbers adjacent to same high vertex. However the degree distribution already accounts for edges count, not adjacency.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352624944,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352625924,"data":"       | Our capacity condition may be sufficient for feasibility? Not necessarily because of adjacency constraints (same high vertex cannot be adjacent to two small low numbers if that would raise its required bound to be S - min(low). Actually if a high vertex is adjacent to two low numbers, the required bound is determined by the smaller of the two low numbers. So if we place two small low numbers adjacent to same high vertex, the required bound is determined by the smaller, but the larger low number's constraint is less stringent. So having multiple small low numbers adjacent to same high vertex doesn't increase its required bound beyond the smallest low neighbor. So clustering small low numbers could be okay as long as they share same high vertex, which may reduce total number of high vertices needing very high labels.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352629657,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352630642,"data":"       | Thus our capacity counting may overestimate needed high labels because it assumes each edge's requirement must be satisfied independently; but high vertex's label must meet the maximum of its incident requirements, which is determined by the smallest low neighbor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352632065,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352633228,"data":"       | Thus we may be able to achieve higher S than our earlier feasibility test (which used worst-case per edge assignment). Our earlier feasibility test assigned each edge separately (by requiring high \u003e= max(t - a_adj) across all adjacent low cells). Actually we computed for each high cell the required minimum = max(t - a_adj) = t - min_adjacent_low. That's correct. Then we sorted these required minima and compared to high numbers. This correctly models the per high vertex requirement (not per edge). So it captures clustering effect. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352635097,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352636061,"data":"       | Thus our test uses the specific arrangement of low numbers we assigned (sorted by degree). It may be suboptimal arrangement; we can try to search for better arrangement that yields higher feasible t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352637449,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352638607,"data":"       | We can attempt to use optimization algorithm to assign low numbers to low cells to maximize minimal sum. Since the problem size is large (1800 low numbers), brute force impossible, but we can use heuristic like simulated annealing.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352639962,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352640939,"data":"       | Let's implement a simple local search: start with initial assignment (as before). Compute minimal edge sum (i.e., compute min_{edge} (low+high) after assigning high numbers optimally given low assignment). Actually we need to also assign high numbers to high cells to satisfy constraints. We can compute the maximal minimal sum given a low assignment using the greedy algorithm: For each high cell compute required lower bound = t - min_adjacent_low. To maximize t, we need to find t such that we can assign high numbers \u003e= required. Equivalent to compute the maximal t that satisfies condition: after sorting required minima r_i, the maximal t is min_i (available_high_i - offset) where available_high_i = 1800 + i (since sorted). Actually we can compute t_max for given low assignment as:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352643184,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352644275,"data":"       | We need for all i: r_i \u003c= 1800 + i (since sorted ascending). r_i = t - min_adjacent_low_i. So condition: t - m_i \u003c= 1800 + i, where m_i is min_adjacent_low for high cell i (sorted by m_i ascending? Wait r_i ascending corresponds to t - m_i descending? Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352645749,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352646732,"data":"       | Let for each high cell j, define m_j = min adjacent low number. Then required r_j = t - m_j. Sorting r_j ascending is same as sorting m_j descending (since t constant). So the condition becomes: for all k (1-indexed), t - m_{(k)} \u003c= 1800 + (k-1) where m_{(k)} are sorted descending.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352648208,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352649185,"data":"       | Thus t \u003c= 1800 + (k-1) + m_{(k)} for each k. So overall t_max = min_k (1800 + (k-1) + m_{(k)}). Since k ranges 1..1800.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352650412,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352652901,"data":"       | Thus given low assignment, we can compute m_j for each high cell (minimum low neighbor), sort m_j descending, then compute min over k of (1800 + k -1 + m_{(k)}). That yields maximum feasible t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.35265429,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352655267,"data":"       | Thus we can compute t_max for a given low assignment efficiently.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352656372,"data":"       | \n"}
