,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352789978,"data":"       | We can try to use local swaps of low labels to increase the minimal value of (1800 + k-1 + m_{(k)}). Equivalent to maximize min over k of (1800 + k-1 + m_{(k)}). Since 1800 + k-1 is fixed, this is essentially maximize the minimum of m_{(k)} + (k-1). Sorting descending m_i, the expression for each rank k is m_{(k)} + (k-1). So we need to maximize the minimum of these sums. This is similar to constructing sequence m_i such that m_i are as large as possible for small ranks (largest m values). Since k-1 increases, the later ranks have larger additive term, so they are less critical. The bottleneck is early ranks (largest m). So we want the largest m (minimum low neighbor) to be as large as possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.35279204,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352793175,"data":"       | Thus we need to maximize the smallest low neighbor among high cells (the worst high cell). That is we need to increase the minimal min_adjacent_low across all high cells. Because for k=1, expression = 1800 + 0 + max_m (since sorted descending, m_{(1)} is largest min_adjacent_low). Wait descending: m_{(1)} is the largest min_adjacent_low (since descending). Actually we sort descending, so m_{(1)} is maximum, not minimum. But the expression for k=1800 (last) will involve smallest m. Let's compute: For k=1800, term = 1800 + 1799 + m_{(1800)} = 3599 + m_{min}. Since m_{min} could be small, that may be bottleneck. Indeed the min of (1800 + k-1 + m_{(k)}) across k will likely be at some intermediate k where m_{(k)} relatively small but k not too large. The worst-case is likely when m is small.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.35279536,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352796315,"data":"       | Thus we need to increase the smallest min_adjacent_low among high cells. That is, ensure each high cell has at least some moderate low neighbor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352797514,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352798467,"data":"       | Thus we need to arrange low numbers such that each high cell's minimum adjacent low is as large as possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.35279963,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352800601,"data":"       | Since each high cell has degree 2-4 low neighbors, its minimum is the smallest among those. So we need to avoid having any high cell adjacent to a very small low number. So we need to distribute small low numbers such that they are not adjacent to same high cell? Actually we need each high cell to avoid having a tiny low neighbor. So each small low number must be placed such that all high cells adjacent to it have other low neighbors that are larger? Wait the min for high cell is the smallest low neighbor among its adjacent low cells. So if a high cell is adjacent to a low cell with value 1, then its min is 1 regardless of other neighbors. So that high cell's min is 1, causing low sum.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352804197,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352805334,"data":"       | Thus to avoid small mins, we must place low numbers such that no high cell is adjacent to a low number that is among the smallest values. That is, each low number should be placed such that its neighboring high cells each have other low neighbors that are larger? No, the min is per high cell, not per edge. So if a high cell has any neighbor low number that is small, its min is that small number, making it low. Therefore, each low number must be isolated such that it is not adjacent to any high cell? impossible because all low cells are adjacent to high cells. So the only way is to ensure that low numbers themselves are not too small: i.e., we need to increase the minimum low number among all low cells (i.e., avoid using 1). But we must use 1 somewhere. So the high cell adjacent to 1 will have min = 1, causing term 1800+something+1 = 1801+something, which may limit t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352807674,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.352808674,"data":"       | But we can mitigate by placing 1 at a corner, which has only 2 high neighbors, but those high cells each have min = 1. So there will be at least 2 high cells with min=1. Then for those high cells, the term 1800 + (k-1) + 1 may be small.\n"}
