,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.35312779,"data":"       | analysisOnly one high cell has min 118. That is likely an interior high cell adjacent to an edge low cell with number 118 (the largest edge low number). So the bottleneck arises from that particular high cell with min 118, and its rank is 1569. So to increase t, we need to increase that min (i.e., raise the smallest edge low number that is the maximum among edge low numbers?). Actually we need to increase the minimal min among high cells at that rank, which corresponds to the edge low cell with highest number among edge low cells (118). Because interior high cells have min = min of its four interior low neighbors, which are at least 119. So interior high cells have min \u003e=119. Edge high cells adjacent to edge low cells have min equal to the smallest of its adjacent low cells; among those, the worst-case is the edge low cell with smallest number among edge low cells that is adjacent to that high cell. Since edge low cells have numbers 3..118, the worst-case for high cell that only adjacent to edge low cells (maybe a corner high cell?) is the smallest edge low number (3). But those high cells may have other interior low neighbors that are larger, but min is the edge low number.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.353130624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.353131611,"data":"       | Thus the high cell with min 118 is the one adjacent only to edge low cells with numbers at least 118? Actually for min=118, that high cell's edge low neighbor is 118 and the other adjacent low cells are higher (maybe interior low numbers \u003e=119). So its min is 118.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.353133074,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.353134047,"data":"       | Thus to increase t, we need to increase the minimal edge low numbers that are adjacent to high cells whose rank is early enough (k=1569). Since 118 is the largest edge low number, we could increase it by swapping with an interior low number (i.e., moving a larger number into that edge cell). That would raise the min for that high cell, but would cause a smaller number to go interior, decreasing min for interior high cells potentially to lower than 119.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.353135721,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.353136744,"data":"       | Thus we need to see if moving a larger number into that edge cell while moving a smaller interior number to interior location leads to overall t increase.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.353137971,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.353139063,"data":"       | Potentially we can try to search via local swaps to maximize t using algorithmic optimization.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.353140207,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.353141187,"data":"       | Let's implement a simple heuristic: start with initial assignment; then compute mins list and t_max. Then try random swaps of low numbers between two low cells (maybe one interior, one edge) and accept if t_max improves. Use simulated annealing.\n"}
