,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.353995528,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.353996511,"data":"       | Observation: For given partition (B,W), the minimal possible sum across edges is determined by the minimal b + minimal w adjacent (but adjacency may not involve the global minima if we can locate minima far apart). However each black cell has at least 2 neighbors (except corners). So the smallest black cell value b_min will be adjacent to some white cells; we can assign those white cells to be among the highest numbers to increase sums. Similarly, smallest white value w_min will be adjacent to black cells; we can assign those black cells to be high numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.353998395,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.353999386,"data":"       | Thus the bottleneck could be the sum of the two smallest numbers overall if they are placed on opposite colors and adjacent. But we can avoid adjacency between the global smallest numbers by placing them far away (e.g., both on same color? No they must be opposite colors to avoid adjacency). If we place both smallest numbers on same color (both black), they are not adjacent (since black cells not adjacent). That's allowed because we can partition numbers arbitrarily: we can put smallest numbers all on black cells, and largest numbers on white cells, ensuring that any edge involves one small (black) and one large (white). Since black cells are never adjacent, the smallest numbers never touch each other.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.354002935,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":18877.354004062,"data":"       | Thus the minimal edge sum will be (minimum black value) + (minimum white neighbor value). The minimum white neighbor value we can assign is the smallest white number, which could be relatively large if we put only large numbers on white squares. So we can make the smallest white number as high as possible by putting the highest numbers on white squares. So we choose a threshold: allocate the largest N numbers to white squares, smallest N numbers to black squares.\n"}
