,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699331921,"data":"       | Interpretation: We have a 7x7 board (49 squares). We place some dominoes (size 1x2 covering two adjacent squares). The condition: No domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remain fixed. Means each domino is \"blocked\" from moving in its orientation direction (i.e., cannot slide left/right if horizontal, up/down if vertical) because other dominoes (or board edges) block its path. The domino can slide only along its longer axis? Actually a domino can slide horizontally if it's horizontal orientation (i.e., along its length) or vertically if vertical orientation. Usually sliding a domino means moving it one unit in its orientation direction (i.e., translation) while staying within board and not overlapping other dominoes. The condition says that for each domino, there is no empty space in front or behind it along its orientation direction (i.e., cannot move left or right for horizontal domino, cannot move up or down for vertical domino) while other dominoes remain fixed. So each domino must be \"blocked\" on both sides along its orientation direction by either board edge or another domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699338338,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699339448,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominoes needed to achieve this property.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.69934066,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699341913,"data":"       | We need to find minimal m such that there exists a placement of m dominoes on 7x7 board where each domino is blocked on both sides along its orientation direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699343278,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.69934435,"data":"       | We can think of covering board partially; empty squares may exist but must not allow any domino to slide.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.69934556,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699346578,"data":"       | Essentially we need to place dominoes such that for each domino, the squares adjacent to its two cells in the direction of its orientation are either occupied (by another domino) or outside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699347959,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699349032,"data":"       | For a horizontal domino covering squares (i,j) and (i,j+1) (row i, columns j and j+1). It cannot slide left: square (i,j-1) must be occupied or off board (j-1\u003c1). It cannot slide right: square (i,j+2) must be occupied or off board (j+2\u003e7). Similarly for vertical domino covering (i,j) and (i+1,j): squares (i-1,j) and (i+2,j) must be occupied or off board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699350916,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699352018,"data":"       | Thus each domino must have its two \"adjacent\" squares in its orientation direction blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699353351,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699354428,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominoes while satisfying this for all placed dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699355618,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699356675,"data":"       | We can treat board as grid of cells. Domino placements are edges between adjacent cells (horizontal or vertical). For each placed domino, we need both neighboring cells along that edge direction to be occupied (by other dominoes) or outside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699360441,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699402488,"data":"       | Observation: Dominoes can block each other. For example, two horizontal dominoes placed adjacent end-to-end (i.e., covering cells (i,1)-(i,2) and (i,3)-(i,4)) would block each other's sliding? Let's examine: first domino's right neighbor is cell (i,3) which is part of second domino, so blocked. Its left neighbor maybe board edge or another domino. Similarly second domino's left neighbor is cell (i,2) part of first domino, blocked. So they block each other.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699404914,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699405979,"data":"       | Thus we can create chains of dominoes along a row or column, each domino blocked by neighbor dominoes on both sides, except at ends where board edge provides block.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699407315,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699408619,"data":"       | Thus minimal number may involve arranging dominoes in lines covering some rows/columns.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699409888,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699411051,"data":"       | But we also need to ensure that any domino placed does not have empty squares adjacent in its orientation direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699412335,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699413485,"data":"       | Thus we need to cover each domino's \"adjacent squares\" with either board edge or another domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699415115,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699416332,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominoes, i.e., we want to use as few dominoes as possible while still satisfying constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699417598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699418803,"data":"       | Potential approach: treat each domino as requiring two \"blocking squares\" (one on each side). Some of these blocking squares can be shared between dominoes (e.g., the right side of one domino is the left side of the next). So we can think of constructing sequences.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699420345,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699421492,"data":"       | We can also place vertical dominoes that block each other.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699422649,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699423699,"data":"       | We need to consider board edges: there are 7 rows and 7 columns. For a horizontal domino at column 1 (covering columns 1-2), its left neighbor is off board (blocked). Similarly at column 6 (covering 6-7), right neighbor off board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699425275,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699426303,"data":"       | Thus we can place dominoes at edges to reduce need for other dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699427596,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.69942865,"data":"       | Goal: minimize total number of dominoes. So we want to use board edges as much as possible to block sides, reducing need for other dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699429984,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.69943102,"data":"       | Potential minimal arrangement: place dominoes only along the outermost rows/columns? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699432228,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699433703,"data":"       | If we place a horizontal domino at row 1, columns 1-2 (top edge). Its left neighbor off board, right neighbor cell (1,3) must be occupied (by another domino) or off board (not). So we need something at (1,3). Could be vertical domino covering (1,3)-(2,3) which would block that square? Wait the right neighbor of the horizontal domino is cell (1,3). If we place a vertical domino covering (1,3)-(2,3), then cell (1,3) is occupied, thus blocked. However the vertical domino's own constraints: its top neighbor (0,3) off board (blocked), bottom neighbor (3,3) must be occupied. So we need something at (3,3). Could be another vertical domino covering (3,3)-(4,3), etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699438118,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699439474,"data":"       | Thus we can create a chain of vertical dominoes down a column, each blocked by board edge at top and by next domino below.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699440784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699441804,"data":"       | Similarly we could have horizontal dominoes across a row.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699442943,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699444039,"data":"       | Thus minimal arrangement may be to fill a single row with dominoes covering the entire row, each domino blocked by neighbor dominoes except at ends blocked by board edges. That would require covering the row with dominoes: row length 7, domino length 2, cannot perfectly tile row of odd length. So we would need at least 3 dominoes covering 6 cells, leaving one cell empty. But then the domino adjacent to empty cell would have an empty neighbor, violating condition. So we need to handle odd length.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699445916,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.69944698,"data":"       | Thus we cannot have a single row fully blocked using only horizontal dominoes because odd length leads to a gap.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699448439,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699449456,"data":"       | Similarly for a column.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699450545,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699451685,"data":"       | Thus we need to combine orientations to handle odd parity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699452931,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699454125,"data":"       | Goal: find minimal number of dominoes such that each domino's adjacent squares are occupied or off board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699455334,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699456355,"data":"       | We can treat board as graph of cells; each domino occupies an edge between two cells. For each placed domino, we need both adjacent cells along that edge direction to be \"occupied\" (by any domino) or off board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.69945796,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699459046,"data":"       | Thus we can think of each domino requiring that the two cells adjacent to its two endpoints along the same line are covered.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.69946034,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699461695,"data":"       | If we think of a domino as covering cells a and b (adjacent). The cells adjacent to a and b in the same direction are c (adjacent to a on the side opposite b) and d (adjacent to b on side opposite a). So we need c and d to be occupied or off board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699463176,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699471779,"data":"       | Thus each domino essentially needs a \"chain\" of three cells in a line: c-a-b-d, where a-b is the domino, and c and d are either board edges (outside) or occupied by other dominoes (which may be part of same chain). So each domino is part of a chain of length at least 3 cells (including its own two cells). The chain can be longer if multiple dominoes placed consecutively.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699476708,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699479109,"data":"       | Thus we can think of constructing chains of dominoes along rows or columns, each chain may start at board edge (c off board) and end at board edge (d off board). For a chain of k dominoes placed consecutively in a line, the interior dominoes have both sides occupied by neighboring dominoes; the end dominoes have one side blocked by board edge, the other side blocked by neighbor domino. So a chain of k dominoes uses k dominoes covering 2k cells, and occupies a line segment of length 2k+1 cells (including the empty cell beyond the end? Wait board edge counts as off board, not a cell). Actually for a chain of k dominoes placed consecutively, the total number of cells covered is 2k. The cells adjacent to the ends (outside the chain) are either board edges (if chain touches board edge) or empty cells that must be occupied by something else (maybe a perpendicular domino). But the condition only requires that for each domino, its adjacent cells are occupied or off board. For the end domino, one side is board edge (if chain touches edge) else must be occupied by something else (maybe a perpendicular domino). So to minimize domino count, we want chains that start at board edges to avoid needing extra dominoes to block that side.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699483298,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699484478,"data":"       | Thus we can place chains that start at board edges and extend inward.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699485752,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699486818,"data":"       | Now the board is 7x7, odd dimensions. Let's think about covering with minimal dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699488011,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699489436,"data":"       | Potential approach: Use a \"spanning tree\" of dominoes such that each domino is blocked by board edges or other dominoes. Equivalent to constructing a set of dominoes where each domino's two \"external neighbor cells\" are either board edges or cells occupied by other dominoes (maybe not necessarily adjacent to the domino's cells? Actually they must be the immediate neighbor cells along the line). So each domino's external neighbor cells are either board edges or cells that are part of some domino (any orientation). So we need to ensure that for each domino, the cells directly adjacent to its two cells along its orientation direction are occupied (by any domino) or off board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699491792,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699492843,"data":"       | Thus we can treat each cell as either occupied (by a domino) or empty. For each domino covering cells (i,j) and (i,j+1) horizontally, we need cells (i,j-1) and (i,j+2) to be occupied or off board. So those cells must be occupied (by any domino) if they exist.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699494508,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699495519,"data":"       | Thus we need to select a set of occupied cells (by dominoes) such that for each occupied pair forming a domino, the adjacent cells are also occupied (or off board). This is reminiscent of a \"domino blocking set\".\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699497008,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699498022,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominoes (i.e., number of occupied cells /2). Equivalent to minimize number of occupied cells subject to constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699501211,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699502381,"data":"       | Thus we need to find minimal number of occupied cells such that the occupied cells can be partitioned into dominoes (adjacent pairs) and each domino's external neighbor cells are also occupied (or off board). This is a combinatorial optimization problem.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699503908,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699505311,"data":"       | We can attempt to solve via integer programming or brute force search using Python, given board size 7x7 (49 cells). Number of possible domino placements is limited: each domino occupies two adjacent cells. There are (7*6)*2 = 84 possible domino positions (horizontal: 7 rows * 6 columns = 42; vertical: 6 rows * 7 columns = 42). We need to select a subset of these dominoes such that they are non-overlapping (no cell used by more than one domino) and satisfy blocking condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699507158,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699508205,"data":"       | We can formulate as exact cover problem with constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699509372,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699510403,"data":"       | Given small board, we could brute force search for minimal m using Python with backtracking or ILP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699511604,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699512631,"data":"       | Let's attempt using Python with MILP (e.g., pulp) but not available. Could implement brute force search using recursion with pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699513933,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699515005,"data":"       | But we need to be careful about combinatorial explosion: there are many subsets of domino placements. However we can search for minimal m, starting from low numbers and see if feasible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699516641,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699517678,"data":"       | We can also use SAT solver approach but not available.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699518829,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699519875,"data":"       | Given board size 7x7, minimal m likely not too large; maybe around 12? Let's guess.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699521083,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699522091,"data":"       | But we can compute using Python with backtracking and pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699523411,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699525681,"data":"       | Define board cells indices 0..48. Domino placements list of pairs (cell1, cell2) and orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699526946,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699528223,"data":"       | We need to select a set of dominoes D such that:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699529368,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699530519,"data":"       | - No overlapping: each cell appears in at most one domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699531855,"data":"       | - For each domino (a,b) with orientation horizontal or vertical, define neighbor cells left/right or up/down: For horizontal (a,b) where b = a+1 (if we index row-major), neighbor left = a-1 (if same row and column\u003e0), neighbor right = b+1 (if same row and column\u003c6). For vertical (a,b) where b = a+7, neighbor up = a-7 (if row\u003e0), neighbor down = b+7 (if row\u003c6). For each neighbor that exists (i.e., within board), that cell must be occupied (i.e., part of some domino in D). For neighbor off board, fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699537261,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699538491,"data":"       | Thus we need to ensure that for each domino, its neighbor cells are occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699539711,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699541101,"data":"       | Thus we need to ensure that the set of occupied cells O = union of cells of D satisfies: For each domino (a,b) in D, for each neighbor cell n (left/right/up/down as appropriate) that is within board, n ∈ O.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699543031,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699757351,"data":"       | Thus O must be \"closed\" under neighbor relation for each domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699773325,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699775659,"data":"       | Thus we can think of O as a set of cells such that each domino's external neighbor cells are also in O.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699777864,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699779874,"data":"       | Thus O must be a union of \"clusters\" where each cluster is a set of cells that is closed under the neighbor relation for the domino orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699782781,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699784466,"data":"       | But we can just brute force.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699786285,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699788215,"data":"       | We need to find minimal |D|.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699790121,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699792044,"data":"       | Let's attempt using Python with recursion and pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699794241,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699796034,"data":"       | We can start from low m and try to find a feasible arrangement.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699797588,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699798911,"data":"       | We can also use BFS: generate all possible sets of occupied cells O that are closed under neighbor condition and can be partitioned into dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699800589,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699801926,"data":"       | But maybe easier: use ILP with pulp? Not available.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699803394,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699804854,"data":"       | We can implement backtracking: choose a cell not yet occupied, try to place a domino covering it (horizontal or vertical) if possible, then enforce neighbor constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699806628,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699808705,"data":"       | But neighbor constraints involve cells that may not yet be occupied; we can enforce them later.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699810669,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699812536,"data":"       | Simplify: we can search for placements D such that after constructing O, the neighbor condition holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699814784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699816804,"data":"       | We can generate all subsets of domino placements of size k and test constraints. For small k, number of subsets may be manageable.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699818968,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699820888,"data":"       | Number of domino placements = 84. Choose k=5 =\u003e C(84,5) ~ 2.5e7 too many. So need smarter.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699831211,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699833301,"data":"       | We can use constraint programming: treat each cell variable: 0 empty, 1 occupied (by domino). But we also need pairing.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699835468,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699837736,"data":"       | Alternative: Use exact cover algorithm (Algorithm X) with constraints: each cell must be covered at most once (0 or 1). Additionally, for each domino placed, we need constraints that its neighbor cells are covered (i.e., must be selected as part of some domino). This is like a covering problem with additional constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699841405,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699843994,"data":"       | We can treat each neighbor cell as a \"must be covered\" constraint if any domino that uses it is selected. This is like a dependency.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699845964,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699847404,"data":"       | We can encode as SAT: each domino variable d_i; each cell variable c_j (occupied). Constraints:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699849395,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699851112,"data":"       | - For each cell j, sum of d_i covering j \u003c= 1 (non-overlap). Also c_j = OR of d_i covering j (i.e., cell occupied if any domino covering it). Could also define c_j as boolean.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699853792,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699855408,"data":"       | - For each domino i, for each neighbor n (if exists), we require c_n = True (i.e., neighbor occupied). This is a clause: d_i =\u003e c_n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699857837,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699861275,"data":"       | - For each cell j, c_j =\u003e (some domino covering it). Actually we can just define c_j as OR of covering dominoes; but we can also not need explicit c_j variables: we can enforce that if a domino is placed, its neighbor cells must be covered by some domino (maybe the same domino if neighbor cell is part of the same domino? Actually neighbor cell cannot be part of the same domino because it's adjacent beyond the domino's cells). So neighbor cell must be covered by some domino (maybe vertical/horizontal). So we need to ensure there exists a domino covering that neighbor cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699865311,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699867111,"data":"       | Thus we can encode as: For each neighbor cell n, there must exist at least one domino covering n (i.e., some domino variable covering n). This is a clause: d_i =\u003e (OR_{j: domino j covers n} d_j). This is a Horn clause.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699869507,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699871371,"data":"       | Thus we can treat as Horn SAT, which can be solved efficiently.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699873149,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699875017,"data":"       | But implementing SAT solver may be heavy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699877031,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699879038,"data":"       | Alternative: Use brute force search with recursion and propagate constraints: When we place a domino, we must also ensure that its neighbor cells are eventually covered. We can add them to a set of required cells that must be covered later.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699881382,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699882684,"data":"       | We can maintain:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699887221,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699889491,"data":"       | - occupied set O (cells already covered by placed dominoes).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699891479,"data":"       | - required set R (cells that must be covered due to neighbor constraints of placed dominoes, but not yet covered). Initially empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699894464,"data":"       | - When we place a domino covering cells a,b, we add a,b to O. For each neighbor n (if within board), if n not in O, we add n to R (must be covered later). If n already in O, fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699896658,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699898382,"data":"       | - Also, when we later place a domino covering a cell that is in R, we remove it from R (since now covered). However, placing that domino may introduce new neighbor requirements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699900444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699901885,"data":"       | - At any point, if a cell is required but cannot be covered because it's already occupied by a different domino (i.e., conflict), we backtrack.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699904078,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699905654,"data":"       | - Also, we need to ensure that we never exceed board boundaries.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699907624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699909281,"data":"       | - The recursion continues until R is empty and we decide to stop (i.e., we have a set of dominoes where all neighbor requirements satisfied). However we also need to ensure that there are no remaining dominoes that have unsatisfied neighbor cells (R empty ensures all placed dominoes have their neighbor cells covered). But also we must ensure that any domino we placed does not have neighbor cells that are off board? Off board is fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699912775,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699914297,"data":"       | Thus we can search for minimal number of dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699915938,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.699917404,"data":"       | We can also prune by lower bound: each domino placed adds at most 2 new cells to O (the domino cells). It may also add up to 2 new required cells (neighbors). So the number of required cells may increase.\n"}
