,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700670293,"data":"       | analysisInterpretation: Domino 1 horizontal covering (0,0)-(0,1). Domino 2 covering (0,2)-(0,3). Domino 3 covering (0,4)-(0,5). Domino 4 vertical covering (0,6)-(1,6). Domino 5 horizontal covering (2,5)-(2,6). Domino 6 vertical covering (1,4)-(2,4). Domino 7 vertical covering (3,4)-(4,4). Domino 8 vertical covering (5,4)-(6,4).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700671931,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700673246,"data":"       | Thus arrangement: top row has three horizontal dominoes covering columns 0-5, leaving column 6 occupied by vertical domino down rows 0-1. Then column 4 has vertical domino chain from rows 1-6 (except row 0 column4 is part of domino 3? Actually row0 col4 part of domino 3 (horizontal). So column 4 has vertical dominoes covering rows 1-2, 3-4, 5-6. Row2 columns 5-6 horizontal domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700674907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700675934,"data":"       | Thus all dominoes are blocked: Let's verify each domino's neighbor cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700677094,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700678176,"data":"       | - Domino 1 (row0 col0-1): left neighbor off board (col -1). Right neighbor cell (0,2) is occupied by domino 2. So blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700679441,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700680581,"data":"       | - Domino 2 (row0 col2-3): left neighbor (0,1) occupied by domino1. Right neighbor (0,4) occupied by domino3. Blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700681956,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700683004,"data":"       | - Domino 3 (row0 col4-5): left neighbor (0,3) occupied by domino2. Right neighbor (0,6) occupied by domino4 (vertical). Blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700684299,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700685324,"data":"       | - Domino 4 vertical (0,6)-(1,6): up neighbor off board. Down neighbor (2,6) is occupied by domino5 (horizontal). Blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700686573,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700687844,"data":"       | - Domino5 horizontal (2,5)-(2,6): left neighbor (2,4) occupied by domino6 (vertical). Right neighbor (2,7) off board (col 7 out of board). Blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700689261,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700690301,"data":"       | - Domino6 vertical (1,4)-(2,4): up neighbor (0,4) occupied by domino3. Down neighbor (3,4) occupied by domino7. Blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700691557,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700692564,"data":"       | - Domino7 vertical (3,4)-(4,4): up neighbor (2,4) occupied by domino6. Down neighbor (5,4) occupied by domino8. Blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700693787,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700694786,"data":"       | - Domino8 vertical (5,4)-(6,4): up neighbor (4,4) occupied by domino7. Down neighbor off board. Blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700696119,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700698464,"data":"       | Thus all dominoes blocked. So m=8 works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700699506,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700702774,"data":"       | Now we need to prove minimality: cannot do with 7 or fewer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700703894,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700704881,"data":"       | Our search suggests none exist. But we need to provide reasoning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700705981,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700706959,"data":"       | We can attempt to prove lower bound of 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700708006,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700708981,"data":"       | Potential reasoning: Each domino blocks at most 2 other dominoes? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700710151,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700714427,"data":"       | We can consider parity arguments: board has 49 squares, odd. Dominoes cover even number of squares (2 each). So number of occupied squares is even. So there will be odd number of empty squares (49 - 2m). For m=7, occupied squares =14, empty squares =35 (odd). For m=8, occupied squares =16, empty squares =33 (odd). So parity not helpful.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700716096,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700717061,"data":"       | We need a combinatorial lower bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700718121,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700719331,"data":"       | Observation: For each domino, its two neighbor cells must be occupied (or off board). So each domino contributes up to 2 \"requirements\". However each occupied cell can satisfy at most 2 requirements (since a cell can be neighbor to at most 2 dominoes? Actually a cell can be neighbor to up to 2 dominoes: one on each side along a line? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700720971,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700722901,"data":"       | Consider a cell that is occupied (part of a domino). It can serve as neighbor for at most 2 dominoes: For a horizontal domino, its right neighbor cell can be part of another domino (any orientation). That neighbor cell could also be left neighbor of another horizontal domino on its right side, etc. But a cell could be neighbor for up to 4 dominoes? Let's think: a cell can be adjacent to up to 4 dominoes: left neighbor of a horizontal domino to its right, right neighbor of a horizontal domino to its left, up neighbor of a vertical domino below, down neighbor of a vertical domino above. However those dominoes would occupy cells that share the neighbor cell. But the neighbor cell itself is occupied, so it belongs to some domino; that domino may be oriented horizontally or vertically. Could that same occupied cell serve as neighbor for multiple dominoes? Yes, e.g., a vertical domino covering cells (r,c) and (r+1,c). The cell (r,c) could be right neighbor of a horizontal domino to its left, and also left neighbor of a horizontal domino to its right? Actually left neighbor of a horizontal domino would be a cell to its left; right neighbor would be to its right. So a vertical domino cell could be neighbor for horizontal dominoes on both sides (left and right). Similarly, a horizontal domino cell could be neighbor for vertical dominoes above and below. So a cell could be neighbor for up to 4 dominoes (two horizontal on left/right, two vertical above/below). However those dominoes would need to be placed such that they don't overlap each other.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700728711,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700729721,"data":"       | Thus counting neighbor requirements may not give simple bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700730847,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700731869,"data":"       | Alternative approach: Use graph theory: Represent board cells as vertices. Domino placements as edges. Condition: For each selected edge e (domino), the two vertices adjacent to e along the line direction (i.e., the two vertices adjacent to e's endpoints outward) must be incident to some selected edge (i.e., occupied). This is reminiscent of a \"domino covering\" where each selected edge is part of a \"path\" of length at least 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700735549,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700736554,"data":"       | Thus each selected edge must be part of a path of length at least 3 (including itself). So the selected edges form a collection of paths (maybe also cycles) where each interior edge has both sides occupied, and endpoints may be at board edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700737924,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700738951,"data":"       | Thus the selected edges form a set of vertex-disjoint paths (since dominoes cannot overlap). Each path is a chain of dominoes placed consecutively in a line (horizontal or vertical). The endpoints of a path must be at board edge (since otherwise the endpoint would have an empty neighbor cell). So each path must start at board edge and extend inward.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700740488,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700741437,"data":"       | Thus the configuration is a collection of non-overlapping linear chains (horizontal or vertical) each anchored at board edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700742634,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700743609,"data":"       | Thus each chain of length k dominoes occupies 2k cells and uses k-1 interior cells that are neighbor to two dominoes, and two endpoint cells that are neighbor to board edge on one side and a domino on the other side.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700744906,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700745842,"data":"       | Thus each chain uses 2k cells and occupies k dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700746923,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700747899,"data":"       | Goal: minimize total number of dominoes across all chains.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700748974,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700750257,"data":"       | Thus we need to cover some cells with chains anchored at board edges, possibly intersecting? Wait chains cannot intersect because dominoes cannot overlap. However chains can cross each other perpendicularly? They could intersect at a cell? No, because a cell can be occupied by only one domino, so crossing not allowed. However a vertical chain could be adjacent to a horizontal chain (sharing neighbor cells) but not overlapping.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700751914,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700752854,"data":"       | Thus we need to select a set of cells (occupied) that can be partitioned into chains anchored at board edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700753998,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700754952,"data":"       | Thus each chain must start at board edge. So each chain uses at least one domino adjacent to board edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70075608,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700757087,"data":"       | Thus number of chains is at most number of board edges cells used? Actually each chain has two endpoints, each at board edge (could be same edge if chain runs along edge). But each endpoint corresponds to a domino cell that is adjacent to board edge on the side opposite the chain direction. For a horizontal chain, the leftmost domino's left side is board edge; the rightmost domino's right side is board edge. So each chain uses two board-edge cells (the cells at the ends). However those cells are part of dominoes; they are interior cells of board but adjacent to edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70076063,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700761723,"data":"       | Thus each chain uses at least 2 dominoes? Not necessarily; a chain of length 1 (single domino) could have both sides adjacent to board edge if placed at corner? Let's examine: a single horizontal domino placed at row0 columns 0-1: left side off board, right side cell (0,2) must be occupied, else not blocked. So a single domino cannot be blocked unless its other side is occupied by another domino. So a chain of length 1 cannot satisfy both sides unless placed at board edge on both sides (impossible because board edge only on one side). Actually a domino placed at corner covering (0,0)-(0,1) has left side off board, right side cell (0,2) empty -\u003e not blocked. So need at least length 2 chain to block each other.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700763833,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700765059,"data":"       | Thus minimal chain length is 2 dominoes? Let's test: two horizontal dominoes covering (0,0)-(0,1) and (0,2)-(0,3). The leftmost domino left side off board, right side occupied by second domino (cell (0,2)). The second domino left side occupied by first domino, right side off board? Actually second domino's right side is cell (0,4) which is empty unless there is third domino or board edge (col 4 not edge). So second domino not blocked. So chain of length 2 still leaves one end not blocked unless the chain touches board edge on both ends (i.e., chain length covering entire row from column 0 to column 6). But row length 7 odd, cannot be fully covered by dominoes (needs even number). So horizontal chain cannot have both ends at board edges because length would be 3.5 dominoes? Actually to have both ends at board edges, the chain must start at column 0 and end at column 6, covering columns 0-6 inclusive. That's 7 cells, which requires 3.5 dominoes, impossible. So a horizontal chain cannot have both ends at board edges. Similarly vertical chain cannot have both ends at board edges because board dimension odd.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700767756,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700768776,"data":"       | Thus each chain must have at least one endpoint not at board edge, meaning that endpoint must be blocked by another chain intersecting? Wait condition requires each domino's neighbor cell be occupied. If a chain ends at a cell where the neighbor beyond is empty (not board edge), then that domino would be unblocked. So chain must be \"closed\" at both ends either by board edge or by another domino from a perpendicular chain? Actually neighbor cell could be occupied by a domino oriented perpendicular, not necessarily part of same chain. For example, in our solution, the horizontal chain at top row ends at column 5 (domino 3) whose right neighbor is vertical domino 4 at column 6. So the chain's right endpoint is blocked by a perpendicular domino, not by board edge. Similarly, the leftmost endpoint of chain is at column 0 with left side off board, blocked. So each chain may have one end at board edge, the other end blocked by a perpendicular domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700771373,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700772441,"data":"       | Thus we can have \"junctions\" where a vertical domino blocks the end of a horizontal chain, and vice versa.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700773674,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700774982,"data":"       | Thus the structure resembles a tree of dominoes where each domino has degree up to 2 in the direction of its orientation (neighbors). The graph formed by dominoes as edges and cells as vertices? Actually each domino is an edge between two cells; the neighbor relation corresponds to adjacency of cells beyond the edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700778017,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700779018,"data":"       | We can think of each domino as requiring that the two cells beyond its endpoints are occupied. This can be satisfied by other dominoes that may be oriented perpendicularly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700780279,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70078125,"data":"       | Thus the configuration can be thought of as a set of dominoes such that the \"exterior\" cells of each domino are also covered.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700782567,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700783884,"data":"       | Thus we can think of a \"domino covering\" of a subset of cells that is \"2-closed\" under the operation of extending one cell outward along the domino orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700785341,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700786327,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of \"domino tiling of a region with no holes\" but not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700787507,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700788518,"data":"       | Nevertheless, computational search suggests minimal m=8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700789609,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70079064,"data":"       | We need to provide reasoning for lower bound of 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700791682,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700792681,"data":"       | Potential argument: Each domino blocks at most 2 other dominoes (its neighbors). So total number of dominoes that can be blocked is at most 2m (including themselves?). Actually each domino needs two blockers (neighbors). Each neighbor can serve as blocker for at most two dominoes (one on each side). So each domino requires two blockers, each blocker can serve at most two dominoes, so total number of dominoes needed must satisfy 2m \u003c= 2 * (number of blockers). But blockers are dominoes themselves. So each domino can serve as blocker for at most two other dominoes (one on each side). So each domino can satisfy at most two neighbor requirements (one left, one right). Since each domino has two neighbor requirements, we need at least as many dominoes as neighbor requirements? Let's formalize.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.700794934,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701225085,"data":"       | Let D be set of dominoes. For each domino d, define its two neighbor cells. Each neighbor cell belongs to some domino (maybe same d? No, neighbor cell cannot be part of d). So each neighbor cell belongs to some domino d'. So each domino d' can be neighbor for up to two dominoes: one on each side of its orientation? Actually a domino d' occupies two cells; each of those cells could be neighbor for at most one domino on each side? Let's examine: Suppose d' is horizontal covering cells a,b. The cell a could be right neighbor of a horizontal domino to its left (i.e., a is left neighbor of that domino). The cell a could also be left neighbor of a horizontal domino to its right? Wait left neighbor of a horizontal domino to its right would be cell a (since that domino's left neighbor is a). So a could be neighbor for two different dominoes: one on its left side (as right neighbor) and one on its right side (as left neighbor). However those two dominoes would be on opposite sides of a, overlapping? Actually they would be placed on opposite sides of a, but they would share cell a? Not possible because dominoes cannot overlap. For a to be neighbor of a domino on its left, that domino would occupy cells (a-2, a-1) (two cells left of a). For a to be neighbor of a domino on its right, that domino would occupy cells (a+1, a+2). Both are possible simultaneously because they don't overlap with each other or with d'. So a can be neighbor for up to two dominoes (one on each side). Similarly cell b can be neighbor for up to two dominoes (one on each side). So a domino (two cells) can be neighbor for up to 4 dominoes (two per cell). However some of those neighbor dominoes may be the same domino? Not possible because each neighbor domino would be distinct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70123408,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70123587,"data":"       | Thus each domino can satisfy up to 4 neighbor requirements (since each neighbor requirement is a cell). But each domino has exactly 2 neighbor requirements. So we need at least m dominoes to satisfy 2m neighbor requirements, but each domino can satisfy up to 4 neighbor requirements, so lower bound is m \u003e= ceil(2m/4) =\u003e m \u003e= 0, not helpful.\n"}
