,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701249197,"data":"       | Let E be number of domino sides that are adjacent to board edge. Each domino can have 0,1,2 sides adjacent to board edge (if placed at corner orientation maybe 2?). Actually a domino can have at most 2 sides adjacent to board edge: if placed along board edge, one side adjacent to board edge; if placed at corner covering cells (0,0)-(0,1) horizontal, left side off board, right side not edge (since column 2 interior). So only one side adjacent to board edge. A vertical domino at leftmost column covering (0,0)-(1,0) has left side off board, right side interior. So at most one side adjacent to board edge unless domino is placed at corner and oriented such that both sides are off board? Not possible because domino length 2, board edge only on one side of each cell line.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701253005,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701255361,"data":"       | Thus each domino can have at most 1 side adjacent to board edge (since board edge is a line; domino orientation is perpendicular to that line). Actually a domino placed at a corner could have both sides adjacent to board edges if oriented along the edge? Let's think: a horizontal domino placed at top row covering columns 0-1: left side off board (col -1), right side interior (col 2). So only left side adjacent to board edge. Similarly a vertical domino placed at leftmost column covering rows 0-1: up side off board (row -1), down side interior (row 2). So only one side adjacent to board edge. A domino placed at a corner covering cells (0,0)-(1,0) vertical: left side off board, right side interior. So still one side.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701258594,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701260706,"data":"       | Thus each domino contributes at most 1 board-edge side.\n"}
