,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701294536,"data":"       | Let's attempt to prove lower bound of 8 via exhaustive reasoning: Suppose we have at most 7 dominoes. Then total occupied cells = 14. The board has 49 cells, leaving 35 empty cells. Consider the set of empty cells. For each domino, its two neighbor cells must be occupied, thus they cannot be empty. So each domino \"protects\" up to 2 neighbor cells from being empty. So total number of cells that must be occupied (including domino cells) is at least number of domino cells (14) plus number of neighbor cells (up to 2 per domino) but neighbor cells may overlap. So we need to bound minimal number of occupied cells needed to block all dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701298254,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701300074,"data":"       | If we have 7 dominoes, each domino has 2 neighbor cells, total neighbor cells count = 14, but they could overlap heavily. To minimize total occupied cells, we want maximum overlap of neighbor cells. Overlap occurs when dominoes are arranged in chains where neighbor cells are shared. In a chain of k dominoes, internal dominoes have both neighbor cells occupied by other dominoes in chain, so they don't add new occupied cells beyond the chain cells themselves. Only the ends of chain may need extra occupied cells (or board edge). So the total occupied cells for a chain of k dominoes is 2k (the domino cells) plus maybe extra cells for ends if not blocked by board edge. But ends may be blocked by board edge (if chain touches edge) or by perpendicular domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701309646,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701311009,"data":"       | Thus to minimize total occupied cells for given number of dominoes, we want to arrange them in as few chains as possible, each anchored at board edge to avoid extra cells. Since board dimensions odd, a chain cannot have both ends at board edges (as argued). So each chain will need at least one extra occupied cell beyond its domino cells (unless the other end is blocked by another chain). That extra cell could be part of another chain (perpendicular). So we can have a network where chains intersect at endpoints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701313314,"data":"       | \n"}
