,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701369369,"data":"       | Maybe we can use parity of colors: Chessboard coloring black/white. Domino always covers one black and one white cell. The neighbor cells of a domino are of same color as the far cell? Let's examine: For a horizontal domino covering black-white (say left black, right white). The left neighbor cell (outside left) would be white (since alternating). The right neighbor cell (outside right) would be black. So neighbor cells are opposite colors of the domino cells? Actually left neighbor cell color equals color of right cell (since pattern). So each domino's two neighbor cells are of opposite colors? Let's compute: Suppose board colored like usual: (r+c) mod 2. For horizontal domino covering (r,c) and (r,c+1). Colors: (r+c) mod2 and (r+c+1) mod2 opposite. Left neighbor cell (r,c-1) has color opposite to (r,c) (since adjacent). That's same as color of (r,c+1). Right neighbor cell (r,c+2) has color opposite to (r,c+1) which equals color of (r,c). So neighbor cells have colors swapped: left neighbor same color as right cell, right neighbor same color as left cell. So each domino's neighbor cells are opposite colors, same as the domino's cells but swapped.\n"}
