,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701391983,"data":"       | Maybe we can use that each domino can satisfy at most 2 neighbor requirements (one on each side) because its cells can be neighbor for at most one domino each? Actually each cell can be neighbor for at most one domino on each side (left/right for horizontal orientation). So a domino's two cells can be neighbor for at most two dominoes (one on each side of the chain). In a chain, each interior domino's both neighbor cells are occupied by other dominoes, but those neighbor cells belong to adjacent dominoes, not the same domino. So each domino's cells serve as neighbor for at most two other dominoes (one on each side). So each domino can satisfy at most 2 neighbor requirements (the ones from its two neighboring dominoes). However in our solution, each domino's cells serve as neighbor for up to 2 dominoes (the ones adjacent along the chain). For example, domino 3's right neighbor cell (0,6) is part of domino 4, left neighbor cell (0,3) part of domino2. So domino3 satisfies neighbor requirements of domino2 and domino4. So each domino can satisfy at most 2 neighbor requirements (one on each side). This seems plausible: a domino cannot be neighbor for more than two dominoes because each side of the domino (its left side and right side) can be neighbor for at most one domino each (the domino that sits adjacent). So each domino can satisfy at most 2 neighbor requirements.\n"}
