,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701753525,"data":"     [Reasoning] (15166 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701754874,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse the problem: \"Place m 1x2 dominos on a 7x7 chessboard such that no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remains fixed. Find the minimal possible value of m.\"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701756346,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701757424,"data":"       | Interpretation: We have a 7x7 board (49 squares). We place some number m of dominos (each covering two adjacent squares horizontally or vertically). The condition: No domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remain fixed. Means each domino is \"blocked\" from moving in either direction (i.e., cannot be shifted one unit left/right/up/down while staying within board and not overlapping other dominos). The board is otherwise empty squares (uncovered). The dominos are fixed in place; we consider the ability to slide a domino by moving it one unit horizontally or vertically (keeping orientation same) while other dominos remain fixed. If there is any empty space adjacent to a domino that would allow it to slide (i.e., both squares it would occupy after shift are empty and within board), then the configuration is not allowed. So we need to place dominos such that each domino is \"blocked\" from sliding in both directions (horizontal for horizontal dominos? Actually sliding horizontally or vertically irrespective of orientation). For a horizontal domino (covers squares (i,j) and (i,j+1)), sliding horizontally left would require squares (i,j-1) and (i,j) empty (but (i,j) currently occupied). Wait sliding left means moving the domino one unit left: new squares would be (i,j-1) and (i,j). Since (i,j) is currently occupied by the domino, after sliding left, the domino would occupy (i,j-1) and (i,j). The square (i,j) remains occupied (the rightmost cell of original becomes leftmost of new). However the original rightmost cell (i,j+1) becomes empty. Similarly sliding right would need (i,j+2) empty. So for a horizontal domino to be blocked from sliding left, either (i,j-1) is outside board (j=0) or occupied by another domino. Similarly blocked from sliding right if (i,j+2) is outside board (j+1=6) or occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701761624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701762951,"data":"       | For vertical domino (covers (i,j) and (i+1,j)), sliding up requires (i-1,j) empty; sliding down requires (i+2,j) empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701764191,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701765234,"data":"       | Additionally, sliding perpendicular direction? The problem says \"no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remains fixed.\" Usually sliding means moving in its orientation direction? Actually a domino could slide in either direction (horizontal or vertical) regardless of orientation? But a domino cannot rotate, only slide. If a domino is horizontal, sliding vertically would mean moving up or down while staying horizontal orientation, i.e., moving to squares (i-1,j) and (i-1,j+1) (up) or (i+1,j) and (i+1,j+1) (down). That would be a vertical shift of the domino (translation). Similarly a vertical domino could slide horizontally to squares (i,j-1) and (i+1,j-1) (left) or (i,j+1) and (i+1,j+1) (right). So the condition likely means any translation by one unit in any of the four cardinal directions is blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70176937,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701770415,"data":"       | Thus each domino must be blocked from moving in any of the four directions (up/down/left/right). For each direction, the two squares that would be occupied after shift must be either outside board or occupied by another domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701771847,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701772872,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominos needed to achieve this blocking condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701774041,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701775072,"data":"       | Essentially we need a set of domino placements such that each domino has at least one occupied neighbor cell in each of the four directions adjacent to its two squares (the cells that would be needed for shift). Equivalent to a covering problem.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701776513,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701777552,"data":"       | We can model board as 7x7 grid of cells. Domino occupies two adjacent cells. For each domino, define its \"potential shift cells\": For each direction d, the set of two cells that would be occupied after shift. For a domino covering cells a and b, shift direction d results in cells a' and b' (translation). For shift to be possible, both a' and b' must be empty and within board. So to block shift, at least one of a', b' must be occupied or outside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701779404,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701780604,"data":"       | Thus we need to place dominos such that for each domino and each direction, at least one of the two shift cells is occupied (by another domino) or outside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701781916,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701782917,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701784017,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701785023,"data":"       | We can treat each domino as an object; the board cells may be covered by at most one domino (since dominos cannot overlap). So we need to choose a set of domino placements (non-overlapping) satisfying constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701786411,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701787429,"data":"       | This is a combinatorial optimization problem; we can attempt to solve via integer programming or brute force search with pruning due to board size 7x7 (49 cells). Max number of dominos is floor(49/2)=24. But we want minimal m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701788864,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701789905,"data":"       | We can attempt to formulate as exact cover problem with constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701791158,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701792176,"data":"       | But maybe there is a theoretical lower bound: each domino must have at least one neighbor cell occupied in each direction. Since each domino occupies two cells, each direction requires at least one of the two adjacent cells (outside board counts). For interior dominos (not on edge), each direction requires at least one occupied neighbor cell among the two cells adjacent to the domino in that direction. So each domino may need up to 8 neighbor cells (two per direction) but overlapping constraints may reduce.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701794044,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701795037,"data":"       | We can attempt to search for minimal m via integer programming using Python's pulp or ortools, but not available. Could implement brute-force using backtracking with pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701798072,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701799111,"data":"       | Given board size 7x7, we can try to search for minimal m by exploring placements of dominos and checking constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701800384,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701801416,"data":"       | But we need to guarantee minimal m; we can also compute lower bound using covering arguments.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701803244,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701804537,"data":"       | Let's attempt to compute via integer programming using Python's pulp? Not available maybe. Could use python's itertools to generate all possible domino placements (there are (7*6)*2 = 84? Actually number of possible domino positions: horizontal: 7 rows * 6 columns = 42. vertical: 6 rows * 7 columns = 42. So total 84 possible domino placements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701806312,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701807327,"data":"       | We need to select subset of these placements such that they are non-overlapping (no cell covered by more than one domino) and satisfy blocking constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701808748,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701809766,"data":"       | We can formulate as a SAT/ILP problem: binary variable x_i for each placement i (0/1). For each cell, sum of x_i covering that cell \u003c= 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701811126,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701812261,"data":"       | For each domino i selected (x_i=1), for each direction d, we need at least one of the two shift cells to be occupied (by some domino j) or outside board. This is a conditional constraint: if x_i=1 then condition holds. This can be linearized using big-M: For each direction d, define set of placements j that would occupy at least one of the two shift cells. Then we need sum_{j in S_{i,d}} x_j \u003e= x_i (since at least one of them must be selected). However note that j could be same as i? No, shift cells are different from original cells, so j cannot be i (since i's cells are not shift cells). So we need at least one other domino covering at least one of the shift cells. But also shift cells could be outside board, which automatically satisfies condition; we can treat that as already satisfied, i.e., we don't need constraint for that direction if shift would go out of board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701814784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701815794,"data":"       | Thus for each interior direction where shift cells are inside board, we need at least one domino covering at least one of those cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701817063,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701818489,"data":"       | But note that a domino covering a shift cell may also be the same domino i if the shift cell overlaps one of i's cells? Actually shift cells are the two cells after translation; they are adjacent to original cells. For a horizontal domino (cells (r,c) and (r,c+1)), shift left cells are (r,c-1) and (r,c). One of them is (r,c) which is already occupied by i. But after shift left, the domino would occupy (r,c-1) and (r,c). Since (r,c) is already occupied by i, the shift would be possible if (r,c-1) is empty (and (r,c) is already occupied). Wait the condition for shift left: both cells after shift must be empty (except the overlapping cell?). Actually moving a domino left by one unit would vacate its rightmost cell (r,c+1) and occupy new leftmost cell (r,c-1). The cell (r,c) remains occupied (by the same domino). So for shift left to be possible, the new cell (r,c-1) must be empty. The other cell (r,c) is already occupied by the domino itself, so that's fine. So the condition reduces to: the cell that becomes newly occupied must be empty. Similarly shift right requires (r,c+2) empty. For vertical domino shift up requires (r-1,c) empty; shift down requires (r+2,c) empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701822966,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701824023,"data":"       | Thus for a horizontal domino, the blocking condition for left/right is that the adjacent cell beyond its ends must be occupied (or outside board). For up/down shift (moving whole domino up/down) requires both cells above (r-1,c) and (r-1,c+1) empty for up; both cells below (r+2,c) and (r+2,c+1) empty for down. So for up/down we need both cells to be occupied or outside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70182579,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701826976,"data":"       | Similarly for vertical domino: left shift requires both cells left of its two cells (r,c-1) and (r+1,c-1) occupied; right shift requires both cells right (r,c+2) and (r+1,c+2) occupied. Up shift requires cell above top cell (r-1,c) occupied; down shift requires cell below bottom cell (r+2,c) occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701828608,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701829632,"data":"       | Thus the constraints differ for direction relative to orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701830777,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701831782,"data":"       | Thus we need to model correctly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701833047,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701834052,"data":"       | Let's define for each domino placement i, orientation (horizontal or vertical) and coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70183534,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701836352,"data":"       | Define shift directions:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701837413,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701838436,"data":"       | If horizontal domino at (r,c)-(r,c+1):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701839514,"data":"       | - Left shift: need cell (r,c-1) occupied or outside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701840655,"data":"       | - Right shift: need cell (r,c+2) occupied or outside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701841776,"data":"       | - Up shift: need both cells (r-1,c) and (r-1,c+1) occupied or outside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70184293,"data":"       | - Down shift: need both cells (r+2,c) and (r+2,c+1) occupied or outside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701844109,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701845127,"data":"       | If vertical domino at (r,c)-(r+1,c):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701846517,"data":"       | - Up shift: need cell (r-1,c) occupied or outside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701847651,"data":"       | - Down shift: need cell (r+2,c) occupied or outside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701848802,"data":"       | - Left shift: need both cells (r,c-1) and (r+1,c-1) occupied or outside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701849987,"data":"       | - Right shift: need both cells (r,c+2) and (r+1,c+2) occupied or outside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701851165,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701852184,"data":"       | Thus each domino must be blocked in each of four directions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70185333,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701855784,"data":"       | Now we need to find minimal number of dominos satisfying these constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701856976,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701858002,"data":"       | We can formulate ILP: binary variables x_i for each placement i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701859148,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701860161,"data":"       | Constraints:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701861251,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701862271,"data":"       | 1. Non-overlap: For each cell (r,c), sum_{i covering cell} x_i \u003c= 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701863506,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701864624,"data":"       | 2. For each domino i and each direction d where shift cells are within board (i.e., not outside), we need at least one of the shift cells occupied by some domino j (j may be i? Not for left/right of horizontal because shift cell (r,c) is already occupied by i, but we need the new cell (r,c-1) to be occupied. So we need at least one domino covering that cell. So we need sum_{j covering that cell} x_j \u003e= x_i. For directions requiring two cells, we need both cells occupied, i.e., sum_{j covering cell1} x_j \u003e= x_i and sum_{j covering cell2} x_j \u003e= x_i? Actually need both cells occupied. So we need each of the two cells to be occupied by some domino (could be same domino covering both? Not possible because a domino covers two adjacent cells; but a cell can be occupied by at most one domino). So we need each cell individually occupied. So we need for each required cell, sum_{j covering that cell} x_j \u003e= x_i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701867299,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701868317,"data":"       | Thus for each direction requiring one cell (horizontal left/right, vertical up/down), we need that cell occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701869538,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701870561,"data":"       | For each direction requiring two cells (horizontal up/down, vertical left/right), we need both cells occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701871823,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701872831,"data":"       | Thus constraints become linear.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701873948,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701874951,"data":"       | Goal: minimize sum_i x_i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701876024,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701877346,"data":"       | Now we can solve using integer linear programming. Since we don't have external ILP solver, we could use brute force search with pruning using Python's pulp? Not available maybe. Could implement using python's `itertools` with branch and bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701878824,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701879831,"data":"       | But board size moderate; we can attempt to use `python-mip`? Not available. Could use `z3`? Not available.\n"}
