,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701908539,"data":"       | Let's attempt to reason.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70190961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701910651,"data":"       | Observation: Edge cells provide natural blocking because shift would go out of board. So placing dominos near edges reduces needed neighbor occupancy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701911971,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701912994,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominos while ensuring each domino's required neighbor cells are occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701914192,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701915569,"data":"       | We can think of each domino requiring certain \"support\" cells to be occupied. These support cells can be provided by other dominos (by covering them). So we need to select a set of dominos such that each domino's required cells are covered by some domino (maybe itself for cells it already occupies). But note that for a direction requiring a cell that is already part of the domino (e.g., horizontal left shift requires cell (r,c) which is part of domino), but we need the new cell (r,c-1) not part of domino. So we need support cells distinct from its own cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70191759,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70191859,"data":"       | Thus each domino requires up to 6 distinct support cells (some may overlap with other dominos' own cells). Let's compute required cells for each orientation:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701919884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701920922,"data":"       | Horizontal domino at (r,c)-(r,c+1):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701922036,"data":"       | - Left: cell (r,c-1) (if inside)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701923128,"data":"       | - Right: cell (r,c+2) (if inside)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701924226,"data":"       | - Up: cells (r-1,c) and (r-1,c+1) (if inside)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701925325,"data":"       | - Down: cells (r+2,c) and (r+2,c+1) (if inside)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701927696,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701928744,"data":"       | Thus up to 6 cells (some may be outside board). Similarly vertical domino requires up to 6 cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701929948,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701930966,"data":"       | Thus each domino may need up to 6 other cells to be occupied. Since each other domino occupies 2 cells, each domino can provide support for up to 2 cells (its own cells) for other dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701932362,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701933375,"data":"       | Thus we can think of a covering problem: we need to cover all required support cells for each domino using other dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701934621,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.701935659,"data":"       | Potential lower bound: each domino needs at most 6 support cells; each other domino provides 2 cells; but support cells may be shared among multiple dominos. So lower bound may be less than 3 per domino.\n"}
