,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70202788,"data":"       | We can generate all possible sets of non-overlapping placements using recursion: choose a placement, then recursively choose placements that do not overlap with already chosen. This is similar to generating all matchings of size m in the adjacency graph of cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702029281,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702030243,"data":"       | Number of possible matchings of size m may still be large, but for small m maybe manageable.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702031356,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702032297,"data":"       | We need to find minimal m such that there exists a matching (set of dominos) satisfying constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702033442,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70203442,"data":"       | We can start with lower bound: Since each domino must be blocked from sliding left/right/up/down. Edge cells provide natural blocking for some directions. Let's compute minimal number of dominos needed to block all directions for each domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702035779,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702036729,"data":"       | If we place a domino at a corner (e.g., top-left corner cells (0,0)-(0,1) horizontal). For this domino:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702037892,"data":"       | - Left shift: outside board (c-1 = -1) -\u003e automatically blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702039101,"data":"       | - Up shift: outside board (r-1 = -1) -\u003e blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702040236,"data":"       | - Right shift: need cell (0,2) occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702041658,"data":"       | - Down shift: need cells (1,0) and (1,1) occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702042746,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702043692,"data":"       | Thus needs support cells: (0,2), (1,0), (1,1). That's 3 cells. Could be covered by other dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70204639,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702047397,"data":"       | Similarly a domino at edge but not corner may need 4 support cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702048512,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70204946,"data":"       | Thus each domino may need up to 6 support cells, but edges reduce.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702050547,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702051491,"data":"       | Thus lower bound on m maybe \u003e? Not trivial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702052769,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702053734,"data":"       | Let's attempt to search using Python with recursion and pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70205483,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702055816,"data":"       | We'll generate all placements (84). Represent each placement as tuple of two cell indices (0..48). We'll also precompute for each placement the required support cells for each direction (list of cells). For each direction, we need either outside board (ignore) or required cells must be occupied by some domino (i.e., at least one of them for one-cell direction, both for two-cell direction). We'll treat each required cell individually: for one-cell direction, we need that cell occupied; for two-cell direction, both cells must be occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70205773,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70205871,"data":"       | Thus we can define for each placement i a set of required cells (maybe with multiplicities) that must be occupied by some domino (could be itself for cells it already occupies? Actually for one-cell direction, required cell is not part of domino; for two-cell direction, required cells are not part of domino either (they are adjacent rows/columns). So we can treat required cells as distinct from domino's own cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70206032,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702061298,"data":"       | Thus each domino i imposes constraints: for each required cell c, there must be some domino j covering c (j may be i? Not possible because i does not cover c). So we need to ensure that each required cell is covered by at least one domino in the set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702062656,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702063605,"data":"       | Thus overall constraints: For each cell c that is required by some placed domino i, there must be a domino covering c. This is similar to a covering problem: we need to select dominos such that the union of their cells includes all required cells for each domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702064988,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702066128,"data":"       | Thus we can think of a set of cells that must be covered: For each domino i, we have required cells R_i (list). The condition is: For each i in selected set, for each cell c in R_i, there exists some j in selected set such that c in cells(j). So we can treat as a closure property: The set of cells covered by selected dominos must be a superset of the union of required cells of each domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702067737,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70206868,"data":"       | Thus we can search for a set of dominos S such that:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702069743,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702070688,"data":"       | - Non-overlap: cells of dominos in S are disjoint.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70207176,"data":"       | - For each domino d in S, for each required cell c in R_d, there exists some domino d' in S covering c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702073907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702074927,"data":"       | Thus we can treat as a directed graph: each domino requires certain cells; those cells must be provided by other dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702076146,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702077137,"data":"       | Thus we can attempt to find minimal size S satisfying these constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70207824,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702079214,"data":"       | We can implement a recursive search that builds S and ensures that for each domino added, its required cells are either already covered by existing dominos, or will be covered later (i.e., we need to add dominos that cover them). This is similar to solving a hitting set problem.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70208062,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70208157,"data":"       | We can use backtracking with heuristic: choose a domino that is not yet \"satisfied\" (i.e., its required cells not all covered) and try to add dominos covering missing cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702082868,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702083868,"data":"       | But we also need to ensure that added dominos themselves become satisfied eventually.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702084975,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702086,"data":"       | We can treat this as a constraint satisfaction problem: each cell can be either empty or occupied by a domino. The condition for each domino is that its required cells are occupied.\n"}
