,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702682154,"data":"       | But first test unsatisfied_requirements for empty set: returns empty, so solution with m=0 would be considered valid (since no dominos, no requirement). But we need at least one domino? Actually condition is about dominos placed; if no dominos, trivially no domino can slide because there are none. But problem likely expects at least one domino? Usually they ask minimal m such that condition holds for all placed dominos. If you place zero dominos, condition vacuously true. But they likely want non-zero because trivial answer 0 is not interesting. So they assume at least one domino placed. So we need minimal positive m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702684078,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.702685102,"data":"       | Our search started at m=1 but didn't find solution up to 14. Might be due to algorithm not exploring enough possibilities because we always pick first unsatisfied requirement and missing cell; but there may be multiple missing cells; we only try placements covering first missing cell. That should be sufficient because to satisfy requirement, each missing cell must be covered; we can choose any missing cell to address. However we may need to add placements covering multiple missing cells simultaneously (i.e., a placement may cover two missing cells from different requirements). Our algorithm picks a missing cell and tries placements covering it; that could still lead to covering other missing cells as side effect.\n"}
