,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704692774,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704693735,"data":"       | We can try to use heuristic: Since we need to cover almost all cells, maybe the minimal m is 24 (i.e., we need to tile almost entire board). Let's test if there exists configuration with 24 dominos satisfying constraints via a more directed search: we can try to use backtracking that places dominos one by one, ensuring that after each placement, we don't create unsatisfied requirements that cannot be satisfied later given remaining empty cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704696958,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7046982,"data":"       | But we can also try to use integer programming via brute force enumeration of all matchings of size 24 (i.e., all ways to leave one empty cell). That's essentially choose which cell is empty, then tile the rest. Number of domino tilings of a 7x7 board with one missing cell is known as number of perfect matchings of a 7x7 board with one cell removed (a monomer-dimer problem). This number may be large but maybe manageable.\n"}
