,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704885813,"data":"     [Reasoning] (13001 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704887027,"data":"       | analysisWe need to understand problem: Place m 1x2 dominos on a 7x7 chessboard such that no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remain fixed. Find minimal possible m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704888319,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704889358,"data":"       | Interpretation: We have a 7x7 board (grid of 7 rows, 7 columns). Dominos are 1x2 rectangles covering two adjacent squares either horizontally or vertically. We place m dominos (non-overlapping). The condition: no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remain fixed. Means each domino is \"blocked\" from moving in its orientation direction? Actually sliding horizontally or vertically means moving the domino one unit left/right/up/down while staying within board and not overlapping other dominos? Usually sliding a domino means moving it parallel to its longer side? Wait: Domino is 1x2; sliding horizontally means moving it left or right by one cell (keeping orientation same). Sliding vertically means moving up/down by one cell. So we need configuration such that each domino cannot be moved one unit in either direction (horizontal or vertical) without overlapping another domino or going off board, while other dominos stay fixed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70489187,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704892845,"data":"       | Thus each domino must be \"blocked\" on both sides in both directions? Actually for a horizontal domino (covering two adjacent squares in same row), sliding horizontally would move it left or right; sliding vertically would move it up or down (i.e., shift both squares up or down). For vertical domino, sliding vertically moves up/down; sliding horizontally moves left/right.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704894484,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704895455,"data":"       | But the condition says \"no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remains fixed.\" So for each domino, any slide (by one cell) in any direction (horizontal or vertical) is impossible because either it would go off board or would overlap another domino. So each domino must be adjacent (in the direction of slide) to either board edge or another domino's occupied squares.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7048971,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704898063,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominos needed to achieve this \"immobility\" property for all placed dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704899495,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704900465,"data":"       | We need to find minimal m such that there exists a placement of m dominos satisfying condition. Equivalent to find minimal number of dominos needed to \"lock\" each other.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704901833,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704903038,"data":"       | We can think of each domino as a 2-cell shape. For a given domino, to prevent sliding horizontally (left/right), each of its two cells must have neighbor cell in that direction occupied (by another domino) or be at board edge. Similarly for vertical slide.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704904457,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704905428,"data":"       | But sliding a domino up or down requires both cells to have empty squares above (or below) them. If any of the two cells would go off board or into occupied cell, slide not possible. So to block vertical slide for a horizontal domino, at least one of its two cells must have occupied neighbor above (or below) or be at top/bottom edge. Actually to block slide up, both cells must have either occupied cell above or board edge? Let's think: Suppose horizontal domino occupies cells (r,c) and (r,c+1). To slide up, we would move to cells (r-1,c) and (r-1,c+1). This requires both squares above to be empty and within board. If either of those squares is occupied or out of board, slide up impossible. So to block slide up, we need at least one of the two squares above to be occupied or out of board. Similarly for slide down.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704909304,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704910287,"data":"       | Thus for each domino, for each direction (left, right, up, down), at least one of the two squares that would move into must be blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704911495,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704912457,"data":"       | Goal: find minimal number of dominos covering some cells such that each domino's four directions are blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704913643,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704914865,"data":"       | We can treat board cells as vertices; dominos as edges covering two adjacent cells. The condition for each domino is that for each of the four directions (north, south, west, east), at least one of the two cells adjacent in that direction is either outside board or occupied by another domino (i.e., part of some domino). Note that the occupied cells are exactly the cells covered by dominos (2m cells). So we need to choose a set S of cells (size 2m) such that they can be partitioned into domino pairs (adjacent) and for each domino pair (cells a,b), for each direction d, at least one of the two cells adjacent to a or b in direction d is either outside board or also in S (i.e., occupied). However note that if the adjacent cell is also in S but belongs to same domino? For horizontal domino, left neighbor of left cell is outside board or maybe occupied by another domino; left neighbor of right cell is the left cell itself (occupied) but sliding left would move both cells left; the left neighbor of left cell is needed; the right neighbor of right cell is needed. Actually for sliding left, we need the cell to the left of the leftmost cell to be empty; if it's occupied (by any domino) or outside board, slide left impossible. So for a horizontal domino, left slide blocked if left neighbor of left cell is occupied/outside. Right slide blocked if right neighbor of right cell is occupied/outside. Up slide blocked if at least one of the two cells above is occupied/outside. Down slide blocked if at least one of the two cells below is occupied/outside.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704918225,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704919177,"data":"       | Thus condition depends on orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704920189,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704921155,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominos (i.e., number of occupied cells) that satisfy these constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704922271,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704923241,"data":"       | We can treat each cell as either occupied (by a domino) or empty. The occupied cells must be paired into dominos (adjacent). The constraints: For each occupied pair (domino), we need:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704924522,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704925461,"data":"       | - If domino is horizontal covering (r,c) (left) and (r,c+1) (right):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7049266,"data":"       |    - left neighbor (r,c-1) must be occupied or outside (c-1\u003c1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70492771,"data":"       |    - right neighbor (r,c+2) must be occupied or outside (c+2\u003e7)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704930013,"data":"       |    - up neighbor condition: at least one of (r-1,c) or (r-1,c+1) must be occupied or outside (r-1\u003c1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704931215,"data":"       |    - down neighbor condition: at least one of (r+1,c) or (r+1,c+1) must be occupied or outside (r+1\u003e7)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704932397,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704933372,"data":"       | Similarly for vertical domino covering (r,c) (top) and (r+1,c) (bottom):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704934894,"data":"       |    - up neighbor (r-1,c) must be occupied/outside (r-1\u003c1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704936039,"data":"       |    - down neighbor (r+2,c) must be occupied/outside (r+2\u003e7)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704937165,"data":"       |    - left neighbor condition: at least one of (r,c-1) or (r+1,c-1) occupied/outside (c-1\u003c1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704938308,"data":"       |    - right neighbor condition: at least one of (r,c+1) or (r+1,c+1) occupied/outside (c+1\u003e7)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704939427,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704940393,"data":"       | Goal: find minimal number of dominos (i.e., minimal occupied cells) satisfying these constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704941529,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704942498,"data":"       | We can attempt to formulate as integer programming or SAT problem and solve via brute force search for minimal m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704943633,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704944594,"data":"       | Board size 7x7 = 49 cells. Each domino occupies 2 cells, so m \u003c= floor(49/2)=24 (since odd leftover). But we want minimal m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70494581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704946778,"data":"       | We can attempt to search for configurations with small m, increasing m until feasible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704947894,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704948848,"data":"       | But enumerating all placements of m dominos is combinatorial. However we can treat as exact cover problem with constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704950024,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704950991,"data":"       | We can use Python with ILP using pulp? Not allowed maybe. Could use brute force search with backtracking and pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704952167,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704953178,"data":"       | Since board is small (49 cells), we can attempt to use SAT solver via python's itertools? Might be heavy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704954317,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704955266,"data":"       | Better approach: Use integer linear programming via pulp or ortools? Not available maybe. Could implement using python's `python-constraint`? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704956488,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704957431,"data":"       | Alternative: Use brute force search with BFS for small m (like up to maybe 8). Since minimal m likely small, maybe 6 or 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704958623,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704959595,"data":"       | We can attempt to search for placements of m dominos using backtracking, checking constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7049623,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704963281,"data":"       | Number of ways to place m dominos without overlap: choose 2m cells and pair them into adjacent pairs. That's large.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704964466,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704965437,"data":"       | But we can prune heavily using constraints: each domino must be blocked on all sides. That likely forces many occupied cells around each domino, maybe requiring many dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704966717,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704967954,"data":"       | Let's think conceptually: To block a domino horizontally (left/right), you need occupied cells at its left and right ends (or board edges). So each horizontal domino must be at column 1 (left edge) or have occupied cell at column c-1; similarly at column 6 (since board width 7, rightmost column index 7). For interior horizontal domino (c between 2 and 5), you need occupied cells at both left and right neighbor cells. Those neighbor cells could be part of other dominos (maybe vertical). So each interior horizontal domino forces at least two extra occupied cells adjacent horizontally.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704969848,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704970795,"data":"       | Similarly vertical domino interior (rows 2-5) need occupied cells above and below.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704971908,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704972907,"data":"       | Thus to minimize number of dominos, we may want to place dominos at edges where some blocking is provided by board boundary. For example, a horizontal domino placed at leftmost columns (c=1, covering columns 1 and 2) has left neighbor outside board, so left slide blocked automatically. It still needs right neighbor at column 3 occupied/outside. So we need an occupied cell at column 3 in same row (maybe part of another domino). Similarly for vertical domino at top row.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704974611,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704975581,"data":"       | Thus we can try to construct minimal configuration using edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70497667,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704977624,"data":"       | Potentially we could place dominos along the perimeter such that each domino's missing side is blocked by edge, and the other side blocked by neighbor domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704978861,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704979833,"data":"       | But also need up/down blocking condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704980898,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704981843,"data":"       | Let's attempt to design manually.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704982883,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704983851,"data":"       | Consider placing a horizontal domino at (row r, columns 1-2). It needs right neighbor at column 3 occupied. Also up/down condition: at least one of the cells above (r-1,1) or (r-1,2) occupied/outside; similarly at least one of cells below (r+1,1) or (r+1,2) occupied/outside.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704985269,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704986241,"data":"       | If we place domino at top row (r=1), then up neighbor is outside board, so up condition satisfied automatically. Down condition still needs at least one of cells (2,1) or (2,2) occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704987537,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704988814,"data":"       | Thus a domino at top-left corner (row1 col1-2) would have left edge outside, up outside, need right neighbor at (1,3) occupied, and need down neighbor at (2,1) or (2,2) occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704994745,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704995764,"data":"       | Similarly we could place a vertical domino at (row1-2, col1). It would have up outside, left neighbor condition: at least one of (1,0) or (2,0) outside (since col0 outside) -\u003e left condition satisfied automatically (since at left edge). Right neighbor condition: at least one of (1,2) or (2,2) occupied. Down neighbor: need cell at (3,1) occupied/outside (if row2 is bottom of domino). Since row2 not bottom edge (board size 7), need occupied at (3,1). So vertical domino at leftmost column also needs right neighbor occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704997628,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704998603,"data":"       | Thus edges reduce required blocking cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.704999691,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705000664,"data":"       | Goal: find minimal set of dominos such that each domino's required neighbor cells are occupied by other dominos (or edges). This is reminiscent of a \"domino locking\" puzzle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70500199,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705002933,"data":"       | We can treat each occupied cell as part of a domino; we need to satisfy constraints for each domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705004059,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705005007,"data":"       | We could attempt to formulate as SAT with variables for each cell whether occupied, and orientation variables for each domino (pair). But orientation also determines which neighbor constraints apply.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705006328,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705007284,"data":"       | Simplify: Since each occupied cell belongs to exactly one domino, we can treat each domino as a variable representing a placement (i.e., a specific pair of adjacent cells). Choose a set of domino placements such that they are disjoint and satisfy constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705008666,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705009624,"data":"       | We can brute force search using Python's `itertools.combinations` of possible domino placements (there are (7*6)*2? Actually number of possible domino placements on 7x7 board: horizontal placements: 7 rows * 6 columns = 42. Vertical placements: 6 rows * 7 columns = 42. Total 84 possible domino positions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705011064,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705012034,"data":"       | We need to select a subset of these placements, no overlapping cells, such that constraints hold.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705013163,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705014405,"data":"       | We can treat each placement as an index; we need to choose subset S of placements with no overlapping cells. For each placement p in S, we need to check constraints using S.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705015672,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705016623,"data":"       | We can search for minimal size of S.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705017671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705018633,"data":"       | Since total placements 84, we can attempt to search for subsets of size m using combinations of placements, but number of combos grows quickly: C(84,m). For m small (like up to 6), C(84,6) ~ 3e9, too large.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705019944,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705023368,"data":"       | But we can use backtracking with pruning: choose placements sequentially, ensure no overlap, and check constraints gradually. Since constraints involve neighbor cells, we may need to know if neighbor cells are occupied by some placement (maybe not yet selected). So we cannot fully verify until full set built.\n"}
