,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705115637,"data":"       | - For each placement p (if x_p=1), we need blocking constraints: For each direction d, at least one of the neighbor cells (as defined) must be either outside board or occupied by some placement (i.e., sum_{p' containing neighbor cell} x_{p'} ≥ 1). However note that neighbor cell may be part of same placement? For horizontal domino, left neighbor of left cell could be part of another domino, but not same domino because left neighbor is distinct cell. So we can just require that neighbor cell is occupied (by any domino). So constraints: For each placement p, for each direction condition, define set N(p,d) of cells that would need to be occupied (or outside). For each direction, we need at least one cell in N(p,d) to be occupied (or outside). Since outside automatically satisfied, we can treat those directions as already satisfied if any neighbor is outside board. So we only need to enforce constraints for directions where both neighbor cells are inside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705118109,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705119053,"data":"       | Thus we can formulate as integer linear constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705120103,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705121111,"data":"       | We can solve using brute force enumeration of subsets of placements with size up to maybe 10 using recursion.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705122283,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70512324,"data":"       | Since each placement uses 2 cells, the maximum number of placements we might need is maybe around 12? Let's guess minimal m maybe around 7 or 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705124458,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705125653,"data":"       | We can attempt to search for minimal m using recursive backtracking with pruning: choose placements, keep track of occupied cells, and also maintain for each placed domino whether its constraints are satisfied given current occupied cells (including future placements). Since constraints may be satisfied later by future placements, we cannot prune too early. However we can prune if a placed domino's constraints become impossible to satisfy given remaining empty cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70512882,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705129807,"data":"       | We can compute for each placed domino the set of neighbor cells that could satisfy each direction. If all neighbor cells for a direction are already known to be empty (i.e., not occupied and cannot be occupied later because they are not part of any remaining possible placement due to overlap), then configuration invalid.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705131281,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705132244,"data":"       | But implementing this may be complex.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705133306,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705134283,"data":"       | Alternative: Use BFS over states of occupied cells (bitmask of 49 bits). Since 2^49 is huge, but we can limit to small number of occupied cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705135518,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705136481,"data":"       | We can generate all possible subsets of cells of size 2m and test if they can be partitioned into dominos and satisfy constraints. For small m, number of subsets C(49,2m) may be manageable for m up to maybe 6 (C(49,12) ~ 1.2e10, too large). So not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705137856,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705138804,"data":"       | Maybe we can use heuristic search (randomized) to find low m configuration, then try to prove minimal via lower bound arguments.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705140011,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705140976,"data":"       | Let's attempt to think about lower bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705142027,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70514298,"data":"       | Each domino must be blocked on four sides. For interior dominos, each side requires at least one neighbor occupied. So each interior domino may require up to 4 neighbor cells, but some neighbor cells can serve multiple dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705144316,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705145264,"data":"       | We can attempt to use counting arguments: each occupied cell can serve as neighbor for up to how many blocking constraints? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705146473,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70514744,"data":"       | Consider a cell that is occupied (part of a domino). It can block sliding for adjacent dominos in various directions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70514859,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70514988,"data":"       | Specifically, for a horizontal domino, its right neighbor cell (if occupied) blocks its right slide. That neighbor cell could be part of another domino (maybe vertical). That neighbor cell can also block left slide of a domino to its right, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705151234,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705152187,"data":"       | Thus each occupied cell can serve as a blocking neighbor for up to 4 adjacent dominos (one on each side). But each domino needs at most 2 neighbor cells per direction? Actually each direction needs at least one neighbor cell occupied. So each occupied cell can satisfy at most one direction for each adjacent domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705153683,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705154643,"data":"       | Thus we can try to bound minimal number of occupied cells needed to satisfy all constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705156841,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705157843,"data":"       | Let D be number of dominos (m). Each domino requires up to 4 blocking conditions (left, right, up, down). However some conditions may be satisfied by board edge (outside). So we can count total number of interior sides that need blocking.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70515923,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70516019,"data":"       | Define for each domino orientation, which sides are interior (i.e., not at board edge). For each interior side, we need at least one occupied neighbor cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705161433,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705162384,"data":"       | Thus total required neighbor-occupied cells (with multiplicity) is sum over dominos of number of interior sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705163536,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705164508,"data":"       | Let S be set of occupied cells (size 2m). Each occupied cell can serve as neighbor for at most 4 sides (since each cell has up to 4 orthogonal adjacent cells). However a side may be satisfied by a cell that is part of the same domino? No, neighbor cell cannot be part of same domino because they are distinct cells (except maybe for vertical/horizontal orientation? Actually for a horizontal domino, the left neighbor of left cell is distinct; the right neighbor of right cell distinct; up/down neighbors distinct). So neighbor cells are distinct from the domino's own cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705166374,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705167324,"data":"       | Thus each occupied cell can be counted at most 4 times across all side requirements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705168437,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705169383,"data":"       | Thus we have inequality: total interior side requirements ≤ 4 * (2m) = 8m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705170593,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705171755,"data":"       | But total interior side requirements is at least something like 4m - (edges contributions). Since each domino has 4 sides, but some sides may be on board edge. The number of sides on board edge is limited.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705173072,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705174037,"data":"       | We can compute minimal possible interior sides per domino if we place them at edges/corners. For a domino placed at a corner (e.g., top-left horizontal covering (1,1)-(1,2)), its left side is edge, up side edge, right side interior (needs neighbor), down side interior (needs neighbor). So interior sides = 2. Similarly a vertical domino at top-left corner (covering (1,1)-(2,1)) has up edge, left edge, down interior, right interior =\u003e 2 interior sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705175761,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705176762,"data":"       | Thus each domino can have as few as 2 interior sides if placed at a corner. If placed along edge but not corner, e.g., horizontal domino at top row columns 3-4: up edge, left interior, right interior, down interior =\u003e 3 interior sides. So corners give 2 interior sides, edges give 3, interior gives 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70517825,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705179203,"data":"       | Thus to minimize total interior side requirements, we should place dominos at corners as much as possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705180364,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705182842,"data":"       | Board has 4 corners. Each corner can accommodate at most one domino? Let's see: corner cell (1,1) can be part of at most one domino. In a corner, a domino can be oriented horizontally covering (1,1)-(1,2) or vertically covering (1,1)-(2,1). So each corner can host at most one domino. So we can have up to 4 corner dominos, each with 2 interior sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705184405,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705185394,"data":"       | Now remaining dominos may be placed on edges (non-corner) to have 3 interior sides each. Edge cells count: total edge cells = perimeter = 7*4 - 4 = 24 (since corners counted twice). Edge non-corner cells = 24 - 4 = 20. Each edge cell can be part of at most one domino. Edge dominos can be oriented along edge (parallel) or perpendicular? Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705186918,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705188438,"data":"       | If we place a domino along edge (parallel to edge) such that both cells are on edge, e.g., horizontal domino on top row covering columns 3-4: both cells are edge cells (top edge). This domino has up side edge, left interior, right interior, down interior =\u003e 3 interior sides. If we place a vertical domino on left edge covering rows 3-4 column 1: both cells edge; left side edge, up interior, down interior, right interior =\u003e 3 interior sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70519018,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705191167,"data":"       | If we place a domino with one cell on edge and the other interior (perpendicular orientation), e.g., vertical domino covering (1,3)-(2,3) where top cell on top edge, bottom interior. Then up side edge, left interior (for both cells?), need to compute interior sides: For vertical domino, up neighbor of top cell (outside) edge; down neighbor of bottom cell interior; left neighbor condition: at least one of left cells of both squares occupied; right neighbor condition: at least one of right cells occupied. So up side edge, down interior, left interior, right interior =\u003e 3 interior sides as well. So any domino with at least one cell on edge will have at most 3 interior sides (maybe 2 if both cells on edge and also at corner? Already considered). So edge dominos have at most 3 interior sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705193431,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705194404,"data":"       | Thus to minimize total interior side requirements, we should maximize number of corner dominos (2 interior sides each) and then edge dominos (3 interior sides each) before using interior dominos (4 interior sides each).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70519575,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705196709,"data":"       | Now we also need to consider that neighbor cells used to block sides must be occupied, which may be other dominos. So we need enough occupied cells to satisfy all interior side requirements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705198005,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705198943,"data":"       | Let’s denote total interior side requirements R = sum_i interior_sides_i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705200045,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705201345,"data":"       | We have inequality: R ≤ 8m (since each occupied cell can serve at most 4 side requirements). Actually each occupied cell can be neighbor for up to 4 sides (one per adjacent direction). But note that a side requirement is satisfied by a specific neighbor cell (the cell adjacent to the domino in that direction). So each occupied cell can satisfy at most one side requirement per adjacent domino direction. However a cell could be neighbor to multiple dominos on same side? For example, a cell could be to the right of a horizontal domino and also above a vertical domino, satisfying two different side requirements (right slide of one domino, up slide of another). So each occupied cell can satisfy up to 4 side requirements (one for each direction). So the bound R ≤ 4 * (number of occupied cells) = 8m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705204954,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705205945,"data":"       | Thus we have R ≤ 8m always true. But we can also get lower bound: each occupied cell can satisfy at most 4 side requirements, so to satisfy R we need at least ceil(R/4) occupied cells. Since occupied cells = 2m, we have 2m ≥ ceil(R/4) =\u003e m ≥ ceil(R/8). But R depends on placement.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705207484,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705208435,"data":"       | Thus we can try to find minimal m such that there exists placement with R ≤ 8m (trivial) and also arrangement possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705209636,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70521059,"data":"       | But we can also derive lower bound on m based on board geometry: each domino interior side must be adjacent to another occupied cell, which itself belongs to a domino. So each interior side corresponds to an adjacency between two occupied cells belonging to different dominos (or possibly same domino? Not possible). So each interior side creates an adjacency edge between two dominos (or between a domino and itself? Not). So we can think of a graph where vertices are dominos, edges represent adjacency where one domino's side is blocked by a cell belonging to another domino. Each interior side corresponds to a directed adjacency (since side belongs to a specific domino). However a single occupied cell can block multiple sides of possibly different dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705212724,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705213675,"data":"       | But maybe we can compute minimal m via covering arguments.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705214771,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705215884,"data":"       | Another approach: Use computational search with ILP using `pyscipopt`? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705217009,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705217973,"data":"       | Maybe we can implement a simple SAT solver using python's `itertools` for small m. Since we suspect minimal m maybe around 7-10, we can try to search for m from 1 upward using backtracking with pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705219293,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705220443,"data":"       | Let's attempt to implement a recursive search that picks dominos one by one, maintaining occupied cells set, and also a list of placed dominos with their orientation and positions. At each step, we can also compute for each placed domino which side requirements are already satisfied (by current occupied cells) and which remain unsatisfied. For unsatisfied sides, we need to ensure there remains at least one potential cell that could be occupied later (i.e., not already occupied and not impossible due to board constraints). If no such cell exists, prune.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705222316,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705223266,"data":"       | Potential cells for a side are the neighbor cells (two cells for up/down for horizontal domino, etc.) that are inside board and not currently occupied. If both neighbor cells are already occupied? Actually if at least one is occupied, side satisfied. If both are empty but could be occupied later (i.e., not already blocked by being impossible), then still possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705225958,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705226956,"data":"       | Thus we can prune when for some placed domino, for some direction, both neighbor cells are either outside board (already satisfied) or occupied (satisfied) or empty but cannot be occupied because they are not part of any possible domino placement due to overlapping constraints? However they could become occupied later if we place a domino covering that cell, but we must ensure that we can still place a domino covering that cell without overlapping existing occupied cells. So we need to check if there exists at least one placement (domino) that includes that cell and does not overlap existing occupied cells. If not, then that cell cannot be occupied later, thus side cannot be satisfied -\u003e prune.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705229123,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705230074,"data":"       | Thus we need ability to query for each empty cell whether there exists any domino placement covering it that does not intersect occupied cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705231295,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705232246,"data":"       | We can precompute list of placements for each cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705233321,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70523431,"data":"       | Implementation steps:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705235306,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705236254,"data":"       | - Represent board cells as (r,c) with 0-index 0..6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705237318,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705238263,"data":"       | - Precompute all possible domino placements: each placement is tuple of two cells (cell1, cell2). Also orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705239712,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705241416,"data":"       | - For each cell, list placements that include that cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705243195,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70524422,"data":"       | - Recursive function search(current_set_of_placements, occupied_cells_set). Parameter target m (max placements allowed). We'll try to fill up to m placements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70524546,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705246423,"data":"       | - At each recursion, we can check constraints for all placed dominos: for each domino p, compute unsatisfied directions. For each unsatisfied direction, compute neighbor cells set N (size 2). For each neighbor cell n in N, if n is outside board -\u003e satisfied; else if n in occupied_cells_set -\u003e satisfied; else if there exists at least one placement covering n that does not intersect occupied_cells_set (i.e., can be placed later) -\u003e still possible; else unsatisfiable -\u003e prune.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705248255,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705249207,"data":"       | - Also we need to ensure that we can still place enough dominos to reach target m (i.e., we have enough remaining empty cells to place remaining dominos). But we can just continue recursion until we have placed m dominos and then check final constraints (all satisfied). If we find a solution, we stop.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705250668,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705251615,"data":"       | - Use ordering: choose next placement from list of possible placements that do not overlap occupied cells. Could use heuristic: choose a cell with few possible placements (minimum remaining) to branch.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705252919,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705255307,"data":"       | - Since we are searching for minimal m, we start with m=1,2,... until find solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705256449,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705257428,"data":"       | Potential complexity: Branching factor large. But for small m maybe manageable.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705258532,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705259501,"data":"       | Let's attempt to implement search for m up to maybe 8 or 9.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705260579,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705261558,"data":"       | We need to also consider that placements are unordered; we can avoid symmetries by ordering placements by index to avoid permutations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705262734,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705263672,"data":"       | Implementation details:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705264654,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705265615,"data":"       | - Represent placement as integer index 0..83.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705266684,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705267863,"data":"       | - Precompute for each placement: cells (c1,c2), orientation (horizontal/vertical), neighbor cells for each direction (list of neighbor cells sets). For each direction, we store list of neighbor cells (size 2) that need to be occupied/outside.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705269236,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705270185,"data":"       | Define directions: 'L','R','U','D'.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705271201,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705272183,"data":"       | For horizontal domino covering (r,c) left cell and (r,c+1) right cell:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705273262,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705274204,"data":"       | - Left neighbor: cell (r,c-1) (single cell). Actually only left neighbor of left cell matters; right neighbor of right cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705275381,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705276351,"data":"       | But the condition for sliding left requires left neighbor of left cell to be occupied/outside. That's a single cell. Similarly for sliding right: right neighbor of right cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705277613,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705278556,"data":"       | For up slide: neighbor cells are (r-1,c) and (r-1,c+1). For down slide: (r+1,c) and (r+1,c+1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705279671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705280632,"data":"       | Thus for horizontal domino, left and right each have single neighbor cell; up/down each have two neighbor cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705281782,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705282721,"data":"       | For vertical domino covering (r,c) top cell and (r+1,c) bottom cell:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705283844,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705284784,"data":"       | - Up neighbor: (r-1,c) (single)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705285796,"data":"       | - Down neighbor: (r+2,c) (single)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705286826,"data":"       | - Left neighbor: (r,c-1) and (r+1,c-1) (two)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70528786,"data":"       | - Right neighbor: (r,c+1) and (r+1,c+1) (two)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705290101,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705291125,"data":"       | Thus we can store for each direction a list of neighbor cells (size 1 or 2). For each direction, if any neighbor cell is outside board, then direction automatically satisfied. So we can treat that as satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705292464,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705293443,"data":"       | Now implement search.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705294443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70529541,"data":"       | We also need to ensure that each domino's own cells are occupied (by definition). So neighbor cells may be part of same domino? Not possible because neighbor cells are distinct from domino cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70529671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705297657,"data":"       | Now implement search for minimal m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705298724,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705299669,"data":"       | We'll use recursion with depth-first search, with early exit when solution found.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705300773,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705301781,"data":"       | Potential pruning: If at any point, for any placed domino, there exists a direction where all neighbor cells are empty and cannot be occupied later (i.e., each neighbor cell is currently empty but all placements covering that cell intersect occupied cells), then prune.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705303197,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705304385,"data":"       | To check if a cell can be occupied later, we need to see if there exists any placement (domino) that includes that cell and does not intersect occupied cells. Since we haven't placed that domino yet, we can still place it later as long as its other cell is also empty (not occupied). So we need to check if there exists any placement p' such that cell in p' and both cells of p' are not in occupied_cells_set (i.e., free). That's simple.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70530606,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70530702,"data":"       | Thus we can precompute for each cell list of placements that include it.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705308117,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70530908,"data":"       | Now implement search.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705310101,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705311087,"data":"       | Potential issue: recursion depth may be high but m small.\n"}
