,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705515317,"data":"       | - At each step, we may compute unsatisfied directions for each chosen placement; for each unsatisfied direction, we need to ensure there exists at least one neighbor cell that can be occupied later (i.e., there is a placement covering that cell that does not intersect occupied cells). If none, prune.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705516741,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705517724,"data":"       | - Additionally, we need to ensure that we can still place remaining dominos (i.e., there are enough free cells). That's simple: remaining free cells count \u003e= 2 * (remaining dominos to place). We'll compute free_cells = total_cells - len(occupied). If free_cells \u003c 2*remaining, prune.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705519211,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705520237,"data":"       | - To choose next placement, we can iterate over possible placements with index greater than last chosen to avoid permutations (ensuring order). But we also need to consider placements that don't overlap occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705521555,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.705522533,"data":"       | - Use heuristic: choose a free cell with few possible placements (minimum) and branch on those placements covering that cell. This reduces branching.\n"}
