,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.706385588,"data":"       | But we can also consider that each domino's own cells cannot serve as neighbor for its own sides (except maybe for up/down for horizontal where two neighbor cells could be the other cell? No). So each interior side requires a distinct neighbor cell? Not necessarily distinct; a neighbor cell could serve as neighbor for multiple sides of the same domino? For a horizontal domino, up side has two neighbor cells; only one needs to be occupied. That occupied cell could be the same as the occupied cell used for down side? Possibly if the same cell is both above left and below right? Not possible because they are different coordinates. So each interior side uses a distinct neighbor cell (or maybe same cell could satisfy both left and up? For horizontal domino, left side neighbor is (r,c-1). Up side neighbor could be (r-1,c) or (r-1,c+1). So they are distinct coordinates. So each interior side uses a distinct neighbor cell (maybe overlapping across different dominos). So each interior side corresponds to a unique cell (the neighbor cell) that must be occupied. However a cell could be neighbor for up to 4 different sides from different dominos (as earlier). So counting sides per cell is okay.\n"}
