,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707010869,"data":"     [Reasoning] (17256 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707013092,"data":"       | analysisGreat.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707014193,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707015265,"data":"       | Now we need to be sure that no solution with 23 dominos exists. Our search suggests none, but we need stronger proof. Could attempt to use integer programming with pulp? Not available.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707016638,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707017658,"data":"       | But we can attempt to prove lower bound using counting arguments.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707018827,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707019899,"data":"       | Observation: For each domino, at least two of its four sides are interior (i.e., not on board edge). Because a domino occupies two adjacent squares; the only way to have only one interior side is if both sides are on edges? Let's examine possibilities:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707021362,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707022403,"data":"       | - Corner domino: two sides are edges (the two outward sides), two sides interior (the side opposite edge and the perpendicular side). Wait earlier we said corner domino has 2 interior sides. Indeed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707023773,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707024813,"data":"       | - Edge (non-corner) domino: one side is edge, three interior sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707026001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707027057,"data":"       | - Interior domino: four interior sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707028167,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707029202,"data":"       | Thus each domino contributes at least 2 interior sides. So total interior sides R \u003e= 2m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707030463,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707031713,"data":"       | Now each interior side must be adjacent to an occupied cell (neighbor). Each occupied cell can be adjacent to at most 4 interior sides (since each cell has 4 neighboring positions). So we have R ≤ 4 * (2m) = 8m. Not helpful.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707033208,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707034247,"data":"       | But we can also bound number of interior sides that can be satisfied by board edges? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707035438,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707036455,"data":"       | Maybe we can use parity or coloring arguments.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707037597,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707038632,"data":"       | Consider checkerboard coloring (alternating black/white). A domino always covers one black and one white cell. Sliding a domino horizontally or vertically would move it to two cells of opposite colors? Actually sliding by one cell changes colors: a domino covering black-white moves to white-black (swap). So sliding changes orientation relative to colors but not relevant.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707040272,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7070413,"data":"       | Maybe we can use graph theory: Represent board cells as vertices; edges between orthogonal neighbors. Dominos are edges selected. The condition that a domino cannot slide horizontally means that the edge cannot be moved to adjacent parallel edge. This is similar to \"matching is rigid\" concept.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707042914,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707045394,"data":"       | In graph terms, a domino corresponds to an edge of the grid graph. Sliding horizontally corresponds to moving the edge to adjacent parallel edge sharing a vertex? Actually moving a horizontal domino left corresponds to shifting both vertices left by one, which corresponds to replacing edge (r,c)-(r,c+1) with edge (r,c-1)-(r,c). This is possible if the vertex (r,c-1) is free and edge (r,c-1)-(r,c) not occupied. So the condition that no slide possible means that for each edge e in matching, the adjacent parallel edges sharing one endpoint are either outside board or already occupied (i.e., also in matching). Similarly for vertical slides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707047505,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707048523,"data":"       | Thus we need a matching (set of edges) such that for each edge, the adjacent parallel edges sharing each endpoint are either outside board or also in the matching.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707049869,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707050875,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of \"maximal matching\" but stronger: not only cannot add an edge, but cannot replace an edge with adjacent parallel edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707052202,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707053442,"data":"       | In a grid graph, a maximal matching ensures that there is no free edge whose both endpoints are free. But our condition is about shifting an existing edge to adjacent parallel edge; that would involve freeing one endpoint and occupying a new one. This is similar to \"matching is a dominating set of edges\"? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707055092,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707056165,"data":"       | But we can think of each vertex (cell) being either matched (occupied) or unmatched (empty). For a horizontal edge (v,w) where w = v + (0,1). The left slide would replace edge (v,w) with edge (v- (0,1), v) i.e., (v_left, v). This requires that vertex v_left is free (unmatched) and edge (v_left, v) not in matching. So to prevent slide left, we need either v_left outside board or v_left matched (i.e., occupied). Similarly for right slide: need right neighbor of w matched or outside.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707058012,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707059029,"data":"       | Thus condition translates to: For each matched vertex v (part of a domino), its neighbor in direction of slide must be matched (or outside). But note that for a horizontal domino, left slide condition only involves left neighbor of left endpoint, not right endpoint. So each endpoint's neighbor in direction opposite to orientation must be matched/outside.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707060647,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707061662,"data":"       | Thus we can view each matched vertex must have its neighbor in the direction opposite to its incident edge matched/outside.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70706291,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707063933,"data":"       | Now consider the graph of matched vertices (occupied cells). For each domino (edge), the two vertices have the following constraints:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707065197,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707066219,"data":"       | - For the left vertex (if domino horizontal), its left neighbor must be matched/outside.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707067395,"data":"       | - For the right vertex, its right neighbor must be matched/outside.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707068539,"data":"       | - For both vertices, at least one of the two upward neighbors must be matched/outside (to block up slide). Similarly for down.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707071343,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707072377,"data":"       | Thus each occupied vertex may need its neighbor(s) in certain directions to be matched.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707073574,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707074593,"data":"       | This suggests that the set of matched vertices must be \"edge dominating\" in each direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707075884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707077237,"data":"       | Maybe we can prove that at least 48 vertices must be matched.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707078369,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707079408,"data":"       | Consider the four corners: each corner cell has only two neighbors (right and down for top-left). If a corner cell is occupied (part of a domino), then its neighbor opposite direction (outside) is automatically satisfied. But the other neighbor(s) may need to be occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707080883,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707081928,"data":"       | But maybe we can prove that any unmatched cell (empty) must be isolated such that it cannot serve as neighbor for any interior side. Since each interior side requires a neighbor occupied, empty cells cannot be adjacent to interior sides. But they could be adjacent to edges that are already satisfied by board edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707083505,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707084518,"data":"       | Thus empty cells must be placed such that they are not needed as neighbor for any interior side. That may restrict them to be in positions where all adjacent sides are already satisfied by board edges or other occupied cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707085929,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707086978,"data":"       | Let's attempt to reason: Suppose there is an empty cell e. Consider any domino d that is adjacent to e in a direction that would block slide for d. For d to be blocked, at least one of the two neighbor cells in that direction must be occupied. If e is empty, then the other neighbor cell must be occupied. That's okay.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707088535,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707089569,"data":"       | Thus empty cells can exist as long as for each direction where they are the only neighbor for a domino, the other neighbor is occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70709084,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707091872,"data":"       | Thus maybe we can have many empty cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707092971,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707094034,"data":"       | But our search suggests only one empty cell possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707095945,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70709698,"data":"       | Let's test by trying to find a solution with 23 dominos but leaving two empty cells (i.e., 46 occupied cells). Our search didn't find any.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707098252,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707099265,"data":"       | Maybe we can try to use SAT solver via python's `python-sat` library? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707100442,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707101458,"data":"       | But we can attempt to use integer programming via `pyscipopt`? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707102618,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70710471,"data":"       | Alternatively, we could attempt to prove lower bound via counting of \"exposed sides\".\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707106044,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707107432,"data":"       | Define for each occupied cell, count number of \"free sides\" where neighbor is empty (and inside board). For a configuration where no domino can slide, each domino must have at most one free side per direction? Actually sliding condition requires that for each direction, at least one of the two neighbor cells is occupied/outside. So for a horizontal domino, up direction has two neighbor cells; both could be empty, but then slide up possible. So at least one must be occupied. So for each direction, there can be at most one empty neighbor among the two (for up/down) and for left/right there is exactly one neighbor; it must be occupied/outside. So left neighbor must be occupied/outside, similarly right neighbor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707109687,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707110787,"data":"       | Thus for a horizontal domino, left neighbor cell must be occupied/outside; right neighbor must be occupied/outside. So each horizontal domino forces its left and right adjacent cells to be occupied/outside. So each horizontal domino forces two specific cells (its left neighbor and right neighbor) to be occupied/outside. Similarly vertical domino forces its up and down neighbor cells to be occupied/outside.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707112514,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707113546,"data":"       | Thus each domino forces at least two cells (its outward neighbors) to be occupied/outside. Since board edges provide some of these automatically, the number of forced occupied cells may be high.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707114898,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707115917,"data":"       | Let's count forced neighbor cells for all dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707117039,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707118138,"data":"       | Consider a placement of m dominos. For each domino, we have two outward neighbor cells (the cells adjacent to its ends along its orientation). Let's call them \"blocking cells\". They must be occupied or outside.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707119622,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707120647,"data":"       | If a neighbor cell is outside, it's satisfied. If inside, that cell must be occupied (i.e., part of some domino). So each interior outward neighbor cell must be occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707121966,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707122991,"data":"       | Thus we can count the number of interior outward neighbor cells required to be occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707124186,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707125222,"data":"       | Now note that a cell can serve as outward neighbor for at most two dominos? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707126408,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70712779,"data":"       | A cell can be right neighbor of a horizontal domino whose rightmost cell is left of it. Also it can be left neighbor of a horizontal domino whose leftmost cell is right of it. So a cell could be outward neighbor for up to two horizontal dominos (one on its left, one on its right). Similarly for vertical orientation: a cell can be up neighbor of a vertical domino below it, and down neighbor of a vertical domino above it. So a cell could be outward neighbor for up to two vertical dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707129624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707133114,"data":"       | Thus a cell could be outward neighbor for up to 4 dominos total (two horizontal, two vertical). However note that if a cell is occupied, it is part of a domino itself, which may also have its own outward neighbor requirements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707134552,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707135569,"data":"       | Thus we can attempt to bound number of interior outward neighbor cells needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707136733,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707137774,"data":"       | Let’s denote O = set of occupied cells (size 2m). For each domino, consider its outward neighbor cells that are inside board. Let count total required interior outward neighbor cells (with multiplicity) = R_out.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707139225,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707140313,"data":"       | We have R_out = sum over dominos of number of outward neighbor cells that are inside board (i.e., not outside). For a corner domino, both outward neighbors are inside? Let's examine corner horizontal domino at top-left covering (0,0)-(0,1). Left neighbor is outside (c=-1) =\u003e satisfied; right neighbor is (0,2) inside. So only one interior outward neighbor. Similarly vertical corner domino at top-left covering (0,0)-(1,0): up neighbor outside, down neighbor (2,0) inside =\u003e one interior outward neighbor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70714227,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707143515,"data":"       | Thus corner dominos require only 1 interior outward neighbor (the side opposite the edge). Edge dominos not at corner: they have one outward neighbor inside (the side opposite the edge) and the other outward neighbor inside (since the edge side is outside). Wait for a horizontal domino on top edge but not corner, covering (0,c)-(0,c+1) where c\u003e=1 and c+1 \u003c=5. Left neighbor (0,c-1) inside, right neighbor (0,c+2) inside. Both are interior (since top edge is up direction, not outward). Actually outward neighbors are left and right (orientation). Since top edge is not along orientation, both left and right are interior cells (unless at corners). So edge horizontal domino on top row has 2 interior outward neighbors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70714581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707146823,"data":"       | Similarly vertical domino on left edge has 2 interior outward neighbors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707148008,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707149037,"data":"       | Thus interior dominos have 2 interior outward neighbors (both sides interior). So each domino contributes either 1 or 2 interior outward neighbor cells depending on whether it touches board edge on one side.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70715043,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707151493,"data":"       | Thus total interior outward neighbor cells required (with multiplicity) R_out = 2m - a, where a = number of dominos that have one outward neighbor outside (i.e., dominos placed with one end on board edge). Actually each such domino reduces required interior outward neighbor count by 1 compared to interior domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707153055,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707154087,"data":"       | Corner dominos have both outward neighbors? Wait corner domino has one outward neighbor outside (the side outward from board) and the other interior. So they reduce count by 1 as well.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707155425,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70715649,"data":"       | Thus a = number of dominos that have at least one outward neighbor outside. Let's denote a = number of dominos that are placed with at least one of its outward neighbor cells outside board. This includes all dominos that touch board edge (including corners). For each such domino, required interior outward neighbor cells = 1 (if one side outside) or 0 (if both sides outside? Not possible because domino length 2 cannot have both outward sides outside unless board size 1? Not). So each edge-touching domino reduces count by 1 relative to interior domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70715993,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70716097,"data":"       | Thus R_out = 2m - a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707162046,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707163273,"data":"       | Now each interior outward neighbor cell must be occupied (i.e., belong to O). So O must contain at least R_out distinct cells? Not necessarily distinct because a cell can serve as outward neighbor for multiple dominos. As earlier, a cell can be outward neighbor for up to 4 dominos (2 horizontal, 2 vertical). So we have inequality: R_out ≤ 4 * |O| = 8m. That's trivial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707164965,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707165986,"data":"       | But we can also bound a maximum a given board size: maximum number of dominos that can touch board edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70716719,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707168246,"data":"       | Edge cells count = 24. Each domino that touches edge uses at least one edge cell. Edge dominos could use two edge cells (if both cells on edge). So maximum number of edge-touching dominos is at most floor(24/1) = 24 (if each uses one edge cell). But they cannot exceed total dominos m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707169752,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707170767,"data":"       | Thus a ≤ min(m, 24). For m small, a could be up to m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707171922,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707172971,"data":"       | Thus R_out = 2m - a ≥ 2m - min(m,24) = if m ≤ 24 then R_out ≥ m. So at least m interior outward neighbor cells required.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707174297,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707175313,"data":"       | Thus we need at least m occupied cells to serve as outward neighbor cells. Since total occupied cells = 2m, this is not restrictive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707176631,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707177665,"data":"       | But we also have up/down blocking constraints: each domino also needs at least one occupied neighbor among the two cells above (or below). Those are \"vertical blocking cells\". Similar counting may give stronger bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707179133,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707180197,"data":"       | Consider for each domino, for each of the two orthogonal directions (perpendicular to orientation), we need at least one occupied neighbor among the two cells. So each domino requires at least 2 such orthogonal neighbor cells (one for up, one for down for horizontal; left/right for vertical). However each such neighbor may be shared among multiple dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707181794,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70718282,"data":"       | Thus total orthogonal neighbor requirements = 2m (since each domino needs up and down each). However if a domino is on top edge, up direction satisfied by outside, reducing requirement by 1. Similarly bottom edge reduces requirement.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707184275,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707186671,"data":"       | Thus total orthogonal interior neighbor requirements = 2m - (number of dominos touching top edge) - (number touching bottom edge) for horizontal dominos; similarly for vertical dominos left/right edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707188085,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707189386,"data":"       | Thus overall orthogonal interior neighbor count = 2m - e_top - e_bottom - e_left - e_right, where e_top is number of horizontal dominos with top edge (i.e., placed on top row), etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707190747,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707191782,"data":"       | Edge counts limited.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707192873,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707193923,"data":"       | But maybe we can combine both constraints to get lower bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707195088,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707196117,"data":"       | Another approach: Use linear programming to compute minimal m. Could implement ILP using `pyscipopt`? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707197344,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707198389,"data":"       | But we can attempt to use brute force search for m up to 23 with more thorough algorithm, maybe using BFS with bitmask representation and memoization.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707199678,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707200722,"data":"       | Given board size 49, we can represent occupied cells as 49-bit integer. For each placement, we can precompute bitmask of its two cells. Then we can search over subsets of placements using recursion with bitmask.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707202107,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707203144,"data":"       | Our earlier search used sets; we can use bitmask for speed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707204281,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707205326,"data":"       | Let's implement a DFS that enumerates placements in order, using pruning as before but with bitmask.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70720652,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707207568,"data":"       | We'll also incorporate memoization: for a given occupied mask and number of placed dominos, we can store visited to avoid recomputation. However state space may be large.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70720888,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707209911,"data":"       | But we can attempt for m=23.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707211008,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707212078,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codefrom functools import lru_cache\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707213224,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707214261,"data":"       | # Precompute bitmask for each placement\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707215375,"data":"       | def cell_to_bit(cell):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70721645,"data":"       |     r,c = cell\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70721753,"data":"       |     return 1 \u003c\u003c (r*7 + c)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707218689,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707219698,"data":"       | placement_mask = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70722078,"data":"       | for p in placements:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707221855,"data":"       |     mask = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707225141,"data":"       |     for cell in p:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707226243,"data":"       |         mask |= cell_to_bit(cell)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707227349,"data":"       |     placement_mask.append(mask)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707228432,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707229442,"data":"       | # Precompute neighbor masks for each placement and direction (list of cells)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707230634,"data":"       | neighbor_masks = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707231707,"data":"       | for nd in neighbor_dict:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707232788,"data":"       |     dir_masks = {}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707233854,"data":"       |     for d, cells in nd.items():\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707234935,"data":"       |         mask = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707236008,"data":"       |         for cell in cells:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707237078,"data":"       |             r,c = cell\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707238144,"data":"       |             if 0 \u003c= r \u003c N and 0 \u003c= c \u003c N:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707239865,"data":"       |                 mask |= cell_to_bit(cell)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70724172,"data":"       |         dir_masks[d] = mask\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707243257,"data":"       |     neighbor_masks.append(dir_masks)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707244359,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707245385,"data":"       | # For each cell, list placements covering it (already have cell_to_placements)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707246571,"data":"       | # We'll also precompute for each cell a mask of placements that include it? Not needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707247783,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707248791,"data":"       | # Function to check if a placement can slide given occupied mask\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70724995,"data":"       | def can_slide_mask(pi, occ_mask):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707251053,"data":"       |     # Determine orientation\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707252125,"data":"       |     (r1,c1),(r2,c2) = placements[pi]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707253233,"data":"       |     dirs = {'L': (0,-1), 'R': (0,1), 'U': (-1,0), 'D': (1,0)}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707254393,"data":"       |     for dr,dc in dirs.values():\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70725547,"data":"       |         # shift both cells\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707256537,"data":"       |         new_cells = [(r1+dr, c1+dc), (r2+dr, c2+dc)]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707257678,"data":"       |         # check inside board\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707259114,"data":"       |         if any(not (0 \u003c= r \u003c N and 0 \u003c= c \u003c N) for r,c in new_cells):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707260365,"data":"       |             continue\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707261407,"data":"       |         new_mask = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707262455,"data":"       |         for r,c in new_cells:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70726355,"data":"       |             new_mask |= cell_to_bit((r,c))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707264668,"data":"       |         # slide possible if new_mask has no overlap with occ_mask\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707265826,"data":"       |         if new_mask \u0026 occ_mask == 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707269098,"data":"       |             return True\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707270176,"data":"       |     return False\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707271265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70727226,"data":"       | def verify_mask(sol_mask, sol_list):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707273339,"data":"       |     # sol_mask is occupied mask\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707274435,"data":"       |     for pi in sol_list:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707275492,"data":"       |         if can_slide_mask(pi, sol_mask):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707276575,"data":"       |             return False\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707277652,"data":"       |     return True\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707278726,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707279746,"data":"       | # Let's verify our 24 solution\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707280821,"data":"       | sol24_mask = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707281889,"data":"       | for pi in sol24:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707282954,"data":"       |     sol24_mask |= placement_mask[pi]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707284062,"data":"       | print(\"verify 24 mask:\", verify_mask(sol24_mask, sol24))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707285282,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707286284,"data":"     [Code]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707287323,"data":"       | from functools import lru_cache\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707288417,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707289453,"data":"       | # Precompute bitmask for each placement\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707290538,"data":"       | def cell_to_bit(cell):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707291593,"data":"       |     r,c = cell\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707292628,"data":"       |     return 1 \u003c\u003c (r*7 + c)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707293755,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707294773,"data":"       | placement_mask = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707295834,"data":"       | for p in placements:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707296899,"data":"       |     mask = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707297945,"data":"       |     for cell in p:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707299,"data":"       |         mask |= cell_to_bit(cell)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707300093,"data":"       |     placement_mask.append(mask)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707301203,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707302604,"data":"       | # Precompute neighbor masks for each placement and direction (list of cells)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707303762,"data":"       | neighbor_masks = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70730483,"data":"       | for nd in neighbor_dict:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707305925,"data":"       |     dir_masks = {}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707306998,"data":"       |     for d, cells in nd.items():\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70730809,"data":"       |         mask = 0\n"}
