,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707446295,"data":"       | Interpretation: We have a 7x7 board (odd dimensions). We place m dominoes (size 1x2). They occupy 2 cells each, presumably axis-aligned (horizontal or vertical). The condition: No domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remain fixed. Means each domino is \"blocked\" from moving in its orientation direction (horizontal slide for horizontal domino, vertical slide for vertical domino) because adjacent cells are occupied or board edge? Actually sliding horizontally: a horizontal domino could slide left or right by one unit (i.e., shift one column) while staying within board and not overlapping other dominos. The condition says that for each domino, there is no possible slide horizontally (if domino is horizontal) or vertically (if vertical) while other dominos remain fixed. Wait they say \"no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remains fixed.\" Could mean any domino cannot slide in either direction (horizontal or vertical) while others fixed. So each domino is \"locked\" in both directions? But a domino can only slide along its longer axis? Actually a 1x2 domino can slide both horizontally and vertically? If you slide a horizontal domino vertically, you would need to rotate? Usually sliding means translation without rotation. A horizontal domino can slide up or down (vertical translation) as a whole shape; but after translation, its orientation remains horizontal, occupying two cells in same row but different columns. So sliding vertically would move it to a different row, same columns. Similarly a vertical domino can slide left/right (horizontal translation). So the condition \"no domino can slide horizontally or vertically\" means for each domino, there is no empty adjacent cell in the direction of translation that would allow moving the domino one unit while staying within board and not overlapping other dominos. So each domino must be \"blocked\" on both sides in its translation directions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707451884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707453147,"data":"       | Thus we need to place dominoes such that each domino is \"blocked\" from moving left/right/up/down. For a horizontal domino occupying cells (r,c) and (r,c+1). To slide left, need cell (r,c-1) empty and also (r,c) becomes occupied after shift? Actually sliding left by one unit would move domino to cells (r,c-1) and (r,c). So need cell (r,c-1) empty (and cell (r,c+1) becomes empty after shift). Similarly sliding right requires cell (r,c+2) empty. For vertical domino occupying (r,c) and (r+1,c). To slide up need cell (r-1,c) empty; slide down need cell (r+2,c) empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707456333,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707457385,"data":"       | Thus each domino must have both adjacent cells in its translation direction occupied or out of board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7074586,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707459659,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominoes needed to achieve this property for all placed dominoes. We can leave empty cells (uncovered). We need to find minimal m such that there exists a placement of m dominoes satisfying blocking condition.\n"}
