,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707606835,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707607864,"data":"       | We could implement a simple branch-and-bound search using recursion and pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707609043,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70761007,"data":"       | Since board is 7x7, total cells 49. Domino placements 84.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707611207,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707612262,"data":"       | We can try to search for minimal m using BFS: start with empty set, add dominoes gradually, maintain occupancy mask (bitmask of 49 cells). Use recursion to add dominoes; after each addition, we can compute which dominoes are \"blocked\" (i.e., have all side cells occupied or out of board). But we need final condition that all placed dominoes are blocked. So we can continue adding until condition satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707614029,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707615034,"data":"       | Goal: find smallest number of dominoes that can be placed such that after placement, each domino's side cells are occupied (by other dominoes) or out of board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707616336,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707617337,"data":"       | Thus we can treat as building a set that is \"self-blocking\". Could be solved via search for minimal size.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707618618,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707619627,"data":"       | We can use BFS over subsets increasing size; but there are many subsets.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707620793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707622033,"data":"       | But we can prune heavily: each domino's side cells must be covered, so we can think of covering requirement: For each domino placed, its side cells must be in the occupied set (by other dominoes). So the occupied set must be a superset of the side cells of each domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707623539,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707624578,"data":"       | Thus we can think of constructing a set of dominoes such that the occupied cells (union) is closed under side adjacency: For any domino in the set, its side cells are also occupied (by some domino). So the occupied cells set must be \"side-closed\".\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707626115,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707627117,"data":"       | Thus we can think of building a set of occupied cells that is a union of dominoes and is side-closed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707628318,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70762933,"data":"       | Thus we can search over subsets of cells (occupied cells) that can be tiled by dominoes (i.e., partition into dominoes) and are side-closed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707630615,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70763166,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominoes = |occupied cells|/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707632783,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707633798,"data":"       | Thus we need to find minimal number of occupied cells (even) such that there exists a domino tiling of those cells and the side-closure property holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70763664,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70763769,"data":"       | Thus we can search over subsets of cells of size 2k, for k small, that are tileable and side-closed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707638902,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707639942,"data":"       | We can attempt to enumerate subsets of cells of size up to maybe 10 (i.e., k up to 5). Number of subsets of 49 cells of size 10 is C(49,10) ~ 2.5e9 huge. Not feasible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707641275,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707642317,"data":"       | But we can use constraint programming.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707643414,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707644415,"data":"       | Maybe we can reason analytically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707645513,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707646528,"data":"       | Observation: For a domino placed at interior (not touching any edge), it needs two side cells occupied. Those side cells are adjacent to its ends. Those side cells could be part of other dominoes that may also be interior, requiring further side cells. This can lead to chain.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70764802,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707649041,"data":"       | Potential minimal configuration may involve a \"cycle\" of dominoes where each domino's side cells are occupied by neighboring dominoes, forming a closed loop. For example, a 2x2 block of four cells can be tiled by two dominoes (horizontal and vertical) that block each other? Let's test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707650632,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707651845,"data":"       | Consider a 2x2 block: cells (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2). Place a horizontal domino covering (1,1)-(1,2). Its side cells: left (1,0) out of board? Actually column 0 out of board, but board is 7x7, so not edge. So left side cell (1,0) is outside board, not occupied. Right side cell (1,3) is empty (unless occupied). So horizontal domino at top row of block not at board edge, so needs both side cells occupied. So not blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7076536,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707654607,"data":"       | Thus need board edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707655675,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707656702,"data":"       | Thus maybe minimal configuration uses dominoes along the perimeter.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70765785,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707658898,"data":"       | Let's think about covering perimeter cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707659997,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707661025,"data":"       | Board 7x7 has perimeter cells: 7*4 - 4 = 24 cells (since corners counted twice). Actually perimeter cells count: 7*4 - 4 = 24. Indeed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707662297,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70766337,"data":"       | If we place dominoes along perimeter, each domino may be placed along edge, covering two adjacent perimeter cells. For a domino placed along edge (horizontal on top row, columns 1-2, etc.), one side is outside board (blocked), the other side is interior cell adjacent to the domino (needs to be occupied). So each edge domino requires one interior neighbor cell to be occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707665022,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707667176,"data":"       | Thus we could place dominoes along all edges, and then need to occupy interior cells adjacent to each edge domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707668423,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707669457,"data":"       | But we can perhaps use interior dominoes to cover multiple such neighbor cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70767063,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70767166,"data":"       | Goal: minimize total dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707672748,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707673854,"data":"       | Let's attempt to formulate as covering problem: Each domino placed somewhere covers two cells. For each domino, we need to \"cover\" its side cells (if interior). So we can think of each domino requiring \"support cells\". Edge dominoes require one support cell; interior dominoes require two support cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707675523,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707676537,"data":"       | Thus we can think of each support cell must be covered by some domino (maybe the same domino that needs it? Not possible). So each support cell is a cell that is adjacent (in the direction of translation) to some domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707677945,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707679122,"data":"       | Thus we need to select a set of dominoes such that the set of support cells is a subset of occupied cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707680342,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707681384,"data":"       | Thus we can think of a directed graph where each domino points to its side cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707682563,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707683568,"data":"       | Goal: find minimal number of dominoes such that the directed graph's support cells are all covered by dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707684798,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707685809,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of a \"feedback vertex set\" maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707687039,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707688051,"data":"       | But perhaps we can find a lower bound: each domino occupies 2 cells, but may require up to 2 support cells. So each domino may \"require\" up to 2 other cells to be occupied. However those cells may be shared among multiple dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707689531,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707690569,"data":"       | Thus we can bound minimal number by considering total number of required support cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707691739,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707692858,"data":"       | Let S be set of side cells that are interior (i.e., not out of board). For each domino, count its interior side cells (0,1,2). Sum over all dominoes of interior side cells = total required interior side cells (counting multiplicities). Each occupied cell can serve as side cell for up to how many dominoes? A cell can be side cell for up to 2 dominoes? Let's examine: a cell can be adjacent to at most two dominoes in the same direction? For a given cell, it could be the right side cell of a horizontal domino to its left, and also left side cell of a horizontal domino to its right. Also could be top side cell of a vertical domino below, and bottom side cell of a vertical domino above. So up to 4? But each cell can be side cell for up to 4 dominoes (two horizontal, two vertical). However those dominoes would occupy neighboring cells that might conflict with each other (overlap). But maybe possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707696565,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707697598,"data":"       | Thus lower bound not trivial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707698682,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707699697,"data":"       | Better to just compute via ILP using custom branch-and-bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70770084,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707701872,"data":"       | We can implement a simple MILP using `python-mip`? Not installed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707703042,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70770405,"data":"       | But we can implement a SAT solver using python's `itertools` and recursion with pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707705242,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707706431,"data":"       | Given small board, we can attempt to search for minimal m using BFS with pruning based on uncovered side cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707707661,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707708701,"data":"       | We can treat each domino as variable; we need to satisfy constraints:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70770987,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707710899,"data":"       | - Occupancy: each cell at most one domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707712002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707713026,"data":"       | - For each chosen domino d, for each side cell s (if inside board), there must be a domino covering s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707714245,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707715248,"data":"       | Thus we can think of building a set of dominoes such that for each domino, its side cells are covered.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707716466,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707717493,"data":"       | We can use recursion: maintain occupancy mask, and also maintain a set of \"unblocked\" dominoes (i.e., those whose side cells are already covered). At the end, all chosen dominoes must be blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707718923,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70771996,"data":"       | But we can also add dominoes that are not yet blocked, as long as we plan to block them later.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707721148,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70772217,"data":"       | Thus we need to search for a set that becomes fully blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707723312,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707724325,"data":"       | We can treat this as a fixed point: after we have a set of dominoes, we can compute which dominoes are blocked given the occupancy. If some domino is not blocked, we need to add more dominoes covering its missing side cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707725739,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707726756,"data":"       | Thus we can think of iterative process: start with some dominoes, then add dominoes to cover missing side cells, repeat until closure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707728008,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707729033,"data":"       | Thus we can search for minimal closure sets.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70773013,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707731175,"data":"       | Thus we can treat as: choose a set of dominoes, then compute closure: repeatedly add any domino that is required to cover missing side cells of existing dominoes, until no more needed. The final set may be larger than initial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707734008,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707735054,"data":"       | Goal: find minimal final size.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707736159,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707737211,"data":"       | Thus we can search over initial seeds (maybe small) and compute closure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707740148,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707741216,"data":"       | But we need to ensure that added dominoes themselves become blocked; they may introduce new missing side cells, requiring further dominoes, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707742485,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707743722,"data":"       | Thus closure algorithm: start with set D. While there exists a domino d in D such that some interior side cell s is not occupied by any domino in D, then we must add a domino covering s (choose one). This may add new dominoes, which may also have missing side cells, etc. This process may not converge uniquely because we have choices of which domino to add to cover s (multiple possibilities). So we need to explore branching.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707745494,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707746496,"data":"       | Thus we can treat as search tree.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707747583,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707748605,"data":"       | Given board size moderate, we can attempt to use BFS with pruning and limit depth.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707749769,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707750807,"data":"       | We can also use heuristic: we want minimal total dominoes, so we should try to cover multiple missing side cells with a single domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707752077,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70775311,"data":"       | Thus we can treat as hitting set: we need to cover all required side cells (the set of side cells of all dominoes) with dominoes (which each cover two cells). So we need to find a set of dominoes that covers a set of cells (the side cells) and also each domino's own side cells are covered.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707754618,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70775564,"data":"       | Thus we can think of a self-consistent set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707756739,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707757763,"data":"       | Maybe we can solve using integer programming via `z3`? Not installed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707758937,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707759967,"data":"       | Alternative: Use brute force enumeration of subsets of dominoes up to some small size, say up to 8 dominoes (16 cells). Number of subsets of size 8 from 84 is C(84,8) ~ 3e10 huge. Not feasible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707761315,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707762338,"data":"       | But we can use constraint programming with pruning: we can treat board cells as variables (occupied/unoccupied) and domino placements as covering pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707763648,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707764659,"data":"       | We can use backtracking to assign domino placements to cells gradually, ensuring that each placed domino's side cells are either occupied (by already placed dominoes) or will be occupied later (i.e., not yet placed but we can plan). This is similar to building a configuration.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707768187,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707769232,"data":"       | We can also use symmetry: board is symmetric under rotations and reflections; we can fix some placements to reduce search.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70777048,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70777152,"data":"       | Given difficulty, maybe minimal m is known: For odd board, minimal number of dominoes to lock them is 7? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707772758,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707774041,"data":"       | Consider placing dominoes along a \"ring\" around the board interior: For each edge cell, place a domino covering edge cell and adjacent interior cell, oriented outward? Actually domino must be axis-aligned; we could place vertical dominoes along left edge: covering cells (r,1)-(r+1,1) for r=1,3,5? That would occupy edge cells and interior cells column 1. Their side cells: left side out of board, right side cells (r,2) and (r+1,2) need to be occupied. Those interior column 2 cells could be covered by other dominoes placed vertically as well, perhaps forming a chain.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7077761,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707777117,"data":"       | Maybe we can design a pattern where dominoes occupy a \"checkerboard\" pattern such that each domino's side cells are occupied by other dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707778477,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707779498,"data":"       | Let's attempt to search computationally using MILP via `python` with `pyscipopt`? Not installed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707780691,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707781745,"data":"       | We could implement a simple branch-and-bound using `python` recursion with pruning using constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707782943,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707783966,"data":"       | Given board size 7x7, we can represent cells as indices 0..48.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70778511,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707786127,"data":"       | Precompute list of domino placements: each as tuple (cell1, cell2, orientation, side cells list). For each domino, compute side cells (left/right for horizontal; up/down for vertical). For each side cell, if inside board, we need coverage.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707787556,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70778856,"data":"       | Now we need to select subset D such that:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707789657,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707790679,"data":"       | - No overlapping cells: each cell appears in at most one domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707791815,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707792825,"data":"       | - For each domino d in D, for each side cell s (if inside board), there exists some domino d' in D covering s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707794041,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707795064,"data":"       | Thus we can treat as constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707796149,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707797158,"data":"       | We can implement a recursive search that picks dominoes and ensures constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707798328,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707801011,"data":"       | We can also use a SAT solver approach: encode as CNF and use pycosat? Not installed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707802236,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707803259,"data":"       | But we can implement a simple backtracking with forward checking:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707804403,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707805436,"data":"       | We maintain:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707806498,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707807517,"data":"       | - occupied set of cells (by placed dominoes)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70780882,"data":"       | - uncovered side requirements: a multiset of cells that need to be covered (but each cell only needs to be covered by at least one domino). Actually each side cell must be occupied by some domino, not necessarily distinct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707810238,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707811264,"data":"       | Thus we can maintain a set required_cells = union of side cells of placed dominoes that are not yet occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707812488,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707813499,"data":"       | Goal: eventually required_cells subset of occupied cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707814611,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707815673,"data":"       | When we place a new domino, we add its two cells to occupied, and we add its side cells (if interior) to required_cells. Some of those side cells may already be occupied (by previous dominoes), thus they are satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707817115,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707818157,"data":"       | Thus after each placement, we can compute unsatisfied required cells = required_cells - occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70781938,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707820426,"data":"       | We need to eventually cover them.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707821503,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707822528,"data":"       | Thus we can continue placing dominoes to cover unsatisfied required cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70782368,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707824705,"data":"       | Thus we can treat as search where we always try to place a domino that covers at least one unsatisfied required cell, to reduce unsatisfied set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707826045,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707827052,"data":"       | We also need to ensure we don't exceed minimal m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70782818,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707829185,"data":"       | We can attempt BFS for increasing m: start with empty set, required empty. At each step, we can add a domino; we can also add dominoes that are not needed for covering required cells but may be needed later.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707830554,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707831573,"data":"       | But we can prune: if number of dominoes placed + lower bound on additional dominoes needed to cover remaining required cells exceeds current best, prune.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707832865,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707833878,"data":"       | Lower bound: each new domino can cover at most 2 new cells (its own cells). To cover each unsatisfied required cell, we need at least ceil(|unsatisfied|/2) additional dominoes (since each domino can cover at most 2 required cells). However some required cells may be adjacent and can be covered by same domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70783722,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707838503,"data":"       | Thus lower bound = ceil(|unsatisfied|/2). Also need to consider that each new domino also adds its own side cells, potentially increasing required cells. So bound may be optimistic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707839853,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707840858,"data":"       | Nevertheless we can attempt search.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707841948,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707842965,"data":"       | Given small board, we can attempt to search for minimal m using recursion with pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707844163,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707845178,"data":"       | Let's implement.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707846236,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707847248,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codefrom itertools import product, combinations\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707848405,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707849435,"data":"       | # board dimensions\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7078505,"data":"       | R, C = 7, 7\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707851545,"data":"       | N = R*C\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707852588,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7078536,"data":"       | def idx(r,c):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707854638,"data":"       |     return r*C + c\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707855693,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707856805,"data":"       | # generate domino placements\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707857866,"data":"       | dominoes = []  # each as dict with cells, side cells\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707858981,"data":"       | for r in range(R):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707860064,"data":"       |     for c in range(C-1):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707861182,"data":"       |         # horizontal domino covering (r,c) and (r,c+1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70786233,"data":"       |         cells = (idx(r,c), idx(r,c+1))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707863415,"data":"       |         # side cells: left of first cell, right of second cell\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70786455,"data":"       |         side = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707865623,"data":"       |         if c-1 \u003e= 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707866714,"data":"       |             side.append(idx(r,c-1))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707867802,"data":"       |         if c+2 \u003c C:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707868875,"data":"       |             side.append(idx(r,c+2))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707869992,"data":"       |         dominoes.append({'cells': cells, 'side': side, 'orient':'H', 'pos':(r,c)})\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707871177,"data":"       | for r in range(R-1):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707872225,"data":"       |     for c in range(C):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707873278,"data":"       |         # vertical domino covering (r,c) and (r+1,c)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70787442,"data":"       |         cells = (idx(r,c), idx(r+1,c))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7078769,"data":"       |         side = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707877987,"data":"       |         if r-1 \u003e= 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707879077,"data":"       |             side.append(idx(r-1,c))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707880161,"data":"       |         if r+2 \u003c R:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707881246,"data":"       |             side.append(idx(r+2,c))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707882375,"data":"       |         dominoes.append({'cells': cells, 'side': side, 'orient':'V', 'pos':(r,c)})\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707883546,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70788459,"data":"       | len(dominoes)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.707885638,"data":"       | \n"}
