,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708876946,"data":"       | Maybe there is no configuration at all? But problem statement expects minimal m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708878111,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708879201,"data":"       | Maybe my interpretation of condition is wrong. Perhaps condition is that no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remain fixed, meaning each domino cannot slide in either direction because other dominos block it (i.e., both directions blocked). That is what we used.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708880732,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708881774,"data":"       | But maybe sliding means moving the domino by one unit in its orientation direction only (i.e., horizontal domino slides left/right, vertical slides up/down). So we need to block both directions for each domino. That's what we used.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708883206,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70888448,"data":"       | Our search found no solution up to 24 dominoes, meaning impossible to lock all placed dominoes. Could be that we need to consider that dominoes can slide only if there is a continuous empty space of length at least 2? Actually sliding a domino horizontally by one cell requires both cells to be empty after move? Wait sliding a domino left by one cell: new cells are (r,c-1) and (r,c). The original cells (r,c) and (r,c+1) become (r,c-1) and (r,c). The cell (r,c+1) becomes empty. So the move only requires that the destination cell (r,c-1) be empty; the other cell (r,c) is already occupied by the domino before move, but after move it's still occupied (by same domino). So only need the new cell to be empty. So our condition that side cell must be occupied is correct: to block sliding left, the cell to left must be occupied (or out of board). Similarly for sliding right, need cell to right of second cell empty? Actually sliding right requires cell (r,c+2) empty. So to block sliding right, that cell must be occupied or out of board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708888838,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708889863,"data":"       | Thus our condition stands.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708890934,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708891979,"data":"       | Thus maybe there is indeed no configuration that locks all placed dominoes; but problem expects minimal m such that you can place m dominoes with the property that no domino can slide (i.e., each domino is blocked). Maybe we are allowed to leave some dominoes not blocked? No, condition says \"no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remains fixed.\" Means each domino cannot slide.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70889377,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708894812,"data":"       | Thus maybe minimal m is 0? Because with no dominoes, trivially no domino can slide. But they likely expect positive m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708896036,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708897079,"data":"       | But perhaps they mean we must place dominoes such that each domino cannot slide because of other dominoes (i.e., each domino is blocked by at least one other domino). Edge of board does not count as blocking? The phrase \"when other dominos remains fixed\" suggests only other dominoes block, not board edges. So edges do not count. So each domino must be blocked by other dominoes on both sides (if interior) or at least one side if at edge? Actually if at edge, sliding outward would go off board, which is not allowed anyway, but they might consider that as not a slide (since board boundary prevents). However phrase \"when other dominos remains fixed\" maybe implies we consider only other dominos as obstacles, not board edges. So a domino at edge could slide outward off board? Not allowed physically; but maybe they consider board edges as also blocking. Usually sliding puzzle includes board edges as obstacles. But phrase ambiguous.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708899798,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708901066,"data":"       | If edges count, we already considered them as blocking (since out of board). Our search found no solution. So maybe we made mistake in side cell definition: For a horizontal domino at columns (c,c+1), sliding left requires cell (r,c-1) empty; sliding right requires cell (r,c+2) empty. That's correct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708902618,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708903643,"data":"       | But we also need to consider that a domino could slide up/down (vertical translation) while staying horizontal orientation? Actually a horizontal domino could slide up or down (i.e., shift vertically) while staying horizontal. That's also a possible move in sliding puzzles (like moving a piece up/down). The problem statement says \"no domino can slide horizontally or vertically\". That could mean any translation in either direction (horizontal or vertical). So a horizontal domino could slide up/down as well, which would require the two cells above (or below) to be empty? Actually sliding up by one cell would move domino to rows r-1, same columns c,c+1. This requires both cells (r-1,c) and (r-1,c+1) empty. Similarly sliding down requires both cells (r+2,c) and (r+2,c+1) empty. So we need to block those as well. Similarly vertical domino can slide left/right requiring both cells left/right empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7089078,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708908856,"data":"       | Thus our earlier condition was insufficient: we only considered sliding along orientation direction. Need to block sliding in both horizontal and vertical directions for each domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708910202,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708911246,"data":"       | Thus each domino has up to 4 possible slide directions (if interior). For a horizontal domino, sliding left/right (one cell) requires one cell each side; sliding up/down requires two cells each (the whole row above or below). For vertical domino, sliding up/down requires one cell each side; sliding left/right requires two cells each.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708912868,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708913883,"data":"       | Thus the condition becomes more stringent; but maybe still possible with fewer dominoes? Actually more constraints may require more dominoes, not fewer. But our earlier search found no solution even with 24 dominoes, maybe because we missed vertical slide constraints. So we need to incorporate them.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708915416,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708916619,"data":"       | Let's update constraints: For each domino, we need to block all four directions (if applicable). For each direction, the cells that need to be empty for a slide are:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708917968,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708918993,"data":"       | - Horizontal domino at (r,c)-(r,c+1):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70892009,"data":"       |    - Slide left: cell (r,c-1) must be empty (if inside board)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708921254,"data":"       |    - Slide right: cell (r,c+2) must be empty (if inside board)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708922412,"data":"       |    - Slide up: cells (r-1,c) and (r-1,c+1) must both be empty (if inside board)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708923585,"data":"       |    - Slide down: cells (r+2,c) and (r+2,c+1) must both be empty (if inside board)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708924759,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708925844,"data":"       | Thus to block sliding left/right, we need those single cells occupied. To block sliding up/down, we need at least one of the two cells occupied? Actually to prevent sliding up, we need that the move cannot be performed because at least one of the required cells is occupied (or out of board). For sliding up, both cells must be empty to allow move. So to block, we need at least one of the two cells occupied (or out of board). So we need to ensure that for each direction, not all required cells are empty. So we need to place obstacles such that for each direction, at least one of the required cells is occupied (by another domino) or out of board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708927946,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708929001,"data":"       | Thus the condition is: For each domino, for each of the four directions, the set of cells that need to be empty for the move must intersect the occupied set (or be outside board). Equivalent: For each direction, at least one cell in the direction's required cells must be occupied (or out of board). So we need to block each direction with at least one occupied cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70893064,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708931654,"data":"       | Thus we need to ensure for each domino:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708932786,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708935323,"data":"       | - Horizontal domino: left side cell (single) must be occupied or out of board; right side cell must be occupied or out of board; up side: at least one of the two cells above must be occupied or out of board; down side: at least one of the two cells below must be occupied or out of board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708936852,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70893787,"data":"       | - Vertical domino: up cell (single) occupied/out; down cell occupied/out; left side: at least one of two cells left must be occupied/out; right side: at least one of two cells right must be occupied/out.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708939258,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708940576,"data":"       | Thus constraints are more complex.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708941688,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708942698,"data":"       | Now we need to find minimal m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708943803,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.708944809,"data":"       | Let's implement search again with these constraints.\n"}
