,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709591596,"data":"       | analysisThe search didn't find any solution up to 24 dominoes within 30 seconds, and returned None (meaning not found). Could be that there is no solution at all (i.e., impossible to lock all dominoes). But problem expects minimal m, maybe they consider that you can place dominoes such that none can slide because of other dominoes, but maybe you need to fill entire board except one cell? Let's test if full tiling (24 dominoes) works. Let's attempt to find any configuration that satisfies constraints using a SAT solver? But we attempted exhaustive search up to 24 and found none (search didn't find any). However our search may be incomplete due to pruning errors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709593732,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709594819,"data":"       | Potential bug: Clause satisfaction condition: we considered clause satisfied if any occupied cell is in clause. That's correct: at least one cell of the block set must be occupied. However we also need to consider that a clause may be satisfied by a cell that is part of a domino that is not yet placed (i.e., future). That's okay; we only consider satisfied if currently occupied. At final stage, all clauses must be satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709596556,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709597633,"data":"       | Our recursion picks an unsatisfied clause and tries to place a domino covering a cell in that clause. This ensures eventual satisfaction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709598914,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709600026,"data":"       | But we also need to ensure that after placing dominoes, the occupied cells also must not overlap (we enforce). However we also need to ensure that each domino's own block sets are satisfied (i.e., each direction). Our clause set includes all block sets for all dominoes, not just placed ones. Wait we generated clauses from block_sets for all possible domino placements, not just those we actually place. That's wrong! We should only require block constraints for the dominoes we actually place, not for all possible domino positions. We incorrectly required that for every possible domino (even those not placed) at least one cell in its block set be occupied. That makes problem impossible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709602229,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709603275,"data":"       | We need to generate clauses only for placed dominoes. So we cannot predefine clause set; they are dynamic based on which dominoes we place.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709606131,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709607226,"data":"       | Thus our earlier ILP approach that used constraints for each placed domino is correct, but we need to incorporate them dynamically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709608509,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709609535,"data":"       | Thus we need to search over subsets of dominoes, and for each placed domino we need to satisfy its block sets.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709610766,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709612026,"data":"       | Our earlier search that considered only left/right constraints (single cells) found no solution up to 24, but that may be due to missing vertical constraints as well. However we need to incorporate both orientation directions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709613463,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709614491,"data":"       | Thus we need to search again with full constraints but dynamic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709615659,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709616688,"data":"       | We can implement DFS that builds set of dominoes and tracks unsatisfied direction requirements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709617886,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709618942,"data":"       | For each placed domino, we have up to 4 direction requirements (sets). For each direction, we need at least one cell occupied (by any domino). So we can maintain a list of unsatisfied direction requirements (each is a set of cells). When we add a domino, we can update: for each existing direction requirement, if the new domino occupies any cell in that set, that requirement becomes satisfied. Also we add new direction requirements for the new domino (its 4 sets). Some of those may be already satisfied by existing occupied cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709620863,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709621885,"data":"       | Thus we can maintain a list (or set) of unsatisfied direction requirements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709623046,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709624073,"data":"       | Goal: at final stage, unsatisfied set must be empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709625216,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709626217,"data":"       | We also need to ensure that we can satisfy remaining unsatisfied requirements with remaining dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709627429,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709628465,"data":"       | We can compute lower bound: each remaining domino can satisfy at most how many unsatisfied requirements? Since each domino occupies two cells, each cell can satisfy multiple requirements (if a requirement set contains that cell). So a domino could satisfy many requirements simultaneously. Upper bound maybe high.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709630027,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709631111,"data":"       | But we can still attempt search with pruning using simple bound: number of unsatisfied requirements \u003e remaining_slots * max_requirements_per_domino (where max_requirements_per_domino is maximum number of direction requirements a domino can satisfy). Let's compute that.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709632657,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709633682,"data":"       | For each domino, we can compute the set of direction requirements (i.e., sets) that it can satisfy: any requirement set that contains either of its cells. Since each requirement set is a set of size 1 or 2. So a domino can satisfy many requirements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709636723,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709638194,"data":"       | We can compute for each domino the number of unique requirement sets (from all possible dominoes) that intersect its cells. But we only need to consider requirements that actually appear in current unsatisfied set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709639608,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709640701,"data":"       | Thus we can compute bound dynamically: for each unsatisfied requirement, we need at least one domino covering a cell in it. This is hitting set again.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709642019,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709643109,"data":"       | Simplify: we can use recursion that selects an unsatisfied requirement, picks a cell from it, then picks a domino covering that cell, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709644395,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709645424,"data":"       | Thus algorithm similar to earlier but with dynamic requirements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709646577,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709647623,"data":"       | Let's implement DFS:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709648694,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709649751,"data":"       | State:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709650814,"data":"       | - occupied set\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709651894,"data":"       | - unsatisfied list of requirement sets (each is frozenset of cells)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709653085,"data":"       | - placed count\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709654144,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70965517,"data":"       | At start, unsatisfied is empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709656254,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709657286,"data":"       | When we add a domino d:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709658362,"data":"       | - new occupied = occupied ∪ d.cells\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709659519,"data":"       | - For each requirement r in unsatisfied, if r ∩ d.cells != empty, then requirement satisfied (remove it).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709660824,"data":"       | - Then we add new requirements from d: for each direction set S (size 0,1,2):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709662001,"data":"       |    - if S empty: ignore (already blocked by edge)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709663132,"data":"       |    - else if S ∩ new_occupied != empty: already satisfied (since new occupied includes d.cells, but S may intersect d.cells? Actually S does not intersect d.cells, but may intersect other occupied cells from before; we need to check intersection with new_occupied (which includes previous occupied plus new cells). If intersection non-empty, requirement already satisfied; else add S to unsatisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709664929,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709665942,"data":"       | Thus unsatisfied set updates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709667023,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709668037,"data":"       | Goal: after placing target dominoes, unsatisfied must be empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709669297,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70967034,"data":"       | We can implement DFS with iterative deepening.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70967146,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709674321,"data":"       | We also need to prune: if len(unsatisfied) \u003e 2 * remaining_slots (since each new domino can cover at most 2 new cells, each cell can satisfy many requirements, but at least each requirement needs a cell, but a single cell can satisfy multiple requirements (if same cell appears in many requirement sets). So bound may be weak.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709676026,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709677053,"data":"       | But we can also prune by checking that any unsatisfied requirement set is of size 0 (impossible) or size 1 where that cell is already occupied (should have been satisfied). Actually if size 1 and cell is already occupied, then requirement would have been satisfied earlier. So unsatisfied size 1 means that cell is free.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709678596,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709679626,"data":"       | Thus we need to be able to cover each size-1 requirement with a domino that includes that cell. That domino will also occupy another cell (maybe also needed for other requirements). So we can try to branch on size-1 requirements first.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709681067,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709682111,"data":"       | Thus algorithm: pick an unsatisfied requirement (prefer size 1). For each domino covering that cell (and not overlapping), place it.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709683393,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709684401,"data":"       | Let's implement.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709685476,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709686507,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codedef minimal_dominoes(limit_time=60):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709687679,"data":"       |     start_time = time.time()\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709688778,"data":"       |     # Precompute for each cell the list of domino indices covering it\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709689933,"data":"       |     cell_to_dominos = [[] for _ in range(N)]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709691058,"data":"       |     for i,d in enumerate(dominoes):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70969214,"data":"       |         for cell in d['cells']:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709693256,"data":"       |             cell_to_dominos[cell].append(i)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709694356,"data":"       |     # Precompute direction sets for each domino\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709695471,"data":"       |     domino_dir_sets = []  # list of list of frozenset (size 1 or 2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709696639,"data":"       |     for d in dominoes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709697699,"data":"       |         dirs = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709698784,"data":"       |         for s in block_sets[dominoes.index(d)]:  # but block_sets aligned with dominoes order\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.70970001,"data":"       |             if s:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709701078,"data":"       |                 dirs.append(frozenset(s))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709702174,"data":"       |         domino_dir_sets.append(dirs)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709703273,"data":"       |     # Actually block_sets list corresponds to dominoes order, we can just use block_sets directly\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709704491,"data":"       |     # We'll use block_sets directly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709705604,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709708158,"data":"       |     # iterative deepening\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709709285,"data":"       |     for target in range(1, 25):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.709710397,"data":"       |         # print progress\n"}
