,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710572264,"data":"     [Turn 5]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710573275,"data":"     [Reasoning] (7937 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710574343,"data":"       | analysisPulp not installed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710575419,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71057647,"data":"       | Maybe we can use ortools? Probably not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710577558,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710578599,"data":"       | Alternative: Use python's MILP via `python-mip`? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710579698,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710580728,"data":"       | Thus we need to implement custom search.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710581815,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710582835,"data":"       | Given board 7x7, we can treat each cell as either empty or occupied by a domino (with orientation). Since dominos cover two cells, we can think of tiling partially.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710584137,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710585173,"data":"       | We can search using backtracking: place dominos one by one, ensuring constraints for already placed dominos are satisfied (i.e., they cannot slide given current placements). However constraints involve future placements as well: a domino may need neighbor cells that are not yet placed but could be placed later. So we need to ensure that at the end all constraints satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710586816,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710587855,"data":"       | We can use SAT/backtracking: assign each cell either empty or part of a domino with orientation and partner.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710589049,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710590068,"data":"       | Simplify: Represent board as 7x7 grid; each cell can be in state: empty, or part of a domino (with orientation). We'll place dominos gradually.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710591364,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710592386,"data":"       | We can also think of each domino's required neighbor cells: For each domino placed, we can mark required neighbor cells that must be occupied (or board edge). Those cells can be satisfied later by placing other dominos covering them.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710593811,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710595551,"data":"       | Thus we can backtrack: maintain set of required cells that must be covered by some domino (maybe multiple requirements). When we place a domino, we also add its own required neighbor cells (if inside board) to the requirement set. Also we need to ensure that any cell that is required cannot remain empty at the end; but we can still leave it empty temporarily as long as we later cover it.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710598808,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710599894,"data":"       | Thus we can search for minimal number of dominos such that there exists a set of dominos covering a subset of cells, and the set of required cells is subset of covered cells (i.e., each required cell is covered by some domino). Also we must avoid overlapping.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710601371,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710602438,"data":"       | Thus we can treat as a covering problem: we need to select dominos such that the union of cells they occupy includes all required cells (including neighbor cells of each domino). This is similar to a fixed point condition: the set of occupied cells must be closed under the neighbor requirement operation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710603981,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710605038,"data":"       | Let O be set of occupied cells (by dominos). For each domino d (edge between two adjacent cells a,b) with both a,b in O, we require that for each direction (horizontal left/right, vertical up/down) the neighbor cells are either out of board or in O. For horizontal domino, left neighbor cell L (i,j-1) must be in O or out; right neighbor R (i,j+2) must be in O or out; up neighbor cells U1,U2 (i-1,j),(i-1,j+1) must be in O or out; down neighbor cells D1,D2 (i+1,j),(i+1,j+1) must be in O or out.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710606882,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710607911,"data":"       | Thus O must be such that for each domino edge within O, the 6 neighbor cells (some may be same) are also in O (or out). So O must be \"closed\" under this operation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710609308,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710610377,"data":"       | Thus we need to find minimal number of dominos (i.e., minimal size of O/2) such that O is a union of domino edges (i.e., O can be partitioned into domino pairs) and O is closed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710611735,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710612759,"data":"       | Thus we can search for minimal O.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710613834,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710615008,"data":"       | Observation: Since board edges count as out-of-board, cells on border may reduce requirements. For a domino placed along border, some neighbor cells are out-of-board, reducing needed occupied cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710616379,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710617398,"data":"       | Thus minimal configuration likely uses border cells heavily.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710618538,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710619575,"data":"       | Potential minimal arrangement: place a domino covering two border cells such that all neighbor cells are either border/out or covered by other dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710620861,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710621888,"data":"       | Let's attempt to think of small patterns.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710623002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710624029,"data":"       | Consider a corner cell (1,1). Could we place a domino covering (1,1)-(1,2) horizontally at top row. Its left neighbor out-of-board, up neighbor out-of-board (since top row). Right neighbor cell (1,3) must be occupied. Down neighbor cells (2,1) and (2,2) must be occupied. So we need to cover (1,3), (2,1), (2,2). Those could be covered by other dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710627068,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710628108,"data":"       | Similarly, a vertical domino covering (1,1)-(2,1) at left column: up neighbor out, left neighbor out, down neighbor (3,1) must be occupied, right neighbor cells (1,2),(2,2) must be occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710629492,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710630542,"data":"       | Thus each border domino still requires 3 other cells (maybe more) to be occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710631711,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710632756,"data":"       | Thus minimal m may be more than 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710633854,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710634866,"data":"       | Let's attempt to use computational search with backtracking and pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710636033,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710637048,"data":"       | We can treat each domino placement as variable; we need to select a set of placements such that for each selected placement, all required neighbor cells are covered by some selected placement.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710638439,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710639493,"data":"       | We can encode neighbor cells as constraints: For each placement p, for each neighbor cell n inside board, there must exist a placement q covering n. This is similar to earlier ILP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710640825,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710641836,"data":"       | We can attempt to solve using Python's `python-constraint` library? Not available.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710643001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710644285,"data":"       | But we can implement a simple recursive search with pruning using constraint propagation: maintain set of covered cells O, and set of required cells R (cells that must be covered). Initially O empty, R empty. When we add a placement p, we add its two cells to O, and add its neighbor cells (inside board) to R. At the end we need R ⊆ O (i.e., all required cells covered). Also O must be partitionable into dominos (by construction). Overlap not allowed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71064612,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710647126,"data":"       | Thus we can search for minimal number of placements such that closure condition holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710648329,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710649389,"data":"       | We can also note that R may include cells already in O (good). It may also include cells not yet covered; we need to later cover them.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710650649,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710651684,"data":"       | Thus we can treat as building O gradually, ensuring that we never create a requirement that cannot be satisfied due to board constraints (e.g., a required cell that is isolated and cannot be covered by any domino without overlapping). This can prune.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710653148,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710654173,"data":"       | Implementation approach:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710655251,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710656286,"data":"       | - Precompute list of all placements (dominos) with their cells (list of two coordinates) and neighbor cells (set of coordinates) that must be occupied (excluding out-of-board). For each placement, compute neighbor cells as described.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710659924,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710661011,"data":"       | - Use recursion: try to add placements one by one, keep O set, R set, and also track which cells are already covered.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710662262,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710663292,"data":"       | - At each step, we can compute uncovered required cells = R - O. If there are uncovered required cells, we must eventually cover them. Choose one such cell and consider possible placements that cover it (i.e., dominos that include that cell). Branch on those placements (if they don't overlap with O). This ensures we eventually satisfy requirements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710664905,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710665936,"data":"       | - Also we can stop when O size (number of dominos) reaches current best solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710667128,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710668149,"data":"       | - Since board small, we can attempt to find minimal solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710669306,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.710670346,"data":"       | Let's implement.\n"}
