,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712744111,"data":"       | analysisWe need to understand the problem: Place m 1x2 dominos on a 7x7 chessboard such that no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remain fixed. Find minimal possible m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712745525,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7127466,"data":"       | Interpretation: We have a 7x7 board (49 squares). We place some dominoes (each covering two adjacent squares horizontally or vertically). The condition: For each domino placed, it cannot slide horizontally or vertically while keeping the other dominoes fixed. That is, there is no empty adjacent cell in the direction of its orientation that would allow it to shift by one unit (i.e., move one square left/right if horizontal, up/down if vertical) while still covering empty squares (i.e., not overlapping other dominoes). In other words, each domino must be \"blocked\" on both sides along its orientation direction by either the board edge or another domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712748808,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712749865,"data":"       | But sliding may also be blocked by board edges? The board edges are fixed; a domino cannot slide off board. So a domino at board edge cannot slide outward because out of board; but could slide inward if there is empty space on other side? For a horizontal domino, sliding left or right one cell: need both squares to be empty after shift. If at leftmost column (col 1), sliding left impossible due to board edge. Sliding right possible if the two squares to the right are empty. So to be \"cannot slide horizontally\", we need both left and right slides blocked (by edge or occupied squares). Similarly for vertical domino: both up and down slides blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712753453,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712754644,"data":"       | Thus each domino must be \"sandwiched\" in its orientation direction: either at board edge on one side, and blocked on the other side by either board edge (if length 2 and board size 7, cannot be both edges) or another domino; or blocked on both sides by other dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712756205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712757228,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominoes needed to achieve this condition for all placed dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712758461,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712759507,"data":"       | Essentially we need to select a set of domino placements such that each domino has no free adjacent pair of squares in its orientation direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712760801,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712761851,"data":"       | We can think of board cells as vertices of a grid graph. Dominoes are edges covering two adjacent vertices. The condition: For each selected edge e (domino), the two parallel edges adjacent to e in same direction (i.e., the edges that would be used to slide) must be unavailable: either because they are outside board (i.e., e is at boundary) or because the squares they'd occupy are already covered by other dominoes (i.e., the edge is occupied by another domino? Actually sliding would need two empty squares that are currently not covered. If those squares are covered by other dominoes (maybe overlapping partially), then sliding not possible. But the squares could be covered by a domino oriented perpendicular, covering one of them? That would block sliding because that square is occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712764214,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71276553,"data":"       | Thus we need to ensure that for each domino e, at least one of the two squares adjacent in each direction is occupied or out of board. More precisely: For a horizontal domino covering cells (r,c) and (r,c+1). Sliding right would require cells (r,c+2) and (r,c+3) to be empty (if within board). Actually sliding right by one cell means the domino moves to cover (r,c+1) and (r,c+2). Wait original domino occupies cells (c,c+1). After slide right one unit, it would occupy (c+1,c+2). So the new cells needed are (r,c+2) (the rightmost new cell) while (r,c) becomes empty. So to be able to slide right, we need (r,c+2) empty (not occupied) and also (r,c+1) already occupied by original domino (so fine). Actually sliding right requires the cell to the right of the domino to be empty, and the cell on the left becomes empty after slide. So only need the cell at far right to be empty. Similarly sliding left requires cell (r,c-1) empty. So condition for no slide: both left and right adjacent cells (outside the domino) must be occupied or out of board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712768291,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712769345,"data":"       | Thus each domino must have its immediate neighbor squares on both sides (in orientation direction) occupied or off-board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712770588,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712771611,"data":"       | Thus we need to place dominoes such that for each domino, its two adjacent cells (one on each side) are occupied (by some domino, possibly same domino? can't be same because domino covers only two cells). So each domino needs \"support\" cells on both sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712774364,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712775397,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712776493,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712777508,"data":"       | We can think of covering board with dominoes and maybe leaving some cells empty. Dominoes can be arranged such that each domino's side cells are occupied by other dominoes (maybe perpendicular). The board is odd size (7x7) so cannot be fully tiled with dominoes (needs even number of cells). So there will be at least one empty cell. But we may need many empty cells to allow minimal domino count.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71277918,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712780264,"data":"       | We need to find minimal m such that there exists a configuration meeting condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712781471,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712782508,"data":"       | Let's attempt to formulate as optimization problem: choose a set of domino placements (edges) on 7x7 grid (grid graph with 7 rows, 7 columns). Each domino covers two adjacent cells (horizontal or vertical). We can have at most one domino covering any cell (no overlapping). So we need a matching (set of edges with no shared vertices). That's a matching on the grid graph.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712784164,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712785394,"data":"       | Condition: For each edge e in matching, consider its two incident vertices v1, v2 (the cells). For each direction along the edge (i.e., left/right for horizontal, up/down for vertical), the adjacent cell beyond each endpoint must be either outside board or occupied by some edge in matching (i.e., the vertex is matched with its neighbor). So each endpoint must be matched to the neighbor in the direction outward? Wait: Suppose we have a horizontal domino covering cells (r,c) and (r,c+1). The left side neighbor is cell (r,c-1). If that cell is occupied by a domino (i.e., matched with some neighbor), then sliding left not possible because that cell is taken. Similarly right side neighbor is (r,c+2). If that cell is occupied, sliding right not possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712787654,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712788691,"data":"       | Thus condition: For each endpoint cell of a domino, the adjacent cell in the direction outward along the domino's orientation must be either out of board or matched (i.e., part of some domino). Equivalent: For each domino edge, both of its two \"external\" neighboring cells (one on each side) must be matched (or off-board). Note that those neighboring cells could be matched with each other (i.e., they could form another domino covering them, maybe perpendicular orientation). Or each could be matched with some other neighbor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712790683,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712791703,"data":"       | Thus we need a matching where each matched edge is \"blocked\" on both sides by other matched vertices (or edges) or board edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712793004,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712794034,"data":"       | We can model as a graph problem: Consider the line graph of the grid? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712795204,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712796217,"data":"       | Simplify: Represent each cell as variable: either empty or matched with neighbor (domino). For each domino, we need both neighboring cells along its orientation to be occupied (i.e., not empty). So empties can only appear in positions where they are not adjacent (in line) to a domino's side.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712798867,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712799907,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominoes (i.e., matched vertices count /2). Equivalent to maximize number of empty cells while satisfying constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712801217,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712802244,"data":"       | Total cells = 49. Let E = number of empty cells. Then m = (49 - E)/2, because each domino covers 2 cells, and cells are either empty or covered, no overlap. So minimizing m is same as maximizing E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71280363,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712804874,"data":"       | Thus we need to find maximum number of empty cells such that there exists a matching covering the remaining cells with the blocking condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712806151,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712807178,"data":"       | Thus we need to find a configuration with many empty cells, but each domino must have its adjacent cells occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712808404,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712809438,"data":"       | Observation: If a cell is empty, then any domino adjacent to it (i.e., sharing a side) must be oriented perpendicular to that side? Actually consider an empty cell at position (r,c). It could be adjacent to up to 4 cells. If any of those adjacent cells is part of a domino oriented towards the empty cell (i.e., the domino's side neighbor is that empty cell), then that domino would have an empty neighbor on that side, allowing slide. So such domino cannot exist. Therefore any domino that has an endpoint adjacent to an empty cell must be oriented perpendicular to that side, i.e., the empty cell must be on the \"side\" of the domino, not along its orientation direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712811684,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71281287,"data":"       | Specifically, suppose empty cell at (r,c). Consider a domino covering (r,c+1)-(r,c+2) horizontally. Its left neighbor is (r,c), which is empty. That would allow sliding left. So such configuration invalid. So any empty cell cannot be directly adjacent in line with a domino's orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712814384,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712815661,"data":"       | Thus for any empty cell, any neighboring domino must be oriented perpendicular to the line connecting them. For example, empty cell at (r,c) could be adjacent to a vertical domino covering (r-1,c)-(r,c) (vertical). The empty cell is at the bottom of that vertical domino; its neighbor above is part of the domino; sliding up/down of that vertical domino would involve cells above top or below bottom; the empty cell is at bottom, which would be a neighbor in sliding direction (down). Wait vertical domino covering (r-1,c)-(r,c). Its bottom neighbor is (r+1,c). Not (r,c) because that's part of domino. The empty cell is (r,c) which is part of the domino, not empty. So not relevant. Let's think: For a vertical domino covering (r,c)-(r+1,c). Its top neighbor is (r-1,c). Its bottom neighbor is (r+2,c). So empty cell adjacent to one of its cells but not in line with orientation (i.e., side neighbor) is e.g., (r,c+1) (right of top cell) or (r+1,c+1) (right of bottom cell). Those are side neighbors, not affecting sliding.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712818384,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.712819414,"data":"       | Thus the condition: Empty cells cannot be directly adjacent along a line (horizontal or vertical) to a domino that is oriented in that direction with the empty cell being the external neighbor of the domino. In other words, for any empty cell, the cells adjacent to it cannot be the \"external side\" of a domino oriented towards the empty cell.\n"}
