,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713036861,"data":"       | Let's attempt to think conceptually.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713038044,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713039089,"data":"       | Observation: For any domino, its external neighbor cells must be occupied. Therefore, the set of empty cells cannot be adjacent (in line) to any occupied cell's external side.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713040464,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71304149,"data":"       | Thus consider the graph where vertices are cells, edges are adjacency in same row or column. For each occupied edge (domino), the two cells adjacent to its endpoints in same line direction must be occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713042886,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713044303,"data":"       | Thus the empty cells must be \"isolated\" in the sense that any neighbor cell in the same row or column must be empty as well? Let's examine: Suppose cell X is empty. Consider a cell Y adjacent to X horizontally. If Y is occupied, then Y must belong to a domino. If that domino is vertical, then its orientation is vertical, so its external neighbor cells are up/down, not left/right. The empty cell X is to left or right of Y, which is a side neighbor, not affecting sliding. So Y could be occupied by vertical domino. That's allowed. So empty cells can be adjacent to occupied cells, as long as the occupied cell's domino orientation is perpendicular to the direction of adjacency.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713046544,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713047592,"data":"       | Thus we can have empty cells adjacent to vertical dominoes (if empty cell is left or right of vertical domino) or to horizontal dominoes (if empty cell is above or below horizontal domino). So empty cells can be placed near dominoes oriented perpendicularly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713049059,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713050074,"data":"       | Thus we can design pattern where empty cells are placed in a checkerboard pattern, and dominoes oriented to block each other accordingly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713051341,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71305237,"data":"       | Goal: maximize empty cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71305349,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713054524,"data":"       | Potentially we could aim for half the cells empty (i.e., 24 empty, 25 occupied? But occupied must be even number, so maybe 24 empty, 25 occupied impossible because occupied cells count must be even. So maximum empties maybe 25? Since total 49, occupied cells even, so empties odd. So empties could be 25, leaving 24 occupied cells = 12 dominoes. That would be minimal m=12. Is that feasible? Let's test plausibility.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713056271,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713057307,"data":"       | If we have 12 dominoes covering 24 cells, leaving 25 empty cells. For each domino, its external neighbor cells must be occupied (i.e., among those 24 cells). That means each domino requires at least two occupied cells adjacent (its external neighbors). So each domino \"needs\" at least two other occupied cells (maybe same as other domino's cells). With only 12 dominoes, each domino's two external neighbor cells could be the cells of other dominoes, possibly overlapping. Could be possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713059232,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713060286,"data":"       | But we need to ensure each occupied cell is part of a domino, cannot be empty. So the set of occupied cells must be such that each occupied cell has at least one neighbor occupied (its partner) and also for each domino, the two external neighbor cells are occupied (maybe same as its partner? No external neighbor cells are distinct from its own cells). So each domino's two external neighbor cells must be among the occupied cells but not its own cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713065031,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713066291,"data":"       | Thus each domino requires at least two other occupied cells adjacent to its ends. So total occupied cells must be enough to satisfy all dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713067632,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713068674,"data":"       | If we have 12 dominoes, each domino has 2 external neighbor cells, total 24 neighbor positions (some may coincide). Since there are only 24 occupied cells total, the external neighbor cells must be exactly the other occupied cells, possibly overlapping heavily.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713070146,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713071163,"data":"       | Potentially a configuration like a 2x2 block of four cells covered by two dominoes crossing each other? But crossing not allowed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713072417,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713073433,"data":"       | Let's attempt to search for minimal m using computational search with pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713074609,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713075611,"data":"       | We can treat each cell as either empty or part of a domino. We'll generate matchings with small number of dominoes and test constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71307687,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713077877,"data":"       | We can start from low m and see if feasible.\n"}
