,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713102192,"data":"       | Observation: The condition essentially requires that the set of occupied cells forms a \"domino-closed\" set: For each occupied cell, its partner is adjacent; also for each occupied cell, the opposite neighbor in line direction must be occupied (maybe by other domino). This suggests that the occupied cells must be a union of \"clusters\" where each cluster is at least length 3? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713104012,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713105067,"data":"       | Consider a horizontal domino covering cells A (left) and B (right). The external neighbor cells are left of A (call L) and right of B (call R). For the domino to be blocked, L and R must be occupied (or off-board). So L and R are occupied cells (maybe part of other dominoes). Thus the pattern along that row must be something like ... L A B R ... where L and R are occupied cells (maybe part of other dominoes). If L and R are part of vertical dominoes, they occupy those cells but may have partners above/below.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713106964,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713107983,"data":"       | Thus each horizontal domino must be part of a \"run\" of at least 4 occupied cells in that row (including its own two cells). Similarly for vertical dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713109374,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713110392,"data":"       | Thus the occupied cells must form rows/columns of at least length 4 contiguous occupied cells, where interior dominoes are blocked by neighbors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713111697,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713112722,"data":"       | But we could also have L or R be off-board, i.e., domino at edge. So edge dominoes need only one side neighbor occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713113965,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713114985,"data":"       | Thus we can place dominoes at edges to reduce required occupied cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713116154,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71311718,"data":"       | Thus to minimize total occupied cells, we could place dominoes mostly at edges, where they need only one neighbor to block.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713118459,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713119479,"data":"       | Edge dominoes: At board boundary, one side is off-board, so only need the other side neighbor occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713120692,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713121696,"data":"       | Thus we can place many dominoes along the perimeter, each requiring only one neighbor interior.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713122904,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71312393,"data":"       | But interior dominoes need two neighbors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713125031,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71312607,"data":"       | Thus we can try to design configuration where most dominoes are on edges, minimizing interior occupied cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713127326,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713128544,"data":"       | Goal: minimize total number of dominoes, not occupied cells. But each domino occupies two cells; edge dominoes still occupy two cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713131509,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713132564,"data":"       | But we can reduce number of dominoes needed to block each other.\n"}
