,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713161764,"data":"       | Maybe we can think of lower bound: each domino must have at least one neighbor occupied (except possibly at edge). Let's compute minimal number of occupied cells needed to block all dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713163129,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713164147,"data":"       | Let D be number of dominoes. Let O be number of occupied cells = 2D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71316531,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713168786,"data":"       | Let B be number of \"blocked sides\" required: For each domino, need up to 2 sides blocked (left/right or up/down). Edge sides count as already blocked (off-board). So total required blocked sides that must be occupied cells = sum over dominoes of number of sides not at edge. Let's denote for each domino, internal sides count = 2 - (# of sides at board edge). Edge domino can have 0,1, or 2 sides at edge? Actually a domino can be placed along board edge such that one side is off-board; cannot have both sides off-board because board width \u003e 2. So maximum one side per domino can be off-board (if placed at extreme edge). So internal sides per domino is either 2 (if not on edge) or 1 (if on edge). So total internal sides = 2D - E, where E is number of edge dominoes (dominoes with a side at board edge). These internal sides must be occupied by cells (external neighbor cells). Each occupied cell can serve as external neighbor for up to how many domino sides? A cell can be external neighbor for up to two dominoes (one on each side) maybe more if multiple dominoes share same neighbor cell? For a given cell, consider the dominoes that have this cell as external neighbor: Could be up to two dominoes, one on each side along same line. For example, a cell could be right neighbor of a left domino and left neighbor of a right domino (both horizontal). Also could be external neighbor for vertical dominoes (above/below). But a cell cannot be external neighbor for both a horizontal and vertical domino simultaneously because that would require it to be adjacent in both directions? Actually a cell could be right neighbor of a horizontal domino and also above neighbor of a vertical domino (different orientations). That's possible if cell is at intersection of row and column. So a cell could block up to 4 domino sides? Let's think: For each direction (left/right/up/down), a domino could have that cell as external neighbor. So up to 4 dominoes could use same cell as external neighbor (one for each direction). But each domino uses two external neighbor cells, each of which may be shared.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713175513,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71317681,"data":"       | Thus the total number of internal side requirements (2D - E) must be satisfied by occupied cells, each can satisfy up to 4 side requirements. So we have inequality: 2D - E \u003c= 4 * O = 8D. That's always true, not helpful.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713178309,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713179343,"data":"       | But we can get lower bound on D based on board geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713180485,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713181542,"data":"       | Maybe we can use parity arguments: Each interior cell that is occupied must be matched with neighbor; the external neighbor cells for a domino are distinct from its own cells. So each domino's two external neighbor cells are distinct from its own two cells (unless overlapping with other domino's cells). So each domino \"requires\" at least two other occupied cells (could be same cell serving as neighbor for two different dominoes). So minimal number of dominoes maybe limited.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713183447,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71318448,"data":"       | Let's attempt to search computationally using backtracking with heuristics for small D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713185674,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71318669,"data":"       | We'll attempt to find configuration with D = 10 dominoes (20 occupied cells). If not possible, increase.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713187907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713188925,"data":"       | Implement recursive search: maintain board occupancy (0 empty, 1 occupied but not yet paired, 2 paired maybe). Actually we need to pair cells into dominoes. We'll maintain a list of placed dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713192105,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713193147,"data":"       | We also need to enforce that for each placed domino, its external neighbor cells (if inside board) must eventually be occupied (by some domino). So when we place a domino, we can check if any external neighbor cell is currently empty and not yet assigned to any domino, but could be later assigned. That's okay as long as we later occupy them.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713194767,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713195779,"data":"       | But we must avoid dead ends where a required neighbor cannot be occupied because it would conflict with parity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713197012,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713198039,"data":"       | Thus we can implement search that tries to assign dominoes gradually, and after each placement, we keep track of \"required cells\" that must be occupied (i.e., external neighbor cells of placed dominoes). These cells must be eventually occupied (by some domino). So we can treat them as forced occupied cells (but not yet paired). We can also allow them to be paired later.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713199775,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713201064,"data":"       | Thus we can maintain set of cells that are forced occupied (must be part of some domino). Initially empty. When we place a domino, we add its external neighbor cells (if within board) to forced set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713202454,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713203462,"data":"       | If a forced cell is already marked as empty (i.e., we decided to leave it empty), conflict.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713204662,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713205677,"data":"       | We also must ensure that forced cells can be paired into dominoes without conflict.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713206867,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713207908,"data":"       | Thus we can treat the problem as: choose a set of dominoes such that the union of their cells (occupied) is a superset of the forced cells (which are the external neighbor cells of all dominoes). Since forced cells are themselves occupied, they must be covered by some domino (maybe the same domino that forced them? No, because forced cells are external, not part of the domino that forced them). So they must be covered by other dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7132097,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713210737,"data":"       | Thus we can think of constructing a matching where each domino's external neighbor cells are also matched (by possibly other dominoes). This is reminiscent of a \"domino graph\" where each edge forces its adjacent edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713212206,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713213235,"data":"       | Thus we can attempt to search for minimal number of dominoes by building a graph of constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713214444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713215458,"data":"       | Let's implement backtracking search for D up to maybe 12.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713216598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713217616,"data":"       | We'll represent board cells as indices 0..48.\n"}
