,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713555312,"data":"       | Now implement recursive search for given D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713556418,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713557422,"data":"       | We'll use ordering of cells maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713558518,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713559517,"data":"       | We'll also need to prune using forced cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713560617,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713563235,"data":"       | Implementation details:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713564529,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713565548,"data":"       | State variables:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713566617,"data":"       | - occupied: set of cells already covered by placed dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713567787,"data":"       | - forced: set of cells that must be occupied (external neighbor cells of placed dominoes). This includes cells already occupied (that's fine) and cells not yet occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713569121,"data":"       | - placed: list of domino indices (maybe not needed).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713570267,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713571282,"data":"       | We also need to know remaining dominoes to place: remaining = D - len(placed).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713572485,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713573495,"data":"       | Pruning conditions:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713574646,"data":"       | 1. If any cell in forced but not in occupied, they must be covered by remaining dominoes. Let needed = len(forced - occupied). Since each domino adds at most 2 new occupied cells, we need needed \u003c= 2*remaining. Also needed must be even? Not necessarily; could be odd but then we need at least one extra cell to pair with something else (maybe an extra empty cell). But overall total occupied cells after placing all D dominoes will be 2D, which is even. forced - occupied size may be odd, but we can still cover with dominoes that also cover other cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713576641,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713577992,"data":"       | 2. Additionally, we must ensure that forced - occupied cells can be matched within the free cells (free = all cells - occupied). Because they need to be paired with neighbor cells (which could be other forced cells or other free cells). So we compute maximum matching on subgraph induced by free cells. If matching size \u003c (len(occupied_needed)+len(forced_not_occupied)/2? Actually we need to be able to cover all forced_not_occupied cells. For each forced_not_occupied cell, it must be incident to a domino placed later, meaning it must be matched with some neighbor (which could be another forced_not_occupied cell or a non-forced free cell). So we need a matching that covers all forced_not_occupied cells. This is a \"matching that saturates a given subset\". Condition: there exists a matching that covers all vertices in subset S (forced_not_occupied). A necessary condition is that in the bipartite graph, the size of maximum matching \u003e= |S|/2? Actually each forced cell must be matched, so the matching must have at least |S| edges covering them, but each edge can cover at most 2 forced cells. So we need matching size \u003e= ceil(|S|/2). That's necessary but not sufficient. However we can use more precise condition: there exists a matching that covers S if and only if for every subset T of S, the neighbor set N(T) has size \u003e= |T| (Hall's condition). Hard to compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713581427,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713582482,"data":"       | But we can approximate with maximum matching on graph where we treat forced cells as left side maybe? Actually we can compute maximum matching on the subgraph induced by free cells, and then check if all forced cells are matched in that maximum matching (i.e., each forced cell is incident to a matched edge). However maximum matching may not match all forced cells even if there exists a matching that does, because maximum matching maximizes total edges, not necessarily covering forced cells. But if we compute maximum matching and see that some forced cell remains unmatched, we could try to see if there exists any matching covering all forced cells; this is a bipartite covering problem, we can transform by forcing edges: we can add dummy nodes? Could also compute maximum matching after removing any edges that don't involve forced cells? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713586554,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713587623,"data":"       | Given small size, we could just attempt backtracking without this pruning; maybe still feasible for D up to 12.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713588856,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713589898,"data":"       | Let's try simple backtracking: choose next free cell (lowest index) not occupied, try to either leave it empty (if we allow empties) or pair it with neighbor (if we still need dominoes). But we need to achieve exactly D dominoes, not exceed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71359136,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713592404,"data":"       | But we also need to ensure final configuration satisfies constraints: For each placed domino, its external neighbor cells must be occupied (i.e., part of some domino). So after we finish placing D dominoes, we can verify condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713593847,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713595185,"data":"       | Thus we can just generate all matchings of size D (choose D dominoes) and test condition. But enumeration of all matchings of size D may be huge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713596477,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713597505,"data":"       | But we can use recursive generation of matchings with pruning based on forced cells as earlier.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713598699,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713599704,"data":"       | Let's attempt to implement recursive search for D starting from low numbers, using heuristics.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713600919,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713601941,"data":"       | We'll choose a cell not yet occupied, and try to place a domino covering it with each possible neighbor (right or down maybe). We'll also consider leaving it empty, but we need to ensure final number of dominoes is D. So we can decide to leave cells empty as we go, but we must ensure we can still place remaining dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713603524,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713604547,"data":"       | Simplify: We'll generate matchings of size D using recursion that picks a free cell and tries to pair it with a neighbor (if neighbor also free). This will produce all matchings of size D (not necessarily all but many). We'll also incorporate forced constraints early: after placing a domino, we add its external neighbor cells to forced set; if any forced cell is left empty (i.e., we later decide to not occupy it), we must avoid that.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713606309,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713607376,"data":"       | Thus we can maintain a set \"forbidden_empty\" = forced cells (must be occupied). So when we consider leaving a cell empty, we can only do so if it's not in forced set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713608763,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713609778,"data":"       | Implementation steps:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71361084,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713611845,"data":"       | Recursive function search(occupied, forced, placed_count, start_cell):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713613028,"data":"       | - If placed_count == D:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713615914,"data":"       |     - Check if forced subset of occupied (i.e., all forced cells are occupied). If not, reject.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71361714,"data":"       |     - Also verify condition (should hold automatically if forced subset satisfied? Actually also need to ensure that for each domino, its external neighbor cells are occupied. That's exactly forced condition. So if forced subset satisfied, condition holds.)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713619017,"data":"       |     - Return solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713626564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713639679,"data":"       | - Else, need to place more dominoes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713641437,"data":"       | - Choose a cell i that is not occupied and not forced? Actually we can choose any free cell (not occupied). But we may want to prioritize forced cells because they must be occupied eventually. So we can first ensure forced cells are covered.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71364289,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713643924,"data":"       | Thus we can pick the smallest index cell that is either forced and not occupied, else any free cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713645162,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713646895,"data":"       | When we pick cell i, we need to pair it with a neighbor j (adjacent) that is also free (not occupied). For each neighbor, we create new occupied set = occupied ∪ {i,j}, new forced set = forced ∪ external_neighbors_of_edge(i,j). Then recurse.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713648444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71364947,"data":"       | We also need to ensure we don't exceed D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713650582,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713651703,"data":"       | Potential issue: forced cells may be left unpaired because they may have no free neighbor left (due to other placements). So we need to check that each forced cell can be paired later.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713653099,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713654134,"data":"       | Thus before recursing, we can check feasibility: For each forced cell not yet occupied, there must be at least one neighbor that is not occupied (could be also forced). If none, prune.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7136555,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.713656655,"data":"       | Also we can compute lower bound on number of dominoes needed to cover forced cells: each domino can cover at most 2 forced cells, so we need at least ceil(len(forced_not_occupied)/2) dominoes remaining. So if placed_count + ceil(len(forced_not_occupied)/2) \u003e D, prune.\n"}
