,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714581443,"data":"       | Observation: For each domino, each of its four directions must have at least one occupied cell among the two adjacent cells. So each domino contributes constraints that involve up to 8 neighboring cells (some may be same across dominos). Since each occupied cell can block many dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714582961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714583985,"data":"       | We can consider covering the board with a pattern of dominos such that each empty cell is isolated (i.e., not adjacent to any domino's side pair?). Actually empty cells can be used as blocking for multiple dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714585394,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714586417,"data":"       | Goal minimal dominos: we want to maximize the number of constraints satisfied per occupied cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714587609,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714588624,"data":"       | Potential lower bound: Each domino has 4 direction constraints, each requiring at least one occupied cell among a pair. So each domino requires at least 2 occupied cells? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714589989,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714591287,"data":"       | But we can consider each occupied cell can be counted for at most how many constraints? An occupied cell can appear in many S(D,dir) pairs. For each direction pair, there are two cells; each occupied cell may belong to many such pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714592726,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714593722,"data":"       | Thus lower bound not trivial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714594784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714595807,"data":"       | Maybe we can attempt to compute minimal m using integer programming via brute force search with constraint programming using python's `itertools` and pruning with MILP style bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714598635,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714599673,"data":"       | Given board 7x7, we could enumerate all subsets of cells of size 2m (occupied) and then check if they can be partitioned into dominos and satisfy constraints. Number of subsets of size 2m from 49 is huge; but for small m maybe manageable.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714601112,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71460212,"data":"       | We can try incremental search for m from 1 upward using backtracking that simultaneously builds dominos and ensures constraints eventually satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714603387,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714604403,"data":"       | But we may need to consider that constraints involve pairs of cells; we can treat them as \"for each domino D, for each direction dir, at least one of the two cells in S(D,dir) must be occupied\". Since we are constructing the set of occupied cells (by dominos), we can check after constructing full set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714606002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714607027,"data":"       | Thus we can generate all possible matchings of size m (i.e., sets of m non-overlapping domino placements). Number of matchings of size m on 7x7 board? Could be large but maybe manageable for small m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714608379,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714609386,"data":"       | We can use algorithm to generate all matchings using recursion: pick a free cell, try pairing with each neighbor (horizontal or vertical) not yet used, continue.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714610696,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714611709,"data":"       | We can prune by symmetry? Not needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714612817,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714613828,"data":"       | We can start with m low (maybe 6) and see if any matching of size m satisfies constraints. If none, increase m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714615047,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714616047,"data":"       | Let's attempt to code a search that enumerates all matchings up to certain size using recursion, and check constraints. Use pruning maybe by early detection of unsatisfied constraints that cannot be satisfied later? Might be complex.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714617462,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714618702,"data":"       | But we can attempt brute force for m up to maybe 8 or 9. Number of matchings of size 8 on 7x7 maybe large but perhaps still manageable with pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714619988,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714620999,"data":"       | Let's attempt to implement search for m starting from low values and see if we find any solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714622192,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714623216,"data":"       | We'll need to generate all matchings of size m. Use recursion: maintain set of occupied cells (bitmask of 49 bits). At each step, find first free cell, try pairing with each adjacent free cell (up/down/left/right). Recursively continue until placed m dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71462469,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714625699,"data":"       | When we have placed m dominos (occupied cells count = 2m), we need to check constraints for each domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714628483,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714629599,"data":"       | We'll need to store list of domino placements (list of pairs of cell indices). Then compute constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714630812,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714631814,"data":"       | Implementation details:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714632852,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714633859,"data":"       | - Map cell (r,c) to index = r*7 + c (0-indexed).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714634982,"data":"       | - For each domino (a,b) where a and b adjacent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714636068,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71463712,"data":"       | - For each direction dir: define neighbor cells set S:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71463824,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714639726,"data":"       |    For direction up: cells (r-1,c) and (r-1,c2) where (r,c) and (r,c2) are the two squares of domino. For horizontal domino (c,c+1), up cells are (r-1,c) and (r-1,c+1). For vertical domino (r,r+1) same column, up cells are (r-1,c) and (r,c)?? Wait vertical domino squares are (r,c) and (r+1,c). Up cells are (r-1,c) and (r,c). Actually after sliding up, the domino would occupy (r-1,c) and (r,c). So the two cells needed to be empty for slide up are (r-1,c) and (r,c). But (r,c) is currently occupied by the domino itself. However after sliding up, the original bottom cell (r+1,c) becomes empty, and the new top cell (r-1,c) becomes occupied. For slide up to be possible, both new cells (r-1,c) and (r,c) must be empty? Wait (r,c) is currently occupied, but after slide up, the domino would occupy (r-1,c) and (r,c). Since (r,c) is currently occupied by the domino, but the domino moves, freeing (r+1,c) and occupying (r-1,c). The move is possible if (r-1,c) is empty (since (r,c) already occupied by the moving domino). Actually we need to consider that the domino moves as a rigid piece; the squares it will occupy after move must be empty except possibly those currently occupied by the domino itself (they become vacated). For sliding up, the topmost cell moves to (r-1,c) which must be empty; the bottom cell moves to (r,c) which is currently occupied by the domino's top cell. So only need (r-1,c) empty. Similarly sliding down requires (r+2,c) empty. For horizontal domino sliding left, need (r, j-1) empty; sliding right need (r, j+2) empty. For sliding up/down for horizontal domino, both cells above need to be empty (since both squares shift up). Actually horizontal domino at (i,j)-(i,j+1): sliding up requires cells (i-1,j) and (i-1,j+1) empty. Similarly sliding down requires (i+1,j) and (i+1,j+1) empty. For vertical domino sliding left requires (i,j-1) and (i+1,j-1) empty; sliding right requires (i,j+2) and (i+1,j+2) empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714643885,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714644901,"data":"       | Thus the condition for blocking each direction depends on orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714646074,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714647186,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute for each domino and each direction the set of cells that must be empty for slide to be possible. Then we need at least one of those cells to be occupied (or out of board) to block.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714648544,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714649553,"data":"       | Let's define for each domino orientation:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714650685,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71465173,"data":"       | - Horizontal domino covering (r,c) and (r,c+1):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714655148,"data":"       |    - left: cell (r,c-1) must be empty (if inside board)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714656293,"data":"       |    - right: cell (r,c+2) must be empty (if inside)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714657415,"data":"       |    - up: cells (r-1,c) and (r-1,c+1) must both be empty (if inside)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714658569,"data":"       |    - down: cells (r+1,c) and (r+1,c+1) must both be empty (if inside)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714659721,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714660774,"data":"       | - Vertical domino covering (r,c) and (r+1,c):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714661879,"data":"       |    - up: cell (r-1,c) must be empty (if inside)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714663016,"data":"       |    - down: cell (r+2,c) must be empty (if inside)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714664124,"data":"       |    - left: cells (r,c-1) and (r+1,c-1) must both be empty (if inside)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714665276,"data":"       |    - right: cells (r,c+2) and (r+1,c+2) must both be empty (if inside)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714666449,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714667763,"data":"       | Thus for each direction, we define a set of required empty cells (size 1 or 2). To block, we need at least one cell in that set to be occupied (or the set is partially out of board, meaning slide impossible). Actually if any cell in the required set is out of board, slide impossible because domino cannot move partially outside board; the whole domino must stay within board. So if any required cell is out of board, slide is blocked automatically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714669564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714670597,"data":"       | Thus we need for each direction where all required cells are inside board, at least one of those cells is occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714671836,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714672871,"data":"       | Now we can implement check.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714673965,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714675004,"data":"       | Let's code search for minimal m using backtracking with pruning maybe using lower bound based on remaining empty cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714676279,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714677287,"data":"       | We'll start with m from 1 upward until we find solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714678422,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714679423,"data":"       | But number of matchings grows quickly; we need to prune heavily.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714680569,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714681596,"data":"       | We can incorporate constraints early: For each placed domino, we can already check if any direction is already impossible to block given current occupied cells plus potential future cells (since we can still occupy more cells). Hard.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714683004,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714684039,"data":"       | But maybe minimal m is relatively high, maybe around 12-15. Then enumeration of matchings of that size may be huge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714685267,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714686275,"data":"       | Alternative: Use integer programming via brute force with `python-mip`? Not installed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714688825,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714689859,"data":"       | Maybe we can use SAT solving by converting to CNF and using python's `pycosat`? Not installed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71469107,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71469213,"data":"       | We could use BFS search on state space of occupied cells and dominos, using heuristic to add dominos gradually and check constraints after each addition for already placed dominos (some directions may already be blocked). However we can add more dominos later to block remaining directions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714693664,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714695,"data":"       | Thus we can treat problem as constructing a set of dominos such that after final set, constraints hold. So we can use backtracking: at each step, we have placed some dominos; we keep track of which direction constraints are already satisfied for each placed domino (by currently occupied cells). For those not yet satisfied, we need future dominos to occupy at least one of the required cells. So we can keep a list of unsatisfied constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714696775,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714697828,"data":"       | We can prune if remaining empty cells insufficient to satisfy all unsatisfied constraints (i.e., each future domino can occupy at most 2 cells, each can satisfy at most some number of constraints). Hard.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714699189,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714700238,"data":"       | Given time, maybe easier to use MILP via `z3`? Not installed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71470141,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714702485,"data":"       | Maybe we can attempt to use brute force with `python` and `itertools.combinations` for m up to maybe 10, see if any solution exists. Let's try to implement enumeration of matchings using recursion but with early termination after finding one solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714703944,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714704969,"data":"       | We'll start with m=6 and see.assistantanalysis to=python codefrom functools import lru_cache\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714706152,"data":"       | \n"}
