,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714935911,"data":"     [Reasoning] (6134 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714936988,"data":"       | analysisIndeed, because left/right/up/down cells are empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714939569,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714940627,"data":"       | Now we need to search for minimal m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71494175,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714942834,"data":"       | We'll implement recursive backtracking generating matchings of size m, but we can prune early by checking constraints for placed dominos that are already impossible to satisfy given current occupied cells (i.e., if for any direction, required cells are all inside board and none of them are occupied yet, but could be occupied later by future dominos). That's not a failure yet because future dominos could occupy those cells. So we cannot prune based on that.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714944636,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714945684,"data":"       | But we can prune if a required cell is already known to be empty (i.e., cannot be occupied later because it's not free? Actually if a cell is already occupied by a domino, that's okay; if it's not occupied yet but still free, it could be occupied later. So no early prune.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71494721,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714948256,"data":"       | Thus naive recursion will explore all matchings of size m. Number of matchings of size m can be huge. But maybe minimal m is relatively large, maybe 12 or more, making search impossible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71494961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714950692,"data":"       | Alternative: Use heuristic to construct a solution with relatively few dominos, then we can try to see if we can reduce further.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714951968,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714953023,"data":"       | Maybe we can think of constructing a pattern: place dominos along a \"checkerboard\" such that each domino is surrounded by other dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714954362,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714955701,"data":"       | Consider covering all cells except one with dominos (i.e., 24 dominos). Then obviously all dominos are blocked because board fully covered except one empty cell, but some dominos may still be able to slide into that empty cell? Let's test: If board is fully tiled except one empty cell, a domino adjacent to empty cell may slide into it if the other cell needed is also empty? For a domino to slide, the required cells must be empty. If only one empty cell exists, slide may be impossible because slide requires either one cell (for horizontal left/right) or two cells (for up/down). For a horizontal domino sliding left, only one cell needed empty. So if there is a single empty cell adjacent to a domino's left side, that domino could slide left into it (assuming the other cell of domino vacates). So full tiling except one empty cell may still allow moves.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714958166,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714959206,"data":"       | Thus we need to design arrangement that blocks all moves.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714960357,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714961374,"data":"       | Potential minimal arrangement: maybe place dominos in a \"cross\" shape to block each other.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71496264,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714963648,"data":"       | We can attempt to use computational search with heuristic: start with empty board, add dominos greedily to block unsatisfied constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71496491,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714967505,"data":"       | Algorithm: while there exists a domino (any possible placement) that is not blocked (i.e., could slide), add a domino that blocks it, maybe choose one that blocks many.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714968855,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714969901,"data":"       | But we also need to ensure final arrangement has no domino that can slide, but also we must include all placed dominos themselves blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714971204,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714972227,"data":"       | We can treat it as a hitting set problem: we need to select a set of cells (occupied) such that for each potential domino (including those we place), the blocking condition holds. However we also must ensure that the set of occupied cells can be partitioned into domino pairs, and that each domino we include is also blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714973844,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714974862,"data":"       | But we can maybe search for minimal m using MILP solved via brute force using `python`'s `itertools` with branch-and-bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714976099,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714977126,"data":"       | We could use `python`'s `pyscipopt`? Not installed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714978246,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714979275,"data":"       | Maybe we can use `z3-solver`? Not installed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714980378,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714981385,"data":"       | Thus we need to implement custom branch-and-bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714982554,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714983872,"data":"       | We can treat each cell as variable 0/1 (occupied). Constraint: total occupied cells = 2m. Additional constraint: there exists a perfect matching covering occupied cells. This matching existence can be enforced by ensuring that the graph induced by occupied cells has a perfect matching. That's a global property not easy to encode linearly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714985478,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714986482,"data":"       | But we could incorporate matching by directly selecting edges (dominos) as variables e. That's what we have.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714987697,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714988732,"data":"       | Thus we need to solve binary integer programming with 84 variables and constraints: each cell incident sum \u003c=1 (non-overlap). For each edge e and each direction where required cells inside board, we need sum_{edges incident to those cells} \u003e= e. That's linear.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714992904,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714993963,"data":"       | Thus we have linear constraints; we can solve using branch-and-bound with linear programming relaxation using `scipy.optimize.linprog`? That's for continuous, not integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714995316,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714996339,"data":"       | But we could implement branch-and-bound ourselves using recursion: choose an edge variable, branch on 0/1, propagate constraints (like unit propagation). Use pruning via lower bound: remaining edges needed to achieve at least some minimal number.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714997835,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.714998857,"data":"       | We can also use heuristic search like BFS on number of edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715001445,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715002513,"data":"       | Given small size, we can attempt to use `python`'s `itertools.combinations` to enumerate subsets of edges of size m, but with pruning using non-overlap.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715003826,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715004872,"data":"       | We can generate all matchings of size m using recursion that ensures non-overlap. That's similar to earlier plan.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715006117,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715007161,"data":"       | Number of matchings of size m grows combinatorially. But we can limit to small m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715008365,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715009398,"data":"       | Let's try to implement recursion that enumerates matchings of size up to maybe 8 and see if any valid solution exists.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715010672,"data":"       | \n"}
