,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715096047,"data":"       | We can incorporate early checking: after placing some edges, we can check if any already placed edge is already unsatisfiable given current occupied cells (i.e., for some direction, required cells are all inside board and none of them are occupied yet, but could be occupied later, so not unsatisfiable). Actually unsatisfiable would be if required cells are all inside board and all of them are already known to be empty and cannot be occupied later because they are not free? But they are currently free (unoccupied). They could be occupied later. So cannot prune.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715098054,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715099077,"data":"       | Thus early pruning not helpful.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7151002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71510124,"data":"       | Maybe we need to incorporate constraint that each direction must have at least one occupied cell among required cells. This can be seen as each direction pair must intersect occupied set. So we can think of covering required pairs with occupied cells. This is like a hitting set problem where each required pair is a hyperedge of size 2 (or 1). We need to select occupied cells that hit all required pairs for the chosen dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715102977,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71510421,"data":"       | Thus we can first decide which cells will be occupied (set O). Then we need to ensure there exists a perfect matching covering O (i.e., O can be partitioned into adjacent pairs). So we can search over subsets O of size 2m, check if O can be tiled by dominos (i.e., has a perfect matching). That's a graph matching problem, which can be solved via algorithm (maximum matching). Then we need to ensure that for each domino in the matching (i.e., each edge in the matching), its direction constraints are satisfied (i.e., each required pair intersects O). However note that the constraints depend on the specific matching edges (the dominos). So we need to find a matching such that for each edge in matching, constraints hold.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715106447,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715107513,"data":"       | Thus we could iterate over all matchings of size m (as before) but we can also use algorithm to generate matchings via augmenting path search.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715108789,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715109846,"data":"       | Maybe we can use networkx's maximum matching enumeration? Not available.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715111079,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715112122,"data":"       | Alternative: Use backtracking but also incorporate direction constraints early: after placing some edges, we can compute for each placed edge the directions that are still unsatisfied (i.e., required cells not yet occupied). For each such unsatisfied direction, we can note that at least one of the required cells must be occupied later. This creates a set of future required cells. We can maintain a list of \"must occupy at least one from each pair\". This is like a hitting set for remaining cells. We can compute a lower bound on number of additional dominos needed to satisfy all these pending constraints. For each pending pair, we need at least one cell, but a single future domino can occupy up to 2 cells, potentially satisfying up to two pending pairs if they share a cell. Hard to compute lower bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715116434,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71511774,"data":"       | Simplify: treat each pending pair as requiring at least one new occupied cell (i.e., at least one cell from the pair that is not already occupied). So the number of new cells needed is at least the size of a minimum hitting set for these pairs. That's NP-hard, but we can approximate lower bound by using the fact that each new domino adds at most 2 new cells, so we need at least ceil(pending_pairs/2) new dominos? Not accurate because one cell can satisfy multiple pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715119563,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71512058,"data":"       | But we can compute a simple bound: number of yet-uncovered required cells (unique cells) that are not already occupied, we need at least ceil(count/2) new dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715121879,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715122885,"data":"       | Thus we can prune if current placed dominos + ceil(remaining_needed/2) \u003e target m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715124122,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715125144,"data":"       | Let's implement recursion that attempts to find solution for given m, with pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71512632,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715127327,"data":"       | We'll need to maintain:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715128377,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715129394,"data":"       | - occupied_mask (bits)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715130467,"data":"       | - placed_edges list\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715131564,"data":"       | - pending_pairs list: for each placed edge and each direction where required cells are inside board and none of those cells are currently occupied, we add the pair (tuple of cell indices). If at least one cell already occupied, direction satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715133036,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715134072,"data":"       | During recursion, when we add a new edge, we update occupied_mask and recompute pending pairs for all placed edges (including new). But recomputing each time may be okay.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715135387,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715136414,"data":"       | We also need to consider that new edge may satisfy some pending pairs (by occupying cells). So we need to remove those satisfied pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715137678,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715138704,"data":"       | Thus we can maintain a set of unsatisfied direction pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715139844,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715140877,"data":"       | Implementation details:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715143312,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715144415,"data":"       | Define function unsatisfied_pairs(placed_edges, occupied_mask) returning list of pairs (tuple of cell indices) that are still unsatisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715145726,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715146767,"data":"       | We can compute each time.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715147848,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715149192,"data":"       | Then compute lower bound: let U be set of cells appearing in unsatisfied pairs that are not occupied yet (i.e., cells not in occupied_mask). Minimum number of additional dominos needed is at least ceil(|U|/2). Because each new domino can occupy at most 2 new cells, and each new cell can help satisfy multiple pairs, but lower bound using unique cells is safe? Actually each new domino can occupy at most 2 cells, but those cells could be from U. So at least ceil(|U|/2) new dominos needed to add those cells. However a new domino could also occupy a cell that is already occupied (can't). So yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715151219,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.715152241,"data":"       | Thus we can prune if len(placed_edges) + ceil(|U|/2) \u003e m.\n"}
