,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7161767,"data":"       | Our search for m=21 timed out; we need better algorithm or more pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716177944,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716179167,"data":"       | We can try to incorporate symmetry reduction: board has rotations and reflections; we can fix some initial placements to break symmetry, e.g., require that cell (0,0) is occupied by a domino (or not). But we need to search for existence, not enumeration.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716180762,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716181863,"data":"       | We could also try to use heuristic to find a solution for m=21 using random local search: start with random matching of size 21, then try to fix unsatisfied constraints by adding/removing dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716183334,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716185942,"data":"       | But we need existence; maybe minimal m is 21 (i.e., you need at least 21 dominos). Let's attempt to find a solution with 21 dominos using heuristic search.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716187357,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716188487,"data":"       | We'll use simulated annealing or greedy improvement: start with random matching of size 21 (i.e., 42 occupied cells). Then evaluate unsatisfied constraints (list of direction pairs not blocked). Then try to modify placement: e.g., randomly replace a domino with another covering some needed cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716190118,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716191204,"data":"       | We also need to maintain matching property (no overlap). Could use local moves: remove one domino, then try to place a new domino covering a needed cell, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716192633,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716193732,"data":"       | Simpler: Use backtracking but with heuristic ordering and maybe use constraint programming library? Not available.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716195067,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716196436,"data":"       | But we can attempt to use `python`'s `itertools.combinations` to choose which cells are empty (since total cells 49, we need 49-2m empty cells). For m=21, occupied cells = 42, empty cells = 7. So we need to choose 7 empty cells. Then we need to tile the remaining 42 cells with dominos (perfect matching). That's a standard problem: given a set of blocked cells (empty), does the board with those holes have a perfect domino tiling? That's a known condition: board bipartite; a perfect matching exists if number of black and white cells are equal (which they are if empty cells count of each color equal). Since total board has 25 black, 24 white. Occupied cells must have equal numbers black and white, i.e., the empty cells must have difference of 1 (since board has imbalance). For 7 empty cells, we need black empty - white empty = 1 (since total black - white = 1). So among the 7 empty cells, there must be 4 black and 3 white (or vice versa). So we can enumerate subsets of 7 cells with that color distribution, then check if remaining cells have a perfect matching and also satisfy blocking constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716199447,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716200523,"data":"       | Number of subsets of size 7 from 49 is C(49,7)=~85 million, too many. But we can prune using matching existence.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716201826,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716202939,"data":"       | But we can use algorithm to find a perfect matching given a set of empty cells quickly using network flow. However enumerating all subsets still huge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71620435,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716205442,"data":"       | Maybe we can search for solution with m=21 using backtracking that builds matching while also ensuring constraints, similar to earlier but maybe more efficient because many cells will be occupied (high density). The earlier search for m=20 took 2.3 seconds, m=21 may take longer but maybe still manageable with some optimization.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716207132,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71620823,"data":"       | We can improve pruning bound: unsatisfied pairs may be many, but we can compute lower bound more tightly: each new domino adds exactly 2 new occupied cells, but also can satisfy many unsatisfied pairs. However our bound using unique cells needed may be weak; but for high density, unsatisfied pairs may be few, so bound may be strong.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716211685,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.716212882,"data":"       | Let's try to run search for m=21 with some time limit maybe 60 seconds.\n"}
