,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717010839,"data":"     [Turn 7]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717011881,"data":"     [Reasoning] (27558 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717012955,"data":"       | analysisThe recursion still heavy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717014035,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717016556,"data":"       | We can try to use a SAT solver? Not available.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717017705,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717018895,"data":"       | Alternative: Use integer programming with pulp not available; we could implement using python's `itertools.combinations` for small m and test all matchings of size m. For m maybe 5 or 6, number of matchings may be manageable.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717020324,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717021382,"data":"       | We can generate all matchings of size m using recursion building matchings incrementally (like we already did). But we need to prune heavily.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717022685,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717023721,"data":"       | Maybe minimal m is relatively large, maybe around 14? Then brute force is impossible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717024932,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717025938,"data":"       | Let's think analytically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717027042,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717028105,"data":"       | Observation: For each domino, its two ends must be blocked. Each interior cell (not on edge) can serve as a blocker for up to two neighboring dominos (one on each side). Actually a cell can be part of a domino; that domino occupies two cells, each of which may block adjacent ends of neighboring dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717029625,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717030685,"data":"       | Consider the graph where vertices are cells, edges are possible domino placements. A placed domino corresponds to an edge selected. The condition that each selected edge is \"blocked\" at both its incident vertices' opposite sides (i.e., the two cells adjacent along the line extending the edge). Equivalent to each selected edge must have both its incident vertices have degree at least 2 in the selected subgraph? Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717032518,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717033561,"data":"       | Take a horizontal edge between cells A (left) and B (right). The left end beyond A is cell L (left neighbor of A). The right end beyond B is cell R (right neighbor of B). For the edge to be blocked, cells L and R must be occupied (by any selected edge). Those cells could be part of edges that are incident to them. So we need that each endpoint cell of the edge has at least one adjacent occupied cell in the outward direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717035319,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717036335,"data":"       | But not exactly degree condition because L may be occupied by a vertical domino that includes L and some other cell not necessarily adjacent to A. So L is just occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717037659,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717038814,"data":"       | Thus each selected edge requires that both outward neighboring cells are occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717040015,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717041035,"data":"       | Thus we can think of a configuration of occupied cells (the union of all domino cells). Let S be set of occupied cells. For each domino (edge) e = {u,v} (adjacent cells), we require that the neighbor of u in direction away from v (call u') is in S (or outside board) and neighbor of v in direction away from u (v') is in S (or outside). So S must be such that for each edge e in the selected matching, the outward neighbors of its endpoints are also in S (or out of board). This is a local condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717044349,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717045401,"data":"       | Thus we can think of constructing a set S of occupied cells (size 2m) such that for each occupied cell that is part of a domino, the outward neighbor in the direction of the domino is also occupied (since that neighbor is the other cell of the domino). Wait that's already true because the domino occupies both cells. The outward neighbor beyond each cell must be occupied (maybe by another domino). So each occupied cell that is at interior of board must have its outward neighbor also occupied (maybe same as other cell of same domino? No outward neighbor is beyond the domino, not the adjacent cell within domino). So each occupied cell must have its outward neighbor occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717047585,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717048605,"data":"       | Thus we can view as a directed condition: For each occupied cell that is part of a domino oriented horizontally, its left neighbor (if cell is leftmost) must be occupied; its right neighbor (if cell is rightmost) must be occupied. For vertical orientation, top neighbor and bottom neighbor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717050105,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717051125,"data":"       | But note each domino has two cells; for the left cell, outward neighbor is left side; for the right cell, outward neighbor is right side. So each cell's outward neighbor is distinct from the other cell of same domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717052525,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717053689,"data":"       | Thus each occupied cell must have its outward neighbor occupied (or be at board edge). So each occupied cell must have a neighbor in the opposite direction also occupied. This suggests that the occupied cells form a \"domino chain\" that cannot have endpoints interiorly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717055265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717056275,"data":"       | Thus interior occupied cells must be part of a chain that continues outward to edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717057454,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717058449,"data":"       | Thus minimal number of dominos may correspond to covering a set of cells that are all adjacent to edge via outward direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717059722,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717060726,"data":"       | Consider placing dominos along a line from edge to edge. Example: place a horizontal domino in row 0 (top row) covering cells (0,0)-(0,1). Its left end beyond (0,-1) is out of board (blocked), right end beyond (0,2) is interior cell (0,2) which must be occupied. So we need a domino covering (0,2) maybe horizontally covering (0,2)-(0,3) etc. This suggests we need a chain of dominos covering contiguous cells along a row, starting at edge and extending to edge, such that each domino's outward neighbor is the next cell in the chain.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717062635,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717063635,"data":"       | Similarly for vertical chains.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717064719,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717065738,"data":"       | Thus minimal configuration may consist of a set of \"paths\" of dominos from edge to edge, with each domino covering two consecutive cells along the path. The path length (in cells) must be even (since dominos cover pairs). The ends of the path are at board edges (so outward neighbor out of board). This ensures each domino's outward neighbor is occupied (by next domino) except at ends where it's edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717069597,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717070975,"data":"       | Thus each path can be thought of as a sequence of cells covering a straight line (horizontal or vertical) from one edge to the opposite edge (or maybe same edge if chain loops? but loops would still satisfy). However if path turns, orientation changes; need to ensure condition for each domino still satisfied: For a domino oriented horizontally, its outward neighbor cells are left and right. If the chain turns at a cell, the next domino may be vertical covering that cell and its neighbor up/down. But then the outward neighbor of the horizontal domino's right cell (which is the turning cell) would be occupied by the vertical domino (since that cell is part of vertical domino). However the outward neighbor of the right cell is to the right, not down. Wait orientation matters: For horizontal domino covering cells A (left) and B (right). The outward neighbor of B is cell to the right of B, not the cell below. So a turn cannot be satisfied because the cell to the right of B would be empty if we turn downwards. So turning is not allowed. Therefore each domino chain must be straight line, no turns.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717073805,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717074831,"data":"       | Thus each domino must be part of a straight line of dominos all oriented same direction, covering a contiguous segment of cells that extends to both edges (or at least each domino's outward neighbor is occupied by another domino in same line). So essentially we need to fill some rows or columns completely (or partially) with a sequence of dominos covering consecutive cells, such that the ends of the sequence are at board edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717076598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717077618,"data":"       | If a sequence does not reach both edges, then the domino at interior of sequence whose outward neighbor beyond its far end would be empty, violating condition. So each sequence must extend to both edges (or at least outward neighbor for each end is edge). That means the sequence must start at left edge (or top edge) and end at right edge (or bottom edge). So a horizontal chain must start at column 0 and end at column 6 (since board width 7). Similarly vertical chain must start at row 0 and end at row 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717079492,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717080494,"data":"       | Thus a horizontal chain covering a whole row from column 0 to 6 (7 cells). But 7 is odd, cannot be tiled by 1x2 dominos fully. However we can have a chain that covers the row except maybe one cell at edge? Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717081915,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717083228,"data":"       | Suppose we have a horizontal chain covering cells from column 0 to column 5 (6 cells) leaving column 6 empty. The domino covering cells (0,4)-(0,5) has right outward neighbor (0,6) which is empty, not edge (since column 6 is inside board). So not allowed. So chain must reach column 6 edge. Since width is odd, we cannot tile entire row with dominos (needs even number of cells). So a straight horizontal chain from left edge to right edge cannot be formed using only 1x2 dominos because length 7 odd.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717085058,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717086091,"data":"       | Thus we cannot have a straight horizontal chain covering entire row. Similarly vertical chain length 7 odd cannot be fully tiled.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717087355,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717088418,"data":"       | Thus we need to consider that a domino's outward neighbor may be occupied by a domino oriented perpendicular? Let's reconsider earlier conclusion about turning not allowed. Perhaps a turn could satisfy condition if outward neighbor cell is part of a perpendicular domino that occupies that cell (but the outward neighbor is a specific cell adjacent to the domino's end, not the cell itself). For a horizontal domino covering cells (i,j) and (i,j+1). Its right outward neighbor is (i,j+2). If we have a vertical domino covering (i,j+2) and (i+1,j+2) (or (i-1,j+2)), then the cell (i,j+2) is occupied, satisfying the condition. So we can have a chain that goes straight but may have a vertical domino occupying the outward neighbor cell, not necessarily continuing horizontally. However the vertical domino's own outward neighbor (say its top or bottom) must also be occupied. So the vertical domino could be oriented such that its outward neighbor is again a horizontal domino, etc. This suggests we can have a \"checkerboard\" pattern where dominos interlock.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717092868,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717093912,"data":"       | Thus the condition is not requiring same orientation chain, just that each outward neighbor cell is occupied by some domino (any orientation). So we can have a pattern where dominos interlock like a \"brick wall\" arrangement.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717095391,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717096421,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominos while satisfying that each outward neighbor cell of each domino is occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717097669,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717098842,"data":"       | Observation: each domino contributes two occupied cells. Each occupied cell can serve as outward neighbor for at most one adjacent domino (the one whose outward neighbor is that cell). Actually a cell can be outward neighbor for up to two dominos: one on its left (if a horizontal domino's right end neighbor is that cell) and one on its right (if a horizontal domino's left end neighbor). Similarly vertical. But due to non-overlap, each cell can be part of at most one domino itself, but can be neighbor for up to 4 different dominos (left, right, top, bottom). However we only need each domino's two outward neighbor cells to be occupied; each occupied cell may satisfy up to two neighbor requirements (one for each side). So we can think of a covering where each domino's two required neighbor cells are provided by other dominos, possibly sharing.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717101267,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717102335,"data":"       | Thus we can think of a graph where each domino has two \"needs\" (outward neighbor cells). Each occupied cell can satisfy up to two needs (if it's the outward neighbor of two dominos on opposite sides). So minimal number of dominos may be when each occupied cell satisfies two needs, maximizing sharing. Since each domino needs 2 cells, total needs = 2m. Each occupied cell can satisfy at most 2 needs, so we need at least m occupied cells to satisfy 2m needs. But we have exactly 2m occupied cells (since each domino occupies 2 cells). So we need each occupied cell to satisfy exactly two needs (i.e., be outward neighbor for two dominos). That suggests a perfect sharing configuration where each occupied cell is the outward neighbor for exactly two dominos (maybe on opposite sides). This would be optimal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717105737,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717106752,"data":"       | Thus we need a configuration where each occupied cell is adjacent to exactly two domino ends (one on each side) such that they are opposite directions. This resembles a \"perfect matching\" of neighbor relationships.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717108218,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717109244,"data":"       | If we can achieve that, then the number of dominos could be minimal maybe equal to number of cells needed to satisfy constraints? But we still need to place dominos; we cannot have zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717112345,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717113379,"data":"       | Let's try to construct such a configuration manually.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717114529,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717115562,"data":"       | Consider a checkerboard pattern of dominos oriented alternately, like a \"brick wall\" where each domino's outward neighbor cells are occupied by adjacent dominos oriented perpendicular.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717116974,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717118115,"data":"       | Example: Place a horizontal domino at (i,j)-(i,j+1). Its right outward neighbor (i,j+2) could be part of a vertical domino covering (i,j+2)-(i+1,j+2). Its left outward neighbor (i,j-1) could be part of another vertical domino covering (i,j-1)-(i+1,j-1). Then the vertical domino's outward neighbor upward/downward must be occupied as well.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717119741,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717120788,"data":"       | We can attempt to tile a 2x3 rectangle with 3 dominos such that each domino is blocked: e.g., arrangement:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717122011,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717123024,"data":"       | Cells:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717124079,"data":"       | (0,0)-(0,1) horizontal domino H1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717125171,"data":"       | (0,2)-(1,2) vertical domino V1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717126285,"data":"       | (1,0)-(1,1) horizontal domino H2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717127368,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717128388,"data":"       | Check blocking: H1 left neighbor out of board? column -1? Not, it's at column 0 left neighbor out of board (since column 0 left is -1). So left side blocked by edge. Right neighbor (0,2) is occupied by V1 (vertical). Good. H2 left neighbor (1,-1) out of board (edge). Right neighbor (1,2) occupied by V1. V1 top neighbor ( -1? Actually V1 top cell is (0,2); outward neighbor upward is (-1,2) out of board (edge). Bottom neighbor of V1 is (2,2) must be occupied; not in this 2x3 rectangle. So V1 not blocked at bottom. So need another domino covering (2,2) maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717130341,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717131365,"data":"       | Thus we need to extend vertical domino chain downwards to edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717132515,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717133552,"data":"       | Thus maybe we need to fill entire columns with vertical dominos to block each other.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717134709,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717135822,"data":"       | Observation: a vertical domino at top row has top outward neighbor out of board (edge). Its bottom outward neighbor must be occupied. So we can chain vertical dominos downwards: each domino's bottom neighbor is the top cell of the next domino. That would satisfy bottom neighbor occupied. However bottommost domino's bottom neighbor would be out of board? Wait bottommost domino at row 5-6 (since rows 0-6). Its bottom neighbor is row 7 out of board (edge). So chain of vertical dominos covering entire column from row 0 to row 6 would have length 7 cells, odd, cannot be tiled fully. So we need some horizontal dominos to fill the odd cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717138031,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717140548,"data":"       | Thus we need to mix orientations to handle odd dimensions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717141715,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717142735,"data":"       | Let's attempt to design a minimal configuration using a \"snake\" covering board cells such that each domino's outward neighbor is the next cell in snake, and the snake ends at board edges on both ends. Since total number of cells occupied is 2m (even). The board has odd total cells 49, but we only occupy some subset.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717144357,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717145375,"data":"       | Goal: minimize m, i.e., minimize number of occupied cells (2m). Since each domino requires its outward neighbor cells to be occupied, the set of occupied cells must be \"closed\" under outward neighbor operation: For any occupied cell that is part of a domino, the cell beyond it in the direction of the domino must also be occupied (or edge). This suggests that the occupied set must be a union of \"paths\" that start and end at board edges, with each step of length 1 (cell) but dominos occupy pairs of adjacent cells along the path.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717147378,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717148471,"data":"       | Thus each path length in cells must be even (since dominos cover pairs). The path endpoints are at board edges (so outward neighbor beyond endpoint is edge). So each path must have an even number of cells between two edges inclusive? Let's think: Suppose we have a horizontal path from left edge column -1 (outside) to right edge column 7 (outside). The cells inside board are columns 0..6 (7 cells). The path includes 7 cells, odd. But the dominos cover pairs of adjacent cells; we cannot cover odd number of cells with dominos. However the path may have a branching where a domino at some point is vertical, causing the path to shift rows, thereby altering parity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717150592,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717151608,"data":"       | Thus we need a more complex structure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717152698,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717153727,"data":"       | Maybe minimal m is relatively large, maybe close to half the board? Let's attempt to compute lower bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717154929,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717156125,"data":"       | Each domino has two ends. Each end either touches board edge or a cell occupied by another domino. Count total ends that are not on board edge. Let E be number of ends that are interior (need a neighbor). Each interior end corresponds to a requirement that a particular cell be occupied. Since each occupied cell can satisfy at most two interior ends (one from each side). So we have E ≤ 2 * (number of occupied cells) = 4m. But each domino contributes 2 ends, so total ends = 2m. So E ≤ 2m (since each end either interior or edge). Actually interior ends count is ≤ 2m (since each domino has at most 2 interior ends). That's trivial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717158325,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717159401,"data":"       | Better bound: For each interior occupied cell (i.e., cell not on board edge) that is occupied, it can serve as neighbor for at most 2 ends (since it has at most two opposite sides). So each interior occupied cell can block at most 2 interior ends. Edge cells can block at most 1 interior end (since one side is outside board). So we can derive lower bound on number of occupied cells needed to block interior ends.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717161145,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717162205,"data":"       | Let’s denote:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717164879,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717165921,"data":"       | - Let N_int be number of occupied cells that are interior (not on board edge).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717167122,"data":"       | - Let N_edge be number of occupied cells on board edge (i.e., cells with at least one side out of board). Edge includes cells on perimeter (rows 0 or 6 or columns 0 or 6). Some cells are corners counted once.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717168515,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717169529,"data":"       | Total occupied cells = 2m = N_int + N_edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717170667,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717171758,"data":"       | Each interior occupied cell can block at most 2 interior ends (since it can be neighbor for at most two dominos on opposite sides). Edge occupied cell can block at most 1 interior end (since the side outward is edge, not interior). Actually an edge cell could block interior ends on the side pointing inward; the outward side is edge, which doesn't need blocking. So each edge occupied cell can block at most 1 interior end.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717173468,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717174592,"data":"       | Now total number of interior ends that need blocking is equal to total ends minus those that are already blocked by board edge. Each domino has 2 ends; each end that lies on board edge (i.e., the outward neighbor of that end is outside board) does not need interior neighbor. For a domino, if its leftmost cell is at column 0, then left end is edge; similarly rightmost at column 6, right end edge; top row 0 top edge; bottom row 6 bottom edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717176355,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717177378,"data":"       | Thus we need to count how many ends are interior. To minimize m, we want as many ends as possible to be on edges, reducing interior blocking requirement. So we should place dominos adjacent to board edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717178771,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717179835,"data":"       | Maximum number of ends on edges per domino? Up to 2 if domino lies along an edge (both ends on edges). For a horizontal domino placed at top row (row 0) covering columns j,j+1. Its top neighbor is edge (since vertical direction not considered). Wait ends are left and right, not top/bottom. Edge condition refers to outward neighbor horizontally for horizontal domino, vertically for vertical domino. So a horizontal domino placed at top row still has left/right outward neighbors; its top/bottom not relevant. So being on top row doesn't affect its ends. So only being at leftmost column (j=0) gives left end edge; being at rightmost column (j+1=6) gives right end edge. So a horizontal domino can have at most 1 edge end (if placed at column 0 or 5? Actually if placed at columns 0-1, left end edge; if placed at columns 5-6, right end edge). It cannot have both ends on edges because width odd. Similarly vertical domino can have at most 1 edge end (if placed at row 0 or row 5). So each domino can contribute at most 1 edge end.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717182548,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717183548,"data":"       | Thus each domino contributes at most 1 edge end, meaning at least one interior end per domino. So total interior ends \u003e= m. Since each domino has at most one edge end, the other end must be interior (unless both ends interior). So minimal interior ends is m (if each domino has exactly one edge end). So we need to block at least m interior ends.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717185215,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717187825,"data":"       | Now each interior occupied cell can block at most 2 interior ends; edge occupied cells block at most 1 interior end. To minimize number of occupied cells (2m), we want to use interior cells efficiently.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717189264,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717190425,"data":"       | Let’s denote I = number of interior occupied cells, B = number of edge occupied cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717191648,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717192701,"data":"       | We have I + B = 2m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717193798,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717194824,"data":"       | We need to block at least m interior ends.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717195934,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717196947,"data":"       | Maximum interior ends blocked = 2I + 1*B (since each interior cell can block up to 2, edge cell up to 1). So we require:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717198179,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717199181,"data":"       | 2I + B \u003e= m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717200322,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717201328,"data":"       | But B = 2m - I, substitute:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717202409,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717203412,"data":"       | 2I + (2m - I) \u003e= m =\u003e I + 2m \u003e= m =\u003e I \u003e= -m (always true). Not helpful.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717204802,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717205812,"data":"       | But we also have each interior occupied cell must be part of a domino; each domino contributes exactly two occupied cells; we cannot arbitrarily choose interior vs edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717207118,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717208122,"data":"       | We need more precise combinatorial analysis.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717209215,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717210285,"data":"       | Maybe we can try to find minimal m via computational search using ILP via brute force with pruning more efficient.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717211521,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717212558,"data":"       | We can use Python's `python-constraint`? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717213674,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717214691,"data":"       | But we can implement a SAT solver using python's `pysat`? Not available.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717215858,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717216879,"data":"       | Alternative: Use BFS search over configurations of occupied cells (set of cells) rather than dominos. Since each domino occupies two adjacent cells, we need to select a matching. Could use algorithm to enumerate matchings of size m using recursion but with heuristics.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717218358,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717219381,"data":"       | Maybe we can use integer programming via `sympy`? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717220525,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717221537,"data":"       | We could attempt to use `python` to call external solver? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717222705,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717223725,"data":"       | Maybe we can do exhaustive search for m up to 8 using recursion with pruning and see if any solution exists. If not, increase m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717226719,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717227764,"data":"       | But earlier attempt timed out after 30 seconds for m up to 12. Could optimize.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717228925,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717229958,"data":"       | We can use bitmask representation for board (49 bits). Represent placements as bitmask of 49 bits (two bits set). Use recursion with bitmask operations for fast overlap checking.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717231302,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717232432,"data":"       | Also we can precompute for each placement its two outward neighbor cells as bitmask (maybe single bits). Then we can maintain occupied mask and also a mask of \"blocked ends satisfied\".\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717233841,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717234869,"data":"       | We need to ensure after final placement, for each domino placed, both outward neighbor cells are occupied or out of board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717236118,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717237131,"data":"       | We can incorporate that condition after full placement.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717238264,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717239302,"data":"       | Search space may still be large but we can prune using bound: need at least m interior ends; each interior occupied cell can satisfy at most 2 interior ends; each edge occupied cell at most 1. However we can compute minimal number of occupied cells needed to satisfy interior ends = ceil(m/2) maybe? Because each interior occupied cell can satisfy two ends, so to satisfy m interior ends we need at least ceil(m/2) interior cells. Since total occupied cells = 2m, we have plenty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717241122,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717242142,"data":"       | Thus bound not strong.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717243192,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717244208,"data":"       | Maybe minimal m is relatively large, maybe around 14 or 15. Then search would be huge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717245387,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717246397,"data":"       | Let's try to use heuristic algorithm to find a configuration with small m, maybe using simulated annealing or greedy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717247605,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717248612,"data":"       | We can treat each cell as variable whether occupied, and we need to partition occupied cells into dominos (matching). Also each domino's outward neighbor cells must be occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717249938,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717250965,"data":"       | We can try to use local search: start with random matching, then try to add/remove dominos to satisfy constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717252182,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71725318,"data":"       | But we need minimal m; we can attempt to find a feasible configuration with small m, then try to reduce.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717254375,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717255395,"data":"       | Let's attempt to use integer programming via `pyscipopt`? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717256555,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7172576,"data":"       | Maybe we can use `python` to call `z3`? Not available.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71726,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71726105,"data":"       | Alternative: Use `networkx` to model as a directed graph and find minimal matching satisfying constraints via flow? Could be formulated as edge cover problem.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717262358,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717263518,"data":"       | We can think of each cell as node. For a domino covering nodes u,v (adjacent), we need that the neighbor of u opposite v (call u') and neighbor of v opposite u (v') are also occupied. This essentially means that for each edge selected, the two nodes opposite must also be covered by some selected edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717265135,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71726614,"data":"       | Thus we can define a directed graph where each node has two opposite neighbor nodes (depending on orientation). This is like a constraint satisfaction problem.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717267457,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717268508,"data":"       | Maybe we can treat each cell as having a \"partner\" (the other cell of its domino). Then the condition becomes: For each cell, the opposite neighbor (relative to its partner) must also be occupied. This forms a kind of 2-step adjacency.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717270125,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717271131,"data":"       | If we think of a placement as a permutation of cells where each cell maps to its partner (adjacent). Then condition: For each cell c, let p(c) be its partner. Then define opposite neighbor o(c) = neighbor of c in direction away from p(c). Condition: o(c) must be occupied (i.e., belongs to some pair). This defines a functional graph where each cell points to its opposite neighbor, which must be in the set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717272842,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717273868,"data":"       | Thus we need a set of cells closed under the operation o (i.e., for each cell in set, its opposite neighbor also in set). This is a closure property. The set must be a union of cycles under the mapping o (since finite). The mapping o depends on orientation of each domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717275368,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717276394,"data":"       | Thus we can think of constructing cycles of length maybe 4? Let's test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717277542,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717278558,"data":"       | Consider a 2x2 block: cells (0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1). Place two dominos: horizontal on top row (0,0)-(0,1) and horizontal on bottom row (1,0)-(1,1). For top domino, left neighbor out of board? No, left neighbor column -1 out of board? Actually column 0 left neighbor out of board (edge). Right neighbor (0,2) empty (interior). So fails. So not locked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717280194,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717281201,"data":"       | Consider arrangement of four dominos forming a 2x3 rectangle? Let's try to search for small patterns using brute force for small board (e.g., 3x3) to see minimal m and pattern. Then maybe extrapolate to 7x7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717282615,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717283754,"data":"       | Let's write a brute-force search for 3x3 board (9 cells) to find minimal m. Use exhaustive enumeration of matchings (max 4 dominos). Use python with bitmask.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717285067,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717287297,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codefrom itertools import combinations\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717288474,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717289542,"data":"       | def minimal_m_for_board(R,C, max_m=6):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717290768,"data":"       |     # generate placements list of (mask, ends_mask)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717291905,"data":"       |     placements = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717292981,"data":"       |     for i in range(R):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717294052,"data":"       |         for j in range(C-1):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717295155,"data":"       |             # horizontal\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717296235,"data":"       |             cells = [(i,j),(i,j+1)]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717297335,"data":"       |             mask = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717298398,"data":"       |             for (r,c) in cells:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717299528,"data":"       |                 mask |= 1 \u003c\u003c (r*C + c)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7173007,"data":"       |             # ends\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717301795,"data":"       |             left = (i, j-1) if j-1 \u003e=0 else None\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71730295,"data":"       |             right = (i, j+2) if j+2 \u003c C else None\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717304105,"data":"       |             ends = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717305177,"data":"       |             if left is not None:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717306262,"data":"       |                 ends.append(left)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.717307368,"data":"       |             if right is not None:\n"}
