,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718413586,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse the problem: \"Place m 1x2 dominos on a 7x7 chessboard such that no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remains fixed. Find the minimal possible value of m.\"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718415013,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718416243,"data":"       | Interpretation: We have a 7x7 board (odd dimensions). We place some number of dominos (size 1x2, covering two adjacent squares horizontally or vertically). The condition: no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remain fixed. Means each domino is \"blocked\" from moving in its orientation direction (i.e., cannot slide left/right if horizontal, up/down if vertical) because there is at least one occupied cell (by another domino) adjacent in that direction that would prevent sliding? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718418211,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718419361,"data":"       | A domino sliding horizontally: For a horizontal domino (covering squares (i,j) and (i,j+1)), it could slide left or right by one unit if the cells to its left (i,j-1) and (i,j) (or to its right (i,j+1) and (i,j+2)) are empty and the board boundaries allow. The condition \"no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remain fixed\" likely means that for each domino, any attempt to slide it (by any number of squares?) in its orientation direction is blocked by either the board edge or another domino. Typically sliding a domino means moving it parallel to its longer side, maintaining orientation, without rotating, like moving one cell at a time. So for a horizontal domino, sliding left or right requires empty cells adjacent to its current positions. Similarly, vertical domino sliding up or down.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718421835,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718422881,"data":"       | Thus we need to place dominos such that each domino is \"blocked\" from sliding left/right/up/down (depending on orientation) by either the board edge or other dominos. The minimal number of dominos needed to achieve this for the whole board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718424421,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718425458,"data":"       | Goal: minimize m such that arrangement satisfies property.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718426622,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71842764,"data":"       | We need to find minimal m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718430114,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71843155,"data":"       | Observation: For a domino to be blocked horizontally, there must be a domino occupying at least one of the cells that would be needed for sliding? Actually sliding a horizontal domino one step to the left would require the cell to the left of its leftmost cell to be empty, and the cell previously occupied by its rightmost cell would become empty. The domino would occupy cells (i,j-1) and (i,j). So to block sliding left, either j=1 (board edge) or cell (i,j-1) is occupied by another domino (or perhaps part of board not empty). Similarly for sliding right: either j+1=7 (right edge) or cell (i,j+2) occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718433682,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718434749,"data":"       | But note that sliding left also requires that the cell (i,j-1) is empty and also that the cell (i,j+1) (the rightmost cell) is empty after moving? Wait sliding left: original cells (i,j) and (i,j+1). After sliding left, cells become (i,j-1) and (i,j). So the original rightmost cell (i,j+1) becomes empty; that doesn't need to be empty before moving. So only need left cell empty and board boundary not exceeded.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718436458,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718437498,"data":"       | Thus to prevent sliding left, we need either j=1 (cannot move left) or cell (i,j-1) occupied by another domino. Similarly for sliding right, need either j+2\u003e7 (i.e., j+1=7) or cell (i,j+2) occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718438923,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718439951,"data":"       | Thus each horizontal domino must have at least one of its two neighboring cells (left of leftmost, right of rightmost) occupied or out of board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71844124,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71844229,"data":"       | Similarly vertical domino must have cell above topmost occupied/out of board, or cell below bottommost occupied/out of board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718443546,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718444583,"data":"       | Thus each domino must be \"blocked\" on both sides? Actually condition says \"no domino can slide horizontally or vertically\". Means cannot slide in either direction (both left and right for horizontal, up and down for vertical). So for a horizontal domino, both left and right adjacent cells must be blocked (occupied or board edge). Similarly for vertical domino, both up and down cells blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718446368,"data":"       | \n"}
