,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718448664,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718449693,"data":"       | Goal: place minimal number of dominos covering some squares (2 per domino) such that each placed domino has both adjacent cells (in its orientation direction) occupied (by other dominos) or outside board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718451089,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718452353,"data":"       | We can think of this as a graph problem: squares are vertices. Domino occupies two adjacent squares (edge). For each domino (edge), the two squares adjacent to its ends along same line must be occupied (by some domino). Equivalent to each domino must be part of a \"chain\" of at least three consecutive squares all occupied, with the domino being middle two? Wait consider horizontal domino covering squares (i,j) and (i,j+1). For it to be blocked left and right, we need squares (i,j-1) and (i,j+2) occupied (or edges). So the domino must be part of a length-4 segment where the outer squares are occupied (by other dominos). Could be that those outer squares are part of other dominos oriented vertically or horizontally; they just need to be occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718456236,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718457333,"data":"       | Thus each domino requires its two neighboring cells to be occupied. This suggests we need to occupy a set of cells such that every occupied cell that belongs to a domino must have its neighbor cells in the direction of the domino also occupied (or board edge). However, the neighbor cells may be part of other dominos, maybe overlapping? They cannot overlap because dominos cannot share squares. So each cell can belong to at most one domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718459136,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718460164,"data":"       | Thus we need to select a set of cells (occupied) partitioned into dominos, such that for each domino, the two cells adjacent to its ends along its orientation are also occupied (by other dominos) unless at board edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718461576,"data":"       | \n"}
