,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718471186,"data":"       | Observation: The board is 7x7 = 49 cells. We can leave many cells empty. The constraint only applies to placed dominos. So we can have isolated dominos that are blocked by board edges on both sides (e.g., a horizontal domino placed at leftmost column j=1 and rightmost column j=6? Wait horizontal domino length 2: leftmost column j=1 -\u003e cells (i,1) and (i,2). To be blocked left, left side is board edge (j=0 out). To be blocked right, need cell (i,3) occupied. So not both edges. Similarly at rightmost j=6 -\u003e cells (i,6) and (i,7). Need cell (i,5) occupied. So only one side edge. So to be blocked both sides, we need either both sides edges (impossible because length 2 cannot span entire row) or one side edge and other side occupied. So each domino must have at least one occupied neighbor cell (unless at both edges which can't happen). So each domino must have at least one neighbor cell occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718473822,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718474866,"data":"       | Thus we need to arrange dominos such that each domino has at least one adjacent occupied cell in the direction of its orientation (left or right for horizontal, up or down for vertical). Actually both sides must be blocked; if one side is edge, other side must be occupied. So each domino must have at least one neighbor occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718476481,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718477505,"data":"       | Thus we can think of building \"clusters\" where dominos are arranged in lines such that each domino is adjacent to another domino on at least one side, possibly both sides.\n"}
