,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718493883,"data":"       | Maybe we need at least three dominos to block each other in a cycle? For example, three horizontal dominos placed such that each domino's right neighbor is occupied by another domino, and left neighbor also occupied (maybe by another). But with three, you could have a row of three dominos covering columns 1-2, 3-4, 5-6, leaving column 7 empty. Then the middle domino (covering 3-4) has left neighbor (col2) occupied (by first) and right neighbor (col5) occupied (by third). So middle domino blocked both sides. The first domino has left edge blocked, right neighbor occupied (by middle). So first blocked both sides. The third domino has right neighbor edge? Actually third occupies columns 5-6, right side column7 empty (edge? column7 is edge but cell (i,7) is empty, not occupied). For sliding right, need column7 occupied or edge? Wait sliding right requires cell (i,7) (col7) to be empty? Actually for domino covering (i,5)-(i,6), sliding right would move to (i,6)-(i,7). That requires cell (i,7) empty (and board within). If cell (i,7) is empty, sliding possible. So third domino not blocked right side. So need column7 occupied, maybe by a vertical domino covering (i,7)-(i+1,7) which would occupy column7 cell. But then sliding right of third domino would be blocked because cell (i,7) occupied. However sliding left of third domino also blocked by middle domino. So third domino blocked both sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718497174,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718498213,"data":"       | Thus we might need to occupy edges with vertical dominos to block horizontal dominos at the far right.\n"}
