,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718518751,"data":"       | Observation: Occupied cells can serve as blockers for multiple dominos. For example, a single cell occupied by a vertical domino can block the left/right sides of up to two horizontal dominos (one above, one below) if placed appropriately? Actually vertical domino occupies two cells in same column. Those cells can serve as left/right blockers for horizontal dominos that are adjacent horizontally (i.e., cells to left or right). So a vertical domino can block up to four horizontal dominos (two cells each can be left neighbor of a horizontal domino on its right side, and right neighbor of a horizontal domino on its left side). Similarly horizontal domino can block vertical dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718520896,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718521901,"data":"       | Thus we need to find configuration that uses each occupied cell efficiently to block multiple dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718523142,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718524156,"data":"       | Potential minimal arrangement might be a checkerboard pattern of dominos? Let's explore via computational search using integer programming or brute force with pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718525453,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718526667,"data":"       | We have 49 cells; each domino occupies two adjacent cells. We need to choose a set of dominos (edges) such that for each selected domino, both its neighboring cells along its orientation are either out of board or occupied by some domino (i.e., belong to the set of occupied cells). Occupied cells are exactly the union of cells covered by selected dominos. So we need to ensure that for each domino (edge) e = (cell a, cell b) oriented horizontally or vertically, the cells adjacent to a on the same line beyond a (i.e., preceding cell) and adjacent to b beyond b (i.e., succeeding cell) are either outside board or belong to the occupied set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71852873,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718529742,"data":"       | Let’s formalize: Represent board coordinates (r,c) with 0-index 0..6. Horizontal domino covering (r,c) and (r,c+1). Condition: (c-1 \u003c0) or cell (r,c-1) occupied; and (c+2 \u003e6) or cell (r,c+2) occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718531188,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718532241,"data":"       | Vertical domino covering (r,c) and (r+1,c). Condition: (r-1\u003c0) or cell (r-1,c) occupied; and (r+2\u003e6) or cell (r+2,c) occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718533568,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718534588,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718537221,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718538325,"data":"       | We can formulate as integer programming: binary variable for each possible domino (there are (7*6)*2? Actually horizontal edges: 7 rows * 6 possible start columns = 42. Vertical edges: 6 rows * 7 columns = 42. So total 84 possible dominos. Choose subset.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718539795,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718540813,"data":"       | Define binary variable x_e = 1 if domino e selected.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718541926,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718542988,"data":"       | Define binary variable y_v = 1 if cell v occupied (i.e., belongs to any selected domino). Relationship: y_v = sum_{e incident to v} x_e (since each cell can belong to at most one domino). Actually we need constraint that each cell is covered by at most one domino: sum_{e incident to v} x_e \u003c= 1. And y_v = sum_{e incident to v} x_e (so y_v is 0 or 1). Could also use y_v = sum_{e incident} x_e.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718544714,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718545743,"data":"       | Then for each selected domino e, we need neighbor cells condition: For horizontal domino covering (r,c) and (r,c+1), we need (c-1\u003c0 or y_{r,c-1}=1) AND (c+2\u003e6 or y_{r,c+2}=1). So constraints: if x_e=1 then y_{r,c-1} \u003e= 1 (or automatically satisfied if out of board). Equivalent to x_e \u003c= y_{r,c-1} (if c-1\u003e=0). Similarly x_e \u003c= y_{r,c+2} (if c+2\u003c=6). For vertical: x_e \u003c= y_{r-1,c} (if r-1\u003e=0) and x_e \u003c= y_{r+2,c} (if r+2\u003c=6).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718547871,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718548915,"data":"       | Thus we can solve integer linear program to minimize sum x_e.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718550083,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71855124,"data":"       | We can use Python's pulp or ortools? Not available maybe. Could brute force with MILP using python's pulp (maybe not installed). Could use brute-force search with backtracking due to small board? Number of possible dominos 84, but we need minimal number maybe small, we can search for solutions with m from low to higher using constraint solving/backtracking.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718552868,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718553893,"data":"       | We can use python's itertools to generate combinations of dominos of size m and test constraints. Since board 7x7, minimal m unknown maybe around 7? Let's estimate lower bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718555223,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718556269,"data":"       | Each domino needs at least one neighbor occupied (unless at edge). Edge cells can be used to block. But each occupied cell can serve as neighbor for at most 4 dominos (as described). So we can bound m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718557635,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7185587,"data":"       | Let O = number of occupied cells = 2m. Each domino requires at least 2 neighbor cells (left and right) to be occupied or edge. Edge cells count as \"occupied\" for blocking but they are not cells. So each domino requires up to 2 neighbor cells, but some may be edges. So total required neighbor occupancies across all dominos is at most 2m, but each occupied cell can satisfy neighbor requirement for up to 4 dominos (since each cell can be neighbor left/right of up to two horizontal dominos (one above, one below) and neighbor up/down of up to two vertical dominos). Actually each cell can be neighbor for at most 4 dominos: as left neighbor of a horizontal domino to its right, as right neighbor of a horizontal domino to its left, as top neighbor of a vertical domino below, as bottom neighbor of a vertical domino above. So each occupied cell can serve up to 4 neighbor requirements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718563003,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718564074,"data":"       | Thus we need 2m neighbor requirements satisfied by O occupied cells each providing up to 4. So O*4 \u003e= 2m =\u003e 2m*2 \u003e= 2m? Wait O=2m. So 4*2m \u003e= 2m =\u003e 8m \u003e= 2m =\u003e always true. So not helpful.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718565685,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718566808,"data":"       | Better lower bound: each domino must have at least one neighbor occupied (unless both sides edges). But both sides edges impossible. So each domino requires at least one occupied neighbor cell. So number of occupied cells O must be at least m (since each occupied cell can serve as neighbor for at most 4 dominos, but we need at least m neighbor relationships). O \u003e= ceil(m/4). Since O = 2m, this is always satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718568597,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718569636,"data":"       | Thus trivial lower bound is m\u003e=1. Not helpful.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718570813,"data":"       | \n"}
