,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718655796,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718656866,"data":"       | Observation: Since each domino must have both neighbor cells occupied or edge, the configuration likely forms \"clusters\" where dominos are arranged in lines of length at least 3 cells (i.e., three consecutive cells occupied). For a horizontal line of occupied cells, we can place dominos covering overlapping pairs? Wait dominos cannot overlap, but we can tile a line of length L (occupied cells) with dominos covering pairs, leaving maybe one cell uncovered if L odd. For each domino within that line, its left and right neighbor cells are occupied (since the line is continuous). For end dominos at the ends of line, one side is edge (if line touches board edge) or neighbor cell outside line but maybe occupied by other orientation vertical domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718659176,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718660232,"data":"       | Thus to minimize number of dominos, we might want to create long lines of occupied cells where each domino is interior, using few dominos per occupied cells? Actually each domino uses 2 cells. In a line of length L, the number of dominos needed to cover it without overlap is floor(L/2). For L even, we can tile completely, using L/2 dominos. For L odd, we will have one leftover cell (maybe used as neighbor for other dominos). So ratio of occupied cells to dominos is 2:1 always (since each domino occupies exactly 2 cells). So cannot reduce that.\n"}
