,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718685311,"data":"       | Maybe minimal arrangement is to fill a 3x3 block of cells (9 cells) with dominos? But 9 odd cannot be tiled fully. Need even number. 3x3 block cannot be tiled with dominos fully (needs 9 cells). So not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71868671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718687985,"data":"       | What about a 2x3 rectangle (6 cells) tiled with 3 dominos. Let's test if each domino blocked: Suppose we have 2 rows, 3 columns. Place horizontal dominos covering (row1,col1-2), (row1,col3-4?) Wait only 3 columns. Actually 2x3 board: cells coordinates (1..2 rows, 1..3 columns). We can tile with three vertical dominos (each covering two rows in a column). So vertical dominos at columns 1,2,3. For each vertical domino, need occupied cell above (row0) edge? row0 out of board, so edge blocks top. Need occupied cell below row3 (since rows 1-2). Row3 is out of board (since board size 2). So both sides are edges! Wait vertical domino covering rows 1-2 (topmost rows) has top edge row0 (outside) and bottom edge row3 (outside). So both sides are edges, satisfying condition! Indeed a vertical domino placed covering the entire column of a 2-row board cannot slide up or down because board edges block both directions. So each vertical domino is blocked without need for other dominos. So on 7x7 board, a vertical domino placed covering rows 1-2 (topmost two rows) at any column would have top edge blocked (row0) and bottom neighbor cell row3 (row index 2) is empty unless occupied. Actually row indices 0..6. If we place vertical domino covering rows 0 and 1 (topmost two rows), then sliding up would need row -1 (outside) -\u003e blocked; sliding down would need row 2 cell (row index 2) to be empty to allow slide. So not blocked unless row2 cell occupied. So only when domino occupies the entire column (i.e., rows 0-6) would both sides be edges, but that's length 7 \u003e2 cannot be domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718693493,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718694553,"data":"       | Thus earlier reasoning wrong: vertical domino covering rows 0-1 not blocked on bottom side.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71869575,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718696777,"data":"       | Thus we need both neighbor cells occupied or edge. Edge only on one side for domino length 2 unless placed at board edge on both sides (impossible). So each domino must have at least one occupied neighbor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718698175,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718699201,"data":"       | Thus we need to provide neighbor cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718700327,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718701547,"data":"       | Potential minimal arrangement: create a \"pairing\" where each domino's neighbor cell is the other domino's cell (i.e., they share neighbor). For example, two horizontal dominos placed adjacent: first covers (i,1)-(i,2), second covers (i,3)-(i,4). The neighbor cell for first's right side is (i,3) occupied by second domino. The neighbor for second's left side is (i,2) occupied by first domino. However second's right side (i,5) empty; first's left side edge blocked. So second still needs right neighbor occupied. Could place third domino covering (i,5)-(i,6). Then second's right neighbor is (i,5) occupied. Third's right neighbor (i,7) edge? Actually column 7 index 6 (0-index). Domino covering (i,5)-(i,6) has right neighbor column 7 out of board, blocked. Left neighbor (i,4) occupied by second domino. So third blocked both sides. So we have three horizontal dominos covering columns 1-2,3-4,5-6. This leaves column 7 empty (index 6). Wait we used columns 0-1,2-3,4-5 (0-index). That's columns 0-5. Column 6 empty. The third domino's right side is column 6 (index 6) which is board edge? Actually board columns 0..6; domino covering columns 4-5 (indices) has right neighbor column 6 (index 6) which is within board, not edge. So sliding right would move to columns 5-6, requiring cell (i,6) empty. Since it's empty, sliding possible. So third domino not blocked on right side. So need column 6 occupied (by vertical domino perhaps). So we need additional domino(s) to block right side of the last horizontal domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718705062,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718706103,"data":"       | Thus we may need to add vertical dominos at column 6 to block.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718707254,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718708288,"data":"       | Thus minimal arrangement may involve using vertical dominos on the rightmost column to block horizontal chain.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718709553,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718710578,"data":"       | Similarly on leftmost column we have edge blocking left side.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718711731,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718712756,"data":"       | Thus we can have a chain of horizontal dominos covering columns 0-1,2-3,4-5, and then vertical dominos covering column 6 rows maybe to block the right side of the last horizontal domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.718714114,"data":"       | \n"}
