,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719957553,"data":"       | We can attempt to find a lower bound using counting argument: each domino D has 2 cells; each cell can block at most some number of directions for other dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719958856,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719959926,"data":"       | What is maximum number of requirements a single cell can satisfy? A cell can be a target for sliding of any domino that would move into that cell. Which dominos have that cell as one of the two target cells after shift? Consider a cell (r,c). It could be target for a domino that is currently at (r,c-1)-(r,c) shifting right (horizontal domino left neighbor). Also for domino at (r,c)-(r,c+1) shifting left. Similarly vertical: domino at (r-1,c)-(r,c) shifting down; domino at (r,c)-(r+1,c) shifting up. So up to 4 dominos could have this cell as one of their target cells (the domino adjacent to it on each side). Each such domino would need at most one of its two target cells occupied; occupying this cell could satisfy that requirement (maybe both target cells for that domino could be same cell? No, each direction's target cells are two cells). So each cell can satisfy up to 4 requirements (one per direction from each adjacent domino). But also a domino may have both target cells occupied by same cell? Not possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71996263,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719963688,"data":"       | Thus each occupied cell can block at most 4 directions of distinct dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719966203,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719967256,"data":"       | Thus total number of requirements R must be ≤ 4 * (2m) = 8m (since each cell can block up to 4). Actually each occupied cell belongs to a domino; but that domino's cells also can serve as blockers for others. So bound: R ≤ 8m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719968762,"data":"       | \n"}
