,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719974143,"data":"       | Let’s compute minimal possible total requirements R_min for m dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719975334,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.71997651,"data":"       | If we place all dominos at corners to reduce requirements, each domino could have 2 requirements (if placed at a corner). However board has only 4 corners; each corner can accommodate at most one domino? Actually a corner cell is a single cell; a domino occupying two cells cannot be placed entirely within a corner; but a domino can be placed covering a corner cell and adjacent cell along edge, e.g., horizontal domino at top-left corner covering (0,0)-(0,1) touches top edge and left edge, so two blocked directions (up and left). So it has 2 interior directions (right and down). So 2 requirements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719978554,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719979575,"data":"       | Thus each corner domino has 2 requirements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719980697,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719981717,"data":"       | Edge (non-corner) domino may touch one edge, giving 3 requirements.\n"}
