,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719983924,"data":"       | Interior domino gives 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719985008,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719986092,"data":"       | Thus total requirements R = 2*a + 3*b + 4*c where a = number of corner-touching dominos (i.e., dominos that occupy a corner cell and are aligned along edge). Actually there are 4 possible corner placements for each orientation? Let's list: At each corner, there are two possible domino placements that include the corner cell: one horizontal along top edge (for top-left corner) and one vertical along left edge. Similarly for each corner. So there are 8 possible \"corner dominos\". But they may overlap each other (e.g., top-left horizontal and vertical share the corner cell, cannot both be placed). So at most 4 corner dominos can be placed simultaneously (one per corner). So a ≤ 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719988363,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719991788,"data":"       | Edge dominos (non-corner) are those that touch exactly one edge (i.e., placed with one cell on edge but not corner). There are many.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719993243,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719994313,"data":"       | Now we need to find minimal m such that there exists configuration satisfying blocking constraints. Not just counting.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719995548,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.719996558,"data":"       | Maybe minimal m is 7? Let's attempt to search computationally using backtracking with pruning for small m (say up to 10). Could be feasible if we prune heavily.\n"}
