,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720009903,"data":"       | We can start with lower bound: m must be at least ? Let's compute minimal possible cells needed to block each domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720011128,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720012172,"data":"       | If we have m dominos, total occupied cells = 2m. For each domino D, its interior directions count d_i (2-4). For each interior direction, we need at least one of its two target cells occupied. So total required target cells (counted with multiplicity) = sum d_i = R.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720013686,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720014723,"data":"       | Each occupied cell can serve as target for at most 4 dominos (as earlier). So we need 2m ≥ R/4? Actually each occupied cell can serve at most 4 requirements, so total capacity = 4 * (2m) = 8m. So need R ≤ 8m. That's always true because d_i ≤4, so sum d_i ≤ 4m, which is ≤8m. So not a strong bound.\n"}
