,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720019895,"data":"       | Maybe we need to think about parity/graph theory.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72002103,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720022281,"data":"       | Consider board graph of cells adjacency (4-neighbor). Domino occupies an edge (pair of adjacent cells). Sliding condition corresponds to moving domino along same orientation direction? Actually shift left/right for horizontal domino corresponds to moving to adjacent edge sharing one cell? Wait shift left moves domino to cells (i,j-1)-(i,j). That's overlapping with original cells partially? Actually original cells (i,j)-(i,j+1). Shift left would occupy (i,j-1)-(i,j). Overlap cell (i,j) remains, but shift requires cell (i,j-1) empty. However typical sliding puzzles require entire domino to move to new cells without overlapping original cells? Usually sliding a domino one unit left would vacate the rightmost cell and occupy the left of leftmost cell. The overlapping cell is allowed because domino slides; but the condition for being able to slide is that the cell beyond the leading edge is empty. However the problem statement may assume domino moves as a rigid piece without overlapping previous cells, i.e., both target cells must be empty. Usually sliding a domino one unit left would require both cells (i,j-1) and (i,j) to be empty? Actually the domino would occupy (i,j-1)-(i,j). Since (i,j) is currently occupied by the domino itself, after moving, that cell remains occupied (by same domino). So only need (i,j-1) empty. However typical sliding puzzle constraints: a domino can slide if the two cells it would occupy after shift are empty (except for the cells currently occupied by the domino). Equivalent to requiring the cell beyond the leading edge to be empty. So the condition reduces to: For a horizontal domino, to slide left, the cell left of its leftmost cell must be empty; to slide right, cell right of its rightmost cell must be empty. For vertical domino, slide up requires cell above its top cell empty; slide down requires cell below its bottom cell empty. That's more realistic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72002805,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720029125,"data":"       | But the problem statement: \"no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remains fixed.\" Could mean the domino cannot be moved at all, i.e., cannot slide in any direction because adjacent cells are occupied or board edge. For a horizontal domino, sliding vertically would require both cells above (or below) to be empty? Actually sliding up would shift both cells up; need both cells above to be empty. So vertical slide for horizontal domino needs two cells empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72003103,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720032107,"data":"       | Thus the condition for a domino to slide in a direction is that the cells in that direction adjacent to its occupied cells are all empty (and within board). For horizontal domino sliding up, need cells (i-1,j) and (i-1,j+1) empty. Similarly down.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72003356,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72003458,"data":"       | Thus our earlier model (both target cells must be empty) is correct for vertical slide of horizontal domino, and for horizontal slide of vertical domino, need both cells empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720035911,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720036964,"data":"       | Thus we need to block each direction by ensuring at least one of the required cells is occupied (by another domino) or out of board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720038226,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720039256,"data":"       | Thus our earlier modeling stands.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720040481,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720041576,"data":"       | Now each direction for a domino corresponds to a set of 1 cell (for sliding along its orientation) or 2 cells (for sliding perpendicular). Actually sliding along orientation requires one cell beyond the leading edge (since the other cell is overlapped). Let's verify: Horizontal domino covering (i,j)-(i,j+1). Slide left: new cells (i,j-1)-(i,j). The cell (i,j) is already occupied, only need (i,j-1) empty. So condition for slide left is that (i,j-1) is empty and within board. Similarly slide right requires (i,j+2) empty. Slide up requires both (i-1,j) and (i-1,j+1) empty. Slide down requires both (i+1,j) and (i+1,j+1) empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720043653,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720044686,"data":"       | Thus we need to block each direction: For horizontal domino, left/right require single cell each; up/down require two cells each.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720045941,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720047126,"data":"       | Similarly for vertical domino, up/down require single cell each; left/right require two cells each.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720048431,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720050853,"data":"       | Thus our earlier modeling using two target cells for each direction overestimates requirement for sliding along orientation.\n"}
