,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720081554,"data":"       | Given board size 7x7, we can attempt to use MILP via `pyscipopt`? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720082705,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720083706,"data":"       | Maybe we can use `python` with `itertools` and backtracking with pruning using constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720084899,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72008592,"data":"       | Since minimal m likely small (maybe 7-10), we can attempt to search for solutions with m up to, say, 10 using recursive backtracking and pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720087216,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720088226,"data":"       | We can treat each domino placement as variable; we need to select a set of placements (size m) such that:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720089436,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720090633,"data":"       | - No overlapping cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720091717,"data":"       | - For each selected placement, for each direction, either the required cell(s) are outside board or are occupied by some selected placement (including possibly same placement? Not needed).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72009311,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720094115,"data":"       | Thus after selection, we can compute condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72009524,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720096253,"data":"       | Thus we can generate all subsets of placements of size m and test condition. For m=8, C(84,8) ~ 2.5e11 too large.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720097475,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720098517,"data":"       | But we can prune using non-overlap: we need a matching of cells. The number of ways to place m non-overlapping dominos is the number of partial domino tilings of board of size 2m cells. This is less than combinations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720100136,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720101173,"data":"       | We could generate all possible placements recursively: choose first empty cell, try placing a domino covering it (if possible) or skip it (i.e., leave empty). This is typical for enumerating tilings. Since we only need m dominos, we can stop when count reaches m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720102671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720103718,"data":"       | We can also incorporate blocking condition early: after placing some dominos, we can compute for each placed domino which directions are already blocked (by board edge or occupied cells). For directions not yet blocked, we need to ensure that future placements will occupy at least one of the required cells. This can be used for pruning: if there is a direction where required cells are both still empty and also cannot be occupied later because they are outside the remaining empty cells? Not easy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72010558,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720106607,"data":"       | But we can attempt to use BFS with heuristic and maybe find a solution with small m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720107774,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720108781,"data":"       | Let's attempt to write a recursive search that tries to place dominos and also possibly fill required cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720111605,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720112655,"data":"       | We can also treat the problem as hitting set: we need to select a set of cells (occupied) such that for each domino placed, each direction's required cells intersect occupied set (or outside). Since occupied set is exactly the cells of the dominos, we can think of selecting a set of cells that can be partitioned into domino pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72011449,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720115516,"data":"       | Thus we can first decide on a set of occupied cells O (size 2m) that satisfies: For each domino D (which must be a pair of adjacent cells within O), the required cells for each direction intersect O (or outside). But D must be a domino within O (i.e., O must contain at least one domino placement). Actually we need to choose a set of domino placements, not just cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720117178,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7201182,"data":"       | But maybe we can search over O sets using backtracking: choose cells to occupy, ensure they can be paired into dominos (i.e., graph of adjacency). That's like finding a matching.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720119525,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720120565,"data":"       | Simplify: we can search over placements directly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720121703,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720122728,"data":"       | Let's attempt to implement depth-first search with pruning based on lower bound of remaining required blocks.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720123996,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720125008,"data":"       | Define board as 7x7 grid. We'll maintain:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720126105,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720127115,"data":"       | - occupied set: set of cells already covered by placed dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720128258,"data":"       | - placements list: list of placed dominos (each as tuple of two cells and orientation).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720129461,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720130501,"data":"       | When we place a domino, we also update a list of \"unblocked directions\" for each placed domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720131773,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720132795,"data":"       | We also maintain a set of \"required cells\" that must be occupied to block those directions: for each unblocked direction, we add the set of cells that would need to be occupied (size 1 or 2). Actually we need at least one of those cells to be occupied. So we have constraints of type: for each direction, a clause (OR) of cells must be occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720134512,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720135522,"data":"       | Thus we have a SAT problem with variables for each cell (occupied). However cells are occupied only if they belong to a domino. So variable for each cell is whether it's occupied. But also need pairing constraint.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720136918,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72013792,"data":"       | We could treat as SAT with additional constraints; but we can approximate.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720139073,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720140119,"data":"       | Maybe we can use brute-force search for small m using Python's `itertools.combinations` of placements but with early pruning using non-overlap.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720142998,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720144158,"data":"       | We can generate all non-overlapping placements of size up to maybe 9 using recursive enumeration; the number may be manageable.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720145424,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720146449,"data":"       | Let's attempt to compute number of ways to place k non-overlapping dominos on 7x7 board. Use recursion to count for k up to 9. Let's try to write code to enumerate all configurations of k dominos (non-overlapping) for small k, and test condition. We'll see if we can find any configuration for each k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720147983,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720148997,"data":"       | But enumeration may still be large. Let's attempt to implement using recursion with pruning and maybe limit to k up to 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720150225,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72015126,"data":"       | We'll need to generate placements list; we can order cells row-major; at each step find first empty cell; we can either leave it empty (skip) or place a domino covering it (if possible). However we need exactly k dominos, not necessarily covering all cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720152732,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720153735,"data":"       | Thus we can recursively place dominos until we have k placed; we can also stop early if remaining cells insufficient to place remaining dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720155013,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720156023,"data":"       | Let's implement enumeration for k=6,7,8 maybe and test condition. We'll also incorporate early check: after placing all k dominos, test condition.\n"}
