,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720924919,"data":"       | We need to start with an immobile configuration. Let's construct a full tiling (24 dominos) that is immobile obviously (since board fully covered except one cell). Actually with 24 dominos we have 48 occupied cells, leaving one empty cell. That empty cell may allow some domino to slide into it? Let's test: a domino adjacent to empty cell could slide into it if the other required cell also empty (or board edge). With only one empty cell, likely no domino can slide because sliding requires either one cell (for orientation move) or two cells (for perpendicular). For a horizontal domino to slide left/right, needs one empty cell beyond its edge. If that empty cell is the single empty cell, the other required cell (overlap) is already occupied by itself, so slide possible? Wait sliding left requires the cell left of leftmost empty. If that empty cell is exactly that cell, then slide would be possible (since other cell overlapped). So a domino adjacent to the empty cell along its orientation direction could slide into it, leaving its other cell still occupied. That would be a valid slide, moving the domino into the empty cell. So a configuration with one empty cell may still have movable domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72092792,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720928956,"data":"       | Thus full tiling may not be immobile. Let's test with a specific tiling: all horizontal dominos covering rows 0-6 columns 0-5, leaving column 6 empty cells (7 empty). That's not immobile obviously.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72093043,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720931759,"data":"       | We need to find a configuration that is immobile. Perhaps we need to fill board densely enough that any empty cell is isolated such that no domino can slide into it. For a domino to slide into empty cell, the empty cell must be adjacent to the domino in the direction of sliding, and the other required cell must also be empty (if sliding perpendicular) or board edge (if sliding along orientation). For sliding along orientation (single cell needed), only one empty cell needed. So if there is any empty cell adjacent to a domino along its orientation direction, that domino could slide into it. Thus to prevent sliding, we must ensure that for any empty cell, any domino adjacent to it must be oriented perpendicular to the direction of adjacency (so sliding would need two empty cells). But still could slide perpendicular if the other cell also empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720935797,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720936859,"data":"       | Thus to block, we need to avoid having empty cells that are adjacent to a domino in the direction of its orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.720938232,"data":"       | \n"}
