,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723255689,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse problem: \"Place m 1x2 dominos on a 7x7 chessboard such that no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remains fixed. Find the minimal possible value of m.\"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723257177,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72325843,"data":"       | Interpretation: We have a 7x7 board (odd dimensions). We place some number of dominos (size 1x2 covering two adjacent squares). The condition: \"no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remains fixed.\" Means each domino is blocked from moving (sliding) in its orientation direction? Let's think: A domino can slide horizontally (left/right) if there is empty space adjacent to it in that direction, and the squares it would occupy after sliding are empty, while other dominos remain fixed (they don't move). Similarly vertical slide. So we need a configuration where each domino cannot move at all (in either direction) because there is at least one occupied square adjacent to each of its two squares in the direction of sliding? Actually for a domino to slide horizontally (assuming orientation horizontal), it would need two empty squares to its left or right (depending on direction) to shift one unit? Wait sliding a domino by one cell horizontally means it moves one square left or right, still covering two squares. For a horizontal domino covering squares (i,j) and (i,j+1). To slide left, need square (i,j-1) empty and (i,j) would become free after move? Actually after sliding left, domino would occupy (i,j-1) and (i,j). So we need (i,j-1) empty (since (i,j) already occupied). Similarly to slide right, need (i,j+2) empty. For vertical domino covering (i,j) and (i+1,j), slide up requires (i-1,j) empty; slide down requires (i+2,j) empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723263616,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72326499,"data":"       | Thus condition: For each placed domino, both possible slides in its orientation direction must be blocked (i.e., the cell beyond each end is either outside board or occupied by another domino). Also perhaps a domino could also slide perpendicular? Usually sliding only along its length? The phrase \"slide horizontally or vertically\" maybe means any direction (horizontal or vertical) irrespective of orientation. So a horizontal domino could also slide vertically (up/down) if there is empty space above both squares and below both squares? But sliding vertically would change orientation? Actually sliding a domino vertically while maintaining orientation (horizontal) would involve moving both squares up or down, still horizontal. That's allowed if both squares above are empty (for up) or both below empty (for down). So a domino could slide in either direction (horizontal or vertical) as long as after moving one unit the two new squares are empty and the board boundaries allow. So the condition is that no domino can slide in any of the four cardinal directions (by one unit) while other dominos remain fixed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723267951,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723269055,"data":"       | Thus each domino must be \"blocked\" in all four directions (if possible). For each direction, at least one of the two cells that would be newly occupied must be occupied or out of board.\n"}
