,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7235854,"data":"     [Reasoning] (8155 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723586528,"data":"       | analysisWe realized earlier that sliding a horizontal domino left only requires one new cell (the cell left of its leftmost square). Similarly sliding right requires one new cell (right of rightmost). For vertical domino, sliding up requires one new cell (above topmost), sliding down requires one new cell (below bottommost). Wait vertical sliding up moves both squares up: new positions (i-1,j) and (i,j). The topmost cell moves up to (i-1,j), bottommost moves to (i,j) which was previously occupied by topmost cell. So only need cell (i-1,j) empty. Similarly sliding down requires cell (i+2,j) empty. So each slide direction requires only one new cell, not a pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72358869,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723589732,"data":"       | But earlier we considered needing both cells above for horizontal sliding up (vertical direction). Actually sliding a horizontal domino up moves both squares up: new positions (i-1,j) and (i-1,j+1). Both cells above must be empty. So for vertical direction (up/down) of a horizontal domino, need two cells. For horizontal direction (left/right) of a horizontal domino, need one cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723591411,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723592443,"data":"       | Similarly for vertical domino: sliding left/right requires two cells (since both squares shift left/right). Sliding up/down requires one cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723593711,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723594743,"data":"       | Thus each direction may involve one or two new cells depending on orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723595907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723597131,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute for each placement and each direction the set of new cells that must be empty for slide to be possible. Then to block, we need at least one of those cells occupied (or off-board). For directions where the required set includes any off-board cell, slide is blocked automatically (since can't move partially off board). Actually if any required cell is off-board, slide impossible. For horizontal domino sliding up, both cells above must be on board; if either is off-board (i.e., top row), slide blocked automatically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723599089,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723600151,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute for each direction the list of required cells (size 1 or 2). If any required cell is off-board, direction blocked automatically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723601452,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723602458,"data":"       | Thus we need to block only directions where all required cells are on board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723603622,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723604653,"data":"       | Implementation: For each placement (cells (a,b) with coordinates (r1,c1),(r2,c2)), compute for each direction:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723605864,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723608374,"data":"       | - N: cells = set of cells directly above each occupied cell (i.e., (r-1,c) for each cell). For horizontal domino, both cells have same row, so both above cells are (r-1,c) and (r-1,c+1). For vertical domino, both cells have same column, above cells are (r-1,c) and (r,c) (since top cell and bottom cell above). Wait vertical domino covering (r,c) and (r+1,c). Above cells: for top cell (r,c) above is (r-1,c); for bottom cell (r+1,c) above is (r,c). So set = {(r-1,c), (r,c)}. That's two cells (could be same if r-1 == r? impossible). So N requires both cells empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723610366,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723611397,"data":"       | - S: cells directly below each occupied cell: (r+1,c) for each. For vertical domino, cells = {(r+1,c), (r+2,c)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723612643,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723613854,"data":"       | - W: cells left of each occupied cell: (r,c-1) for each. For horizontal domino, left cells = {(r,c-1), (r,c)}? Wait left of leftmost cell (r,c) is (r,c-1); left of rightmost cell (r,c+1) is (r,c). So set = {(r,c-1), (r,c)}. That's two cells, but note (r,c) is already occupied by domino; sliding left would move domino into cells (r,c-1) and (r,c). So the required empty cell is only (r,c-1) because (r,c) is already occupied by the domino itself before move. However after move, the rightmost cell (r,c+1) becomes empty. So we only need the new cell that becomes occupied, which is (r,c-1). So for horizontal domino sliding left, only need cell left of leftmost square. Similarly sliding right only need cell right of rightmost square.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7236161,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723617136,"data":"       | Thus our earlier set-of-cells approach double counts. So we need to compute required new cells for each direction correctly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723618366,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723619374,"data":"       | Simpler: For each placement, we can compute the cells that would be newly occupied after sliding one step in each direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723620609,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723621636,"data":"       | Method: For a placement covering cells S = {p1,p2}. For direction d, define translation vector t (dx,dy) where dx,dy are -1,0,1 for up/down/left/right. Sliding by t means each cell p moves to p + t. The new occupied set after slide is S' = {p + t for p in S}. The cells that are newly occupied (i.e., in S' but not already in S) are those cells that were not in S. Since S and S' overlap in one cell (the cell that was previously occupied by the other part) for horizontal/vertical slides? Let's compute:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723623519,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723624532,"data":"       | - Horizontal domino (cells (r,c) left, (r,c+1) right). Sliding left (t = (0,-1)): new cells are (r,c-1) and (r,c). Overlap with original S: (r,c) is common; new cell is (r,c-1). So required new cell set = {(r,c-1)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723625937,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723626952,"data":"       | - Sliding right (t = (0,+1)): new cells (r,c+1) and (r,c+2); overlap (r,c+1); new cell (r,c+2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723628152,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723629189,"data":"       | - Sliding up (t = (-1,0)): new cells (r-1,c) and (r-1,c+1); overlap none (since original row r). So required new cells set = {(r-1,c), (r-1,c+1)} (both need to be empty). Actually both must be empty for slide to be possible; to block we need at least one occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723632245,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723633298,"data":"       | - Sliding down (t = (+1,0)): new cells (r+1,c) and (r+1,c+1); required set = {(r+1,c), (r+1,c+1)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723634509,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723635514,"data":"       | Similarly for vertical domino (cells (r,c) top, (r+1,c) bottom). Sliding up (t = (-1,0)): new cells (r-1,c) and (r,c); overlap (r,c) common; new cell set = {(r-1,c)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723636826,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723638052,"data":"       | Sliding down (t = (+1,0)): new cells (r+1,c) and (r+2,c); overlap (r+1,c); new cell set = {(r+2,c)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723639271,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72364028,"data":"       | Sliding left (t = (0,-1)): new cells (r,c-1) and (r+1,c-1); required set = {(r,c-1), (r+1,c-1)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723641477,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723642488,"data":"       | Sliding right (t = (0,+1)): new cells (r,c+1) and (r+1,c+1); required set = {(r,c+1), (r+1,c+1)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723643705,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723644712,"data":"       | Thus each direction yields required set of size 1 (for slides along orientation) or size 2 (perpendicular slides). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723645962,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723647002,"data":"       | Now we can compute for each placement and each direction the required set of cells, and if any of those cells are off-board (i.e., row or col out of range), then that direction is automatically blocked (no need to occupy). So we only need to consider directions where all required cells are on board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723648567,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723649611,"data":"       | Now the condition: For each selected placement p, for each direction d where required_set is on-board, there must exist another selected placement q (q != p) such that q's occupied cells intersect required_set (i.e., at least one cell of required_set is occupied). Equivalent to union of other placements' masks intersect required_set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723651251,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723652271,"data":"       | Thus we can test a set of placements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723653395,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723654416,"data":"       | Now we need minimal number.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723655494,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723656527,"data":"       | Let's implement placements list with required_sets.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723657646,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723658654,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codeR, C = 7, 7\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723659744,"data":"       | def idx(r,c):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723660787,"data":"       |     return r*C + c\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723661888,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723662897,"data":"       | placements = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723663934,"data":"       | # orientation: 'H' or 'V'\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723665001,"data":"       | for r in range(R):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723666056,"data":"       |     for c in range(C-1):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723668735,"data":"       |         cells = (idx(r,c), idx(r,c+1))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723669854,"data":"       |         mask = (1\u003c\u003ccells[0]) | (1\u003c\u003ccells[1])\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723671123,"data":"       |         # required sets per direction\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723672239,"data":"       |         req = {}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.723673344,"data":"       |         # left\n"}
