,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725129644,"data":"       | Our recursion currently tries all possibilities for each unsatisfied direction cell, leading to combinatorial explosion.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725130913,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725131953,"data":"       | We can incorporate heuristic to choose direction with smallest number of possible placements to block, to reduce branching.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72513319,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725134256,"data":"       | For each unsatisfied direction set (size 1 or 2), we can compute candidate placements that could occupy any cell in that set. Choose the unsatisfied direction with minimal candidate placements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725135637,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725136657,"data":"       | Let's modify selection: Among unsatisfied direction sets across all unsatisfied placements, pick the one with fewest possible placements (i.e., sum of placements covering any cell in set that are still feasible). That reduces branching.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725138097,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725139157,"data":"       | Also we can precompute for each cell the list of placements that could occupy it and are compatible (non-overlap) with current occupied mask.\n"}
