,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725673464,"data":"       | Interpretation: We have a board of squares (7x7 grid). We place m dominos, each covering two adjacent squares either horizontally or vertically (size 1x2). The rest squares may be empty. The condition: No domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remain fixed. That is, each domino is \"blocked\" from moving along its orientation direction (i.e., sliding left/right for a horizontal domino, up/down for vertical) because adjacent squares in that direction are occupied or board edge? But sliding could be moving the domino by one unit (i.e., shifting its position by one square) while keeping orientation same. Typically sliding means moving the domino one unit in its long direction (so horizontal domino could slide left or right; vertical domino could slide up or down). The condition says that for each domino, there is no empty square adjacent to it along its orientation direction that would allow it to slide while other dominos fixed. More precisely, consider a domino covering squares (i,j) and (i,j+1) horizontally. It could slide left if square (i,j-1) is empty and (i,j) becomes (i,j-1) and (i,j+1) becomes (i,j). But sliding left would also vacate square (i,j+1). Actually sliding left would move the domino to cover squares (i,j-1) and (i,j). So for slide left to be possible, squares (i,j-1) must be empty (not occupied by another domino) and (i,j+1) must be empty after move? Wait the move vacates (i,j+1). That's okay. So slide left is possible if the square to the left of the leftmost cell of the domino is empty (i.e., not occupied by another domino). Similarly slide right if square to the right of the rightmost cell is empty. So to block sliding, for each domino we must have both squares adjacent in its orientation direction either outside board (edge) or occupied by another domino (i.e., part of another domino covering that square). However, note that the adjacent squares could be part of the same domino? Not possible because domino occupies two adjacent squares; the adjacent squares beyond those are not part of that domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7256793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72568037,"data":"       | Thus we need to place dominos such that for each domino, both sides along its orientation direction are blocked: either board edge or covered by another domino (i.e., the square adjacent to each end is occupied). However, there is also the possibility that a domino could slide by moving both ends simultaneously? Actually sliding a domino horizontally by one cell requires that the square to the left of the leftmost cell is empty and the square to the right of the rightmost cell is empty? Wait sliding left moves the domino left one cell; after move, the former rightmost square becomes empty, and the new leftmost square must be empty. So for slide left, need empty square on left side; the right side emptiness after slide is irrelevant. So to block sliding left, we need left side occupied or board edge. Similarly slide right need right side occupied or board edge. So each domino must be blocked on both sides (if we consider both directions) to be \"cannot slide horizontally\" meaning cannot slide either left or right. Similarly vertical domino blocked up and down.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725683247,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725684267,"data":"       | Thus the condition for each domino: for each of its two ends along its orientation, the adjacent cell beyond that end must be either occupied (by any domino) or outside board. So each domino must be \"sandwiched\" between occupied squares or edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725685773,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72568678,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominos needed to achieve this property for all placed dominos. We can leave many squares empty; but each domino must be blocked on both sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725688088,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725689462,"data":"       | We need to find minimal m such that there exists a configuration of m dominos meeting condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725690644,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72569166,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of \"blocking\" each domino with neighbors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725692869,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725693888,"data":"       | We can think of graph representation: each domino occupies two adjacent cells (edge). The condition requires that each endpoint of the edge must have an adjacent cell in the same direction that is either outside board or occupied by some domino (maybe overlapping? cannot overlap). So each endpoint must be adjacent to a cell that is covered by some domino (maybe the same domino? Not possible). So each endpoint must be adjacent to a cell that is covered by some domino (different from the given domino) or be at board boundary.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725695805,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725696818,"data":"       | Thus we can treat each cell as either empty or covered by domino. For each domino, the cells adjacent to its two ends in the orientation direction must be non-empty or out-of-bounds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725698195,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72569923,"data":"       | Thus we need to design a set of domino placements that \"cover\" these adjacent cells to block each other.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725700492,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725701522,"data":"       | Observation: If we place a domino at the board edge, one side is automatically blocked by edge. So edges help reduce needed dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725702774,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725705285,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominos. So we want each domino to be blocked on both sides using as few other dominos as possible, perhaps using board edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725706603,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72570764,"data":"       | We can attempt to find lower bound: Each domino has two ends; each end either at edge (cost 0) or must be adjacent to a cell occupied by a domino. Occupied cells belong to some domino. So each occupied cell can block at most two ends? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725709083,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725710298,"data":"       | A domino occupies two cells. Those cells can serve as blocking cells for neighboring dominos' ends. For a given domino A, its left end may be blocked by a cell that belongs to domino B (adjacent). That cell is part of B and may block multiple ends of possibly multiple dominos (if B is adjacent to multiple dominos). For example, a vertical domino could block left ends of horizontal dominos on its left side? Wait orientation difference: The blocking cell must be directly adjacent along the direction of the domino. So for a horizontal domino, the blocking cell is left of leftmost cell (same row) or right of rightmost cell (same row). So a vertical domino occupying that cell would block the horizontal domino's slide. So a domino can block ends of other dominos in orthogonal orientation as well.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725712655,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.725713667,"data":"       | Thus each occupied cell can block up to 4 ends (left/right/up/down) for dominos oriented accordingly.\n"}
