,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72573611,"data":"       | We can treat each cell can be empty or part of a domino. Represent board as 49 cells. Variables for each possible domino placement (binary). Constraints: each cell can be covered by at most 1 domino (since dominos cannot overlap). Condition: For each domino selected, for each of its two ends, the adjacent cell in its orientation direction must be either outside board (i.e., edge) or covered by some domino (selected). That means: If a domino is selected, then for its left end, if there is a cell to the left within board, then that cell must be covered by some domino (i.e., at least one selected domino covering that cell). Similarly for right end, up, down accordingly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72573824,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72573928,"data":"       | Thus we can encode constraints as linear: For each domino d, if d is selected, then for each adjacent cell a (if exists), the sum of selected dominoes covering cell a must be \u003e= 1 (i.e., at least one domino covering that cell). But note that the cell a could be part of the same domino? Not possible because a is outside the domino's two cells. So we just need that some domino covers that cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72574102,"data":"       | \n"}
