elected[-1]+1 = 5+1 =6 \u003e2, so we wouldn't consider placement 2 after required_set empty. However such configuration would have the same set of placements as [2,5] (just order swapped). Since we always consider placements in increasing order initially, we would have considered [2,5] earlier when we started with placement 2 first (if that leads to a solution). But maybe [2,5] does not satisfy constraints due to order of placement? The constraints are independent of order; only final set matters. So any solution set can be ordered in increasing index; our search will consider that ordering (by always picking placements in increasing order when required_set empty). However there is scenario where you need to place a later-index domino first to generate required cells that later a lower-index domino can satisfy. But the final set is same regardless of order; the solution set may be [5,2] but also [2,5] is same set. However does the algorithm allow [2,5] to be generated? Starting with empty required_set, we will try placement 2 as first domino (since we iterate from start_idx 0). That will produce required_set maybe; then later we can add placement 5 (since required_set may be nonempty, we allow any placement covering required cell). So yes, we can generate that set. So ordering constraint likely does not miss any solution.\n"}
