,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727403932,"data":"       | We'll use recursion adding dominoes until all requirements satisfied or we exceed k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727405115,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727406143,"data":"       | We also need to consider that adding a domino may create new requirements (its own directions) that need to be blocked. So we need to ensure final set is self-consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727407448,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72740846,"data":"       | Thus we can treat problem as searching for a fixed point: set of dominos such that each domino's directions are satisfied by set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727409712,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727410736,"data":"       | This is similar to finding a \"stable\" set under constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727411925,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727412946,"data":"       | We can attempt to use BFS of states: each state is set of dominos (represented by mask). Starting from empty set, we can iteratively add dominoes that block unsatisfied directions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727415819,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727416845,"data":"       | But we also need to ensure that after we stop adding (size \u003c=k), there are no unsatisfied directions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727418143,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727419432,"data":"       | We can treat it as SAT: variable x_i for each placement. Clause: For each i, for each dir with both target cells inside, (x_i -\u003e (x_j1 or x_j2 ...)), where x_j's are placements covering either cell. This is Horn clause (implication with disjunction). This is a Horn SAT problem, solvable in polynomial time using unit propagation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72742111,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72742211,"data":"       | Indeed each clause is Horn: (not x_i) or (x_j1 or x_j2 ...) . Since disjunction of many variables, it's not Horn? Horn clause requires at most one positive literal. Here we have many positive literals. That's not Horn. So not trivial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727423545,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727424563,"data":"       | But we can attempt to solve using SAT solver, but not available.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727425723,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727426757,"data":"       | Maybe minimal m is small enough we can brute force with pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727427902,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727428945,"data":"       | Let's attempt to implement recursion with bound k=6,7,8 etc. We'll use heuristic to choose next domino to add: pick a domino with unsatisfied direction and choose a cell to occupy (one of the target cells) and then choose a domino covering that cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727430443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727431475,"data":"       | But there could be many possibilities.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727432587,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727433599,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can use BFS enumerating all subsets of placements up to size k but with early pruning using overlap and also using requirement satisfaction check incremental.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727434925,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727435943,"data":"       | We can generate subsets recursively: at each step we maintain occupied mask and also for each placed domino, which directions are satisfied (by currently occupied cells). When we add a new domino, we update satisfaction for all dominos (including new). At any point, if any placed domino has a direction where both target cells are inside and none of them are occupied yet, that's okay because we may later occupy them. So we cannot prune based on unsatisfied yet.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727437753,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727438772,"data":"       | But we can prune if we exceed k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727439882,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727440885,"data":"       | Thus enumeration still large.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727441961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727443004,"data":"       | Maybe we can try to use integer programming via `pyscipopt`? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727444172,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72744518,"data":"       | Given difficulty, maybe minimal m is 12 or something. Let's attempt to use heuristic search to find a solution with small m, then try to see if we can reduce.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727447935,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727449165,"data":"       | We'll implement random search: start with empty set, iteratively add random domino that blocks some unsatisfied direction, continue until all satisfied, maybe also try to remove redundant dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727450561,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727451582,"data":"       | We'll run many trials to find small m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72745267,"data":"       | \n"}
