,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727645294,"data":"       | analysisIt returned 0 because my random_solution started with empty selected set and unsatisfied loop had none (since selected empty). So empty set is trivially stable (no dominos to block). But the problem asks to place m dominos such that no domino can slide; we need at least one domino? Actually m is number placed; we want minimal possible m such that configuration exists where each domino is immobile. If we place zero dominos, vacuously condition holds (no domino can slide). But problem likely expects at least one domino placed, otherwise trivial. Usually they ask for minimal number of dominos to make the configuration \"locked\", meaning each domino placed is immobile. Zero dominos trivial but not interesting. So we need m\u003e0. So we need minimal positive number.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727647713,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727648735,"data":"       | Thus need to find smallest m \u003e0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727649852,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727650875,"data":"       | Our random search returns empty set; we need to enforce at least one domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72765206,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72765308,"data":"       | Let's modify random_solution to start with one random domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.727654223,"data":"       | \n"}
