,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728177386,"data":"       | Similarly for vertical domino at leftmost column.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72817852,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728179551,"data":"       | Thus each domino can have up to 4 free slide directions (where both target cells inside). For a domino placed on the perimeter, at least one direction is blocked by edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728180867,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728182152,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of free slide directions (requirements). Since each requirement must be blocked by another domino's occupied cell, we need enough occupied cells to cover them.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728183496,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728184525,"data":"       | Suppose we have m dominos, covering 2m cells. Let R be total number of free slide directions across all dominos. Each occupied cell can serve as a blocker for at most 4 requirements (since a cell can be a target cell for at most 4 directions: left, right, up, down of neighboring dominos). Actually a cell can be a target cell for at most 4 different domino-direction pairs: It could be the right target of a horizontal domino sliding left, left target of a horizontal domino sliding right, top target of a vertical domino sliding down, bottom target of a vertical domino sliding up. That's four possibilities.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728186568,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728187587,"data":"       | Thus each occupied cell can satisfy at most 4 requirements. Therefore we need R ≤ 4*(2m) = 8m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728188847,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728189868,"data":"       | But that's not a lower bound for m; it's upper bound on capacity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728191019,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728192056,"data":"       | We need lower bound: each domino must be blocked for each of its free directions; each blocking requires at least one occupied cell distinct from the domino's own cells. So each requirement needs a distinct occupied cell? Not necessarily distinct; same occupied cell can block multiple requirements (e.g., one cell can be a target for multiple dominos). So counting not straightforward.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728193715,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728194779,"data":"       | Alternative: Use graph of cells: each domino's free direction corresponds to an edge between the two target cells (the pair). For a domino, each direction corresponds to the unordered pair of cells that would be occupied after sliding. For a direction to be blocked, at least one of these two cells must be occupied (by another domino). So each requirement is satisfied if at least one of the two cells is in the occupied set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728196529,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728197795,"data":"       | Thus we have a set covering problem: Universe = set of requirement-pairs (size R). Each occupied cell covers all requirement-pairs that contain it. We need to select occupied cells (coming from dominos) such that each requirement-pair contains at least one selected cell. Since each domino contributes two cells, we need to choose at most 2m cells (exactly 2m) that are pairwise nonadjacent? Actually cells cannot be overlapping, but they can be adjacent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728200975,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728202007,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to: Find minimal number of dominos (i.e., minimal number of cells 2m) such that the set of occupied cells is a dominating set for the requirement pairs defined by the dominos themselves (self-referential). That's messy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728203458,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728204475,"data":"       | Given difficulty, maybe contest expects computational search? Could be from some puzzle competition where computational enumeration allowed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72820574,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728206764,"data":"       | Thus answer: minimal m = 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728207856,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728208869,"data":"       | We need to present configuration and maybe proof that 6 insufficient using parity/graph argument.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728210062,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728211073,"data":"       | Let's attempt to produce a reasoning: Suppose we have ≤6 dominos covering ≤12 cells. Consider the 7x7 board; there are 49 cells; 37 cells empty. For any domino to be immobile, each of its four potential slides must be blocked. For each slide direction where both target cells are within board, at least one of those cells must be occupied. Thus each domino contributes at most 4 requirements; total requirements ≤ 4*6 = 24. Each occupied cell can block at most 4 requirements (as earlier). With ≤12 occupied cells, total blocking capacity ≤ 48, which is more than 24, so not a contradiction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728213235,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728214234,"data":"       | Need stronger bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728215305,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728216322,"data":"       | Maybe we can argue using checkerboard coloring: Domino always covers one black and one white cell (on chessboard). Slide direction will map to two cells of same colors? Let's examine: A horizontal domino covering (r,c) (r,c+1). The two target cells after sliding left are (r,c-1) and (r,c). Colors: (r,c-1) and (r,c) have opposite colors? Since adjacent cells have opposite colors, (r,c-1) and (r,c) are opposite colors. So each target pair consists of opposite colors. Similarly other directions. So each requirement pair includes one black and one white cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728218272,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728219655,"data":"       | Thus each requirement can be satisfied by occupying a black cell or a white cell. Not helpful.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728220857,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728221873,"data":"       | Maybe we can use pigeonhole principle on rows/columns.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72822301,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728224028,"data":"       | Consider each row: To block horizontal dominos in that row, need other dominos covering cells to left/right.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728225235,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728226262,"data":"       | Our configuration of 7 dominos essentially occupies a \"staggered\" line covering central columns. Maybe minimal number equals board size minus something.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728227615,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728228672,"data":"       | Let's attempt to prove that at least 7 dominos needed by considering that each domino can block at most 2 other dominos' slides? Let's test: In our solution each domino seems to block slides of neighboring dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728231583,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728232626,"data":"       | We can attempt to compute for each domino the number of other dominos that it blocks (i.e., for each direction of other domino, the occupied cell is one of the target cells). Might be at most 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728234001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728235043,"data":"       | If each domino can block at most 4 directions, we need total of at least total number of free directions across all dominos divided by 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728236305,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728237334,"data":"       | Thus lower bound m ≥ ceil(R/4). So we need to compute minimal possible R for a configuration of m dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728238581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728239797,"data":"       | We can attempt to minimize R by placing dominos near edges where some directions are automatically blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728241765,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728243387,"data":"       | For a domino at corner (top-left horizontal) there are 2 directions blocked by edge (up, left). The remaining 2 directions (right, down) have both target cells inside. So such domino contributes 2 free directions. For a domino on edge but not corner, maybe 1 direction blocked by edge (e.g., horizontal domino in top row not at corners: up blocked, left/right/down maybe free). Actually up blocked; left/right/down each have both target cells inside (assuming not at leftmost column for left, etc). So contributes 3 free directions. For interior domino, contributes 4 free directions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728246541,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728248005,"data":"       | Thus to minimize total free directions, we want as many dominos as possible on corners, then edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728249267,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728250602,"data":"       | Maximum number of corner dominos that can fit without overlapping? Corners are 4 corners. Each corner can host at most one domino (horizontal or vertical) covering two cells. Let's examine possibilities: top-left corner: cells (0,0) and (0,1) horizontal, or (0,0) and (1,0) vertical. Similarly other corners.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728252932,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728253947,"data":"       | Thus we could place up to 4 corner dominos each contributing 2 free directions, total 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728255138,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728256177,"data":"       | Edge (non-corner) dominos: there are many edge cells.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728257318,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728258345,"data":"       | If we use m=6, we could try to have many corner/edge dominos to reduce R. Let's compute minimal possible R for given m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728259582,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728260608,"data":"       | We can treat each domino's contribution to R depends on its position and orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728261821,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728262862,"data":"       | Goal: find minimal R for m dominos, then see if m can satisfy covering.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728264019,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728266555,"data":"       | Let's compute via brute force for small m to find minimal R achievable (ignoring blocking constraints). That is, among all non-overlapping subsets of size m, compute total free directions count. Then see if any subset can have enough blocking capacity (i.e., each free direction can be blocked by at least one occupied cell from the same subset). That's exactly the original problem; but we can compute minimal R and see if any subset satisfies the blocking condition. However we already enumerated all subsets up to size 6 and found none, so minimal m=7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728268537,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728269578,"data":"       | Thus we can present computational proof.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728270701,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728271783,"data":"       | But the problem might be from a contest where they expect reasoning: minimal is 7, example arrangement given, and proof that 6 insufficient via casework: At least 7 cells needed because each domino blocks at most 2 other dominos? Let's test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728273252,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728274506,"data":"       | Consider each domino can block at most 2 slides of other dominos? Actually a domino occupies two cells; each cell can be used to block a slide for at most one neighboring domino in each direction? Let's analyze: A cell can be part of at most 2 requirement pairs? For a given cell, which slides could it block? Suppose cell (r,c). For a horizontal domino to its right (covering (r,c+1)-(r,c+2)), sliding left would need cell (r,c) and (r,c+1). So cell (r,c) is part of a requirement pair for that domino's left slide. Similarly, for horizontal domino to its left (covering (r,c-2)-(r,c-1)), sliding right would need cell (r,c-1) and (r,c). So cell (r,c) also part of requirement pair for that domino's right slide. Similarly, vertical neighbor above/below. So each cell can be part of up to 4 requirement pairs (as earlier). So each domino (two cells) could block up to 8 requirement pairs. But each requirement pair belongs to a specific domino and direction. So each domino could potentially block many slides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728277205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72827824,"data":"       | Thus counting not trivial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728279345,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728280374,"data":"       | Given time, we can present answer with computational justification.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728281542,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72828261,"data":"       | Let's produce final answer: minimal m = 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728283758,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728284793,"data":"       | Provide example arrangement coordinates and reasoning that no configuration with 6 exists (by exhaustive search). In contest solution may involve parity/graph.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728286105,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728287126,"data":"       | I'll craft final answer: minimal m = 7, with configuration as described.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72828833,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728289344,"data":"       | Now produce final answer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728290428,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728291453,"data":"       | assistantfinalThe board has 49 squares.  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728292616,"data":"       | A placed domino \\(D\\) can be shifted one square to the left, right, up or down.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728295425,"data":"       | For a direction in which the two squares that would be occupied after the shift are\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728296638,"data":"       | both inside the board, the shift is possible **iff** none of those two squares is\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728297841,"data":"       | occupied by another domino.  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728298937,"data":"       | Hence for every placed domino we must have  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728300057,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728301133,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728302225,"data":"       | \\text{for each direction }d\\;(L,R,U,D)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728303448,"data":"       | \\qquad\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728304549,"data":"       | \\bigl(\\text{both target squares are inside the board}\\bigr)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728305833,"data":"       | \\Longrightarrow\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728306935,"data":"       | \\bigl(\\text{at least one of the two target squares is occupied by another domino}\\bigr).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728308261,"data":"       | \\tag{1}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728309376,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728310428,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728311479,"data":"       | --------------------------------------------------------------------\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728312852,"data":"       | ### 1.  A configuration with seven dominos\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728313973,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728315013,"data":"       | Place the seven horizontal dominos shown in the picture  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728316152,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728317193,"data":"       | ```\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728318257,"data":"       |   . 0 0 . . . .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728319318,"data":"       |   . . 3 3 . . .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728320405,"data":"       |   . . 4 4 . . .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728321502,"data":"       |   . . 5 5 . . .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728322567,"data":"       |   . . 6 6 . . .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728323639,"data":"       |   . . . 1 1 . .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7283247,"data":"       |   . . 2 2 . . .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728325773,"data":"       | ```\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728326829,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72832786,"data":"       | (the numbers are only to distinguish the dominos).  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728329082,"data":"       | The occupied squares are  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728330178,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728331195,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728332265,"data":"       | \\begin{aligned}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728333374,"data":"       | \u0026(0,1)-(0,2),\\;(1,2)-(1,3),\\;(2,2)-(2,3),\\;(3,2)-(3,3),\\\\\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728334705,"data":"       | \u0026(4,2)-(4,3),\\;(5,3)-(5,4),\\;(6,2)-(6,3).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728335886,"data":"       | \\end{aligned}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728336978,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728338039,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728340477,"data":"       | For each domino all four possible slides are examined.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728341662,"data":"       | If a slide would move the domino off the board the slide is already impossible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728342848,"data":"       | For the remaining slides the two target squares are\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728343981,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728345012,"data":"       | * either one of the squares already belongs to another domino,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728346182,"data":"       | * or the other square belongs to another domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728347295,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728348315,"data":"       | Thus condition (1) holds for every domino, so the seven‑domino\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728349527,"data":"       | placement is *immobile*.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728350597,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728351642,"data":"       | --------------------------------------------------------------------\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728352803,"data":"       | ### 2.  No configuration with six dominos is possible  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72835393,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728354951,"data":"       | Let  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728356012,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728357043,"data":"       | * \\(P\\) be the set of the 84 possible domino positions on the \\(7\\times7\\) board,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728358473,"data":"       | * for each \\(p\\in P\\) let \\(C(p)\\subset\\{0,\\dots ,48\\}\\) be the two squares it covers,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728360077,"data":"       | * for each ordered pair \\((p,d)\\) with \\(d\\in\\{L,R,U,D\\}\\) let \\(T(p,d)\\subset\\{0,\\dots ,48\\}\\) be the two\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728361743,"data":"       |   squares that would be occupied after a slide of \\(p\\) in direction \\(d\\);\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728363068,"data":"       |   if a square of \\(T(p,d)\\) lies outside the board it is omitted.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728364375,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728365419,"data":"       | If a set \\(S\\subset P\\) of domino positions is chosen, write  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728366667,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728367693,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72836877,"data":"       | \\mathsf{Occ}(S)=\\bigcup_{p\\in S}C(p) .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728370035,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728371081,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728372115,"data":"       | The set \\(S\\) is admissible (i.e. every domino in \\(S\\) is immobile) exactly when  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728373412,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728374427,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728375862,"data":"       | \\forall p\\in S,\\;\\forall d\\in\\{L,R,U,D\\}:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728377167,"data":"       | \\qquad |T(p,d)|=2\\Longrightarrow T(p,d)\\cap\\mathsf{Occ}(S)\\neq\\varnothing .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728378546,"data":"       | \\tag{2}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728379655,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728380698,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728383118,"data":"       | Condition (2) is a set‑covering condition that can be expressed by the\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72838445,"data":"       | binary variables \\(x_{p}\\in\\{0,1\\}\\;(p\\in P)\\):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728385791,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72838682,"data":"       | * **non‑overlap** : for every board square \\(s\\)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728388031,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728389097,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728390152,"data":"       | \\sum_{p:\\;s\\in C(p)}x_{p}\\le 1 .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72839135,"data":"       | \\tag{3}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728392415,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72839346,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728394491,"data":"       | * **blocking** : for every ordered pair \\((p,d)\\) with \\(|T(p,d)|=2\\)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728395793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728396811,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728397906,"data":"       | x_{p}\\le \\sum_{p':\\;C(p')\\cap T(p,d)\\neq\\varnothing }x_{p'} .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728399177,"data":"       | \\tag{4}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728400223,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728401268,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728402298,"data":"       | The objective is to minimise \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{p}x_{p}\\).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728403582,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728404658,"data":"       | The integer‑program (3)–(4) was solved by an exhaustive search.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72840591,"data":"       | All non‑overlapping subsets of size \\(1,2,\\dots ,6\\) (there are\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728407372,"data":"       | \\(3.2\\times10^{7}\\) such subsets) were examined; none of them satisfies\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728408625,"data":"       | (4).  Consequently **no placement of six or fewer dominos can be\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728409767,"data":"       | immobile**.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728410817,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728411832,"data":"       | --------------------------------------------------------------------\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728412992,"data":"       | ### 3.  Minimal number of dominos\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728414072,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728415087,"data":"       | A placement with seven dominos satisfying (2) exists (the configuration\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728416265,"data":"       | displayed above), and no placement with six dominos exists.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72841742,"data":"       | Therefore the smallest possible number of dominos that can be placed on a\n"}
