,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728988777,"data":"       | Given board 7x7, we might be able to search for minimal m up to maybe 7 or 8 using backtracking with pruning based on requirements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728992948,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728994004,"data":"       | We can incorporate requirement constraints early: when we place a domino, we can enforce that its external neighbor squares must eventually be occupied; we can keep track of which required squares are already occupied, and which are pending. If we cannot satisfy pending requirements with remaining squares, prune.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728995575,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728996591,"data":"       | But perhaps easier: Use BFS over states representing occupied set and placed edges. Since board small, we can use BFS incremental building of placements, track minimal number of edges achieving closure property.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728998018,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.728999051,"data":"       | Define state as occupied set (bitmask of 49 squares) and edges placed (matching). However number of states large.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729000295,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729001298,"data":"       | But we can use search for minimal m using iterative deepening depth-first search (IDDFS) with pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729002504,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729003538,"data":"       | Implement IDDFS: for depth limit k, attempt to place k dominos such that after placement, the closure condition holds. Use recursion as earlier but incorporate early check: after placing some edges, we can compute which external neighbor squares are already required but not yet occupied; we must be able to fulfill them later with remaining edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729005155,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729006485,"data":"       | Specifically, for each placed edge e, we have external neighbors left/right (or up/down). For each neighbor that is within board, if that neighbor is already occupied, fine. If not, we must ensure that later we will occupy it. So we can maintain a set required = set of squares that must be occupied (external neighbors not yet occupied). At any point, we must have |required| ≤ 2 * (remaining edges) because each future edge can add at most 2 new squares (its two squares). Also each required square must be free (not already occupied by other domino) else impossible because each square can be occupied only once. So we need to ensure required squares are not already occupied by other domino (but they could be later occupied by some edge that includes that square; but that would conflict because if it's already occupied, can't be used again). Wait required squares must be occupied eventually, but they cannot be already occupied (by a different domino) because then they'd already be occupied (good). Actually if required square is already occupied, then requirement satisfied; we can remove from required set. So we maintain required set of squares not yet occupied that must be occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729011052,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729012117,"data":"       | Thus at each step, after placing an edge, we update required set: add external neighbor squares that are inside board and not already occupied; if they become occupied later, we remove them.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729013488,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729014518,"data":"       | We also need to ensure that we can still match required squares with future edges respecting matching constraints. This is similar to building a set of required vertices that must be covered by future edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729015918,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729016945,"data":"       | A necessary condition: The number of required squares must be ≤ 2 * remaining edges (since each future edge can cover at most 2 new squares). Also each required square must have at least one neighbor (adjacent square) not already occupied (so that it can be paired). That's more complex.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729018495,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729019511,"data":"       | Nevertheless we can try simple pruning: if len(required) \u003e 2 * (k - placed_count), prune.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729020764,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729021808,"data":"       | Let's implement IDDFS with this pruning.\n"}
