,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729792227,"data":"       | analysisWe need to interpret problem: \"Place m 1x2 dominos on a 7x7 chessboard such that no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remain fixed. Find the minimal possible value of m.\"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729793654,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729794936,"data":"       | Interpretation: We have a 7x7 board (49 squares). We place m dominos (each covering two adjacent squares horizontally or vertically). The condition: each domino is \"blocked\" from sliding horizontally or vertically, given the other dominos are fixed. That is, you cannot move any domino (by translation) along its orientation direction (horizontal for horizontal domino, vertical for vertical domino) because there is an adjacent domino or board edge that blocks it? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729796823,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729797901,"data":"       | Dominos can slide horizontally (if oriented horizontally) or vertically (if oriented vertically). The condition \"no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remain fixed\" means each domino is immobile: it cannot be moved one unit left/right (if horizontal) or up/down (if vertical) without overlapping another domino or moving off board, assuming other dominos stay fixed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729799677,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729800771,"data":"       | Thus each domino must be \"blocked\" on both sides in its direction. For a horizontal domino covering cells (i,j) and (i,j+1) (row i, columns j, j+1). It can slide left if j-1 \u003e=1 and cells (i,j-1) and (i,j) are empty (i.e., not occupied by other domino). Since (i,j) is part of the domino, sliding left would move domino to cover (i,j-1) and (i,j). Similarly slide right to (i,j+1) and (i,j+2). For the domino to be immobile, at least one of left or right must be blocked? Actually the phrase \"no domino can slide horizontally or vertically\" might mean cannot slide in either direction. For a horizontal domino, sliding vertically is irrelevant (since orientation is horizontal). But maybe they consider that a horizontal domino could slide vertically as a whole (i.e., translate up or down) while staying horizontal? In typical sliding puzzles, a domino can move in its orientation direction only (like a 1x2 piece sliding along its long side). Usually a domino can slide only along its long axis. The phrase \"slide horizontally or vertically\" maybe means any domino can slide in either direction (like a piece that can move both ways). But a domino oriented horizontally can slide horizontally; a vertical domino can slide vertically. So condition: each domino cannot move in its allowed direction because blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729804183,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729805421,"data":"       | Thus for each domino, both adjacent cells in its direction must be occupied or out of board. However, maybe only need one side blocked? Let's parse: \"no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remains fixed.\" If a domino could slide left, that would be sliding horizontally (since orientation horizontal). So to prevent sliding horizontally, you need at least one side blocked? Actually sliding left is horizontal movement; sliding right also horizontal. If only left is blocked but right empty, the domino could slide right. So to prevent any sliding, both sides must be blocked. Similarly for vertical domino both up and down blocked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729808998,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729810043,"data":"       | Thus each domino must have both neighboring cells (in its direction) either occupied (by other domino) or off board (edge). So each domino must be \"sandwiched\" between either board edge or another domino on both sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729811531,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729813399,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominos placed while satisfying that each placed domino is blocked both sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729814613,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729815637,"data":"       | We can leave empty squares elsewhere. The board is 7x7. We need to place m dominoes covering 2m squares, leaving 49-2m empty squares.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729816893,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729817918,"data":"       | We need to find minimal m such that we can arrange m dominos each blocked both sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729819107,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729820134,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of \"domino packing with no moves\" problem.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729821339,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729822404,"data":"       | We need to find arrangement with minimal dominos meeting constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729823561,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729824619,"data":"       | We can attempt to search computationally: brute-force search for placements of dominoes (up to certain number) that satisfy condition. Since board size 7x7, there are 49 squares, 2*? placements. Exhaustive search for m minimal maybe doable with backtracking.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.7298261,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729827123,"data":"       | But we need to be careful about combinatorial explosion. But minimal m likely relatively small, maybe 7 or something.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729828366,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729829393,"data":"       | Let's attempt to brute-force search for placements with m up to maybe 10-15 using Python with backtracking and pruning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729830635,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729831886,"data":"       | Define board cells coordinates (0-6,0-6). Domino placements: each domino can be horizontal covering (r,c) and (r,c+1) for c=0..5; vertical covering (r,c) and (r+1,c) for r=0..5. So total possible domino positions = 7*6*2 = 84? Actually horizontal: 7 rows * 6 columns = 42; vertical: 6 rows *7 columns = 42. So total 84 possible positions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72983349,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729834528,"data":"       | We need to select a subset of these positions such that they are non-overlapping (disjoint cells) and each selected domino is blocked both sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729835803,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729836833,"data":"       | Define for each domino position its two cells. For each orientation, define neighbor cells in direction: For horizontal domino covering cells (r,c) and (r,c+1). Left neighbor cell: (r,c-1) if c\u003e0 else edge. Right neighbor cell: (r,c+2) if c+2\u003c7 else edge. For vertical domino covering (r,c) and (r+1,c). Up neighbor: (r-1,c) if r\u003e0 else edge. Down neighbor: (r+2,c) if r+2\u003c7 else edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729840025,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729841063,"data":"       | Condition: For each domino, both neighbor cells must be either occupied by some other domino (i.e., part of selected set) or outside board (edge). Not empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72984237,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729843404,"data":"       | Thus we need to ensure for each selected domino, the set of neighbor cells (two) are not empty squares.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729844606,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729845633,"data":"       | Implementation: after selecting a set of dominoes, we can compute occupied cells set. Then for each domino, check neighbor cells: if neighbor cell inside board and not occupied, condition fails.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72984699,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729848015,"data":"       | Goal: find minimal m such that there exists a selection.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729849155,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72985018,"data":"       | We can search increasing m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729851253,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729852278,"data":"       | We need to find minimal m. Since board is odd sized 7x7, cannot tile fully; but we are not trying to tile, just placing some dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729853557,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729854589,"data":"       | Edge blocking: a domino at board edge may have neighbor out of board, considered blocked. So edge can help reduce needed other dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72985585,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729856856,"data":"       | Thus we can perhaps place dominoes along edges to block them.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729857993,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729859041,"data":"       | Goal minimal number. Could be as low as maybe 5? Let's test via brute force search using Python with integer programming maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729860303,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729861522,"data":"       | We can formulate as SAT/ILP: binary variables for each of 84 possible domino placements. Constraints: each cell can be covered by at most one domino (i.e., sum of variables of dominoes covering that cell \u003c=1). For each selected domino variable x_i, we need that for each neighbor cell (left/right for horizontal; up/down for vertical) that is inside board, there must be some domino covering that cell (i.e., sum of variables covering that neighbor cell \u003e= 1). However neighbor cell may be covered by the same domino? Actually neighbor cells are distinct from the domino's own cells (since they are one cell away). So we need at least one domino covering each neighbor cell. This is a covering condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729863763,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729864793,"data":"       | Thus we can set constraints: For each domino i, for each neighbor cell n inside board, we require x_i \u003c= sum_{j covering cell n} x_j . This ensures that if domino i is placed (x_i=1), then neighbor cell n must be covered by some domino (maybe the same as i? can't because i doesn't cover n). So we need at least one domino covering each neighbor cell.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729867993,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729869038,"data":"       | But this also requires that neighbor cells can be covered by any domino, not necessarily distinct for each neighbor. That's fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729870286,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729871291,"data":"       | Now we want to minimize sum x_i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729872409,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729873443,"data":"       | That's a 0-1 integer linear programming problem with 84 variables, constraints: 49 cell capacity constraints (\u003c=1) plus for each domino up to 2 neighbor constraints (x_i \u003c= sum_{j covering neighbor}). That's manageable.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729874911,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729875942,"data":"       | We can solve using Python's pulp or ortools, but we may not have those libraries. However we can use brute-force search with pruning, maybe use python's itertools combination for small m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729877271,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729878313,"data":"       | But there are many combos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729879374,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729880394,"data":"       | Alternative: use BFS search adding dominos gradually and checking constraints local.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729881558,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729882594,"data":"       | Given minimal m maybe small, we can brute force combinations of up to maybe 10 dominos among 84 possibilities. Number of combos C(84,10) ~ 1.6e12 huge, not feasible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729883908,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729884908,"data":"       | Thus need smarter search using ILP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729885981,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729886998,"data":"       | We can implement ILP using python's pulp (maybe not installed). Could use `python-mip`? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729888223,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729889479,"data":"       | Maybe we can use `sympy`'s `linsolve`? Not for integer constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729890671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729891748,"data":"       | Could use `itertools` with backtracking: recursively place dominos, maintain occupancy, and also maintain that any placed domino's neighbor cells must be covered eventually (maybe later). This is like constraint satisfaction where neighbor cells must be covered by some domino (maybe later). So we can enforce that after final placement, condition holds. During search we can prune if impossible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729893429,"data":"       | \n"}
