,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729902936,"data":"       | Maybe we can think combinatorially: each domino needs two neighbor cells (maybe same cell if board is 1 wide? Not). So each domino requires covering of its neighbor cells, which may be shared between multiple dominos, reducing count.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729904384,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729905393,"data":"       | Goal minimize dominos =\u003e maximize sharing of neighbor cells among dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729906594,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729907634,"data":"       | Edge cells provide free blocking (no neighbor required). So we might place dominoes adjacent to edges so that one side is edge (blocked) and other side blocked by another domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729908963,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729909971,"data":"       | Thus each domino may need only one neighbor domino if placed at edge (the other side edge). So we can chain dominoes along edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729911233,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.72991251,"data":"       | Example: place a horizontal domino at row 0 (top edge) covering cells (0,0)-(0,1). Its left neighbor out of board (blocked), right neighbor (0,2) must be occupied by another domino. That could be horizontal covering (0,2)-(0,3). Continue chain along top row. At the far end, the last domino may have its right neighbor out of board, left neighbor blocked by previous domino. So we can cover top row with a chain of horizontal dominoes covering entire row length 7, but 7 is odd, can't tile fully with 1x2. So we need to leave a single cell empty somewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729914488,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.729915496,"data":"       | But we could also place vertical dominoes along left column similarly.\n"}
