,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731206206,"data":"       | Now we need to verify that indeed no solution with 7 dominos exists. The search attempted all possibilities via DFS with pruning, but we must ensure algorithm correctly explores all possibilities. It uses recursion with heuristic ordering; but may miss some because of candidate selection limited to dominos covering required cells when required_set nonempty. That heuristic may prune valid solutions where you place a domino that does not cover any required cell but later a domino covers it. However we also allow such placements when required_set is empty. But once required_set becomes nonempty, we restrict to only dominos covering a required cell. Could there be a situation where you need to place a domino that does not directly cover a required cell, but creates new required cells that later satisfy others? For example, you might place a domino that creates neighbor requirements that are later satisfied, but you could also place a domino that doesn't cover any current required cell but still needed to eventually satisfy them. However if required_set is nonempty, you already have some cells that need to be occupied. Any further placement that does not occupy any of those required cells will leave those required cells still unsatisfied, and you will have less remaining capacity. It might still be possible to later satisfy them, but you could also place a domino that doesn't cover them now but later you cover them. However you could also postpone covering required cells and place other dominos that create new requirements, potentially increasing total required cells, which may be okay as long as capacity remains. So restricting to dominos covering required cells might cut off some valid solutions where you need to place a domino that doesn't cover any required cell yet but is necessary to create a structure that eventually covers them. But is that ever needed? Suppose we have a required cell X that must be