,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731213,"data":"       | We can define required cells after placing some subset of S as the set of neighbor cells of placed dominos not yet occupied. Starting with empty set, required set is empty. If we place a domino from S that does not cover any required cell (since required is empty initially), that's allowed. After that, required set may include neighbor cells. Eventually we need to place dominos covering those required cells. So there may be a solution where the first domino is not covering any required cell (since none). That's okay; our algorithm allows any domino when required_set empty. After first placement, required set may be nonempty; then we restrict to dominos covering required cells. This may still allow us to find the solution because we can place any domino that covers a required cell. Could there be a case where after some placements, the required set includes cells that can only be covered by a domino that also introduces new required cells that are not yet covered, and you may need to place a domino that does not cover any current required cell but is needed to later satisfy them? But you could always place the needed covering domino later; you could postpone it. The only issue is capacity: you have limited remaining dominos. Adding extra dominos that don't cover required cells consumes capacity and may prevent solution. So any solution that uses such extra dominos could be reordered to place covering dominos earlier without affecting feasibility, because the set of dominos is the same. So we can always reorder placements to prioritize covering required cells when possible, without breaking constraints (since covering required cells earlier only reduces required set earlier). So heuristic should not miss any solution.\n"}
