,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731491513,"data":"       | analysisWe have to interpret problem. We have a 7x7 chessboard (odd dimension). Place m 1x2 dominos (covering two adjacent squares) such that no domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remain fixed. Means each domino is \"blocked\" from moving in its orientation direction? Let's parse: Domino placed covers two adjacent cells. A domino can slide horizontally (if oriented horizontally) or vertically (if oriented vertically) by moving one cell left/right or up/down while still covering empty cells? The condition: No domino can slide horizontally or vertically when other dominos remain fixed. So each domino must be \"blocked\" from moving in its direction due to other dominos occupying necessary cells. Equivalent to each domino must have at least one of its two possible slide directions blocked? Wait sliding: A domino can slide along its long axis; horizontal domino slides left or right (by one cell), vertical domino slides up or down. So to prevent any sliding, each domino must be blocked on both sides? Actually if there is empty cell adjacent to one side, domino could slide into that empty cell (if other side also empty after movement). For a domino to slide, both squares of destination must be empty (since domino moves as a whole). So for a horizontal domino occupying cells (i,j) and (i,j+1), sliding right would require cells (i,j+2) empty (and also (i,j+1) vacated?). Actually sliding right by one moves domino to cells (i,j+1) and (i,j+2). Since (i,j+1) is currently occupied by the domino, after movement it remains occupied; the new cell required is (i,j+2). So the condition for sliding right is that (i,j+2) is empty and also (i,j) is empty after leaving? but that cell becomes empty after sliding; not required initially. So only need the new cell empty. Similarly sliding left needs (i,j-1) empty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731497237,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731498335,"data":"       | Thus each domino must have both adjacent cells along its orientation blocked (occupied) or off board, to prevent sliding both directions. For vertical domino at (i,j) and (i+1,j), need cells (i-1,j) and (i+2,j) either occupied by other domino or off board.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731499827,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731500913,"data":"       | Goal: place minimal number of dominos such that every placed domino is blocked both sides. No requirement about empty cells that are not used; they can be left uncovered.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731502234,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731503297,"data":"       | Thus we need to find minimal m such that we can place m dominos on 7x7 grid where each domino is blocked on both sides (by either board edge or another domino). The dominos can be oriented either way.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731504685,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731505726,"data":"       | We need to minimize number of dominos while still achieving blocking condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731506913,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731507967,"data":"       | Observation: Each domino needs at least one neighbor (adjacent along its axis) to block each side. So each domino requires at least 2 neighboring occupied cells (maybe the same cell could block side for two dominos?). But overlapping constraints could reduce count.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731509484,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731510558,"data":"       | We can think of graph representation: cells are vertices; placing a domino corresponds to covering an edge (adjacent cells). For a domino oriented horizontally, we need that the two cells to left of its leftmost and right of its rightmost are occupied (by other dominos). Those cells are also vertices possibly part of other dominos (either orientation). Off board counts as occupied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731512232,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.73151326,"data":"       | Goal: select a set of edges (dominos) covering some cells, such that for each selected edge, its two neighboring edges along same line are either selected or board edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731514589,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731515864,"data":"       | But note neighboring cells could be part of a domino oriented perpendicular; e.g., a cell might be occupied but part of a vertical domino; that would block sliding for a horizontal domino adjacent to it.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731518325,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731519369,"data":"       | Thus we just need those neighboring cells to be occupied, irrespective of orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731520558,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731521623,"data":"       | Thus we need to place dominos to occupy cells such that each domino's two cells adjacent along its direction are also occupied (by any domino). This is like a domination condition.\n"}
