,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731974773,"data":"       | Goal: minimize number of dominos = occupied cells/2. Occupied cells may be as few as needed to provide blocking.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731976002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731977065,"data":"       | We need each domino's non-edge side occupied. So we need at least one \"link\" per domino (except those whose both sides are edges, which would be at corners maybe). Can we place a domino in a corner such that both sides are board edges? For a horizontal domino at top-left covering (0,0)-(0,1): left side off board, top side not relevant; its right side neighbor (0,2) not edge. So not both edges. For vertical domino at top-left covering (0,0)-(1,0): up side off board, left side off board? Wait vertical domino's left side not considered; only up/down. So up side off board, down neighbor (2,0) not edge. So not both edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731980906,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731981973,"data":"       | Thus no domino can have both sides off board because board edges are only one direction for each orientation; the other side always internal unless domino placed at opposite edge? For horizontal domino placed at leftmost column covering (i,0)-(i,1): left side off board, right side internal unless at column5-6? Actually right side is column2. So not both edges. Similarly a horizontal domino placed at columns5-6 has right side off board, left internal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731983781,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731984826,"data":"       | Thus each domino at least needs one occupied neighbor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731985961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731987231,"data":"       | Thus total number of required neighbor relations equals m (since each domino needs exactly one occupied neighbor (the internal side) because other side may be edge). However some dominos may have both sides internal (need two occupied neighbors). But we can aim to arrange each domino to have exactly one side at edge. So each domino requires exactly one occupied neighbor (the internal side). So total required occupied neighbor cells count is m. Each occupied neighbor cell belongs to some domino; each domino contributes two cells that could serve as neighbor for up to 2 other dominos (one per its cells). Since each domino has two cells, it can serve as neighbor for at most two other dominos (one per cell). If each domino serves as neighbor for exactly one other domino (to satisfy its need), then we would need at least m dominos to provide m neighbor slots; each domino can provide at most 2 slots, so maybe we could have fewer? But each domino must also need neighbor. This is reminiscent of matching problem.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731995088,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731996168,"data":"       | If each domino provides neighbor slots to exactly one other domino, then total neighbor slots provided = m (since each domino provides one). This equals total required neighbor slots = m. So feasible. This suggests we could arrange a pairing where each domino's internal side is occupied by exactly one other domino's cell, and each domino's cell serves as neighbor for exactly one other domino.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731997878,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.731999255,"data":"       | Thus we could have a configuration where dominos are paired in a \"chain\" that loops back to edge at both ends? Wait each domino needs neighbor on one side; that neighbor is a cell of another domino. That other domino also needs neighbor on its internal side, maybe the first domino's other cell. Could be a 2-cycle? Let's see: Domino A needs neighbor on its right side cell B; Domino B needs neighbor on its left side cell A. This would create two dominos adjacent to each other, each touching edge on opposite sides. Example: Horizontal domino A at columns 0-1 (left edge off board). It needs right neighbor (cell column2) occupied by domino B. Domino B could be vertical covering cells (0,2)-(1,2) perhaps. B's left neighbor (cell (0,1)) is occupied by A (good). B's other side (down neighbor (2,2)) must be edge? Not edge. B vertical so needs up/down neighbor; up side off board? B's up neighbor is ( -1,2 ) off board because top row row0, up neighbor off board, good. So B's up side off board, down neighbor (2,2) empty =\u003e not blocked. So B would need another domino to block down side, unless we orient B horizontally? Let's try orientation: Domino B horizontal covering (0,2)-(0,3). Then B's left neighbor (0,1) occupied by A, right neighbor (0,4) empty need block. So not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732004125,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732005174,"data":"       | Thus a 2-cycle may not satisfy both sides edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732006321,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732007393,"data":"       | Thus maybe need longer chain where ends both have edge block. That's like a path of dominos where each internal domino uses neighbor on both sides (occupied by previous and next). Ends have edge on one side and neighbor on other side.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732008841,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732009875,"data":"       | Thus total dominos = number of internal dominos + 2 ends.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732011027,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732012086,"data":"       | If we make internal dominos have both sides occupied, they need two neighbors each. So each internal domino consumes two neighbor slots. Ends each consume one neighbor slot.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732013425,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732014446,"data":"       | Total neighbor slots needed = 2*internal + 2*1 = 2*internal +2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732015597,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732016643,"data":"       | But each domino provides up to 2 neighbor slots (its cells). If we design that each internal domino provides two neighbor slots used by its neighbors, and ends provide one neighbor slot each (used by neighbor). So total slots provided = 2*total dominos = 2*(internal+2). This is greater than needed, meaning we may have surplus.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732018259,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732019274,"data":"       | Thus minimal m could be as low as 2? but we earlier argued impossible.\n"}
