,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732051156,"data":"       | Let's try to think of a more efficient pattern: perhaps place dominos forming a \"spiral\" that covers a path from edge to edge, each domino blocked on one side by previous domino, the other side by next domino, except the start and end blocked by edge. That's essentially a simple path covering cells without gaps and without overlapping, but each domino's cells are not contiguous along path (since path would have overlapping cells). However we could use domino orientation alternately to make a path where cells of dominos are spaced by one cell gaps that are occupied by next domino? Wait earlier we considered chain of domino placements where each domino's outward cells are occupied by neighbor domino. For that to happen, the neighbor domino's cells must be exactly those outward cells. That requires the neighbor domino to be offset by 1 cell relative to the first domino (i.e., shifted by one cell). But that leads to overlap as previously seen? Let's examine:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732053838,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732054886,"data":"       | D1 horizontal covering cells (c, c+1). D2 must occupy cells (c-1) and (c+2). To be a domino, those cells must be adjacent. Are c-1 and c+2 adjacent? No, distance 3. So impossible. So a domino cannot simultaneously block both sides of another domino. So each domino must rely on two distinct dominos to block its left and right sides, not a single domino for both.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732056559,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732057596,"data":"       | Thus each domino needs two distinct neighbor dominos (unless edge). So each internal domino needs two neighbor dominos, each providing one of its sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732058894,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732061118,"data":"       | Thus the adjacency graph where vertices are dominos and edges represent that one domino blocks a side of another (directed perhaps). Since each domino needs two incoming edges (left and right) from other dominos (or edge). If edge counts as source, we can treat board edge as \"dummy\" vertex providing blocking for any side that touches edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732062805,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732063858,"data":"       | Thus we need to assign each domino's two sides to either edge or other dominos, ensuring each domino provides its cells to block at most two sides of other dominos (since each of its cells can block at most one side of some adjacent domino). Indeed each occupied cell is adjacent to at most one side of a neighboring domino (the side facing it). For horizontal domino, its rightmost cell is adjacent to the left side of the domino to its right (if any). Its leftmost cell is adjacent to right side of domino to its left (if any). For vertical similarly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732065793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732066811,"data":"       | Thus each domino can provide at most two \"blocks\": one to a neighbor on its left side (using its leftmost cell) and one to a neighbor on its right side (using its rightmost cell). Therefore each domino can block at most two other dominos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.73206835,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732069911,"data":"       | Thus we can view each domino as providing up to two \"outputs\" (blocks). The requirement is that each domino receives two \"inputs\" (blocks) from other dominos or edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732071436,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732072646,"data":"       | Edge can provide infinite blocks (since board edge can block many dominos side). For a given side (e.g., left side of a horizontal domino at column0), that's blocked by edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732073996,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732074998,"data":"       | Thus we can aim to minimize number of dominos by using many edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.73207617,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732077239,"data":"       | Observation: each domino at edge uses edge for one side, needs only one internal block. So edges reduce need for internal blocks.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732078469,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732079475,"data":"       | Thus to minimize m, we should place each domino such that it touches edge on one side, using edge as a free block. Then each domino needs exactly one neighbor domino to block its other side.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732080845,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732081846,"data":"       | Thus each domino must be paired with another domino that provides its needed block. Since each domino can also provide a block for at most one neighbor (using its far cell). So we can pair dominos: D1's internal side blocked by D2; D2's internal side blocked by D1 perhaps? That would be mutual block, satisfying both with each other providing one block each. That could work if orientation and positions allow.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732083546,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732084556,"data":"       | Thus we could have two dominos placed adjacent such that each's internal side is the cell of the other. Example: D1 horizontal at left edge columns 0-1 (left side edge). Its right neighbor cell column2 should be occupied by D2. D2 could be vertical covering column2 rows 0-1? That would occupy cell (0,2) but not (0,2) also left neighbor of D2? D2 vertical's up side edge off board (since row0) good. D2 needs down neighbor cell (2,2) occupied by something else (maybe D3). So D2 still needs another block, not satisfied by D1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":19207.732089857,"data":"       | \n"}
